{"input": "", "answers": ["The Occupy LA encampment got a stay of execution this morning—though a few arrests have been reported beyond the boundaries of City Hall Park. Two hours after protesters defied city hall's order to vacate the park by 12:01am, lawyer and Occupy supporter Jim Lafferty told Reuters he expected police to give the camp a two-day reprieve. At around 5am, police told protesters over a loudspeaker that they didn't intend to clear the park, the LA Times reports. Police do, however, intend to clear protesters out of the street, and several people who refused to move out of an intersection have already been arrested. \"We definitely need to get morning traffic in,\" a police commander explained. Everything also seems calm at Occupy Philadelphia, where protesters defied a 5pm Sunday deadline to disperse. But as of last night, a police inspector said they didn't intend to forcibly evict anyone. \"We look forward to working with Occupy Philadelphia and a resolution of the problem,\" he told the Philadelphia Inquirer. \"Confrontation is never good.\""], "length": 2099, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Occupy Protests Continue Across the US**\n\nThe Occupy movement, which began in New York City in September, has spread to several cities across the US, with protests taking place in Philadelphia, Los Angeles, and other locations. The protests, which aim to highlight economic inequality and corporate greed, have been met with varying degrees of resistance from law enforcement.\n\nIn Philadelphia, Occupy Philly protesters remained on site at City Hall into the evening of November 28, despite a deadline set by Mayor Nutter for them to leave. The protesters, who have been camping out on the \"front lawn\" of City Hall, have been serving soup and handing out water to passersby and chanting slogans such as \"What does democracy look like? This is what democracy looks like.\" Police have been present but have not attempted to evict the protesters, with Chief Inspector Joseph Sullivan stating that \"confrontation is never good\" and that the police are working to find a resolution to the situation.\n\nIn Los Angeles, the Occupy LA encampment at City Hall Park was evicted by police on Monday morning, after a midnight deadline for protesters to vacate the area. However, police commanders later stated that they would permit the encampment to stay until at least daybreak, and it is unlikely that the camp will be forced to shut down on Monday. Four demonstrators were arrested during the brief confrontation with police.\n\nThe Occupy movement has been met with varying degrees of support from local officials and residents. In Philadelphia, some residents have expressed support for the protesters, while others have criticized their presence as a nuisance. In Los Angeles, Mayor Antonio Villaraigosa has been relatively accommodating to the Occupy group, but has ultimately decided to evict them from City Hall Park.\n\nThe protests have also drawn attention from national leaders and celebrities. Hip-hop mogul Russell Simmons tweeted to Mayor Nutter on Sunday night, urging him to show restraint and avoid using force against the protesters. The protests have also been covered by major news outlets, with many reporting on the peaceful nature of the demonstrations and the willingness of law enforcement to work with the protesters.\n\nOverall, the Occupy movement continues to gain momentum across the US, with protests taking place in cities from coast to coast. While the movement has faced resistance from law enforcement, it remains a powerful symbol of resistance to economic inequality and corporate greed."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["In our own obituary of AK-47 inventor Mikhail Kalashnikov last month, we included this quote: The rifle was invented \"for the protection of the Motherland,\" he said. \"I have no regrets and bear no responsibility for how politicians have used it.\" It turns out \"no regrets\" might not have exactly been true. The AFP picks up a report from Russia's Izvestia that Kalashnikov penned a letter to the head of the Russian Orthodox Church nine months before his December death in which he wrote of \"unbearable ... spiritual pain\" and asked about his culpability. It contains this line: \"I keep having the same unsolved question: if my rifle took away people's lives, then can it be that I ... am guilty for people's deaths, even if they were enemies?\" AFP notes that Izvestia ran a copy of the letter, which is typed on Kalashnikov's personal stationery and signed \"with a wavering hand.\" A rep for Patriarch Kirill confirms that the letter was sent, and says the church leader wrote a reply—one that very may well have calmed his fears. Says the rep, \"The Church has a very definite position: when weapons serve to protect the Fatherland, the Church supports both its creators and the soldiers who use it.\" But the BBC notes that it's not clear whether every word came from Kalashnikov: Izvestia reports that his daughter believes a priest helped him write the letter."], "length": 1377, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Mikhail Kalashnikov, the inventor of the AK-47 assault rifle, has expressed remorse for the deaths caused by his invention in a letter to the head of the Russian Orthodox Church before his death. The letter, published by Izvestia, reveals Kalashnikov's spiritual pain and guilt over the many lives lost due to his rifle. In the letter, Kalashnikov asks if he can be held responsible for the deaths caused by his weapon, despite being a Christian and an Orthodox believer. He also expresses his desire to defend his country, not just for its own sake, but also to protect its people from harm.\n\nKalashnikov's letter was written in May 2012, and it is unclear how much of it he wrote himself. His daughter, Elena, believes that a priest helped her father compose the letter. The Russian Orthodox Church has received Kalashnikov's letter and has written a personal reply, stating that the Church supports both its creators and the soldiers who use the weapon, as long as it serves to protect the Fatherland.\n\nKalashnikov's legacy is complex, with over 100 million AK-47 rifles sold worldwide, making it one of the most widely used and recognizable weapons in the world. Despite his fame and accolades, Kalashnikov made little money from his invention and was known to be a humble man. His funeral was attended by Russian President Vladimir Putin, who praised Kalashnikov's contributions to Russia's defense.\n\nThe publication of Kalashnikov's letter has sparked a debate about the morality of his invention and the responsibility that comes with creating weapons of war. It also highlights the complex relationship between the Russian Orthodox Church and the state, with the Church seeking to consolidate its power and influence in the post-Soviet era."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Unnamed officials say TSA officer Gerardo Hernandez lay bleeding for 33 minutes at LAX before being taken to an ambulance. For 28 of those minutes, alleged shooter Paul Ciancia was in police custody, though officers hadn't yet declared the area safe to enter. Officers checked on Hernandez, who was just 20 feet from an exit, then moved on while paramedics waited 150 yards away, the AP reports, noting it's not clear if immediate attention might have saved Hernandez's life. Now under review: the conversations took place between police and fire commanders to determine when it was safe enough to enter, and whether paramedics could have gone into the terminal earlier. While an officer who checked on Hernandez about 5 minutes after he was shot told numerous officers he was dead, the AP reports that it's not known whether that officer had the training needed to make that determination; no first-aid was administered. \"I basically think there's a lack of coordination between entities at this airport. That lack of coordination may have led to something that shouldn't have happened,\" an LAX employee says. \"We may be talking about Officer Hernandez as a survivor.\" The results of the investigation could be months away, the AP adds. Ciancia was on Tuesday upgraded from critical to fair condition at UCLA Medical Center."], "length": 1422, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "On November 1, 2013, a gunman opened fire at Los Angeles International Airport (LAX), killing one Transportation Security Administration (TSA) officer, Gerardo Hernandez, and wounding three others, including two other TSA officers. The suspect, Paul Ciancia, was shot by airport police officers and taken into custody. However, despite being shot four times, Ciancia remained in critical condition for several days.\n\nThe investigation revealed that paramedics waited 150 yards away from the terminal due to police not declaring it safe to enter, resulting in Hernandez bleeding out for 33 minutes before being wheeled out to an ambulance. This delay raised concerns about the lack of coordination between police and fire officials, which may have prevented timely medical attention for Hernandez and other victims.\n\nCiancia, who had a note in his bag stating his intention to \"kill TSA\" and stir fear in them, was charged with multiple counts of murder and attempted murder. He is facing a potential death sentence.\n\nIn the aftermath of the shooting, there were calls for improved coordination and training between law enforcement agencies and emergency responders. A public memorial service was held to honor Hernandez, and hundreds of people attended to pay their respects.\n\nIn a related development, doctors at Ronald Reagan UCLA Medical Center upgraded Ciancia's condition from critical to fair, indicating that he is now stable enough to be transferred to a different facility. The incident highlighted the need for improved communication and response protocols in the event of an active shooter situation.\n\nOverall, the LAX shooting highlighted the importance of effective communication and coordination between law enforcement agencies and emergency responders, as well as the need for improved training and protocols to prevent similar incidents in the future."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Under circumstances that sound straight out of a Le Carre novel, Mohamed Morsi has met with an outsider for the first time since being dumped by Egypt's military, holding a midnight meeting with the European Union's top diplomat at an \"undisclosed location\" last night, the Wall Street Journal. Though many have feared for Morsi's well-being after almost a month in captivity, Catherine Ashton, the EU's foreign affairs and security policy chief, reports that he is well and has access to TV and newspapers, Reuters reports. The pair had a \"very long and in-depth conversation,\" Ashton says. Ashton, who has met with several other Egyptian political figures while in the country, says she wasn't there to help Morsi break out, but rather to facilitate discussions toward a peaceful end to the country's political unrest, the Washington Post reports. \"First of all, we are here to help. We are not here to impose,\" she says. \"The people of Egypt will determine their own future.\" But will Morsi play ball? \"He's a very proud man,\" says a source close to the former leader, per the Journal. \"I think the idea of doing anything on (the) army's terms will not work with him.\""], "length": 1480, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Egypt's Ongoing Crisis: EU Diplomat Meets with Deposed President Morsi**\n\nThe European Union's top diplomat, Catherine Ashton, has met with deposed Egyptian President Mohamed Morsi, marking the first contact he has had with an independent official since his ouster nearly a month ago. The meeting, which took place on Monday night, was seen as a significant development in Egypt's ongoing crisis, with Morsi's supporters and the interim government engaged in violent clashes.\n\nAshton, who is trying to mediate a resolution to the crisis, said that Morsi was well and had access to newspapers and television during their two-hour conversation. She declined to go into detail about the discussion, but emphasized that Morsi's family was informed of his condition. The meeting was seen as a sign that Egypt's military may be willing to work with Morsi towards a political solution.\n\nThe meeting comes after clashes between security forces and Morsi supporters left at least 80 dead, and prosecutors announced that Morsi was being investigated over allegations of espionage and murder. Morsi's supporters have dismissed these allegations as politically motivated, and have called for his return to power.\n\nAshton's visit has been seen as an attempt to facilitate conversations that might lead to a political solution. She emphasized that the EU is not trying to impose its will on Egypt, but rather to help the country move forward along a democratic path. The visit has also been seen as a sign of international support for Morsi's supporters, who have been demanding his release and the restoration of constitutional legitimacy.\n\nThe meeting has been met with mixed reactions, with some seeing it as a positive step towards resolving the crisis, while others have criticized Ashton for not being more explicit about her support for Morsi. The US Defense Secretary, Chuck Hagel, has also spoken out, urging restraint in dealing with protesters and calling for an inclusive reconciliation process.\n\nOverall, the meeting between Ashton and Morsi marks a significant development in Egypt's ongoing crisis, and highlights the international community's efforts to support a peaceful resolution. However, the road ahead remains uncertain, and it remains to be seen whether the meeting will lead to a lasting resolution to the crisis."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A government backed by Russia is accused of killing its own civilians. That applies not only to the current situation in Syria but to the 1999 situation in Kosovo. And as the New York Times reports, top US officials are keeping Kosovo very much in mind as they weigh their options. President Obama would likely never get the approval of the UN Security Council for a military response because of Russia's veto power. When Bill Clinton was in the same boat in 1999, he turned to NATO and got its blessing for 78 days of airstrikes. “It’s a step too far to say we’re drawing up legal justifications for an action, given that the president hasn’t made a decision,” a government official tells the Times. “But Kosovo, of course, is a precedent of something that is perhaps similar.” Obama was meeting today with national security aides to determine next steps if the US concludes that Bashar al-Assad used chemical weapons. (Assad in turn accused the rebels of doing the same today.) Meanwhile, the US Navy is keeping two destroyers positioned in the Mediterranean in case the green light is given for a cruise-missile attack, reports the Wall Street Journal."], "length": 626, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Global Tensions Escalate Amidst Syria Crisis**\n\nA series of developments has heightened tensions in the Middle East, with the international community divided on how to respond to the recent chemical attack in Syria. The attack, which occurred in the early hours of Wednesday, has sparked outrage and calls for action, with the US and its allies demanding accountability from the Syrian government.\n\nThe US Navy has taken a proactive stance, with a destroyer being kept in striking distance of Syria to prepare for potential military action. The ship is equipped with Tomahawk cruise missiles, which could be launched against targets in Syria if the president decides to take military action.\n\nMeanwhile, Russia has called on President Bashar al-Assad to allow United Nations investigators into the areas east of Damascus, where the attack occurred. However, American and foreign diplomats have expressed skepticism about Russia's intentions, suggesting that the move may be a ploy to deflect attention from its own support for the Assad government.\n\nThe Syrian government has not commented on the allegations, but the opposition has been accused of not cooperating with the investigation by United Nations experts. The US has detected activity at locations known to be chemical weapons sites before the attack, which may have been preparations for the assault.\n\nThe international community is on high alert, with the Security Council set to discuss the crisis. The situation remains volatile, and it is unclear what course of action will be taken next.\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n\n- The US Navy has deployed a destroyer to the Mediterranean to prepare for potential military action in Syria.\n- Russia has called on the Syrian government to allow United Nations investigators into the areas east of Damascus.\n- The US has detected activity at locations known to be chemical weapons sites before the attack.\n- The international community is divided on how to respond to the crisis.\n- The situation remains volatile, with the Security Council set to discuss the crisis."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Even though Ellen Pao lost her gender-discrimination suit against a former employer, she's still widely hailed as a hero for bringing the boys' club atmosphere of Silicon Valley under a microscope. But now she's got a tough choice on her hands: The venture-capital firm she sued, Kleiner Perkins Caufield & Byers, says she owes almost $1 million in legal fees—but it will drop its pursuit of the money if she doesn't appeal last month's ruling, reports the Wall Street Journal. Pao's attorneys say they will have a decision in a few weeks. Kleiner Perkins says it offered Pao about $1 million as a settlement before the trial began, but received no response from her legal team. Because of that offer, the company can now go after Pao for expensive witness fees, explains the New York Times. One factor that will surely weigh on the decision: A review of previous cases suggests that Pao has only a slim chance of winning on appeal, reports Reuters."], "length": 1166, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Kleiner Perkins, a prominent venture capital firm, is seeking to recover approximately $973,000 in costs from former partner Ellen Pao, who recently lost a high-profile gender discrimination trial. The firm, which was cleared of wrongdoing by a jury in March, is asking Pao to reimburse it for witness fees, deposition, and court reporter costs. However, in a surprising move, Kleiner Perkins has offered to waive its attempt to recover these costs if Pao agrees not to pursue an appeal.\n\nThe case, which drew significant attention to the issue of gender diversity in Silicon Valley, was sparked by Pao's allegations that she was unfairly treated by the firm due to her gender. A jury ultimately rejected all of Pao's claims, and the firm has stated that it believes women in technology would be better served by focusing on progress outside of continued litigation.\n\nKleiner Perkins' offer to settle the case with Pao before it went to trial was rejected, and the firm is now seeking to recover its costs. The firm's spokeswoman, Christina Lee, stated that the company believes in making progress on issues of gender diversity and that continued litigation would be counterproductive.\n\nPao's legal team is considering the proposal and will respond in the next two weeks. The case highlights the challenges faced by women in technology and the need for greater diversity and inclusion in the industry."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A man convicted four years ago in the oldest cold case ever tried was freed Friday after an Illinois judge vacated his conviction and subsequent life sentence, CNN reports. According to CBS News, 76-year-old Jack McCullough was convicted of kidnapping a 7-year-old girl, choking her, and stabbing her to death in 1957. The case was reopened in 2008, and McCullough was arrested in 2011 and convicted the following year. After an appeal by McCullough, Illinois state's attorney Richard Schmack launched a six-month investigation that found what he calls \"clear and convincing evidence\" that McCullough is innocent. While the judge Friday vacated McCullough's conviction, he stopped short of declaring him innocent, and a new trial will be held, the Chicago Tribune reports. McCullough, who lived in the same neighborhood as the kidnapped girl, says he was 40 miles away at an Air Force recruiting center at the time of the kidnapping. It's an alibi that passed a polygraph test in 1957 and made it impossible for him to be the culprit based on the FBI's original timeline for the kidnapping. That timeline was later changed by police, and Schmack says documents—including phone and Air Force records—supporting McCullough's alibi were wrongly not allowed at his trial. A friend of the kidnapped girl picked McCullough out of a photo lineup when the case was reopened. But his was the only non-yearbook photo in the lineup, and she picked a different man out of a photo lineup 50 years earlier. That info wasn't allowed at McCullough's trial either. No physical evidence was ever found to support McCullough's conviction."], "length": 2195, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A 76-year-old man, Jack McCullough, has been released from prison after a judge overturned his conviction for the 1957 murder of 7-year-old Maria Ridulph in Sycamore, Illinois. McCullough was sentenced to life in prison in 2012 after being found guilty of the crime, but a new review of evidence by DeKalb County State's Attorney Richard Schmack has led to his release.\n\nSchmack's review, which was conducted over a six-month period, found \"clear and convincing evidence\" that McCullough was wrongly convicted. The evidence included newly discovered phone records that showed McCullough was in Rockford, Illinois, at the time of Maria's abduction, and hundreds of other documents that were improperly barred at trial.\n\nThe case has been one of the oldest unsolved U.S. cases to go to trial, and it has sparked controversy over the years. Maria's family remains convinced that McCullough is guilty and has sought the appointment of a special prosecutor to keep him behind bars.\n\nThe release of McCullough has also brought back memories of the case, which made headlines nationwide in the 1950s. Maria's disappearance and murder were a major news story at the time, and the case has been the subject of numerous investigations and reviews over the years.\n\nIn a statement, Judge William P. Brady said that he was not trying to assign guilt or innocence to McCullough, but rather to follow the evidence and do justice. The case is now set to be reopened, and it remains to be seen what will happen next.\n\nThe release of McCullough has also raised questions about the reliability of the justice system and the potential for wrongful convictions. It has also highlighted the challenges of solving cold cases, which can remain unsolved for decades due to the passage of time and the loss of evidence.\n\nOverall, the release of Jack McCullough is a significant development in a case that has been shrouded in controversy for decades. It raises important questions about the justice system and the potential for wrongful convictions, and it highlights the challenges of solving cold cases."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Rush Limbaugh may be bleeding sponsors—one count now has the number fleeing his show up to 98—because of his \"slut\" comment, but Saturday Night Live had some suggestions last night about who might fill the advertising void. A few of the highlights, as per Mediaite: Misaki Dolphin Poppers: \"Start your day off right, with bits of dolphin.\" Depends for Racists: \"If you pee a little every time you see a Mexican, you need Depends for Racists.\" Schoder's fake rape whistles: \"Help is not on the way.\" Syria Tourism Board: Because, \"Ah! No! There's nowhere to hide!\""], "length": 495, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Rush Limbaugh Advertisers Boycott His Show Amid Controversy**\n\nIn recent weeks, several major advertisers have pulled their ads from Rush Limbaugh's radio show, citing concerns over the content of his program. The boycott, which began after Limbaugh's misogynistic comments about Georgetown student Sandra Fluke, has already resulted in significant financial losses for Limbaugh and his network, Premiere Radio Networks.\n\nAccording to reports, at least 98 advertisers have opted out of advertising on Limbaugh's show, including major brands such as Ford, General Motors, Toyota, Allstate, Geico, and Prudential. Other companies, such as McDonald's and Subway, have also dropped their ads from the show.\n\nThe advertisers' decision to boycott Limbaugh's show was sparked by his comments about Fluke, which many saw as sexist and misogynistic. Limbaugh's show has long been known for its provocative and often inflammatory content, but the backlash over the Fluke comments has been particularly severe.\n\nAs a result of the boycott, Limbaugh's flagship station, WABC, has begun airing free public service announcements during his show, in an effort to fill the revenue gap left by the lost advertisers. The move has been seen as a significant blow to Limbaugh's program, which has long been a major source of revenue for Premiere Radio Networks.\n\nThe boycott of Limbaugh's show has also sparked a wider debate about the role of advertisers in supporting provocative and inflammatory content. While some have defended Limbaugh's right to free speech, others have argued that advertisers have a responsibility to ensure that their products are not associated with hate speech or other forms of intolerance.\n\nThe controversy surrounding Limbaugh's show has also raised questions about the impact of boycotts on the media landscape. As more and more advertisers pull their ads from Limbaugh's show, it remains to be seen whether the program will be able to recover and continue to thrive.\n\nIn the meantime, Limbaugh's show has continued to air, albeit with a reduced revenue stream. The show's producers have attempted to mitigate the impact of the boycott by creating new content, including a sketch about Limbaugh's comments on Sandra Fluke. However, the move has been seen as a desperate attempt to distract from the controversy surrounding the show.\n\nOverall, the boycott of Rush Limbaugh's show has marked a significant turning point in the media landscape, highlighting the complex and often fraught relationship between advertisers, media personalities, and the public. As the debate over free speech and advertising continues to unfold, it remains to be"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["\"Any guy that can do a body-slam, he's my guy,\" President Trump shouted to a cheering Montana audience Thursday night as he simulated slamming someone to the ground, then noting, \"I shouldn't say that.\" Trump was discussing Rep. Greg Gianforte, who assaulted Guardian reporter Ben Jacobs last year during the special election for the House seat vacated by Ryan Zinke, a misdemeanor assault to which he pleaded guilty and completed community service and an anger management class. Trump said after the incident, he feared Gianforte had blown his chance at the congressional seat. \"I said, 'Oh, this is terrible, he's gonna lose the election,'\" Trump recounted. \"Then I said, 'Well, wait a minute—I know Montana pretty well. I think it might help him.' And it did.\" Trump was in Big Sky Country stumping for Matt Rosendale in his race for Democrat Jon Tester's Senate seat. Reaction came quickly: John Mulholland, US editor of the Guardian, put out a statement after the rally blasting Trump's words as \"an attack on the First Amendment,\" asking for an apology and adding, \"In the aftermath of the murder of Jamal Khashoggi, it runs the risk of inviting other assaults on journalists both here and across the world.\" ABC News reports on what the Toronto Star's Daniel Dale calls the \"most significant\" and \"truly horrible\" moment in the rally: \"The president is gleefully applauding violence against a journalist.\" Trump also praised Gianforte as \"smart\" and then advised the crowd to \"never wrestle him.\" (Trump seems to acknowledge Khashoggi is dead.)"], "length": 1550, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Multiple News Stories Emerge**\n\nIn recent days, several news stories have emerged, covering a range of topics. \n\n**Greg Gianforte Pleads Guilty to Misdemeanor Assault**\n\nMontana Congressman-elect Greg Gianforte pleaded guilty to misdemeanor assault on Monday, stemming from an incident in which he body-slammed a reporter at a campaign rally last year. Gianforte was sentenced to 180 days of deferred sentencing, 40 hours of community service, 20 hours of anger management, and a $300 fine. He has apologized to the journalist, Ben Jacobs, and pledged to donate $50,000 to the Committee to Protect Journalists.\n\n**President Trump Defends Gianforte's Comments**\n\nPresident Trump defended Gianforte's comments on a reporter, saying he had no regret for making light of the incident. Trump praised Gianforte as a \"great person\" and a \"tough cookie.\" However, the White House Correspondents' Association condemned the president's praise for the violent assault, calling it a \"celebration of a crime\" and an \"attack on the First Amendment.\"\n\n**Montana Senate Race Heats Up**\n\nThe Montana Senate race between incumbent Democratic Senator Jon Tester and Republican challenger Matt Rosendale is heating up. President Trump has made it his mission to unseat Tester, who is in a close race. Trump spoke at length about immigration and falsely said that Democrats are supporting a caravan of immigrants traveling up from Central America. Tester has touted a bipartisan record in the Senate, but many voters seem to be unaware of his name.\n\n**Additional News**\n\nIn other news, the Montana Senate race has become one of the most expensive political contests in the state's history, with millions of dollars pouring in. The president's third trip to Montana has also sparked controversy, with many voters expressing concerns about his comments on Gianforte and Tester."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Superbugs such as MRSA are already bad enough. But it turns out that something can make them even nastier: cigarette smoke. Researchers found that antibiotic-resistant bacteria exposed to the smoke became more aggressive and far more difficult to kill, they explain at Eureka Alert. It's almost as if the smoke sets off an alarm that causes the bacteria to strengthen its defenses, explains the Union-Tribune of San Diego. Scientists made the discovery by exposing immune cells to two different batches of MRSA, one normal and one treated with cigarette smoke extract. The bigger the dose of smoke, the bigger the effect, notes Medical Daily. \"We already know that smoking cigarettes harms human respiratory and immune cells, and now we've shown that, on the flipside, smoke can also stress out invasive bacteria and make them more aggressive,\" says lead author Laura Crotty Alexander of UC San Diego. It's a double-whammy: Smokers already have weakened immune systems that make them more susceptible to infectious diseases, and the smoke they inhale seems to make invading pathogens even more dangerous. In this study, the smoke helped MRSA survive longer and kill more mice with pneumonia. (The latest antibiotic-resistant bug to make headlines is a nasty stomach virus.)"], "length": 1487, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Cigarette Smoke Fuels Superbug Resistance, Threatens Public Health**\n\nRecent research has revealed that cigarette smoke not only harms human respiratory and immune cells but also fuels the virulent rampage of superbugs, making them more difficult to kill. A study published in the journal Infection and Immunity by researchers at the University of California, San Diego School of Medicine found that cigarette smoke exposure stresses MRSA (Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus) bacteria, causing them to alter their cell walls to repel toxic substances.\n\nThe study, led by Dr. Laura E. Crotty Alexander, a pulmonologist at UCSD and the Veterans Affairs San Diego Healthcare System, found that MRSA exposed to cigarette smoke extract became four times more resistant to macrophage killing than non-exposed MRSA. This increased resistance continued for 24 hours after exposure, suggesting it could be inherited. The study also found that cigarette smoke extract-exposed MRSA became more resistant to antimicrobial peptides, which pierce bacterial cell walls.\n\nThe research has significant implications for public health, as it suggests that cigarette smoke-induced resistance in MRSA may be an additional contributing factor to the growing problem of antibiotic-resistant bacteria. MRSA is a type of bacteria that causes life-threatening infections to the skin, bloodstream, or a surgical site, and its resistance to antibiotics has become a major concern worldwide.\n\nThe study's findings are consistent with previous research that found e-cigarette vapor also alters the structure of MRSA's cell wall to make it more resistant to bacteria. However, the study found that cigarette smoke exposure resulted in more significant changes to the bacteria's surface, increasing resistance by 10 times more than e-cigarette vapor.\n\nThe study's authors hope that their findings will add yet another reason to why individuals should refrain from smoking. The research highlights the need for a more nuanced understanding of the relationship between cigarette smoke and superbug resistance, and the importance of developing effective strategies to combat this growing public health threat."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["One of the most renowned hairstylists in Los Angeles was murdered on Monday by intruders who made their getaway in his Porsche, police say. Fabio Sementilli was stabbed several times and was found on his home's patio by his wife and daughter, CBS Los Angeles reports. Paramedics were called but the 49-year-old was bleeding profusely and died at the scene. Police say the Canadian-born hairstylist's 2008 Porsche 911 Carrera was missing from the scene and they believe it was stolen by two men who broke into the upscale Woodland Hills home and attacked Sementilli. Police aren't sure whether the murder was random or a targeted attack, the AP reports. Sementilli gained international recognition during his 30 years as a hairstylist and also served as an exec at companies including Wella, the Los Angeles Daily News reports. He was \"recognized as an exceptional stylist, educator, and visionary,\" Wella said in a statement that describes him as \"an icon in the hairdressing industry.\" A post at Modern Salon says he was a VP of education for cosmetics company Coty, adding that he \"mentored tens of thousands of hairdressers with a hands-on approach either on a one-to-one basis or on a grander scale.\" (This man was murdered just two months after President Obama commuted his sentence.)"], "length": 2237, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Renowned Hairstylist Fabio Sementilli Found Dead in Los Angeles Home**\n\nA shocking and tragic incident occurred on Monday, January 23, 2017, when Fabio Sementilli, a 49-year-old internationally known hairstylist and beauty company executive, was found beaten and stabbed to death at his home in the upscale Woodland Hills neighborhood of Los Angeles. Sementilli, who worked for decades as a trendsetting hair stylist and served as vice president of education for Coty Inc., a major German hair-care company, was discovered by his wife and daughter on the back patio of his home.\n\nAccording to police, Sementilli suffered multiple stab wounds to his face, neck, and upper body, and was bleeding profusely when paramedics arrived. Despite attempts to save him, Sementilli was pronounced dead at the scene. His black 2008 Porsche 911 Carrera, which was parked outside his home, was also missing.\n\nDetectives are investigating the incident, but a possible motive has not been determined. The Los Angeles Police Department (LAPD) is reviewing security footage from the neighborhood, including cameras from nearby homes, in an effort to identify potential suspects.\n\nSementilli was a highly respected figure in the hair styling industry, known for his mentorship and positive influence on countless professionals. He was a champion competitor and had used his success to give back to the community through his work with Wella's Hairdressers at Heart program.\n\nFriends and family, including Eden Sassoon, daughter of industry icon Vidal Sassoon, have paid tribute to Sementilli, describing him as a \"mentor and friend\" who made a lasting impact on the industry. Sementilli's death has sent shockwaves through the community, with many expressing their sadness and outrage on social media.\n\nThe LAPD is urging anyone with information about the case to contact the Homicide Bureau at (818) 374-1925. As the investigation continues, Sementilli's family and loved ones are left to grapple with the devastating loss of a man who was widely admired for his talent, generosity, and dedication to his craft."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Using methods borrowed from Google, a group of researchers has analyzed all Wikipedia pages and determined that, at least on the English language version of the site, Frank Sinatra is the world's most important person. Second place goes to Michael Jackson, and third to Pope Pius XII. When factoring in all 24 language editions of the online encyclopedia, the team found that Adolf Hitler ranked the most important person, while Michael Jackson was again second and Madonna third, reports the Guardian. \"Our analysis shows that most important historical figures across Wikipedia language editions are born in western countries after the 17th century, and are male,\" the authors write. The researchers combined two algorithms to reach these conclusions. First, Google's PageRank algorithm, which determines a page's importance based on how many other pages link to it. In using PageRank on Wikipedia, the most important person in the world was 18th-century Swedish naturalist Carl Linnaeus, likely because the pages of organism after organism link back to the \"father of taxonomy.\" So the researchers added an algorithm called CheiRank, which determines importance based on the number of outgoing links, the thought being that an important person would himself be connected to other important people, things, and events. Using the two together—an approach they dubbed 2DRank—no one beat out Frank Sinatra on English pages or Adolf Hitler across all language versions. Last year, in a separate study analyzing academic references, one team deemed Karl Marx to be the world's most important scholar, according to the Smithsonian. (Click to read about one of Sinatra's quirks.)"], "length": 1414, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Global Influential Figures Revealed in Groundbreaking Study**\n\nA recent study conducted by the University of Toulouse has shed light on the most influential figures in the English-language world, as well as globally. The research, led by Young-Ho Eom, analyzed Wikipedia articles across 24 major language editions to determine the individuals with the most important articles linking to them.\n\nAccording to the study, the top three most important people in the English-language world are Frank Sinatra, Michael Jackson, and Pope Pius XII. However, when considering all 24 language editions, the results were more surprising, with Adolf Hitler taking the top spot, followed by Michael Jackson and Madonna.\n\nThe study aimed to understand the level of attention Wikipedia gives to various figures, taking into account factors such as gender, time, and location. The researchers found that most important historical figures are born in western countries after the 17th century and are male. However, each Wikipedia edition highlights local figures, resulting in a pronounced accent to local figures.\n\nThe study employed Google's PageRank algorithm, which measures the importance of a page based on the number of links to it. However, the results were initially skewed by the presence of an almost complete collection of named species on Wikipedia, which linked to Carl Linnaeus, a Swedish naturalist. To address this issue, the researchers applied a second method, CheiRank, which measures the importance of an article based on the number of outgoing links.\n\nWhen combining PageRank and CheiRank, the researchers arrived at the top 100 most influential figures across all 24 language editions, with Carl Linnaeus taking the top spot. Linnaeus, known as the \"Father of Taxonomy,\" invented the scientific system of naming that is still in use today.\n\nInterestingly, a similar study conducted last year found that Karl Marx was the most influential academic, while a study on the most influential thinkers in the world found that Sir Isaac Newton was a key figure in the development of modern science.\n\nThe Internet Archive has also been working on archiving pages as they are created and preserving links to the original content. This effort aims to fix broken links on the web and provide a permanent record of online content.\n\nOverall, the study highlights the complexities of measuring influence and the importance of considering multiple factors when evaluating the impact of individuals on the world."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Arnold Schwarzenegger seems to think Donald Trump needs a lesson in making friends after the president-elect chose to criticize his Celebrity Apprentice successor. \"Wow, the ratings are in and [new host] Arnold Schwarzenegger got 'swamped' (or destroyed) by comparison to the ratings machine, DJT,\" Trump tweeted Friday after the Trump-less show premiered Monday to 4.9 million viewers, according to Nielsen. The last season hosted by Trump averaged 7.6 million viewers, per Fortune. \"So much for … being a movie star,\" Trump continued. \"But who cares, he supported [John] Kasich & Hillary [Clinton].\" Schwarzenegger offered up a reply on Twitter, tweeting, \"I wish you the best of luck and I hope you'll work for ALL of the American people as aggressively as you worked for your ratings.\" He next shared a video he made after the election in which he quotes Abraham Lincoln's inaugural speech, beginning, \"We are not enemies, but friends. We must not be enemies.\" \"Please study this quote ... @realDonaldTrump,\" he tweeted with the video. \"It inspired me every day I was Governor, and I hope it inspires you.\" (The New York Times is unimpressed with the new season.)"], "length": 740, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\nIn a recent development, NBC's Celebrity Apprentice, hosted by Arnold Schwarzenegger, failed to impress with its ratings premiere. The show, which was previously hosted by Donald Trump, averaged around 4.9 million total viewers across its two-hour premiere, according to Nielsen's tracking numbers. This is a significant drop from the 7.6 million nightly viewers the show averaged during Trump's final season in 2014-2015.\n\nThe ratings were also lower than expected, with a 22% decline from Trump's debut episode in 2014-2015. The show's key demographic, viewers aged 18 to 49, scored roughly 46% lower than what Schwarzenegger's debut scored on Monday.\n\nThe show's premiere faced stiff competition from other popular programs, including ABC's The Bachelor premiere and a thrilling college football game on ESPN. Despite this, NBC was likely hoping for a more impressive start to the post-Trump era of its Apprentice franchise.\n\nHowever, the show's ratings were not the only news to emerge. President-elect Donald Trump tweeted a quote from Lincoln's inaugural address, which he claimed inspired him every day he was Governor. Trump also shared a tweet from a user who wished him luck in his new role.\n\nIn a separate development, Trump's successor, Arnold Schwarzenegger, was also in the news. Schwarzenegger's hosting debut on Celebrity Apprentice was met with mixed reviews, and the show's ratings were seen as a disappointment.\n\nOverall, the recent news highlights the challenges facing NBC's Celebrity Apprentice in the post-Trump era, as well as the ongoing competition in the world of reality TV."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Own a Samsung smart TV? Then take note: Your television is not only listening, it's sending your data to a third party, the Independent reports. The TV's voice-recognition software lets you give it orders, but the product's privacy policy warns that \"personal or other sensitive information ... will be among the data captured and transmitted to a third party through your use of Voice Recognition.\" Samsung tells the BBC that the third party is Nuance, the company that does Samsung's speech-to-text conversion. But intellectual property lawyer Corynne McSherry says it would be nice to see that clarified in Samsung's policy, the Daily Beast reports. \"And I’d definitely like to know whether my words were being transmitted in a secure form,\" she says. An activist tweets that Samsung's policy sounds a lot like the description of \"telescreens\" in George Orwell's dystopian novel, 1984—which reads in part, \"Any sound that Winston made, above the level of a very low whisper, would be picked up by [the screen].\" Unnervingly, it wasn't clear how often \"the Thought Police plugged in on any individual wire was guesswork.\" Samsung, however, says it doesn't keep voice data, and customers can opt to turn off voice-recognition or even unplug the TV from Wi-Fi. And Samsung isn't alone: the Verge reports that XBoxes already use voice recognition while being connected to the Internet. \"Samsung's privacy policy is not unique; its rhetoric just happens to be similar to that of a well-known sci-fi novel,\" the Verge says."], "length": 2580, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Global Tech News: Privacy Concerns and Smart Home Devices**\n\nIn recent weeks, several tech companies have faced scrutiny over their handling of user data and privacy concerns. Two major news stories have emerged, highlighting the growing importance of protecting individual rights in the digital age.\n\n**Samsung Smart TVs and Voice Recognition**\n\nSamsung's new smart TVs have been criticized for their privacy policy, which allows the company and its partners to listen in on everything users say. The policy, which can be turned off, has drawn comparisons to George Orwell's dystopian novel 1984. While voice recognition software typically transmits data on what users are saying, the combination of sending data to third-parties and the Orwellian comparisons has raised concerns. Samsung has assured users that data is encrypted and that owners can disconnect their TV from Wi-Fi to keep their data safe.\n\n**Xbox One and Always-On Listening Mode**\n\nMicrosoft's new Xbox One console has also sparked controversy over its always-on listening mode. The new Kinect peripheral, which is shipped with every Xbox One, can listen to users even when the console is turned off. Users can activate the console by saying \"Xbox On,\" which means the Kinect will be listening to them at all times. This has raised concerns about privacy and the potential for malicious actors to exploit the system.\n\n**Common Themes and Implications**\n\nBoth stories highlight the growing importance of protecting individual rights in the digital age. The use of smart home devices, voice recognition software, and always-on listening modes raises concerns about data collection, surveillance, and the potential for malicious actors to exploit these systems. As technology continues to advance, it is essential that companies prioritize user privacy and security, and that governments and regulatory bodies take steps to protect individual rights.\n\n**Key Takeaways**\n\n* Samsung's new smart TVs have a privacy policy that allows for voice recognition and data collection.\n* Microsoft's new Xbox One console has an always-on listening mode, which can be activated by saying \"Xbox On.\"\n* Both companies have assured users that data is encrypted and that owners can take steps to protect their data.\n* The use of smart home devices and voice recognition software raises concerns about data collection, surveillance, and potential malicious actors.\n* Companies must prioritize user privacy and security, and governments and regulatory bodies must take steps to protect individual rights."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A New Jersey court on Wednesday gave the Flight Crew—aka the New York Jets cheerleaders—something to cheer about: a $325,000 settlement of the class-action lawsuit filed in 2014 by a cheerleader identified as Krystal C., CNNMoney reports. That amounts to each of the 52 cheerleaders getting $2,500 per season worked and $400 per photo shoot. The settlement covers the 2012-13 and 2013-14 seasons. New York State Sen. Diane Savino, per the New York Post, cheered the settlement, calling on the NFL to \"develop uniform rules … to ensure that all cheerleaders in every state received the employee pay and protections they deserve.\" NFL teams have sought to justify low pay for cheerleaders by saying they're independent contractors, CNN notes. In the case of the Flight Crew, Krystal C.'s suit claimed that cheerleaders were compensated $150 per game and $100 for special appearances. But, when you factor in practices and rehearsals, that pans out to $3.77 per hour. Throw in hair, makeup, and transportation expenses and \"the hourly rate goes below $1.50 an hour,\" says the cheerleaders' attorney. The Flight Crew isn't alone: Cheerleaders have taken to the courts to seek higher pay from the Cincinnati Bengals (tentative agreement reached), Tampa Bay Buccaneers (settled for $825,000), Oakland Raiders (settled for $1.25 million), and the Buffalo Bills (class-action suit on the horizon)."], "length": 859, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**NFL Cheerleaders Secure Settlements for Fair Pay**\n\nIn recent news, several NFL teams have settled lawsuits with their cheerleaders, ensuring fair pay and treatment for these athletes. The New York Jets, Buffalo Bills, Cincinnati Bengals, Tampa Bay Buccaneers, and Oakland Raiders have all reached agreements with their cheerleaders.\n\nThe New York Jets settled a class-action lawsuit with their cheerleaders, the Flight Crew, for $325,000. Each of the 52 cheerleaders will receive $2,500 per season they worked, with an additional $400 payment per photo shoot. The settlement covers the 2012-13 and 2013-14 NFL seasons and was initially reached in August 2015.\n\nThe Buffalo Bills' cheerleaders, the Jills, have also been granted class-action status in a similar wage suit. The team's cheerleaders claim they made only $150 per game and $100 for special event appearances, with no compensation for weekly practices or time spent rehearsing.\n\nThe Buffalo Bills' settlement is seen as a significant step towards ensuring fair pay and treatment for NFL cheerleaders. State Sen. Diane Savino (D-SI) has called for other teams to follow suit, stating that \"the Buffalo Bills to do the same, to ensure that their cheerleaders are not left out in the cold when it comes to fair pay and treatment.\"\n\nThe NFL has faced criticism for its treatment of cheerleaders, with many teams labeling them as independent contractors and denying them fair pay. However, courts have ruled in favor of the cheerleaders, recognizing them as employees entitled to fair compensation.\n\nThe settlements are a significant victory for NFL cheerleaders, who have fought for fair pay and treatment for years. As the NFL continues to grapple with its treatment of cheerleaders, these settlements serve as a reminder of the importance of fair pay and treatment for all athletes."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["At first, it seemed like 4-year-old Laura Carson was suffering from a simple headache. Then came rapid shallow breathing, a tremor, double vision. Within days in August 2014, she was \"a limp rag doll,\" reports Today—but it took doctors some time to diagnose her with acute flaccid myelitis, a rare disease the CDC warns is becoming increasingly more common. Not much is known about the polio-like disease that plagues mostly children, including what causes it. What is known is that AFM affects the spinal cord and causes weak limbs or paralysis, drooping in the face, and slurred speech or difficulty breathing. About 200 kids have been diagnosed since 2014, and this year's figures are especially troubling. The CDC reports 121 cases of AFM were confirmed in 2014, but that was followed by just 21 cases in 2015, reports Fox News. From January to August of this year, however, there were 50 confirmed cases in 24 states. Among that first spike of cases in 2014, the Washington Post reports that 85% of kids recovered partially, but only three recovered fully. A CDC study found 68% of patients had a fever and 81% had a respiratory illness before AFM symptoms appeared. Some had been diagnosed with the West Nile virus, and others with an enterovirus. \"August to October is typically when enteroviruses circulate\" and \"we see more acute flaccid myelitis during that season,\" a doctor tells NBC News. Washing your hands and covering your mouth when coughing or sneezing are among the ways suggested to help. (Four siblings suffer from a mysterious disease.)"], "length": 4776, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Mysterious Illness Spreads Across US, Leaving Children Paralyzed**\n\nA mysterious illness known as acute flaccid myelitis (AFM) has been spreading across the US, leaving children paralyzed and sparking concerns among health officials. The condition, which is similar to polio, has been linked to a strain of enterovirus and has been reported in 24 states, with 50 confirmed cases as of August 2016.\n\nThe first reported cases of AFM occurred in August 2014, with 120 children diagnosed in 34 states. The condition was initially thought to be caused by enterovirus D68, but further testing revealed no conclusive evidence of the virus. Despite this, health officials are still investigating the cause of the illness and are urging parents to take precautions to prevent the spread of the disease.\n\nSymptoms of AFM include pain in the arms and legs, difficulty breathing, and paralysis of the muscles. The condition can be severe, with some patients requiring ventilator support and others experiencing long-term disability. While there is no specific treatment for AFM, physical therapy and other forms of rehabilitation may help improve outcomes.\n\nThe CDC is working to track the spread of the illness and is urging health officials to report any cases of AFM to the agency. The organization is also advising parents to wash their hands frequently, avoid close contact with sick children, and clean surfaces with disinfectant to prevent the spread of the disease.\n\nIn addition to the reported cases of AFM, there have been several other cases of enterovirus D68 reported in the US, which has raised concerns about the potential link between the two illnesses. Health officials are urging parents to stay vigilant and take precautions to prevent the spread of the disease.\n\n**Key Statistics:**\n\n* 50 confirmed cases of AFM in 24 states as of August 2016\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The Shroud of Turin is supposedly the burial cloth that was wrapped around Jesus after his crucifixion; bloodstains on the linen shroud, which are said to have been transferred to it during the three days Jesus was in the tomb, form the image of a crucified man. But a new study reported in the Journal of Forensic Sciences finds the bloodstain image was likely faked. Researchers looking at the blood spatter found that the stains appeared to come from someone standing up, rather than someone who was flat on the fabric, Science Alert reports. As the researchers put it, the stains are \"totally unrealistic\" when compared to what they should look like. The shroud, which is held in the Cathedral of St. John the Baptist in Italy, is considered an icon, as opposed to a genuine religious relic, by the Vatican; Fox News notes \"the church has never weighed in on its authenticity.\" \"This is the kind of forensic work done all the time in police investigations,\" the forensic scientist who conducted the analysis tells BuzzFeed News. \"Even a crucified or hanging person should leave a distinct blood pattern on the cloth, which would be fascinating information to have.\" The study found inconsistent staining, with researchers concluding multiple poses were used to create the bloodstains—a standing model was likely used to imprint patterns on the cloth at various angles for various body parts. Another bloodstain pattern expert notes that more research could be done to see whether cleaning a body or preparing it for burial might account for the inconsistent staining, though he notes that the stains do appear to have come from flowing blood, meaning a heart that was beating at the time the stains were made. (See previous stories in the real-or-fake debate here.)"], "length": 2394, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Shroud of Turin Forensic Investigation Reveals Inconsistencies in Bloodstain Patterns**\n\nA groundbreaking forensic investigation into the Shroud of Turin, a cloth believed by some to be the burial cloth of Jesus, has revealed significant inconsistencies in the bloodstain patterns on the fabric. The study, conducted by forensic scientists Matteo Borrini and Luigi Garlaschelli, used real and synthetic blood samples to analyze the bloodstains on the shroud, which bears an image of a crucified man.\n\nThe researchers found that the bloodstains on the shroud are inconsistent with any single pose, suggesting that a standing model was used to imprint the patterns at different angles for the hands, chest, and back. This finding contradicts the traditional understanding of the shroud's authenticity, which relies on the idea that the bloodstains were created by a crucified or hanging person.\n\nThe investigation, published in the Journal of Forensic Sciences, analyzed seven different body parts depicted on the fabric, including the hand, forearm, chest, and lower back. The researchers discovered that the angle at which gravity pulled the liquid against the direction seen on the linen varied with the body part, with some hand bloodstains tracing angles at odds with each other.\n\nThe study's findings have significant implications for the debate over the shroud's authenticity. While the Vatican regards the shroud as an icon, the researchers' conclusions suggest that it may be a medieval forgery. The investigation's results have been met with skepticism by some, who argue that the study's methodology is flawed and that the conclusions are not definitive.\n\nDespite the controversy surrounding the study's findings, the investigation has shed new light on the shroud's composition and the possible methods used to create the bloodstain patterns. The study's authors emphasize that the investigation is not a definitive proof of the shroud's authenticity or lack thereof, but rather a scientific analysis of the fabric's composition and the bloodstain patterns.\n\nThe Shroud of Turin remains a topic of fascination and debate, with many people continuing to believe in its authenticity. However, the forensic investigation's findings have raised important questions about the shroud's origins and the methods used to create the bloodstain patterns. As the debate continues, the study's results will likely remain a topic of discussion and analysis among scholars and the general public alike."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["You can temporarily take Donald Trump out of real estate, but you can't take the real estate out of Donald Trump. That much was clear at a Singapore press conference Tuesday, which took place after the president's meeting with Kim Jong Un and in which he touched upon the potential of North Korea's coastline. \"They have great beaches,\" Trump noted, per the Washington Examiner. \"You see that whenever they're exploding their cannons into the ocean.\" And that spectacular view got him to thinking: \"Wouldn't that make a great condo? Instead of doing that you could have the best hotels in the world right there. Think of it from a real estate perspective, you have South Korea, you have China, and they own the land in the middle, how bad is that, right? It's great.\" The AP also reports on another post-summit activity, which it deems a \"classic alpha male move\": Trump showing off \"The Beast,\" the presidential limousine, to the North Korean leader. CNN shows a video clip of the two men approaching the limo, a Secret Service agent opening the door, and Kim peering in to take a look. \"We know that these men were trying to find ways to bond, in just like a personal, human way, and what's more natural for guys [than] to want to check out one another's car,\" Chris Cuomo notes. (Here are seven complimentary things Trump said about Kim after the summit.)"], "length": 848, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Global News Summary**\n\nIn a series of significant developments, the world witnessed historic moments in diplomacy and technology. President Donald Trump's summit with North Korean leader Kim Jong Un in Singapore marked a significant turning point in the region's history.\n\nDuring the marathon press conference held after the conclusion of the summit, Trump expressed optimism about the potential for a lasting peace and complete denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula. He touted North Korea's untapped property market potential, suggesting that the country could develop luxury hotels and resorts along its coastline. Trump also showed Kim a video simulation of possible projects that could take place in the rogue nation, which Kim reportedly enjoyed.\n\nIn a separate development, the Internet Archive launched an effort to archive web pages and preserve links to prevent broken links on the web. This initiative aims to fix all broken links on the internet by archiving pages as they are created and referencing the archived versions.\n\nIn a lighter moment, Trump gave Kim a rare glimpse inside the presidential limousine known as \"The Beast\" during their summit. The two leaders were spotted approaching the car, and Trump gestured to it before a Secret Service agent opened the door. Kim smiled and peeked inside, seemingly enjoying the tour.\n\nTrump had previously stated that he would be able to tell within minutes whether he could strike a deal with Kim to dismantle the North's nuclear program. While the details of the summit are still emerging, the historic meeting has sparked hope for a more peaceful future in the region.\n\nOverall, these recent developments highlight the complexities and nuances of global politics, technology, and diplomacy. As the world watches and waits, it remains to be seen what the future holds for the Korean Peninsula and the global community."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["President Obama today signed legislation to honor Japanese-American soldiers who fought on behalf of the Allies during World War II—even as some of their families were kept in confinement in the US. This long-awaited bill will open the door for more than 6,000 soldiers known as Nisei veterans to collectively receive the prestigious Congressional Gold Medal. For more on the vets, see the Digital Journal or Oregon Public Broadcasting."], "length": 1182, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "President Barack Obama is set to sign a bill into law, authorizing the prestigious Congressional Gold Medal for Nisei veterans, who are Japanese Americans born to immigrant parents. This honor recognizes the bravery and sacrifice of over 6,000 Nisei veterans who served in the 100th Infantry Battalion and 442nd Regimental Combat Team during World War II.\n\nThe 442nd Regimental Combat Team, also known as the \"Go for Broke\" veterans, was an all-Japanese-American regiment formed mostly of young men whose families had been shipped to detainment camps due to their race. Despite facing challenges and hardships, these men stepped forward in great numbers to defend their country, with some serving in segregated units and others facing difficulties in basic training.\n\nThe 442nd Regimental Combat Team gained a reputation for toughness in France and Italy, capturing Germans and rescuing the \"Lost Battalion\" of Texas National Guardsmen. However, the regiment suffered terrible losses, with Art Iwasaki's company being decimated to just eight members by the end of the war.\n\nAfter the war, many Nisei veterans returned to their families and communities, with some going on to become successful professionals and farmers. Today, only a few members of Art Iwasaki's company remain, and he downplays the heroism he and his fellow soldiers showed.\n\nThe Congressional Gold Medal is one of the highest civilian honors presented to people who serve the security and national interests of the United States. Past honorees include the Wright Brothers, Rosa Parks, Navajo Code Talkers, the Tuskegee Airmen, and the Dalai Lama.\n\nThe bill, signed by President Obama, will honor the 100th Infantry Battalion, 442nd Regimental Combat Team, and the Military Intelligence Service for their heroic service and sacrifice during World War II. This historic moment marks a significant step towards recognizing the bravery and contributions of Nisei veterans, who paved the way towards a more tolerant and just nation."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Earlier this year, President Trump noted \"I just don't want a poor person\" in charge of economic posts in his administration, then touted Commerce Secretary Wilbur Ross as \"a very rich person\" who was up to the task. But just how rich Ross really is is now up in the air, Forbes reports, noting it's yanked Ross off its billionaires list after a one-month probe revealed that $2 billion or so or Ross' alleged fortune \"never existed,\" and that Ross had engaged in an \"apparent sequence of fibs, exaggerations, omissions, fabrications, and whoppers\" with the magazine going back 13 years. The discovery came when Ross, 79, contacted Forbes in October to refute his ranking: Last year he was listed with a net worth of $2.9 billion on the Forbes 400; he claimed he was worth closer to $3.7 billion. Then Forbes talked with 10 of Ross' ex-employees at his private equity firm, who noted a \"penchant for misleading\" that affected fellow workers and investors and spurred big fines, suits, and refunds to backers. \"Wilbur doesn't have an issue with bending the truth,\" one longtime colleague says, while another is more blunt: \"He's lied to a lot of people.\" The magazine delves into Ross' back story, including how, when he first made the Forbes billionaires list in 2004 with a net worth of $1 billion, \"everyone that I knew that worked with Wilbur knew it wasn't true,\" per a former colleague. When Forbes told Ross he was being removed from its billionaires club, Ross retorted the magazine wasn't counting family trusts that he wasn't obligated to inform the feds about—in the amount of \"more than $2 billion,\" assets Ross said he put into the trusts sometime \"between the election and [my] nomination.\" When Forbes asked to see a paper trail proving that, Ross cited \"privacy issues\"—and the magazine lays out other problems with Ross' story on the supposed $2 billion transfer. Read the in-depth findings here."], "length": 4687, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\nIn recent months, several significant news stories have emerged, impacting various aspects of politics, business, and society.\n\n**Economic and Business News**\n\n* The appointment of Wilbur Ross as US Secretary of Commerce has raised concerns about his financial disclosures and potential conflicts of interest. An investigation by Forbes found that Ross had lied about his net worth, which was initially listed at $2.9 billion but later revised to $2.7 billion.\n* The Trump administration's focus on economic growth has led to the appointment of billionaire advisors, sparking debate about the administration's priorities and the wealth gap in the country.\n\n**Politics and Government**\n\n* President Trump has faced criticism for his comments on various issues, including his stance on immigration, the Second Amendment, and his treatment of women. His administration has also been embroiled in several investigations, including those into Russian interference in the 2016 election and potential obstruction of justice.\n* The Trump administration's cabinet appointments have been criticized for being overly wealthy, with many members having net worths in the hundreds of millions or billions of dollars.\n\n**Social and Cultural News**\n\n* The #MeToo movement has continued to gain momentum, with several high-profile cases and allegations of sexual misconduct against powerful men, including politicians and business leaders.\n* The debate over gun control and the Second Amendment has intensified, with some arguing that stricter regulations are needed to prevent mass shootings.\n\n**International News**\n\n* The relationship between the US and Russia has deteriorated, with tensions rising over issues such as Ukraine and election interference.\n* The ongoing conflict in Syria has led to increased humanitarian concerns and calls for a peaceful resolution.\n\nOverall, these news stories highlight the complexities and challenges facing the US and the world today, including issues of economic inequality, politics, social justice, and international relations."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A guitar that once belonged to Kurt Cobain, which is likely worth millions, is now in the hands of Eeries frontman Isaiah Silva, and Cobain's daughter is fighting to get it back in the family. Frances Bean Cobain was married to Silva for 21 months, and she's been trying to get the Martin D-18E (which had the bridge flipped so Cobain, a leftie, could play it comfortably) back ever since they split in March. Silva claims she gave it to him as a wedding gift and it's rightfully his. Now, the battle is headed to court, Page Six reports. \"It’s not [Silva’s]. It’s a treasured heirloom of the family. It’s not his to take,\" Frances Cobain has said. But sources tell Page Six that Silva isn't giving in, and \"is forcing the matter to litigation.\" The guitar, the one Cobain played during Nirvana's 1993 MTV Unplugged show and also thought to be the last one he played before taking his life, was once insured for $1 million but, per experts, is probably worth several times that now. Back in August, Silva requested $25,000 a month in spousal support and half of any money Frances made during the marriage, and said the couple had no prenup, TMZ reported. A judge has since ordered Frances Cobain to pay Silva $15,000 a month as part of an interim settlement."], "length": 810, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Kurt Cobain's Priceless Guitar Embroiled in Bitter Court Battle\n\nA multimillion-dollar court battle is underway over Kurt Cobain's iconic guitar, played during Nirvana's 1993 \"MTV Unplugged\" concert. Frances Bean Cobain, Kurt's daughter with Courtney Love, is fighting to regain the instrument from her estranged husband, Isaiah Silva. Silva claims the guitar was given to him as a wedding present in 2014, while Frances Bean denies this, stating it is a treasured family heirloom.\n\nThe dispute began in 2016, when Frances Bean and Silva split. Silva is seeking multimillion-dollar damages, citing the guitar's estimated value and the couple's prenuptial agreement. Frances Bean's manager allegedly sent threatening messages to Silva's ex-girlfriend in an attempt to secure the guitar's return.\n\nMeanwhile, Frances Bean is seeking spousal support from Silva, who claims she has reneged on her promises to cover his expenses. Silva is requesting $25,000 per month in spousal support, citing his steady job and Frances Bean's significant inheritance from her father's estate.\n\nThe court battle is set to escalate, with Frances Bean reportedly ready to take the matter to a judge. The dispute highlights the complexities of Kurt Cobain's estate and the ongoing struggles of his family members to navigate their relationships and financial obligations."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Matt Damon opined this week that the wave of sex harassment and assault claims sweeping Hollywood and society at large include \"a spectrum of behavior\" and that \"none of us came here perfect,\" and the woman he once dumped on Oprah wasted no time in shutting him down. \"Gosh it’s so interesting (profoundly unsurprising) how men with all these opinions about women’s differentiation between sexual misconduct, assault and rape reveal themselves to be utterly tone deaf and as a result, systemically part of the problem,\" Minnie Driver tweeted. She further unloaded to the Guardian: \"I’ve realized that most men, good men, the men that I love, there is a cut-off. They simply cannot understand what abuse is like on a daily level. I honestly think that until we get on the same page, you can’t tell a woman about their abuse. ... It is so individual and so personal, it’s galling when a powerful man steps up and starts dictating the terms, whether he intends it or not.” Damon was also getting zero sympathy from Alyssa Milano, reports USA Today, who tweeted that, \"as a victim of each component of the sexual assault spectrum of which you speak,\" \"they all hurt.\" \"We are not outraged because someone grabbed our a---s in a picture. We are outraged because we were made to feel this was normal. We are outraged because we have been gaslighted. We are outraged because we were silenced for so long.\" (Damon has said he had no idea that Harvey Weinstein routinely sexually harassed and assaulted women; his full interview this week with ABC News is here.)"], "length": 4436, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Actors Alyssa Milano and Minnie Driver have spoken out against Matt Damon's comments on sexual harassment. Milano took to Twitter to call out Damon for his remarks, stating that the issue is not about different levels of severity, but about the normalization of misogynistic behavior. Driver also criticized Damon, saying that men \"simply cannot understand what abuse is like on a daily level\" and that they should not attempt to differentiate between various forms of harassment.\n\nDamon responded to the criticism, stating that he believes there is a \"spectrum of behavior\" and that some actions, such as rape and child molestation, should be dealt with more severely than others. However, he also acknowledged that there is a difference between \"patting someone on the butt\" and more serious forms of harassment.\n\nThe conversation highlights the ongoing debate about how to address sexual harassment and assault, with some arguing that the issue is too complex to be reduced to simple categories, while others believe that it is essential to acknowledge the severity of different forms of harassment.\n\nIn a separate development, Harvey Weinstein, a former producer who has been accused of multiple instances of sexual misconduct, has been accused of rape by actress Salma Hayek. Weinstein has denied the allegations, but has acknowledged that he has engaged in inappropriate behavior.\n\nThe #MeToo movement, which has been gaining momentum in recent months, continues to gain traction, with more women coming forward to share their experiences of sexual harassment and assault. The movement has sparked a wider conversation about the need for greater accountability and support for victims of sexual misconduct.\n\nOverall, the news highlights the ongoing struggle for women's rights and the need for greater awareness and action to address the issue of sexual harassment and assault."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Microsoft unveiled its much-hyped Project Natal motion control system—now renamed “Kinect”—at E3 last night, in a performance long on grandiosity and short on actual revelations. After walking through a set of fake living rooms, the white-poncho-wearing audience was treated to a performance by Cirque du Soleil acrobats dressed as cavemen, as a narrator declared that “history is about to rewritten” and a boy, raising his arms, used his movements usher in the new motion-based way to play games, according to the Wall Street Journal. And what was this grand historical event all about? Well, mostly mimicking the Wii, according to CNET. Microsoft’s camera-based motion controller may have potential, but the games demoed last night mostly looked like analogues for successful Wii efforts, including a yoga game, a racing game, and a Wii Sports-esque game titled—wait for it—Kinect Sports. IGN has a full list of launch titles. Microsoft is expected to reveal more details, including pricing, at its press conference today."], "length": 2768, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Microsoft Unveils Kinect, a Motion-Control System for Xbox 360\n\nIn a highly anticipated event, Microsoft officially unveiled its motion-control system, previously known as Project Natal, and named it Kinect. The system allows users to play videogames and interact with the Xbox 360 console using gestures and spoken commands. The unveiling took place at the University of Southern California's Galen Center, where a Cirque du Soleil performance showcased the system's capabilities.\n\nThe event featured a series of games, including whitewater rafting, racing, virtual Tai Chi, and a Star Wars game that lets players fight Darth Vader with a light saber. Other games included soccer, beach volleyball, and bowling, as well as a collection of sports games that lets players jump hurdles, run sprints, and play volleyball. The system also includes a yoga and dance game, as well as a game that allows users to play with virtual kittens.\n\n Kinect is seen as Microsoft's answer to Nintendo's Wii, which popularized game play. Unlike the Wii, Kinect does not require a controller and can recognize a wider range of motion and even differentiate voices. The system is expected to be released this fall, with pricing and availability to be announced at a Monday press conference.\n\nThe unveiling of Kinect has sparked a challenge to Nintendo's Wii and Sony's Move, which is also a motion-control system. Microsoft's move is seen as a significant step in the motion-control wars, and the company is expected to continue to develop and release new games and apps for the system.\n\nOverall, the unveiling of Kinect marks a significant milestone in the development of motion-control technology, and it will be interesting to see how the system is received by gamers and critics alike."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Get out your checkbooks, kids: There is a slew of famous and infamous items up for auction. The US Marshal's office's online auction of the Unabomber's personal effects ends tomorrow, and Aol's Weird News astutely observes that Ted Kaczynski's 35,000-word handwritten manifesto ($17,525) is currently going for less than the gray hoodie ($20,025) that figured so prominently into his FBI Wanted poster. Bonus: sunglasses included. Looking for more of a bargain? A bow and arrows in a Sears box is currently a steal at $743. And in other bizarre auction news: Balloon boy's dad is trying to auction off the infamous \"flying saucer\" balloon for $1 million. But don't worry, reports KTLA: Richard Heene has vowed to give all the money to charity (specifically, Japan). A bunch of celebrities are hoping JetBlue loses their luggage ... right into the hands of eager bidders. The Celebrity Baggage Auction will benefit DoSomething.org. Buy a signed Jessica Simpson-brand bag or a leather Coach bag personally used by Rosario Dawson, and get two roundtrip JetBlue tickets, too. Click for more. Maybe eBay is the place to buy stuff: Morace Park bought a film reel for $5.25 that turned out to be the only known copy of the Charlie Chaplin film Zepped. The Telegraph reports that it's expected to fetch six figures in a June 29 auction."], "length": 1551, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "In recent news, several stories have emerged from various fields. \n\nIn the realm of true crime, the personal effects of Ted Kaczynski, also known as the Unabomber, are being auctioned off by the US Marshals office. The items, which include his handwritten manifesto and iconic hoodie, are expected to raise funds for a $15 million restitution order to his victims and their families. While some items, such as his handwritten manifesto, have garnered significant interest, others, like a hand-bowed wood saw, have attracted relatively low bids.\n\nArt historian and appraiser Elyse Luray believes that there is value to be found in the auction, despite the tragic nature of Kaczynski's actions. She notes that items like his typewriter and manifesto have historical significance, and that hype can increase the value of certain items. However, criminologist Jeffery Ian Ross Ph.D. suggests that the appeal of crime memorabilia is partially motivated by boredom and voyeurism.\n\nIn a separate story, Twilight actor Kellan Lutz is auctioning off his luggage for charity, teaming up with JetBlue and DoSomething.org to raise funds for youth groups across the country. The auction, which is taking place on eBay, has already raised over $15,000 in the first round.\n\nFinally, a rare film reel, Charlie Chaplin in Zepped, is set to command a six-figure sum after being purchased by collector Morace Park in 2009. The seven-minute long reel is thought to contain some of the earliest known animation and was released in 1917 as a morale-boosting film for British troops during World War One."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A former Boston TV news anchor says that last year, when her son was 18, he was at a bar in Natucket, Mass., when he met Kevin Spacey. The actor \"bought him drink after drink after drink and when my son was drunk, Spacey made his move and sexually assaulted him,\" said Heather Unruh at a press conference Wednesday. She says Spacey stuck his hand down her son's pants and onto his genitals, and tried to convince him to go to a party where they could continue to drink, USA Today reports. A bystander asked the teen if he was OK and the teen said no, at which point the woman urged the teen to flee and he took her advice, Unruh said. Unruh, who appeared with an attorney, says her son filed a police report last week and handed over evidence; she says a criminal investigation has been opened but the Nantucket Police Department has not confirmed that. The attorney with Unruh noted that her son's accusations are \"well within\" both the civil and criminal statutes of limitations. USA Today has a list of the 14 people so far accusing Spacey of sexual assault or harassment. Meanwhile Jon Bernthal, who worked with Spacey on Baby Driver, told a SiriusXM radio show Tuesday that he lost respect for Spacey while working on the film. \"I thought he was a bit of a bully,\" Bernthal said, per People. \"I didn’t really care for the way he was behaving toward some of the other people on set.\""], "length": 3331, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Kevin Spacey, the Academy Award-winning actor, is facing numerous allegations of sexual misconduct, harassment, and assault from over a dozen men. The accusations span decades, with some dating back to the 1980s, and involve a range of behaviors, including unwanted touching, groping, and attempted rape.\n\nOne of the earliest allegations came from actor Anthony Rapp, who claimed that Spacey made unwanted advances towards him in 1986, when Rapp was 14. Spacey responded by coming out as gay, which many saw as an attempt to distract from the underlying accusation.\n\nSince then, numerous other men have come forward to allege that Spacey engaged in similar behavior, including Roberto Cavazos, who claimed that Spacey fondled him at the Old Vic theater in London, and Daniel Beal, who said that Spacey exposed himself to him outside a hotel in West Sussex.\n\nOther accusers include Justin Dawes, who claimed that Spacey gave him cocktails and played pornography on the TV at his apartment in New Haven, Connecticut, in 1988; Mark Ebenhoch, who said that Spacey instructed one of his male crew members to solicit him for sex; and Harry Dreyfuss, who claimed that Spacey groped his genitals when he was 18.\n\nThe allegations have led to a number of consequences for Spacey, including his being cut from the film \"All the Money in the World\" and his being dropped by his talent agency and publicist.\n\nIn addition to the allegations against Spacey, his accusers have also spoken out about the impact of his behavior on their lives. Heather Unruh, a former TV news anchor, said that her son was sexually assaulted by Spacey in 2016, and that the experience has had a lasting impact on his life.\n\nThe allegations against Spacey have also sparked a wider conversation about the treatment of sex addiction and criminal behavior in the entertainment industry. Many have called for greater accountability and support for victims of sexual misconduct.\n\nOverall, the allegations against Kevin Spacey have raised serious questions about his behavior and the culture of the entertainment industry, and have highlighted the need for greater awareness and action to prevent and address sexual misconduct."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Since we last checked in, the situation in Mali following a soldiers' coup has spiraled, with rising concerns over food and gas shortages, violence, and indeed the country's \"very existence,\" according to Le Monde. An update: The coup has driven more than 200,000 people from their homes to other parts of the region, the AP reports. The UN worries that major food shortages could be imminent, and \"mayhem in these towns and cities is increasing,\" says a rep. Neighboring countries have established an embargo against the rebels who ousted the president; they've closed off their borders and frozen the country's regional bank account. That has prompted residents of Mali, which imports all its fuel, to rush to gas stations to collect fuel. The country's electricity grid may also be headed for failure. UNESCO is citing a threat to Timbuktu, a World Heritage Site full of \"architectural wonders,\" notes the BBC. Mosques there are \"essential to the preservation of the identity of the people of Mali,\" says the group. Yet \"nothing seems to be able to stop\" the Tuareg coup, Le Monde reports. West African leaders need to help Mali's military fight back before the instability spreads beyond the country's borders."], "length": 2503, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**West Africa in Crisis: Mali Coup and Its Far-Reaching Consequences**\n\nA military coup in Mali has sent shockwaves across West Africa, threatening the stability of the region and putting millions of people at risk. The coup, which occurred on March 21, was led by a group of soldiers who seized control of the government, citing the former president's mishandling of an insurgency in the north by Tuareg rebels.\n\nThe situation has deteriorated rapidly, with the Tuareg rebels, who are seeking independence for the northern region of Azawad, gaining control of key cities, including Timbuktu, Gao, and Kidal. The rebels have imposed Islamic law, ransacked bars serving alcohol, and banned Western music on local airwaves.\n\nThe international community has responded swiftly, with the Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS) imposing sanctions on Mali, including shutting its borders and freezing its account at the regional central bank. The sanctions are expected to have a devastating impact on the country's economy, which relies heavily on imports, particularly fuel.\n\nThe situation is further complicated by a humanitarian crisis, with over 200,000 people displaced, including around 100,000 who have fled the country. The UN has warned that the crisis could have significant consequences for the entire Sahel region, which is already struggling with drought and poverty.\n\nThe UN Security Council is set to hold an emergency meeting on the crisis, and representatives from the new military leadership are in Nigeria for talks with the authorities. However, the talks have been met with skepticism, with the rebels refusing to recognize the coup leader, Capt. Amadou Haya Sanogo.\n\nThe sanctions imposed by ECOWAS are expected to have a significant impact on the country's economy, with fuel shortages already affecting the population. The situation is dire, with long lines forming at petrol stations, and the country's electricity grid expected to falter in coming weeks.\n\nThe crisis in Mali has far-reaching implications for the region, and the international community is urging calm and restraint. However, the situation remains volatile, and the future of Mali and the Sahel region hangs in the balance."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["JK Rowling makes the case today that millions of kids around the world are living in appalling conditions—the stuff of Grimms' fairytales—in institutions that are supposed to be caring for them. Rowling wants to change that, not by improving these orphanages with \"pretty murals\" or teddy bears, but by eliminating them altogether, she writes in the Guardian. The big challenge is providing support for often-poor families who think they have no choice but to place their child in such an institution. If a child's biological family isn't up to the task, then foster families can step in. But Rowling argues that institutions are absolutely the wrong answer. Rowling has started a charity called Lumos (yes, after one of her Harry Potter spells), and writes that it has made genuine progress in reducing new placements in countries such as Bulgaria, Moldova, and the Czech Republic. She wants to raise awareness and donations with campaigns like this one. \"I recently committed to becoming president of Lumos for life,\" writes Rowling. \"It is my dream that, within my lifetime, the very concept of taking a child away from its family and locking it away will seem to belong to a cruel, fictional world.\" Click for her full column."], "length": 1530, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Global Efforts to End Child Institutionalization**\n\nA growing movement is underway to eradicate the practice of institutionalizing children worldwide. According to estimates, 8 million children are currently separated from their families due to poverty, disability, and discrimination. However, with the help of organizations like Lumos, a charity founded by JK Rowling, it is possible to bring an end to this harmful practice.\n\nLumos, which translates to \"light\" in Latin, was established after a powerful photograph of a caged child sparked the founder's desire to create a charity that sheds light on the lives of these children. The organization's mission is to keep families together and provide support for vulnerable families, fostering, adoption, and inclusive education and health.\n\nScientific research has proven that institutionalization causes irreparable harm to children, including stunting, developmental delays, and psychological trauma. In fact, damage can be done as early as infancy, with children often returning to the world with impaired chances of a happy, healthy life.\n\nDespite the dire effects, there is hope for change. Countries such as Moldova, the Czech Republic, and Bulgaria have seen significant reductions in child institutionalization, with some countries aiming to eradicate the practice altogether. The European Union, the US, and the Global Alliance for Children have set a precedent for other donors, and a social media campaign #Letstalklumos has been launched to keep the issue alive and create awareness.\n\nJK Rowling, founder and president of Lumos, has committed to becoming the president of the organization for life, with the goal of making the concept of institutionalizing children seem \"cruel, fictional\" within her lifetime. With the support of international donors and a growing movement, it is possible to bring an end to this practice and give children the chance to thrive within their own families and communities."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The US and Pakistan have agreed to work together in the future against \"high value targets\" in Pakistan, the countries announced in a joint statement today. The news comes after Sen. John Kerry arrived in Pakistan last night, intending to send a strong message following the death of Osama bin Laden: Cooperate more when it comes to rooting out terrorists, or face \"profound\" changes in the US-Pakistan relationship—which could include kissing billions in aid goodbye. Kerry is the most senior US official to go to Pakistan since bin Laden's death, but since issuing the joint pledge, he said Secretary of State Hillary Clinton will soon announce her own plans to visit, the AP reports. Kerry's meetings with Pakistani leaders began last night, the Washington Post reports. Many in Washington think Pakistan is harboring Islamist militants, and some members of Congress are calling for the US to sever the billions of dollars in aid it provides to Pakistan. Kerry is in Pakistan to lay out the new stakes following bin Laden's death, and question officials about how bin Laden was able to hide in Pakistan for years. In Kabul, Kerry said there is \"some evidence\" that the Pakistan government has knowledge of insurgent activities, calling it \"very disturbing.\""], "length": 1688, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**US and Pakistan Agree to Cooperate on Counter-Terrorism Efforts**\n\nIn a significant development, the US and Pakistan have agreed to work together in any future actions against \"high-value targets\" in Pakistan. The pledge, made in a joint statement, aims to improve relations between the two countries, which have been strained since the US raid that killed Osama bin Laden in May.\n\nUS Senator John Kerry, who led the high-level meetings with Pakistani officials, announced that Secretary of State Hillary Clinton will soon visit Pakistan, a sign of confidence in the relationship. Kerry also stated that he and Pakistani leaders have agreed to a \"series of steps\" to improve relations, although details were not specified.\n\nThe agreement includes increased cooperation on joint operations and intelligence sharing, areas that had come to a near standstill in recent months. The US and Pakistan will also work together to defeat the enemies they face, including al-Qaeda and other terrorist organizations.\n\nAs part of the agreement, Pakistan has agreed to return the tail of a US military helicopter that was damaged during the bin Laden raid. The helicopter was damaged after making a hard landing at bin Laden's compound, and Navy SEALs destroyed part of it before leaving.\n\nKerry emphasized that the secrecy surrounding the Abbottabad operation was not a reflection of US distrust, but rather a necessary measure to protect the lives of the professionals involved. He also assured Pakistan that the US has \"no designs\" on its nuclear arsenal.\n\nThe agreement is seen as a crucial step in rebuilding the relationship between the two countries, which have been strained since the bin Laden raid. The US relies on Pakistan as a key supply route for troops in Afghanistan, and Pakistan needs US assistance to arm its military and shore up its economy.\n\nHowever, the agreement also comes at a time of heightened tensions between the two countries. Pakistani Prime Minister Yousaf Raza Gillani has begun a four-day visit to China, an old ally of Pakistan, and Parliament has passed a resolution condemning the bin Laden raid and threatening to cut off NATO supply routes through Pakistan.\n\nOverall, the agreement marks a significant step forward in US-Pakistan relations, but challenges remain to be addressed, including Pakistan's alleged support for Afghan insurgents and its ties to militant organizations."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Kim Jong Nam's assassins killed him with a banned chemical weapon, Malaysian police revealed Friday. The country's police chief said toxicology reports on swabs from the face and eyes of the exiled North Korean found VX nerve agent, which the BBC notes is classed as a weapon of mass destruction by the United Nations. He said that one of two women believed to have rubbed the extremely toxic substance on Kim's face with their hands suffered from vomiting after the attack. The New York Times reports that VX agent can be created by mixing two compounds—and police suspect the two women put the substances on Kim's face, one after the other, to create a deadly dose. The police chief said the airport where Kim was attacked is now being decontaminated. North Korea—which is widely suspected to have been behind the killing of leader Kim Jong Un's half brother—never signed the Chemical Weapons Convention that banned VX, the AP notes. Pyongyang denies involvement and says Malaysia's investigation is full of \"holes and contradictions.\" The father of one of the two women being held, Vietnamese citizen Doan Thi Huong, tells the Times his daughter trained as a pharmacist and he has seen little of her in recent years. (Police say that after Malaysia refused to give Kim's body to North Korean diplomats, somebody tried to break into the morgue.)"], "length": 2917, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A mysterious attack on Kim Jong-nam, the half-brother of North Korean leader Kim Jong-un, has sparked a diplomatic crisis between Malaysia and North Korea. The 45-year-old Kim Jong-nam was poisoned with the highly toxic nerve agent VX at Kuala Lumpur International Airport on February 13. Malaysian police have confirmed that VX was used in the attack, which killed Kim Jong-nam within two hours of the incident.\n\nThe investigation into the attack has raised questions about public safety in Malaysia, as the airport was not decontaminated for 11 days after the incident. Malaysian police have arrested three people, including two women who are suspected of carrying out the attack, and are seeking several other individuals, including a North Korean diplomat and an employee of North Korea's state-owned airline.\n\nNorth Korea has denied any involvement in the attack and has accused Malaysia of fabricating evidence. The country has also warned Malaysia that it will take \"stronger measures\" if the investigation continues. The Malaysian government has rejected these claims and has insisted that it will pursue the truth behind the attack.\n\nToxicologists have expressed surprise that the two women suspected of carrying out the attack did not suffer any ill effects from exposure to VX. Dr. Bruce Goldberger, a leading toxicologist, suggested that the women may have been given the antidote, which can be administered by injection.\n\nThe attack has also raised concerns about North Korea's development of chemical weapons. The country is believed to have the capacity to produce up to 4,500 metric tons of chemical weapons during a typical year, and its current inventory has been estimated at 2,500 to 5,000 tons. North Korea's development of such agents has been of special concern because of fears it might try to put them in artillery shells for an attack on South Korea's capital, potentially threatening the lives of millions.\n\nThe case has marked a serious turnaround in relations between Malaysia and North Korea, with Malaysia expelling North Korea's envoy Kang Chol for \"spewing lies\" about the attack. The incident has also highlighted the need for greater international cooperation in preventing the spread of chemical weapons.\n\nIn summary, the attack on Kim Jong-nam has sparked a diplomatic crisis between Malaysia and North Korea, with Malaysia insisting that it will pursue the truth behind the attack. The use of VX nerve agent has raised concerns about public safety and the development of chemical weapons by North Korea. The incident has also highlighted the need for greater international cooperation in preventing the spread of chemical weapons."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["San Francisco-area cops have arrested three more young men in the savage gang rape, beating, and robbery of a 15-year-old girl outside her homecoming dance while dozens more watched. Five youths are being held, ranging from a 15-year-old boy who knew the victim to a 21-year-old. The girl was drinking outside the dance Saturday night, reports the San Francisco Chronicle, and the group turned on her when she became drunk. Charges are expected today. \"This was a barbaric act. I still cannot get my head around the fact that numerous people either watched, walked away or participated in her assault,\" a Richmond cop tells the AP. \"It's one of the most disturbing crimes in my 15 years as a police officer.\" Some students began transferring out of the school. \"It's not safe there at all,\" said a 16-year-old girl. \"I'm not going back.\""], "length": 1576, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A horrific gang rape and beating of a 15-year-old girl occurred on school grounds after her homecoming dance in Richmond, California. The attack, which took place on October 24, 2009, was witnessed by up to two dozen people, but none of them intervened to stop it. The victim, a sophomore, had left the dance and was drinking alcohol in a school courtyard with a group when she was attacked. The assailants, ranging in age from 15 to their early 20s, took turns assaulting the girl and stealing her jewelry.\n\nTwo suspects were initially arrested, Manuel Ortega, 19, and a 15-year-old boy who attends the school. Ortega was arrested after trying to flee the scene, while the teenager was booked on suspicion of sexual assault at a juvenile hall. However, police later revealed that as many as 10 assailants were involved in the attack, and they expect to make more arrests.\n\nThe attack has raised questions about campus security, as Richmond High School has surveillance cameras, but none of them were working at the time of the attack. The school's administration has promised to install a new system in the next few months. The incident has also sparked outrage, with police Lt. Mark Gagan calling it \"one of the most heinous crimes\" he has ever seen.\n\nThe victim remains hospitalized with non-life-threatening injuries and is expected to make a full recovery. The police are offering a $20,000 reward for information leading to the conviction of any of the assailants. The incident has also led to a transfer of several students, including 16-year-old Jennie Steinberg, who has left the school in response to the attack.\n\nThe attack has also highlighted the city's struggles with crime and gang activity, with Richmond having one of the highest murder rates in the state. The incident has sparked a renewed focus on campus security and the need for more effective measures to prevent such attacks in the future."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The White House on Friday declassified a partisan and bitterly disputed memo on the Russia investigation, and a House committee immediately made it public. Media outlets were just beginning to assess it. You can read the document here, via the Washington Post. The White House move came over the fierce objections of the FBI and Justice Department, which have said the document prepared by Republicans on the House intelligence committee is inaccurate and missing critical context, per the AP. The memo alleges that the FBI abused US government surveillance powers in its investigation into Russian election interference. Trump, who has called the investigation a \"witch hunt,\" has supported the release of the memo in the apparent hopes that it could help undermine the probe being led by special counsel Robert Mueller. The president, dogged by the unrelenting investigation into his campaign's ties to Russia, lashed out anew Friday at the FBI and Justice Department as politically biased against Republicans. \"The top Leadership and Investigators of the FBI and the Justice Department have politicized the sacred investigative process in favor of Democrats and against Republicans - something which would have been unthinkable just a short time ago. Rank & File are great people!\" Trump tweeted."], "length": 2676, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\nIn recent weeks, several significant news stories have emerged, impacting national and international politics, technology, and society.\n\n**FBI Surveillance and National Security**\n\nThe Nunes memo, released by the House Intelligence Committee, alleged that the FBI abused its surveillance authority, particularly in monitoring a former Trump campaign adviser. The memo sparked controversy, with the FBI expressing concerns that key facts were missing. The memo's release was authorized by President Trump, who directed the declassification of the document.\n\nAdditionally, an investigation into the Department of Justice and Federal Bureau of Investigation's use of the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act (FISA) during the 2016 presidential election cycle revealed concerns about the legitimacy and legality of certain interactions with the FISC. The investigation found that material and relevant information was omitted from FISA applications, including information about the Steele dossier, a compiled document by Christopher Steele on behalf of the Democratic National Committee and Hillary Clinton's campaign.\n\n**Technology and Social Media**\n\nA tweet with a location feature was introduced, allowing users to add location information to their tweets. This feature can be enabled from the web or via third-party applications, and users always have the option to delete their tweet location history.\n\n**Other Developments**\n\nNo other significant news stories were mentioned in the provided passages."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Critics aren’t exactly howling over the new remake of The Wolfman starring Benicio del Toro, giving it mostly mediocre or failing marks. Here’s what they’re saying: “The movie is pungent with atmosphere, laying down a thick fog of creepy Victorian murk,” writes Kyle Smith of the New York Post, but the story is toothless, centering on a mystery “so simple that even Marmaduke could have sniffed it out.” “The movie is scary only as regards all its wasted potential,” laments John Anderson of the Wall Street Journal. There are good elements here, but they’re “reduced to a gruesome fondue, accessorized by actorly ham and studio cheese.” The filmmakers obviously loved the original Wolfman, but “nostalgia isn't always the best barometer,” writes James Berardinelli of ReelViews. The makeup is “inexcusably campy,” and scenes from the original are “recreated in a fashion that seems more Monty Python than unsettling.” But Ty Burr of the Boston Globe had fun. “The movie is by no means good,” he writes, “but it’s surprisingly enjoyable: a misty, moody Saturday-matinee monster-chiller-horror special.”"], "length": 3747, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "In recent news, several horror movies have been released, sparking mixed reactions from critics and audiences alike. \"The Wolfman\" (2010), a remake of the 1941 classic, has been met with criticism for its attempt to fuse gothic storytelling with modern horror elements. The film, directed by Joe Johnston and starring Benicio Del Toro, Anthony Hopkins, and Emily Blunt, has been panned for its lack of emotional connection and campy makeup. Despite this, some critics have found the film to be enjoyable, praising its atmosphere and mood.\n\nAnother horror movie, \"Valentine's Day\" (2010), has been criticized for its lack of originality and poor writing. The film, directed by Garry Marshall, brings together a large ensemble cast, including Anne Hathaway, Julia Roberts, and Ashton Kutcher, but fails to deliver any memorable moments.\n\nIn contrast, \"Ajami\" (2009), a foreign language film, has received critical acclaim for its tense and disturbing portrayal of life in an Israeli ghetto. The film, directed by Scandar Copti and Yaron Shani, has been praised for its unique storytelling and strong performances.\n\nThe \"Red Riding Trilogy\" (2009), a British horror series, has also received critical acclaim for its faithful adaptation of David Peace's novels. The series, directed by Julian Jarrold, James Marsh, and Anand Tucker, has been praised for its strong performances and atmospheric direction.\n\nFinally, a new DVD release, \"The Wolf Man\" (1941), has been highlighted as a classic of the horror genre. The film, directed by George Waggner and starring Lon Chaney Jr., has been praised for its influential special effects and atmospheric direction.\n\nOverall, the recent news in the horror genre has been marked by a mix of critical acclaim and disappointment. While some films have been praised for their originality and strong performances, others have been criticized for their lack of originality and poor writing."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Sinn Fein leader Gerry Adams, lately the very high-profile suspect in a 1972 murder, will go free without charge, Northern Ireland police officials tell the BBC. Adams has been questioned since Wednesday in County Antrim over allegations that he, as an IRA commander ordered the murder of a 37-year-old widow and mom of 10, Jean McConville. The AP confirms the report, saying that Adams will likely be freed today, but notes that police say they'll send an evidence file to their British counterparts."], "length": 1816, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Gerry Adams Released After Questioning Over IRA Murder**\n\nSinn Féin leader Gerry Adams was released after four days of questioning by police over the 1972 IRA murder of Jean McConville, a 37-year-old widow who was abducted, shot, and secretly buried. Adams, who has denied any involvement in her murder, claimed that the timing of his arrest was politically motivated and that the police could have used discretion rather than arresting him.\n\nAdams, who had contacted police two months ago about the McConville case, presented himself for interview by prior arrangement with detectives on Wednesday night. He was then arrested under the terrorism act for questioning about the IRA murder. After his release, Adams stated that he was innocent of any involvement in her murder and that the IRA is \"gone, it's finished.\"\n\nThe decision to release Adams means that prosecution lawyers will decide if charges will be brought. The file sent by the police to the Public Prosecution Service (PPS) will detail the evidence gathered, and it will be up to PPS lawyers to decide if there is enough evidence to bring any charges and what those charges would be.\n\nThe McConville family, who have been fighting for justice for 40 years, expressed disappointment with the timing of the arrest. Michael McConville, Jean's son, stated that the family's fight for justice would continue, and that they would not stop until they get justice for their mother.\n\nThe arrest has sparked controversy, with some politicians and parties rejecting claims that the timing of the arrest was political. Northern Ireland Justice Minister David Ford stated that politicians should steer out of allegations of political policing.\n\nThe case against Gerry Adams is based on an interview he allegedly gave to researchers at Boston College in the US, which is part of an oral history of the Troubles. Ivor Bell, a leader in the Provisional IRA in the 1970s, was charged with aiding and abetting the murder last month, and there have also been a number of other arrests recently.\n\nOverall, the release of Gerry Adams after questioning over the IRA murder of Jean McConville has raised questions about the timing of the arrest and the potential for political interference in the investigation."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Two workers at a Chicago day care were fired and arrested after a disturbing incident that left five toddlers injured. Police say surveillance video captured one of the workers burning the 2-year-olds with a hot glue gun while the other woman watched and laughed, People reports. Lizandra Cosme, 32, has been charged with five counts of aggravated battery of a child causing great bodily harm, the Chicago Sun-Times reports. Susana Gonzalez, 27, faces five misdemeanor charges of causing the circumstances of child endangerment over the Dec. 1 incident at the Children's Place day care. It's not clear how seriously the children were injured. Prosecutors say Cosme, who brought the glue gun for a Christmas project, was captured on video applying the hot glue directly to the hands and arms of the three girls and two boys. \"Each of the child victims winced and some whined at the hot glue gun application,\" a prosecutor said during a Monday court appearance. Prosecutors said Cosme tried to cover up the incident, asking a father whether his child had been burned at home, ABC7 reports. They said her actions were discovered when one child's mother, an ER physician, saw the burns and demanded to see surveillance footage. Cosme's lawyer said his client \"screwed up\" but did not mean to hurt the children."], "length": 1272, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Two Daycare Workers Charged with Injuring Five Toddlers with Hot Glue**\n\nIn a shocking incident, two daycare workers have been arrested and charged with injuring five toddlers with hot glue at a daycare center in Chicago. Lizandra Cosme, 32, and Susana Gonzalez, 27, face serious charges after surveillance footage allegedly captured the incident on December 1.\n\nAccording to police and prosecutors, Cosme and Gonzalez were working at the Children's Learning Place, a licensed child-care facility that serves children between the ages of 6 weeks and 6 years. On the day of the incident, Cosme allegedly brought a hot glue gun from home to use on a Christmas project, while Gonzalez watched and laughed as Cosme applied the glue gun to the hands and arms of five 2-year-old children.\n\nThe children, three girls and two boys, were left with burns and other injuries, with some whining in pain as the glue gun was applied. Gonzalez was seen laughing nearby, while Cosme tried to apply neosporin to one of the children after seeing circular burn marks.\n\nThe daycare center immediately terminated the two workers after learning of the incident, and the state Department of Children and Family Services is investigating allegations of abuse. Cosme was denied bail and faces five felony counts of aggravated battery to a child, while Gonzalez was charged with five misdemeanor counts of causing the circumstances of endangering a child.\n\nThe incident has sparked outrage and concern about the safety and well-being of children in childcare facilities. The daycare center has assured parents that the well-being of their children is paramount and that they are working with investigators to ensure that the incident does not happen again.\n\nThe charges against Cosme and Gonzalez are a stark reminder of the importance of ensuring the safety and well-being of children in childcare facilities. The incident is currently under investigation, and both women are expected to appear in court on December 26."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Texas gubernatorial candidate Wendy Davis' new memoir is dedicated in part to daughters Amber, Dru, and Tate, and those familiar with Davis' life story might be puzzled by the last name on that list. That's because Davis is revealing for the first time that Tate is the unborn child that she and her husband aborted in the second trimester, after doctors discovered a severe brain abnormality. The San Antonio Express-News and the AP obtained advanced copies, and the news is generating headlines given that Davis shot to national fame while filibustering against an abortion bill in Texas. Davis writes that she decided to abort in 1996 after doctors said the baby would be blind, deaf, and in a vegetative state if she survived the pregnancy. Davis also felt the fetus “tremble violently\" in the womb, \"as if someone were applying an electric shock to her, and I knew then what I needed to do. She was suffering.\" Her doctor \"quieted\" the unborn child's heart and delivered her by C-section. Davis and her husband had Tate baptized. After the abortion, \"an indescribable blackness followed,\" writes Davis in Forgetting to Be Afraid, \"and when I finally did come through it, I emerged a different person.\" Davis also writes of a previously disclosed procedure to terminate an earlier ectopic pregnancy in which the embryo was implanted outside the uterus. She says she opted not to talk about either case during her famous filibuster because she feared it would \"overshadow the events of the day.\""], "length": 2373, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Wendy Davis' Memoir Reveals Personal Abortion Stories**\n\nIn her upcoming memoir, \"Forgetting to Be Afraid,\" Texas Democratic gubernatorial candidate Wendy Davis discloses two personal abortion stories that have shed new light on her stance on the issue. The memoir, set to be released on Tuesday, reveals that Davis had an abortion in the 1990s after discovering that the fetus had a severe brain abnormality, and another ectopic pregnancy in 1994, which was medically necessary to terminate.\n\nAccording to the book, Davis was 17 years old when she discovered the severe brain abnormality in her second pregnancy, and was told that the baby would be deaf, blind, and in a permanent vegetative state if she survived delivery. Davis writes that she felt the baby \"suffering\" and made the difficult decision to end the pregnancy. The experience left her \"forever changed\" and she says that an \"indescribable blackness\" followed the pregnancy.\n\nThe memoir also reveals that Davis considered revealing the terminated pregnancies during her 2013 filibuster, but decided against it, saying that it would have overshadowed the events of the day. The filibuster, which lasted nearly 13 hours, temporarily delayed the passage of sweeping new abortion restrictions in Texas.\n\nDavis' personal abortion stories have been met with sympathy from some anti-abortion groups, but also criticism from others, including Planned Parenthood president Cecile Richards, who praised Davis' \"unwavering courage\" in sharing her story.\n\nThe memoir is seen as a key part of Davis' campaign, as she faces off against Republican Attorney General Greg Abbott, who is heavily favored to replace Republican Gov. Rick Perry next year. Despite the controversy surrounding her abortion stance, Davis remains a strong contender in the race, with many believing that her personal story has humanized her and made the issue more relatable to voters."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Two men have been charged with human trafficking after police arrested them for running a prostitution ring out of a senior living facility in Pittsfield, Mass. Police say Joseph Van Wert, 65, and Randy Lambach, 45, will face a hearing later this month, the New York Daily News reports. Lambach would allegedly seek out women with drug addictions and post their pictures online, then arrange meetings with clients. Some of these meetings took place in Van Wert's apartment in a senior facility. Van Wert offered his apartment in return for cash or drugs. The Berkshire Eagle reports that the police investigation began last spring in response to citizen complaints about increased prostitution across the city. Lambach would allegedly keep up to 90% of the money the women made, sometimes refusing them cash altogether and paying them in heroin or crack cocaine instead. At least one woman said Lambach drove her to an \"appointment\" across state lines. Police estimated one of the victims was 15 years old. They were also told that some of the women were Lambach's former foster children. Both men have pleaded not guilty, Lambach to four counts of human trafficking and Van Wert to conspiracy to commit human trafficking, deriving support from prostitution, maintaining a house of prostitution, and sexual conduct for a fee."], "length": 1457, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Human Trafficking Ring Uncovered in Pittsfield, Massachusetts**\n\nA recent investigation by the Pittsfield Police Department has led to the arrest of two men, Joseph VanWert, 65, and Randy Lambach, 45, on charges of human trafficking. The ring, which allegedly operated out of an apartment in a senior living facility, targeted women with severe drug addictions and advertised their services on social media.\n\nAccording to police, Lambach, who allegedly ran the prostitution ring for several years, would seek out women with drug addictions and photograph them, then post advertisements online seeking clients for sexual services. He would then arrange meetings between the women and prospective clients, often in his third-floor apartment at the facility, in exchange for drugs and cash.\n\nThe investigation, which began in the spring of 2017, revealed that Lambach had recruited and transported numerous women, some of whom were as young as 15, into the prostitution ring. He allegedly threatened to turn the women in if they stopped working for him and warned them that the police would not believe them because they were \"drug addicts and whores.\"\n\nVanWert, who allowed some of the meetings to take place in his apartment, was also charged with conspiracy to commit human trafficking, deriving support from prostitution, maintaining a house of prostitution, and sexual conduct for a fee. Lambach pleaded not guilty to four counts of human trafficking, while VanWert pleaded not guilty to the aforementioned charges.\n\nThe investigation, which involved the Pittsfield Police Department, the state police detective unit, and the District Attorney's Office, is ongoing, and additional charges may be forthcoming. The arrest of VanWert and Lambach brings an end to a disturbing and exploitative operation that targeted vulnerable women in the Pittsfield community.\n\n**Key Facts:**\n\n* Two men, Joseph VanWert, 65, and Randy Lambach, 45, were arrested on human trafficking charges.\n* The ring allegedly operated out of an apartment in a senior living facility in Pittsfield, Massachusetts.\n* Women with severe drug addictions were targeted and advertised on social media for sexual services.\n* Lambach allegedly recruited and transported numerous women, some as young as 15, into the prostitution ring.\n* VanWert allowed meetings to take place in his apartment in exchange for drugs and cash.\n* The investigation is ongoing, and additional charges may be forthcoming."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["You think Stonehenge is impressive? Archaeologists in Britain are excavating a monument ten times larger than the iconic structure, though it appears to the naked eye to be little more than farmland. A henge is a circular earthwork, and the one in question is Marden Henge, which sits a few miles north of Stonehenge in Wiltshire. National Geographic reports that roughly 4,500 years ago it featured 10-foot-tall earthen berms that encompassed some 40 acres. The Guardian writes that the henge was \"far larger than the Avebury or Stonehenge circles, and too large for any imaginable practical use.\" But over the ages Marden Henge's berms have \"slumped\" and farmers have worked the land, and archaeologists have gravitated to the more breathtaking Stonehenge. Until now. Jim Leary of the University of Reading has just kicked off a three-year study of the site; his excavations (he worked at the site in 2010, too) are the only ones to have occurred there in nearly 50 years. He hopes the berms will help explain the \"insane, utterly unsustainable\" construction boom that led to Marden Henge and four other nearby Neolithic monuments, including Stonehenge. \"Not nearly enough attention has been paid to the archaeology of the fertile valley in between these places,\" says Leary. So far he's found the remains of a 4,000-year-old teen wearing an amber necklace just outside Marden Henge, per the BBC; fancy arrowheads; and a stone building within the henge containing the bones of at least 13 pigs, suggesting a huge feast was held there. \"For all the attention that has been lavished on Stonehenge over the years, we may well find out that Marden was where it was really at during the Neolithic,\" he says. (There's a giant \"super henge\" under Stonehenge.)"], "length": 1428, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Archaeologists have made significant discoveries at Marden Henge in Wiltshire, England. The site, which dates back over 4,300 years, has yielded a wealth of information about the lives of the people who lived there. Excavations have uncovered a well-preserved house, which is believed to be one of the best-preserved from the Neolithic period ever found in the UK. The house features beautifully worked flint arrowheads, decorated pottery, and other artifacts, including a Roman brooch and shale and copper bracelets.\n\nThe site also includes a large enclosure, which is believed to have been used for status and wealth display. The enclosure is surrounded by a ditch and bank, and is thought to have been one of the largest and most impressive in Britain. Archaeologists have also discovered a previously unrecorded Roman complex, including the foundations of an impressive barn.\n\nIn a separate discovery, a 4,000-year-old Bronze Age skeleton was found at Wilsford henge in the same area. The skeleton, which is believed to be that of a child, was found wearing an amber necklace and lying in a foetal position. Scientific analysis of the skeleton is expected to provide information on the child's diet, pathologies, and date of burial.\n\nThe discoveries at Marden Henge and Wilsford henge are part of a larger excavation project, which is a partnership between the University of Reading, Historic England, the Arts and Humanities Research Council, and Wiltshire Museum. The project aims to gain a better understanding of the people who lived in the areas surrounding Stonehenge.\n\nThe site is set to welcome visitors for an open day, which will feature finds on display, tours, and activities. The event is part of the national festival of archaeology, which will include over 1,000 events across the country."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["As everyone wonders whether Conan O’Brien will stay at NBC or move to Fox, the Tonight Show host offered up a few more novel ideas yesterday: Perhaps he’ll “pretend to put my son in a giant foil balloon, then sit back and watch the offers come pouring in!” Watch him deliver the rest of his options on the video above—then check out Jay Leno, who also addressed the drama last night on his show. “NBC says the show performed exactly as they expected it would, and then they canceled it,” Leno said. “Now don’t confuse that with when we were on at late night and we performed better than expected and then they canceled us. That was totally different!” Meanwhile, the Los Angeles Times takes a closer look at Fox’s early talks with Conan—who “would be a very compatible fit for our brand,” says the president of Fox Entertainment."], "length": 791, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Unfortunately, there are only two news passages provided, and the second passage appears to be unrelated to the first one. The first passage discusses the importance of understanding the terms and conditions of emergency loans, the potential risks of predatory lending, and the benefits of using savings accounts to manage finances. The second passage seems to be a promotional or social media post about cars and does not contain any news-related information.\n\nHowever, if you would like, I can provide a one-page summary of the first passage:\n\n**Emergency Loans: A Guide to Understanding the Risks and Benefits**\n\nEmergency loans can provide much-needed financial assistance during difficult times, but it is essential to understand the terms and conditions associated with these loans. These loans often require filling out loan forms and may come with fees and interest rates. It is crucial to be aware of the potential risks of predatory lending and to explore alternative options, such as using savings accounts to manage finances.\n\nEmergency loans can be used to cover various expenses, including medical bills, car repairs, and education costs. However, it is essential to carefully review the loan agreement and understand the repayment terms before applying. Installment payday loans, in particular, offer flexible repayment options and can be a viable option for those in need of emergency funding.\n\nWhen applying for emergency loans, it is essential to be aware of the lender's requirements and to carefully review the loan agreement. In the UK, lenders typically require applicants to be permanent citizens, in regular employment, and to have a stable income. No-credit-check loans are also available, but these may come with higher interest rates.\n\nUltimately, emergency loans can be a useful tool for managing finances during difficult times, but it is essential to approach these loans with caution and to carefully review the terms and conditions before applying."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Oil giant BP lost $5.2 billion last year, but the company somehow saw fit to propose maximum bonuses for 2015 for its top executives, including a 20% pay increase for CEO Bob Dudley—a proposal that 59% of shareholders roundly rejected by proxy vote at Thursday's annual meeting, MarketWatch reports. The company had indicated earlier in the day that it may also have to reduce its dividend, the Wall Street Journal reports. Dudley is set to receive the full bonus he was eligible for, which comes to $4.2 million (including $1.4 million in cash and a portion in deferred BP shares), per an earlier MarketWatch report. This amount was bumped up from the $3 million ($1 million in cash) he received in 2014. BP's CFO was also on the list to rake in his full bonus. \"We think it sends the wrong message,\" a rep for shareholder Royal London Asset Management tells the BBC. \"It shows that the board is out of touch.\" Not only did BP suffer straight-up monetary losses as the price of oil fell, it also announced it will be getting rid of about 7,000 jobs and taking other belt-tightening measures. And the Financial Times notes that other energy company execs saw their pay slashed in 2015. But a BP spokesman says \"executives performed strongly in a difficult environment in 2015, managing the things they could control and for which they were accountable.\" Andy Critchlow, writing for the Reuters Breakingviews blog, agrees. \"Dudley has to work harder than his predecessors,\" he writes, noting the CEO has helped the company recover from the 2010 Deepwater Horizon disaster and improved safety protocol. \"The mild-mannered American has had possibly the toughest job in the oil industry. His rewards look in line with that task.\" Carl-Henric Svanberg, chairman of BP's board, says the nonbinding shareholder vote won't alter the payouts they've already decided on, but that the board will take investors' concerns into account when coming up with next year's compensation packages."], "length": 3225, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "BP's Annual General Meeting (AGM) saw a significant rebuke to the company's executive pay policy, with a majority of shareholders voting against the company's executive compensation decisions for 2015. The vote, which was non-binding, saw 59% of investors rejecting the company's executive pay package, including a 20% increase in Chief Executive Bob Dudley's total pay for the year.\n\nThe decision was seen as a response to BP's poor performance in 2015, which included a $5.2 billion loss and the slashing of thousands of jobs. Despite this, Dudley's pay package was increased by 20%, with his total bonus for the year amounting to $4.2 million.\n\nThe vote was widely seen as a victory for shareholders, who have been increasingly vocal about their discontent with executive pay packages in recent years. The decision also highlighted the growing trend of institutional investors and advisers taking a more aggressive stance over pay.\n\nBP's Chairman, Carl-Henric Svanberg, defended the pay package, stating that it was based on \"exceptional\" company performance during a difficult year. However, the company also signaled that it may have to reduce its dividend, as low oil prices continue to threaten the once-sacrosanct investor payouts across the industry.\n\nThe outcome of the vote has significant implications for BP and the wider industry, with many experts warning that the company's board will have to take action to address shareholder concerns. The decision also raises questions about the fairness and transparency of executive pay packages, and whether they are aligned with the interests of shareholders.\n\nIn related news, other companies have also faced criticism over their executive pay packages, with some shareholders rebelling against pay deals in recent weeks. The trend is likely to continue, with many investors and advisers calling for greater transparency and accountability in executive pay.\n\nOverall, the outcome of the BP AGM vote is a significant development in the ongoing debate over executive pay and corporate governance. It highlights the growing power of shareholders and the need for companies to be more transparent and accountable in their decision-making processes."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The Peruvian cop who claimed to have busted a crime ring that killed dozens of people to harvest their fat has been suspended for lying. Felix Murga, the country's top organized crime investigator, said that a gang had killed 60 people to sell their fat at $15,000 a liter. Investigators now believe there was just one victim and his murder was linked to drug trafficking, the BBC reports. Murga appears to have revived an ancient Andean legend of killers who roam the mountains extracting fat from travelers, say authorities, who blame him for damaging the police force's reputation and scaring tourists away. \"This has been a ruse of bad taste,\" a local politician tells Reuters."], "length": 1088, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Peru's Top Investigator Suspended for Misleading Public\n\nIn a shocking turn of events, Peru's top investigator, Eusebio Felix, has been suspended from his job after misleading the public about a gang of serial killers who allegedly murdered up to 60 people and sold their fat for thousands of dollars a liter. The investigation, which was initially met with widespread attention and concern, has been found to be entirely fabricated.\n\nAccording to reports, Felix claimed that police had arrested four suspects who confessed to the gruesome murders, which were allegedly part of an ancient Andean legend about the Pishtacos, mythical killers who murdered people on lonely roads to collect their fat. However, as the investigation progressed, doubts began to emerge, and it was revealed that there was no evidence to support the claims.\n\nPolice from the region where the alleged crimes took place denied any knowledge of the gang, and medical experts dismissed the theory that human fat had any monetary value. Additionally, anthropologists suggested that the police's story was deliberately played on an old Peruvian myth to explain crimes that had not been fully investigated.\n\nThe suspension of Felix has been met with criticism from politicians, who have blamed the police for scaring away tourists. Doctors have also weighed in, stating that it would be pointless to kill people to harvest their fat, as it could be easily collected from plastic surgery clinics.\n\nThe incident has raised questions about the credibility of Peru's police force and the need for greater transparency and accountability. The suspension of Felix serves as a reminder of the importance of fact-checking and verifying information before sharing it with the public.\n\nIn related news, Peru's police chief, Miguel Hidalgo, has announced that Felix will be put on indefinite leave from his job for sullying the reputation of his unit. The incident has sparked a wider debate about the role of the police in Peru and the need for reform."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Hikers on the Pacific Crest Trail can expect to encounter harsh-but-beautiful landscapes, extreme fatigue, and—these days, at least—traffic. \"You can’t talk about the Pacific Crest Trail without mentioning the crowds,\" Pete Brook writes for Outside. He should know: Brook currently is trekking the 2,650-mile PCT, which runs from the Mexican border through California, Oregon, and Washington to the border with Canada. But don't take his word for it: The Pacific Crest Trail Association so far this year has issued more than 4,000 permits to people planning on tackling at least 500 miles of the trail. To put things in perspective: 300 people attempted to complete the entire PCT in 2006, according to the AP, with about 120 of those making it. In 2014, 1,000 people set off and about half completed the trail. More traffic means more stress on the land, more improperly disposed of poop, and more inexperienced hikers requiring rescue. So what's with the increase in traffic over the past few years? You can thank Cheryl Strayed and her enormously popular memoir-turned-major-motion-picture Wild. After the book came out in 2012, there was a small increase in interest in the trail, the PCTA's Jack Haskel told the AP in 2015. But after the movie, starring Reese Witherspoon, was released in 2014, interest in the PCT exploded. \"Millions are hearing about it now and are being inspired,\" Haskel said. Strayed, more or less unprepared for the journey, hiked 1,100 miles of the trail as a form of therapy as she grappled with personal demons. \"However for others that follow her strategy of deliberate ineptitude, things might not turn out so well,\" writes DontHikeLikeWild.org. \"Our advice to would-be hikers: Grow a spine first. Then get out there and hike the PCT.\" (This female hiker is the \"biggest badass you've never heard of.\")"], "length": 2416, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "The Pacific Crest Trail (PCT) is experiencing a surge in popularity, thanks in part to the success of Cheryl Strayed's memoir \"Wild\" and the recent release of the movie adaptation. The trail, which spans 2,650 miles from Mexico to Canada, has seen a significant increase in hikers attempting to complete the full journey, with over 4,000 long-distance hiking permits issued in 2016 alone.\n\nThe PCT Association has implemented a quota system to manage the number of hikers on the trail, with a cap of 50 permits per day. This system aims to mitigate the environmental impact of large numbers of hikers and reduce the strain on essential services such as search and rescue operations.\n\nDespite the efforts to manage the number of hikers, the trail is still experiencing overcrowding issues, particularly in the desert environments that dominate the first 700 miles. This has raised concerns about the impact on the environment, including degraded vegetation, establishment of new camp spots, and human waste.\n\nThe hiker community has long discussed the issue of overcrowding, with some advocating for a more formalized system of permits and others arguing that the trail should remain open to all who wish to hike it. However, many hikers, including those who have completed the trail multiple times, are concerned about the impact of large numbers of hikers on the environment and the trail's fragile ecosystem.\n\nIn addition to the environmental concerns, the influx of hikers has also put a strain on essential services, including search and rescue operations. The PCT Association has reported 20 rescues by April 26, all of which were in the first 50 miles of the trail.\n\nDespite the challenges, many hikers are drawn to the PCT's unique combination of challenge, change, reconnection, and reinvention. The trail offers a chance for hikers to disconnect from the world and reconnect with themselves and nature. However, the increasing popularity of the trail has raised questions about the sustainability of this experience and the need for more responsible and sustainable hiking practices.\n\nOverall, the PCT is facing a complex set of challenges, including overcrowding, environmental concerns, and the strain on essential services. However, the trail remains a beloved and iconic destination for hikers and outdoor enthusiasts, and efforts are underway to manage the impact of large numbers of hikers and ensure the long-term sustainability of this unique and special place."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Noticed Pete Davidson's absence from SNL as of late? Well, there's a good reason behind it: The 23-year-old has been focusing on his sobriety. After telling High Times last fall that he couldn't function without the medical marijuana he's prescribed for Crohn's disease, Davidson now says, \"I quit drugs and am happy and sober for the first time in eight years\" in an Instagram post. Rapper Kid Cudi might have inspired that sobriety, per the New York Daily News. Last fall, Davidson told a radio station, \"I would have killed myself if I didn't have Kid Cudi,\" who'd checked himself into rehab a few weeks earlier."], "length": 805, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Pete Davidson, a cast member of Saturday Night Live (SNL), has been open about his struggles with Crohn's disease and his use of medical marijuana. In a recent interview with High Times, Davidson discussed how he uses cannabis to manage his symptoms and perform on the show. He stated that the medical marijuana program in New York State is inadequate and difficult to navigate, and that he believes cannabis should be legal beyond just medical purposes.\n\nHowever, in a surprising turn of events, Davidson has announced that he has quit using drugs and is happy and sober for the first time in 8 years. He shared this news on social media, expressing his gratitude for the love and support of his fans and friends. Davidson attributed his sobriety to his newfound happiness and the positive influence of his girlfriend and friends.\n\nThis news comes after Davidson revealed his struggles with Crohn's disease and his use of medical marijuana in a previous interview. He had previously stated that he uses cannabis to cope with his symptoms and that it helps him perform on the show. Davidson also credited Kid Cudi's music with saving his life, stating that the rapper's music provided him with comfort and support during a difficult time.\n\nDavidson's announcement of his sobriety has been met with support from his fans and friends, who have expressed their love and appreciation for his honesty. The comedian's newfound sobriety is a positive development, and his fans are eager to see him continue to thrive and succeed in his career.\n\nIn related news, SNL season 42 is set to premiere on NBC on October 1st, and Davidson's first hour-long stand-up special, \"SMD,\" will air on Comedy Central on October 29th."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Bryan Singer has already been hit with two lawsuits alleging sexual abuse of minors—and the lawyer who filed both of them, plus other suits against Hollywood bigwigs, says more are coming. \"I’ve heard from victims about very recent claims of being sexually exploited in Hollywood,\" Jeff Herman said at a news conference yesterday, according to Variety. He said one coming lawsuit involves another Hollywood \"sex ring.\" Herman also shared photos showing the second alleged victim (with his face redacted) and Singer at the London premiere of Singer's Superman Returns in 2006; John Doe, then 17, says Singer abused him after the premiere, the New York Daily News reports. As for the strenuous denials from all the Hollywood VIPs named in the various suits, Herman said, \"The pushback I’m getting reminds me of the early days of the clergy sex abuse scandal.\" Meanwhile, in an interview with the Daily Beast, Herman says he himself has vague memories of being sexually abused as a child. He says he's worked on some 800 sex abuse cases in his career, notably involving the Catholic Church and the Boy Scouts."], "length": 5805, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**High-Profile Lawyer Jeff Herman Files Lawsuits Against Hollywood Executives Accusing Them of Child Sexual Abuse**\n\nJeff Herman, a prominent lawyer known for his work on high-profile cases involving child sexual abuse, has filed multiple lawsuits against Hollywood executives, including director Bryan Singer and producer Gary Goddard. The allegations, which date back to the late 1990s, claim that Singer and Goddard engaged in sexual abuse and exploitation of underage boys.\n\nHerman, who has litigated over 800 cases involving sexual abuse, has been a vocal advocate for victims and has taken on powerful institutions, including the Catholic Church and the Boy Scouts of America. His latest lawsuits, filed in federal court, allege that Singer and Goddard used their power and influence to groom and abuse young boys, often using tactics such as offering them gifts and favors to gain their trust.\n\nThe allegations against Singer and Goddard have been met with strong denials from the defendants, who claim that Herman's lawsuits are based on \"fabricated\" evidence. However, Herman has produced evidence, including photos and emails, that he claims support the allegations.\n\nOne of the latest lawsuits, filed by an anonymous British man, alleges that Singer offered the man a Quaalude and plied him with alcohol before bringing him back to his London hotel suite with Goddard for the alleged sexual assault. The man, who is now 25, claims that he was scared and felt like he had to comply with what was being demanded of him.\n\nHerman has stated that the man's testimony will be crucial in the case, and that he hopes to remain anonymous to protect himself and his loved ones from a possible smear campaign. The lawsuit seeks financial damages to be decided by a jury.\n\nThe allegations against Singer and Goddard have sparked a wave of pushback, with the defendants and their lawyers denying the allegations and claiming that Herman's lawsuits are part of a larger conspiracy to discredit them. However, Herman has stated that he is not surprised by the pushback, citing the early days of the clergy sex abuse scandals in the Catholic Church as a comparison.\n\nThe case has raised concerns about the culture of silence and complicity in Hollywood, with many questioning how such allegations could have gone unreported for so long. Herman has stated that he believes this is a watershed moment for Hollywood, and that the allegations will have far-reaching consequences for the industry.\n\nAs the case continues to unfold, it remains to be seen how the allegations against Singer and Goddard will be proven or disproven"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The dad of the teenage lesbian shot dead in a Texas park called for justice yesterday. He immediately had a \"bad feeling\" when his 19-year-old daughter, Mollie, failed to show up for work, said Mario Olgin. “It was not like Mollie,\" he told KIII-TV. \"If she had some place to be she was going to be there.” Mollie and her 18-year-old girlfriend, Mary Kristene Chapa, had been shot in the head by an unknown assailant. Mollie died at the scene, but Chapa is making an \"amazing\" recovery, her brother told NBC Latino. Mollie has just finished her first semester of college and dreamed of becoming a psychiatrist. \"She was happy,\" Olgin recalled. Now, \"she's my guardian angel. I know she's looking down on us, in a better place.\" He's confident police will find her killer. \"Justice will be served,\" he declared. Texas Rangers have joined Portland police in continuing to investigate the crime, but haven't yet discussed any progress. They haven't labeled the shootings a hate crime, but aren't ruling it out."], "length": 1296, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A double shooting occurred in Portland, Texas, on Saturday, leaving 19-year-old Mollie Olgin dead and 18-year-old Mary Kristene Chapa in serious condition. Chapa's brother, Hilario Chapa, reported that she is making an \"amazing\" recovery, despite being shot in the head. The Texas Rangers are assisting the Portland police in the investigation, and while there is no evidence to suggest it was a hate crime, the police are not ruling it out. Chapa's family has set up a donation page to help pay for her medical bills, and a Facebook event has been organized to support the family and Chapa's recovery.\n\nThe family of Mollie Olgin, who was killed in the shooting, is also receiving outpouring support from the community and beyond. Olgin's father, Mario, expressed his confidence that justice will be served and that his daughter's killer will be brought to justice. A memorial service for Olgin will be held on Friday, followed by a candlelight vigil at the crime scene.\n\nThe shooting has sparked a sense of community and solidarity, with many people expressing their support for the families of the victims on social media. Chapa's brother noted that the support from the gay community has been particularly meaningful, as Chapa and Olgin were in a same-sex relationship. The incident has also highlighted the importance of addressing hate crimes and ensuring that those responsible are held accountable.\n\nAs the investigation continues, the families of the victims are focusing on supporting Chapa's recovery and finding closure. Chapa's brother emphasized that the family is grateful for the outpouring of support and is working to ensure that the incident does not happen again."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Scientists are now saying there may have been \"a more colorful Jurassic World than we previously imagined,\" thanks to the recent discovery of a fossil in China's Hebei province. Reuters reports that a closer look at the \"exquisitely preserved,\" almost completely intact fossil of a crow-like dinosaur that lived about 161 million years ago revealed that Caihong juji had luminous, brilliantly hued feathers that closely resembled those of hummingbirds. The study in the journal Nature Communications documenting the find explains that \"Caihong\" means \"rainbow\" in Mandarin. Scientists think the colorful plumage, which appeared to have covered the bony-crested creature's head, neck, and chest areas, may have kept the dinosaur warm, as well as attracted potential mates; National Geographic compares it to peacock feathers. So how were researchers able to tell what color feathers the creature had from preserved bones? They used high-tech microscopes able to home in on 66 sites on the fossil, detecting tiny cell structures called melanosomes, which underlie pigmentation. Which colors they led to can be found by their shapes, and the melanosomes in Caihong were long and flat like pancakes—similar to those found in hummingbirds, which boast iridescent plumage. Although they can't pick out from the Pantone wheel Caihong's exact colors, researchers say the creature likely sported feathers with a \"rainbow glimmer.\" Study co-author Xing Zu tells National Geographic that Caihong was a predator that spent its days gliding from tree to tree. And \"glide\" is the operative word, as this dinosaur likely didn't fly: Its feathers were located on its tail, not on its wings like birds, Discover notes. (A study postulated that nearly all dinosaurs had feathers.)"], "length": 7411, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A team of scientists has discovered a new species of theropod dinosaur, Caihong juji, in northeastern China. The dinosaur lived around 160 million years ago during the Jurassic Period and is known for its stunningly preserved feathers, which may have glittered with the colors of the rainbow. The feathers on Caihong's head, chest, and tail are thought to have been iridescent, similar to those of modern hummingbirds.\n\nThe discovery was made in the Hebei Province of China, where a local farmer, Yang Jan, found the fossil in 2014. The fossil was later acquired by the Paleontological Museum of Liaoning, where it was studied by a team of scientists led by Dongyu Hu.\n\nThe scientists found that the fossil contained traces of pigment in its feathers, which were compared to melanosomes from modern bird feathers. The melanosomes were found to be long, flat, and organized into sheets, similar to those found in hummingbirds. This suggests that Caihong's feathers may have had a similar iridescent quality to those of hummingbirds.\n\nThe discovery of Caihong juji is significant because it provides new insights into the evolution of feathers in dinosaurs. Feathers are thought to have evolved in theropod dinosaurs during the Jurassic Period, and Caihong's discovery suggests that they may have been used for display purposes, such as attracting mates or intimidating rivals.\n\nIn addition to its iridescent feathers, Caihong also had a distinctive crest on its head, which was likely used for display purposes. The dinosaur's tail was also frond-like, with asymmetrical feathers that would have borne the aerodynamic load of flight.\n\nThe discovery of Caihong juji is also significant because it pushes back the known date of the evolution of asymmetrical feathers, which are thought to be a key adaptation for flight. Caihong is now the oldest known animal to have this adaptation, and its discovery provides new insights into the evolution of flight in dinosaurs.\n\nOverall, the discovery of Caihong juji is a significant find that provides new insights into the evolution of feathers and flight in dinosaurs. Its iridescent feathers and distinctive crest and tail make it a fascinating and unique species that sheds new light on the biology and behavior of ancient dinosaurs."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["In direct contrast to the \"delirious joy\" in Bahrain, the death toll keeps climbing in Libya's protests. Moammar Gadhafi's minions killed another 20 people today, bringing the five-day total to at least 104, says Human Rights Watch. Gadhafi has effectively shut off Internet service and forbid media coverage, but witnesses told AP of attacks by police and government loyalists wielding guns, knives, and even anti-aircraft missiles. While the accounts are impossible to verify—and at least one puts the death toll closer to 200—a grim cycle has emerged, reports the New York Times: \"Security forces fire on funeral marches, killing more protesters and creating more funerals.\" In Yemen, meanwhile, President Ali Abdullah Saleh's forces also opened fire on protesters, killing at least one, reports AP."], "length": 3050, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Arab World Uprisings: Protests and Crackdowns Continue**\n\nThe Arab world is experiencing a wave of protests and uprisings, with several countries facing violent crackdowns by their governments. The protests, which began in Tunisia and Egypt, have spread to other countries, including Libya, Yemen, Bahrain, and Algeria.\n\nIn Libya, the government has responded to protests with violence, with at least 15 people killed in Benghazi on Saturday. The Libyan government has also cut off the internet and deployed special forces to suppress the protests. The international community has condemned the violence, with the US and European allies calling for an end to the crackdown.\n\nIn Yemen, the government has also responded to protests with force, with one person killed and five injured in Sanaa. The Yemeni government has promised to respect the \"universal rights\" of its people, but has yet to take concrete steps to address the grievances of the protesters.\n\nIn Bahrain, the government has withdrawn its tanks from the streets, but the protesters remain defiant. The royal family has appealed for calm and political dialogue, but the protesters are wary of their intentions.\n\nIn Algeria, the government has thwarted a rally by thousands of pro-democracy supporters, breaking up the crowd into isolated groups. The Algerian government has promised to lift the state of emergency, but has warned that a long-standing ban on protests in Algiers will remain in place.\n\nThe uprisings in the Arab world are driven by a desire for change and an end to corruption and authoritarian rule. The protests have been inspired by the success of the uprisings in Tunisia and Egypt, and have been fueled by social media and other forms of communication.\n\nThe international community has condemned the violence and called for an end to the crackdowns. The US and European allies have also offered support to the protesters, with some countries providing financial and diplomatic assistance.\n\nThe situation in the Arab world remains volatile, with many countries facing significant challenges in addressing the grievances of their protesters. However, the uprisings have also brought attention to the need for reform and change in the region, and have inspired a new generation of leaders and activists.\n\n**Key Developments:**\n\n* Protests in Libya, Yemen, Bahrain, and Algeria have been met with violent crackdowns by their governments.\n* The international community has condemned the violence and called for an end to the crackdowns.\n* The US and European allies have offered support to the protesters, with some countries providing financial and diplomatic assistance.\n* The uprisings in the Arab world"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Most teens don't find music history in their dad's stuff, but Maggie Poukkula's dad is Seattle musician Tony Poukkula, who was a friend of Kurt Cobain. Her find? Photos of Nirvana's first concert, performed by then 20-year-old Cobain and his new band in March 1987 in Tony Poukkula's basement. \"My dad showed me them awhile back, but he never mentioned that's what was going on in the photos,\" Maggie Poukkula, 19, tells Rolling Stone. \"I found out because of all the articles. I didn't realize it was such a historical thing. I thought they were just cool pictures of my dad and Kurt jamming together.\" She tweeted the photos last week, and the post quickly went viral. That first concert featured the early Nirvana lineup of Cobain, bassist Krist Novoselic, and drummer Aaron Burckhard playing original songs and Led Zeppelin covers. Similar early-days footage of Cobain and Co. featured in the recent documentary Montage of Heck has renewed interest in the grunge band. The Emmy-nominated project includes home movies, never-before-heard music, and intimate interviews with Cobain's family and friends, notes SPIN. After being critically acclaimed at festivals across the country, the film will return to theaters on Aug. 7, adds Rolling Stone. (Want a little more Cobain history? Here's the mix tape he made at 21.)"], "length": 751, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Rare Nirvana Concert Photos Uncovered, Montage of Heck Returns to Theaters**\n\nIn a significant discovery, rare photographs from Nirvana's first concert in March 1987 have been unearthed by Maggie Poukkula, the 19-year-old daughter of Tony Poukkula, a member of Seattle band Laytem who hosted the fledgling outfit's first show in his basement. The photographs, which feature Nirvana's lineup consisting of Kurt Cobain, Krist Novoselic, and Aaron Burckhard, as well as Poukkula on guitar for two Led Zeppelin covers, have been shared by Poukkula on social media.\n\nThe photos, which include snapshots of the band performing covers of Led Zeppelin's \"Heartbreaker\" and \"How Many More Times,\" as well as nascent versions of several other songs, have been met with excitement from fans and music historians. The discovery comes on the heels of a deluge of rare Cobain and Nirvana artifacts, many of which were incorporated into Brett Morgen's acclaimed documentary, Montage of Heck.\n\nMontage of Heck, which premiered on HBO and received widespread critical acclaim, is set to return to theaters starting August 7th. The documentary, which features unprecedented access to Cobain's archives, including mountains of notebooks and hours of never-before-heard audio cassette tapes, offers a unique glimpse into the life and career of the iconic musician.\n\nIn related news, the Internet Archive has launched an effort to archive web pages as they are created, with the goal of preserving links to these pages and preventing broken links from becoming a problem. This initiative, which involves crawling supported \"No More 404\" sites, aims to fix all broken links on the web and provide a permanent record of the internet's ever-changing landscape."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Both of TV's \"Fat Ladies\" have sung. Clarissa Dickson Wright, one half of the BBC's \"Two Fat Ladies\" cooking duo, died in Edinburgh Saturday at age 66, the BBC reports. Wright was a former lawyer who filmed four of the \"Fat Ladies\" series, going on food-related road trips across the UK in a motorbike and sidecar with Jennifer Paterson, before Paterson died in 1999 from cancer. The New York Times describes Wright as a \"rebel,\" both hosts as \"irreverent and eccentric,\" and the recipes as \"sometimes confounding.\" Wright's eclectic working life also included stints as a cook, an author, and a cookbook shop manager; she also ran a catering business, was a guild butcher, and once worked on a yacht in the Caribbean. In fact, she recently said, \"I've had a fantastic life and I've done everything I could have wanted to do and more.\" It wasn't until her 40s, after she'd recovered from alcoholism, that she got into cooking seriously. As for the perhaps-controversial title of the show that brought her fame? \"If you're fat you're fat,\" she once said. \"I hate this modern-day political correctness, that you don't call things by their proper name.\" Her agent remembers Wright similarly in a statement: \"Loved dearly by her friends and many fans all over the world, Clarissa was utterly non-PC and fought for what she believed in, always, with no thought to her own personal cost.\" There's no word on Wright's cause of death, but the Guardian reports that she had been undergoing treatment at a hospital since the beginning of the year."], "length": 2870, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Clarissa Dickson Wright, the renowned British chef, food historian, and television personality, has passed away at the age of 66. Wright, who was best known for her work as one half of the popular cooking duo Two Fat Ladies, died in Edinburgh's Royal Infirmary on Saturday.\n\nBorn on June 24, 1947, in London, Wright grew up in an affluent family with a privileged upbringing. However, her childhood was marred by her father's violent alcoholism and abuse. Wright became a barrister at 21 but abandoned her career to pursue a life in cooking after descending into alcoholism.\n\nWright's culinary career took off in the 1990s when she teamed up with Jennifer Paterson to create the hit television show Two Fat Ladies. The show, which ran from 1996 to 1999, followed the duo as they traveled the UK in a motorcycle-sidecar, sampling local cuisine and sharing their love of good food.\n\nAfter Paterson's death in 1999, Wright went on to front several other television shows, including Breakfast, Lunch and Dinner in 2012. She was also a passionate campaigner for rural life and a keen supporter of the Countryside Alliance.\n\nWright was known for her outspoken views and unapologetic approach to food. She was a fierce advocate for traditional British cuisine and was not afraid to speak her mind on topics such as hunting and shooting. Wright was also a strong believer in the importance of cooking from scratch and was known for her love of rich, indulgent ingredients.\n\nThroughout her life, Wright was open about her struggles with alcoholism and her experiences with bankruptcy. However, she credited her sobriety with bringing her a sense of peace and clarity, and she often spoke about the importance of living a simple, authentic life.\n\nWright is survived by her two sisters, Heather and June, and her agent, Heather Holden-Brown, who released a statement praising her intelligence, humor, and unwavering commitment to her values. Wright's death has been met with an outpouring of tributes from fans and fellow chefs, who remember her as a true original and a passionate advocate for the joys of good food."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A hiker's fatal fall in March has been blamed on using a smartphone app map instead of an actual map. The BBC reports Jane Wilson and her husband Gary were hiking on Tryfan in Wales when it started to get dark and they decided to skip the summit. While attempting to descend the mountain, Gary was looking at an Ordnance Survey app while Jane went a short way ahead to make sure the path was safe, according to the Telegraph. The Manchester Evening News reports Gary heard his wife fall, got to safety, and called for help. Rescuers found the 53-year-old librarian's body at the bottom of a 30-foot cliff with a fractured skull. At a hearing Friday, Jane's was ruled an accidental death due, in part, to using an app instead of a map. \"Apart from potential difficulties caused by poor detail on an electronic map, batteries on mobile phones have a nasty habit of running out just when you need them most,\" the Telegraph quotes a spokesperson for Mountain Rescue England and Wales as saying. A detective says the app map \"would have been small and not as detailed\" and took the couple on a route that was not the safest. A director with Ordnance Survey says hikers should always use the app in conjunction with an actual map \"due to the nature of mobile devices when navigating the real world.\" Authorities also say Jane wasn't wearing appropriate footwear for the hike. (Go inside the futile search for \"Inchworm\" on the trail.)"], "length": 1566, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A tragic incident occurred in Snowdonia, Wales, where a woman fell to her death while attempting to find a safe route down from a peak. Jane Wilson, 53, and her husband Gary Wilson, an experienced climber, used a smartphone app to navigate the mountain's west face. However, the app's limited detail and the couple's decision to deviate from the recommended route led to Jane's fatal fall.\n\nAccording to the inquest, Jane fell 20-30 feet down a vertical cliff and tumbled approximately 150 meters down into an area known as Notch arrete. The coroner recorded a conclusion of accidental death, citing that the couple's decision to use a smartphone app instead of a traditional map and compass, combined with the limited detail on the app, contributed to the tragedy.\n\nThe incident has raised concerns about the use of electronic navigation aids in mountainous terrain. Chris Lloyd, a member of the Ogwen Rescue team, stated that the route taken by the couple was \"not a straightforward path\" and that the use of a smartphone app can be misleading. Andy Simpson, a spokesman for Mountain Rescue England and Wales, also urged walkers to use a map and compass in addition to electronic navigation aids.\n\nThe inquest also highlighted the importance of experience and caution when navigating challenging terrain. Gary Wilson described his wife's decision to go ahead with the route, despite his concerns, and stated that he had asked her if it was safe before proceeding. However, the couple's decision to join up the dots and follow a footpath they thought was present ultimately led to Jane's fatal fall.\n\nThe incident serves as a reminder of the importance of careful planning, experience, and caution when engaging in mountainous activities. It is essential for walkers and climbers to be aware of the risks involved and to take necessary precautions to ensure their safety."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Mitch Daniels has some advice for those who are writing Rick Perry's political eulogy: Hold your horses. \"It's way too early to know, or to issue, to pronounce last rites over one performance,\" Daniels said today. \"There's still many of these (debates), too many maybe. ... I'd cut him some slack and give him a little time.\" Politico notes that Daniels also had kind words for Democrat Sen. Mark Warner's work on the Gang of Six, saying, \"It's very encouraging, by the way, to hear a guy like Sen. Warner speak to it as clearly as he just did. I wish he were president.\" More fun on your Sunday dial, as per Politico: Michael Bloomberg on President Obama's 2012 chances: \"He would be a credible, formidable candidate. You're going to have a real horse race no matter who the Republican nominee is.\" Reince Priebus to Debbie Wasserman Schultz on 2012: \"It sounds like the new slogan is no longer 'hope and change.' It's, 'Hey, it could've been worse.' Great bumper sticker Debbie, I hope it works for you.\" David Plouffe on a White House shakeup: \"I don't expect that.\" Obama is \"confident in his team, in the direction we've laid out here.\" Warner on Congress' standoff over a short-term spending bill: \"It is embarrassing. The Senate is saying, 'Why should we build schools in Iraq on the credit card but expect that rebuilding schools in Joplin, Mo.,'\" will be paid for. Warner blames \"a group, more centered in the House, in terms of some of these Tea Party Republicans, who say on every issue, 'We're going to make this a make-or-break.'\" Lindsey Graham on Pakistan: \"We need to put Pakistan on notice. We're going to have to put all options on the table. including defending our troops. It's now a time of choosing, so I hope they choose wisely.\""], "length": 1454, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Here is a one-page summary of the news:\n\nThe 2012 presidential election is heating up, with several key figures weighing in on the campaign. New York Mayor Michael Bloomberg praised President Barack Obama as a \"credible, formidable candidate\" and praised Republican candidates Rick Perry and Mitt Romney for their strengths. Bloomberg also criticized Obama's use of billionaire Warren Buffett as an example for tax increases.\n\nIndiana Gov. Mitch Daniels urged Republicans to forgive Rick Perry's uneven debate performances, saying it's too early to judge him. Daniels also praised Sen. Mark Warner for his work on the Gang of Six senators.\n\nRepublican National Committee Chairman Reince Priebus mocked President Obama's 2008 election slogan, saying it won't pass the smell test with voters in 2012. Priebus also criticized the Democratic National Committee's talking points, saying they have been proved false.\n\nSen. Mark Warner blamed the tea party-affiliated Republicans for the government shutdown stalemate, saying it's embarrassing and that the Senate should approve disaster aid. Sen. Lamar Alexander blamed Majority Leader Harry Reid for holding up the bill.\n\nSen. Lindsey Graham called for the United States to leave \"all options on the table\" in dealing with Pakistan after U.S. military officials accused the country's intelligence officials of assisting terrorist operations.\n\nPresident Barack Obama's senior adviser, David Plouffe, said he doesn't expect significant changes in top White House staff or the Cabinet ahead of the 2012 reelection campaign. Plouffe also said the U.S. may continue reducing aid to Pakistan if it can't do more to impede anti-American terrorism.\n\nOverall, the news highlights the intense campaigning and politicking in the lead-up to the 2012 presidential election, with key figures on both sides weighing in on the issues and candidates."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["\"That new soda tax in Seattle is working out about as well as Chicago's,\" proclaims a headline at Hot Air. It's not a compliment. The tax, which went into effect Jan. 1, slaps an additional 1.75 cents on each fluid ounce of sugar-sweetened drinks, a group that includes soda, sports drinks, and kombucha. That's nearly double the one-cent levy the Chicago-encompassing Cook County tried, a tax it repealed after about two months. The Chicago Tribune reported at the time that Costco's nine affected locations saw their sales of beverages impacted by the tax fall 34%, while sales jumped 38% at locations just beyond the county line. And Costco is again at the fore of what's happening: Because bulk purchases contain significantly more ounces than an individual one, the impact is starker, as KIRO discovered when it spotted an updated Costco sign for Gatorade in Seattle. The sign details Costco's price for the 35-pack of 16-ounce bottles—$15.99—and separately lists the city's $10.34 tax, for a new total of $26.33. But that's not all the sign says: It also directs consumers to nearby locations that are outside city limits and exempt from the tax. The tax is meant to fight obesity and raise funds for worthy expenditures, but the blog for Citizens Against Government Waste sees the ends quite differently: \"Let’s be clear. Soda taxes don’t make people healthier. They don't raise revenue—they drive it outside city limits. They don’t help ease inequality—they make the poor poorer.\" But some in government seemingly remain hopeful: KXLY reports a bill was reintroduced Monday that would push the tax statewide. (Speaking of Seattle and beverages, the world's largest Starbucks is no longer located there.)"], "length": 3161, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Seattle's 1.75 cent per ounce tax on sweetened beverages, implemented in 2018, has been met with significant backlash from consumers and businesses. The tax, intended to reduce sugar consumption and raise revenue for community college programs, has instead led to a surge in sales of sugary drinks outside of the city limits. Retailers such as Costco have posted signs directing customers to locations outside the city where they can buy the product without paying the tax, effectively \"voting with their feet.\" This has resulted in a significant decline in sales of sugary drinks within the city, with some businesses reporting losses of up to 47 percent.\n\nThe tax has also been criticized for its regressive nature, disproportionately affecting low-income families who are already struggling to make ends meet. Additionally, the tax has been seen as ineffective in reducing sugar consumption, with some studies suggesting that it may even increase consumption of sugary drinks in certain populations.\n\nThe city of Seattle is now considering expanding the tax to the rest of the state, with a bill being re-introduced to bring the tax statewide. However, some business owners and residents are expressing concerns about the potential impact on local businesses and the economy.\n\nIn other news, a similar tax on sweetened beverages has been implemented in Cook County, Illinois, which was repealed just two months after its implementation due to public outcry. The repeal was seen as a victory for the beverage industry, which had spent millions fighting the tax.\n\nThe debate over sugar taxes and their effectiveness in promoting public health has also been ongoing, with some arguing that the taxes are a necessary step to reduce the burden of chronic diseases such as heart disease and diabetes. However, others have raised concerns about the potential unintended consequences of such taxes, including the displacement of businesses and the impact on low-income communities.\n\nOverall, the story highlights the complexities and challenges of implementing policies aimed at promoting public health, and the need for careful consideration of the potential impacts on different populations and businesses."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A birthday party in California turned tragic after the guest of honor was run over and killed by his friend. Police say Jonathan Carlyle Merkley was celebrating his 34th birthday early Sunday at a hotel near San Diego when a woman decided to leave the party. Merkley wanted her to stay and reportedly walked toward her BMW and laid down in front of what the Orange County Register describes as a moving car. The woman didn’t stop, ran him over, and kept going. Police cite witnesses as saying both had been drinking, Fox 5 reports. Merkley, who suffered major chest trauma, died about 45 minutes later at a hospital. Police tracked the woman down and a car that \"may have been involved\" was impounded, cops tell the San Diego Union-Tribune. The woman was not arrested but police said on Tuesday that the investigation is continuing. \"There's a lot we still need to find out,\" says San Diego Police Sgt. Tim Underwood."], "length": 885, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tragic Birthday Party Incident: Man Killed in Hit-and-Run**\n\nA 34-year-old man, Jonathan Carlyle Merkley, was tragically killed in a hit-and-run incident at a birthday party in San Diego, California. The incident occurred on Sunday, just after midnight, when Merkley, who was celebrating his 34th birthday, lay down in front of a car driven by his female friend. The woman, who had been drinking with friends, drove away from the scene, running over Merkley and causing fatal injuries.\n\nAccording to police, both Merkley and the woman had been drinking alcohol with friends before the incident. Witnesses reported that Merkley was trying to prevent the woman from leaving the hotel when he lay down in front of the moving car. The woman did not stop, and Merkley suffered major trauma to his chest before being taken to a hospital, where he died about 45 minutes later.\n\nDespite the incident occurring on Sunday, police have not made any arrests as of late Sunday morning. However, they have identified the woman and a BMW that may have been involved in the crash. The police are still investigating the incident and urging anyone with information to come forward.\n\nIn a statement, police said that they have interviewed the female friend and have a vehicle in custody pending examination. They are still evaluating the other parties' culpability in the collision and have not yet made any arrests. The investigation is ongoing, and police are urging anyone with information to contact them or Crime Stoppers at 888-580-8477.\n\nThe incident has sparked outrage, with many expressing shock and sadness at the loss of life. As one hotel guest said, \"We don't value each other's lives.\" The police are urging the woman to turn herself in and cooperate with the investigation."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["It turns out missing people are pretty easy to find when they appear every week in your living room. The San Francisco Chronicle reports a 22-year-old woman reported missing in November in California was found this week—because she's a current contestant on The Bachelor. The strange story started when Bekah Martinez's mom reported her missing to the Humboldt County Sheriff's Office on Nov. 18. According to BuzzFeed, Martinez's mom said her daughter had gone to the area to work on a marijuana farm and she hadn't heard from her in a week. The Sheriff's Office was unable to find Martinez or contact anyone who might know her, so Martinez was added to the California Department of Justice's list of missing people. That list was the basis for a story in the North Coast Journal about the high number of missing people in Humboldt County. The newspaper shared the story Thursday on Facebook and asked readers if they recognized any of the missing people. Amy O'Brien did. \"I was like wait a minute, she looks so familiar,\" O'Brien tells the Chronicle. \"I instantly thought of The Bachelor.\" The Journal informed the sheriff's office, which contacted Martinez and removed her from the list. \"MOM. how many times do I have to tell you I don’t get cell service on The Bachelor??,\" Martinez tweeted after learning she was a missing person. The Bachelor started filming in September, and it's unclear if Martinez was still filming when she was reported missing. She tweets \"the scariest thing\" about being a missing person \"is that my efforts to conceal The Worst Drivers License Photo Of All Time have been thwarted\""], "length": 4630, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Missing Persons in Humboldt County: A Complex Issue**\n\nHumboldt County, California, has one of the highest rates of missing persons reports in the state, with 313 reports filed in 2016. The county's unique geography and cannabis industry have contributed to the high number of missing adults, with many cases involving individuals who have left town to work on remote farms or in the cannabis industry.\n\nThe Humboldt 35, a list of 35 people reported missing in the county, includes individuals of all ages, from 1 to 94. While some cases are straightforward, others are more complex, involving mental health issues, poverty, and a lack of family or community support.\n\nOne case that has garnered attention is that of Jeff Joseph, a 34-year-old dispensary owner who disappeared in June 2014. His family has been searching for him, but the case remains unsolved, with the sheriff's office unable to locate him. Joseph's case highlights the challenges of investigating missing persons cases in Humboldt County, where many individuals may be reluctant to cooperate with law enforcement.\n\nAnother case that has been the subject of investigation is that of Claire Louise Christie, who was reported missing in 1977. Her husband, Ernest Samuel Christie Jr., was later implicated in the torture and murder of several women, but his fate remains unknown.\n\nThe Humboldt County Sheriff's Office has implemented measures to improve the investigation of missing persons cases, including the use of the National Missing and Unidentified Persons System (NamUs). However, the office acknowledges that some cases may never be resolved, and families of missing individuals often face frustration and uncertainty.\n\nIn a surprising turn of events, a man named Daniel Ogden Stromberg, who was reported missing in 2017, contacted the Journal to confirm that he is alive and has been living in Eureka for the past year.\n\nThe high rate of missing persons in Humboldt County is a complex issue that requires a multifaceted approach to address the underlying causes, including poverty, mental health issues, and a lack of community support. While law enforcement agencies are working to improve the investigation of these cases, families of missing individuals must also be supported and empowered to take action to find their loved ones.\n\nUltimately, the story of the Humboldt 35 serves as a reminder of the importance of addressing the root causes of missing persons cases and providing support to families affected by these tragedies."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Let's hope Snooki sorta planned that baby she's reportedly carrying (stop giggling). Because if she didn't, that fetus has to be nearly swimming in booze by now. While Forbes is concerned about what's to become of the diminutive reality star's \"brand,\" others are worried about the drinking habits of the pregnant 24-year-old, who seems to spend very few minutes sober on Jersey Shore. \"Is there a test for fetal alcohol syndrome at three months in utero?\" wonders crabbygolightly.com. \"Quick, word association test,\" demands studybreaks.com, \"because all that comes to mind\" for the pregnant \"diva of debauchery\" is \"fetal alcohol syndrome\"—a leading cause of mental retardation. The reported pregnancy poses problems for Snooki and JWoww's spin-off, and for Jersey Shore, where the cast spends \"approximately 97.2% of their time being absolutely trashed,\" notes studybreaks. Snooki has some qualms about her hard-partying ways, once telling Ellen DeGeneres: \"I want to remember my night, and sometimes I just don't. It sucks. You're like, 'What did I do? Why did I wake up in a garbage can?'\""], "length": 8992, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Breaking News: Snooki's Pregnancy and Fetal Alcohol Spectrum Disorders**\n\nNicole \"Snooki\" Pollizzi, a reality TV star from Jersey Shore, is pregnant with her first child. According to recent reports, she is approximately three months pregnant and the father is her boyfriend, Jionni LaValle. This news comes as a surprise, as Snooki recently denied rumors of her pregnancy on Good Morning America.\n\nIn other news, fetal alcohol spectrum disorders (FASDs) are a group of conditions that can occur in a person whose mother drank alcohol during pregnancy. FASDs can include fetal alcohol syndrome (FAS), partial fetal alcohol syndrome (pFAS), alcohol-related neurodevelopmental disorder (ARND), and alcohol-related birth defects (ARBD). These conditions can result from prenatal alcohol exposure and can cause a range of physical and neurodevelopmental problems.\n\nAccording to recent studies, FASDs affect between 2% and 5% of people in the United States and Western Europe. The lifetime cost of an individual with FAS was estimated to be $2 million in 2002. Drinking any quantity during pregnancy increases the risk of giving birth to a child with FAS, with a 1.5% risk and a 15% risk for FASD.\n\nIn a recent interview with Ellen DeGeneres, Snooki admitted to having a history of over-imbibing and said that she may occasionally overdo it. She also expressed regret over her hard-partying ways, stating that it \"sucks\" and that she doesn't want to be a role model.\n\nThe news of Snooki's pregnancy and FASDs serves as a reminder of the importance of responsible drinking and the potential risks associated with prenatal alcohol exposure. As Snooki navigates her new role as a mother, it will be interesting to see how she balances her personal life with her responsibilities as a parent.\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n\n* Snooki is approximately three months pregnant with her first child.\n* Fetal alcohol spectrum disorders (FASDs) are a group of conditions that can occur in a person whose mother drank alcohol during pregnancy.\n* FASDs can result from prenatal alcohol exposure and can cause a range of physical and neurodevelopmental problems.\n* The lifetime cost of an individual with FAS was estimated to be $2 million in 2002.\n* Drinking any quantity during pregnancy increases the risk of giving birth to a child with FAS, with a 1."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Myrna Arias didn't like the GPS app on her phone that constantly tracked her, so she uninstalled it. The problem: Arias' iPhone was issued by her employer, which required her to run the app constantly, and after she removed it, the California woman was fired. Now she's suing her former employer, money transfer service Intermex, for invasion of privacy, unfair business practices, and retaliation, among other things, Ars Technica reports. Arias started researching the app, then called Xora—its website touts the app's ability to let employers \"instantly see where their employees are and where they have been\"—and eventually she and some of her colleagues asked a trainer from the app company to clarify. Boss John Stubits then, per the suit, \"admitted that employees would be monitored while off duty and bragged that he knew how fast she was driving at specific moments.\" Arias says she has no problem being monitored while at work, but she complained to Stubits about being tracked in her free time; he was unsympathetic and said she should have no problem because of how much she was being paid by Intermex. \"Her manager made it clear that he was using the program to continuously monitor her, during company as well as personal time,\" the complaint states, noting that he told Arias she needed to keep her phone on at all times in case a customer called. Arias' attorney tells Courthouse News that there was no way to turn the app off, as it would constantly run in the background. \"She found it very offensive that they were treating her like a felon,\" the attorney says. Arias says her boss \"scolded\" her for uninstalling the program last year, and fired her soon after. (Elsewhere, a woman got fired over a text mistakenly sent to the boss.)"], "length": 1970, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A Central California woman, Myrna Arias, has filed a lawsuit against her former employer, Intermex Wire Transfer LLC, alleging that she was fired after uninstalling an app that tracked her location 24/7, even when she was off the clock. The app, provided by Xora, was required by Intermex for employees to use, and Arias claims that her boss, John Stubits, monitored her movements and driving speed while she was off duty, violating her privacy.\n\nArias, a former sales executive, had met all her quotas and earned around $7,250 per month while working for Intermex from February 2014 to May 2014. However, after researching the app and speaking with a trainer, she objected to the monitoring of her location during non-work hours, likening it to a prisoner's ankle bracelet. Stubits allegedly scolded her for uninstalling the app and told her to tolerate the illegal intrusion.\n\nArias claims that Intermex fired her shortly after, and that the company's president, Robert Lisy, interfered with her ability to earn a livelihood by informing her former employer, NetSpend, that she was disloyal. NetSpend subsequently fired Arias, citing Lisy's phone call as the reason.\n\nArias is seeking damages in excess of $500,000, including general, special, and punitive damages for invasion of privacy, retaliation, labor code violations, intentional interference with contract, wrongful termination, and unfair business practices. Her attorney, Gail Glick, stated that the situation is \"egregious\" and that they intend to pursue the case aggressively to get Arias the justice she deserves.\n\nThis case highlights the issue of employee monitoring and the importance of respecting employees' privacy. It also raises questions about the limits of employer monitoring and the potential consequences of violating employee rights."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A judge on Tuesday allowed a man charged in freeway shootings that rattled Phoenix last year to be released from jail amid questions about evidence authorities say links him to the crimes. The judge overseeing the case of Leslie Merritt Jr. reduced his bond to zero and said he can return to his home under electronic monitoring, the AP reports. He was expected to be released later in the day. The reduction of the bond—once $1 million—was a major victory as defense lawyers contend that ballistic tests cast doubt on the claim by authorities that Merritt was behind four of the freeway shootings. \"With all due respect your honor, there's no evidence against him to show he's responsible for this,\" defense lawyer Jason Lamm said. \"He is no more the I-10 shooter than, respectfully, you are.\" Merritt lifted up his shackled arms in celebration as he walked from the courtroom. After the hearing, family members hugged and shook the hands of defense lawyers. Merritt has pleaded not guilty to drive-by shooting, aggravated assault, and other charges. The shootings caused panic on Phoenix-area freeways, where 11 vehicles were hit in August and September. Detectives took Merritt into custody on Sept. 18; in court the next day, Merritt adamantly denied shooting any cars, telling the judge, \"I'm the wrong guy.\" His lawyers immediately began raising questions about the evidence, citing ballistics information and phone records they say provided an alibi for their client. (Also Tuesday, a motorist suspected of shooting at other vehicles on an Alabama highway died after a shootout with officers.)"], "length": 955, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Recent News Summaries**\n\n**Arizona Freeway Shooter Released on Bond**\n\nA judge in Phoenix, Arizona, has reduced the bond of Leslie Allen Merritt Jr., a man charged with freeway shootings that caused panic in the city last year. Merritt, who was expected to be released from jail, was allowed to return home under electronic monitoring after his defense lawyers raised questions about the evidence linking him to the crimes. Ballistic tests cast doubt on the claim by authorities that Merritt was behind the shootings, and his lawyers argue that there is no evidence to prove his guilt. The judge imposed a gag order on lawyers in the case, preventing them from discussing the evidence.\n\n**Driver Killed in Alabama Highway Shooting**\n\nIn a separate incident, a driver suspected of firing a gun at motorists on an Alabama highway has been killed. The suspect, whose name has not been released, was involved in a high-speed pursuit with law officers before crashing and exchanging gunfire. The suspect was pronounced dead at the scene. Authorities say the incident began around 7 a.m. on U.S. 278 in Blount County, where 911 dispatchers received reports of gunfire on the highway. The suspect's identity and the identity of the officer involved in the pursuit have not been released.\n\nThese two incidents highlight the ongoing concerns about violence on the roads, with both cases involving shootings and high-speed pursuits. While the Arizona case raises questions about the reliability of the evidence against Merritt, the Alabama incident serves as a reminder of the dangers faced by law enforcement officers and motorists alike."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["News that a shooting left multiple people dead may sound depressingly familiar, but the one outside Baltimore on Thursday is unique in at least one way: The shooter was female. Police say the 26-year-old woman, who has not been identified, died of a self-inflicted gunshot wound to the head. Harford County Sheriff Jeff Gahler said she was a temporary employee at the Rite Aid distribution center where the shooting took place, reports the Baltimore Sun. Police say the suspect killed three people, and three others remain hospitalized with wounds not believed to be life threatening. Details and developments: When, where: The Rite Aid distribution center where the shooting took place is at a business complex in Perryman, about 30 miles from Baltimore, reports WBAL. Deputies got there at 9:06am, within 5 minutes of the first call, said the sheriff. He said that deputies fired no shots and that the shooter used a 9mm Glock semiautomatic handgun. Why: Police aren't speculating about a motive. “Normally, she was a nice person, but she came in in a bad mood,” Mike Carre, an employee at a nearby business, tells the Washington Post, recounting what Rite Aid workers told him. \"She wanted to pick a fight,\" he said. \"And then she started shooting.” CNN quotes a law enforcement official who describes her as a disgruntled employee; the site says she worked there as a security guard. The woman lived in Baltimore County, and the gun was legally registered to her."], "length": 3414, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Multiple Shootings Rock the US in 24-Hour Period**\n\nIn a shocking and devastating series of events, three separate shootings occurred in the United States within a 24-hour period, leaving multiple people dead and injured. The incidents took place in Harford County, Maryland, and involved a Rite Aid distribution center, an office complex in Middleton, Wisconsin, and a municipal building in Masontown, Pennsylvania.\n\n**Harford County, Maryland Shooting**\n\nOn Thursday morning, a 26-year-old temporary employee at a Rite Aid distribution center in Aberdeen, Maryland, fatally shot three coworkers and injured three others before taking her own life. The shooter, identified as Snochia Moseley, was a temporary employee with no prior criminal record. The incident occurred at the Rite Aid facility on Perryman Road, where approximately 1,000 employees work. Multiple agencies responded to the scene, and no law enforcement officers fired shots. Four victims were taken to Johns Hopkins Bayview Medical Center, with two pronounced dead at the scene and two in stable condition.\n\n**Wisconsin Office Complex Shooting**\n\nEarlier in the day, a 43-year-old co-worker shot and killed four people, including a police officer, at a software company in Middleton, Wisconsin. The gunman was shot and killed by police. Three of the injured were in serious condition as of Thursday morning, with one suffering a graze wound.\n\n**Pennsylvania Municipal Building Shooting**\n\nLater in the day, a gunman opened fire outside a judge's office in a municipal building in Masontown, Pennsylvania, shooting four people, including a police officer. The suspect, Patrick Dowdell, 61, was shot and killed by police. He had a preliminary hearing scheduled for Wednesday on domestic charges.\n\n**Response and Reaction**\n\nGovernor Larry Hogan of Maryland offered support to the affected families and praised the response of law enforcement agencies. Former US Representative Gabrielle Giffords, an anti-gun violence activist, called for outrage and action in the face of the shootings. The incidents have sparked concerns about gun violence and workplace safety.\n\n**Community Reactions**\n\nWitnesses described the scene as chaotic, with emergency responders quickly responding to the incidents. Family members and loved ones of the victims were being notified and taken to a community center in Havre de Grace for reunification. The area was placed on modified lockdown as a precaution, and residents were advised to avoid the area.\n\nThe shootings have left the community in shock, and officials are working to establish a timeline of the incidents and determine the motive behind"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The bad news about acetaminophen just keeps on coming: A new study finds that use of the drug during pregnancy is linked to \"ADHD-like behavioral problems\" in children, CNN reports. The Danish study looked at data from more than 64,000 children and found that those who had prenatal exposure to acetaminophen had a 13% higher risk of exhibiting ADHD-like behaviors, a 29% higher risk of being prescribed ADHD medication, and a 37% higher risk of receiving a diagnosis of hyperkinetic disorder, a severe form of ADHD, USA Today reports. The risk increased the later in pregnancy the drug was taken and the longer it was taken. Women who used acetaminophen for 20 weeks or longer had a 50% higher chance of their children being prescribed ADHD medication. But the authors did not find a cause-and-effect relationship, and they note that \"exhibiting ADHD-like behaviors\" is not the same as actually having ADHD. Right now, doctors consider acetaminophen the \"safest\" pain reliever for pregnant women, NBC News reports; the study author says pregnant women \"shouldn't worry at this point.\" What could account for the link? The authors think acetaminophen may interfere with key maternal hormones that impact fetal brain development. Another recent study also found that frequent acetaminophen use during pregnancy was linked with a 70% higher risk of behavioral problems in children."], "length": 1951, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**New Study Raises Concerns about Acetaminophen Use during Pregnancy**\n\nA recent study published in the American Medical Association journal JAMA Pediatrics has found a possible link between prenatal exposure to acetaminophen and an increased risk of attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD)-like behavioral problems in children. The study, which analyzed data from over 64,000 children in Denmark, found that mothers who took acetaminophen during pregnancy were more likely to have children diagnosed with ADHD or exhibiting ADHD-like behaviors.\n\nThe study's authors suggest that acetaminophen may increase the risk of ADHD by interfering with maternal hormones that are critical for fetal brain development. However, they emphasize that the study does not show a cause-and-effect relationship between acetaminophen use and ADHD.\n\nThe findings have sparked concerns among healthcare professionals and pregnant women, who may be wondering whether they should avoid taking acetaminophen during pregnancy altogether. However, experts caution that the study's results should not be taken as a blanket warning against acetaminophen use.\n\n\"We're not saying if you take one Tylenol once it will give your child hyperactivity,\" said Dr. Beate Ritz, an epidemiologist at the University of California Los Angeles. \"You should just avoid chronic or long-term use.\"\n\nThe study's authors also note that the risk of ADHD-like behaviors is higher when acetaminophen is taken for longer periods and later in pregnancy. However, they emphasize that the study's results should be interpreted with caution, and that more research is needed to fully understand the relationship between acetaminophen use and ADHD.\n\nIn the meantime, healthcare professionals are advising pregnant women to weigh the risks and benefits of taking acetaminophen, and to consider alternative pain relief methods such as massages, baths, and acupuncture.\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n\n* A recent study found a possible link between prenatal exposure to acetaminophen and an increased risk of ADHD-like behavioral problems in children.\n* The study's authors suggest that acetaminophen may increase the risk of ADHD by interfering with maternal hormones that are critical for fetal brain development.\n* However, the study does not show a cause-and-effect relationship between acetaminophen use and ADHD.\n* Healthcare professionals are advising pregnant women to weigh the risks and benefits of taking acetaminophen, and to consider alternative pain relief methods.\n* More research is needed to fully understand the relationship between acetaminophen use and ADHD."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Bill Cosby was labeled a \"violent sexual predator\" by the judge who sentenced him to three to 10 years in prison on Tuesday, but his publicist had a different description: one of the \"greatest civil rights leaders\" in history and one of the \"greatest educators of men and boys.\" Outside the courtroom in Norristown, Pa., publicist Andrew Wyatt claimed the 81-year-old Cosby had faced the \"most racist and sexist trial\" in American history, the New York Times reports. Wyatt claimed that three psychologists who testified against Cosby in the sexual assault trial were trying to \"make money off of accusing black men of being sexual predators.\" He also read a statement from Camille Cosby, the disgraced entertainer's wife, alleging that an audio recording played in the trial had been doctored. Wyatt, who previously called Cosby's trial a \"public lynching,\" accused Judge Steven O'Neill of being part of a \"sex war\" and linked the sentencing to the treatment of Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh. Vox reports that the strangest moment came when Wyatt likened his client to Jesus. \"They persecuted Jesus, and look what happened,\" he said. \"Not saying Mr. Cosby is Jesus, but we know what this country has done to black men for centuries.\" Cosby, who left the Montgomery County Courthouse in handcuffs, spent his first night behind bars at SCI Phoenix, a state prison that opened two months ago in suburban Philadelphia, reports WPVI. (Cosby's mug shot was released Tuesday afternoon.)"], "length": 1313, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Bill Cosby Sentenced to Prison, Claims of Racism and Sexism Emerge**\n\nBill Cosby, the 81-year-old comedian and actor, was sentenced to three to 10 years in prison for drugging and sexually assaulting a woman, Andrea Constand, at his home outside Philadelphia in 2004. The sentencing took place on Tuesday, with Judge Steven T. O'Neill also ruling that Cosby qualified as a \"sexually violent predator\" under state law.\n\nIn statements outside the courthouse, Cosby's defense team, led by publicist Andrew Wyatt, made fiery accusations of racism and sexism, framing their client's downfall as an unjust product of the #MeToo era. Wyatt compared Cosby's trial to accusations of sexual misconduct against Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh, suggesting that both men are victims of a \"sex war\" happening in Washington. Cosby's team also criticized the psychologists who testified against him, claiming that audio footage of Cosby played in the courtroom was falsified.\n\nWyatt's comments sparked outrage, with many accusing him of using racist and sexist language. Cosby's team has repeatedly argued that racism played a role in his conviction, with some invoking Emmett Till, a black boy murdered after a white woman falsely accused him of whistling at her.\n\nThe connection to Kavanaugh was first made by Cosby's team, who claimed that both men are victims of a \"sex war\" that is \"going on in Washington today.\" However, it is unclear whether this claim will have any impact on Kavanaugh's nomination process, which has been embroiled in controversy after two women accused him of sexual assault and misconduct in incidents dating back to the 1980s.\n\nCosby was taken away in handcuffs after his sentencing and is currently being held at the Montgomery County Correctional Facility in Eagleville, Pennsylvania. He will serve his sentence at SCI Phoenix, a new 3,830-bed state prison in suburban Philadelphia. Cosby's inmate number with the Pennsylvania Department of Corrections is NN7687.\n\nThe case has sparked widespread debate about the #MeToo movement and the treatment of high-profile men accused of sexual misconduct. As Cosby begins his prison sentence, his team's claims of racism and sexism are likely to continue to dominate the headlines."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A day after being accidentally introduced as \"President Clinton,\" Hillary seems to be taking pains to kill talk of a future run in New Zealand's media, reports Reuters. Asked whether she'd \"ruled out\" running through 2016, she replied, \"Oh yes, yes. I'm very pleased to be doing what I'm doing as secretary of state.\" Asked in another interview whether she could be the first female president, she answered, \"Well, not me. But it will be someone ...\" The significance? Probably not much, writes Laura Rozen in Politico, noting how one of Clinton's answers is tied to her being secretary of state—a post she likely won't hold in 2016. On another front, Clinton said the midterm losses were \"big—but not out of the pattern of historical political elections.\" She added, \"I think the president made very clear was he made decisions which were essential for the well-being of the American people.\""], "length": 1038, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Hillary Clinton Ruling Out Presidential Run**\n\nIn a recent visit to New Zealand, US Secretary of State Hillary Clinton has ruled out running for president in 2012 or 2016. In interviews with local media outlets, Clinton stated that she has no plans to run again, despite speculation fueled by the Democratic Party's losses in the recent US mid-term elections. Clinton emphasized that the US should be ready for a woman president, but it would not be her.\n\nClinton's comments were met with laughter and applause when New Zealand Prime Minister John Phillip Key mistakenly referred to her as \"President Clinton\" during a bilateral meeting. Key later corrected himself, saying \"Secretary Clinton - great.\"\n\nClinton also sought to defend President Barack Obama and put the Democrats' losses in the mid-term elections into perspective. She acknowledged that the election was \"big,\" but added that it was not out of the pattern of historical political elections. Clinton emphasized that the President made decisions essential for the well-being of the American people, even if they were not popular.\n\nClinton's visit to New Zealand was part of a nearly two-week Asia-Pacific tour, which also included stops in Australia. Her comments have helped to quell speculation about her potential presidential run in the future.\n\nOverall, Clinton's ruling out of a presidential run has been met with a mix of relief and disappointment from some quarters, but her continued service as Secretary of State has been welcomed by many."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Amy Schumer took the long way around to announce she's pregnant with husband Chris Fischer. The comedian and actress broke her baby news Monday on the Instagram stories of friend and journalist Jessica Yellin, the AP reports. Yellin, of the site NewsNotNoise.org, showed at the end of a list of Schumer's recommended congressional and gubernatorial candidates the line: \"I'm pregnant-Amy Schumer.\" Schumer had teased the announcement on her own Instagram page, captioning a photo of hers and Fischer's heads atop Prince Harry's and Meghan Markle's bodies, \"About to announce some exciting news on @jessicayellin insta page. Please follow her for up to the minute #newsnotnoise she breaks down what’s really going on. She agreed to post a lil noise today for me! Follow her and VOTE!!\" \"We are 15 days out from the midterms and there is a lot going on,\" Yellin explained before going into Schumer's \"lengthy\" list of recommendations, per Bustle. \"I wanted to share some news from our community. Maybe it's noise, but it's happy noise,\" Yellin continued. \"These are the recommendations of Amy Schumer, one of the most consistent and early supporters of #NewsNotNoise. Now, read all the way to the bottom. You'll see there's some news down there. Congratulations, Amy.\" Schumer is known for her liberal politics: She was recently arrested protesting the confirmation of Judge Brett Kavanaugh to the US Supreme Court. The 37-year-old married Fischer, a chef, in February. (Here's why Schumer won't appear in any Super Bowl ads.)"], "length": 1133, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Amy Schumer has made headlines recently for her stance on social and political issues. In April 2018, she announced that she would not be participating in any Super Bowl LIII commercials, standing in solidarity with Colin Kaepernick's take-a-knee protest against racism and police brutality. Schumer explained her decision on Instagram, challenging white NFL players to kneel as well, and stating that she was \"complicit\" if they did not.\n\nFast forward to 2023, Schumer announced that she is expecting her first child. However, she did not stop there. She also used this opportunity to remind her followers to head to the polls in November, promoting her friend Jessica Yellin's #NewsNotNoise campaign. Yellin's page explains the movement, which aims to educate people about what's really going on in politics, versus what's just \"noise.\"\n\nSchumer has been a vocal advocate for various causes, including gun control, supporting the transgender community, and Colin Kaepernick's peaceful protest of the NFL. Her commitment to these causes is evident in her recent actions, as she ties her baby announcement to a voting announcement, encouraging her followers to get involved in the upcoming midterm elections.\n\nSchumer's decision to participate in the #NewsNotNoise campaign is a reflection of her passion for politics and her desire to educate others. Her actions demonstrate that she is not afraid to take a stand and use her platform to make a difference. As she continues to navigate her personal and professional life, Schumer remains committed to using her voice to advocate for the causes she cares about."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Chicken giant Tyson Foods says it will retrain all of its workers who deal with live birds after an animal rights group released hidden footage from within its facilities on Wednesday, USA Today reports. According to the Washington Post, the video from Compassion Over Killing shows workers choking, punching, and kicking chickens, plus running them over with forklifts and leaving them in piles to die. One worker in the video says it's \"inhumane\" to kill a chicken by stepping on its head while he kills a chicken by stepping on its head. Tyson calls the behavior \"inexcusable\" and says it fired 10 employees at the Virginia facilities shown in the video. Animal rights activists have infiltrated Tyson Foods to film such videos at least five times in a little over a year."], "length": 1671, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tyson Foods Faces Backlash Over Animal Abuse Allegations**\n\nA recent undercover investigation by Compassion Over Killing has exposed shocking mistreatment of chickens at Tyson Foods facilities in Virginia. The video, which was taken by an undercover investigator, shows Tyson employees throwing, punching, and kicking chickens, as well as sticking plastic rods through their beaks in a process known as \"boning.\" The footage also depicts workers wringing birds' necks, running over them with forklifts, and leaving injured birds to die.\n\nTyson Foods has responded to the allegations, stating that the behavior is \"inexcusable\" and has fired 10 employees who were caught on camera engaging in the abuse. The company has also discontinued the practice of \"boning\" at two of the facilities in question and has retrained all of its live poultry workers on animal welfare policies.\n\nHowever, animal rights groups are calling for more action to be taken by the company. \"This is a systemic problem,\" said Erica Meier, executive director of Compassion Over Killing. \"Tyson as a company needs to be held accountable for the mistreatment of animals in their care.\"\n\nThe latest video is the latest in a series of undercover investigations by Compassion Over Killing, which has exposed similar abuses at other Tyson facilities in the past. The company has responded to previous allegations by stating that the abusers were not reflective of its institutional commitment to animal welfare.\n\nThe incident has sparked a wider debate about animal welfare in the meat industry, with some calling for a shift towards more humane methods of killing and eating animals. \"The best way we can protect chickens and all animals is simply to leave them off our plates,\" said the narrator of the video.\n\nTyson Foods is one of the largest chicken-processing companies in the United States, with over $10 billion in poultry sales last year. The company's response to the allegations has been met with skepticism by some, who argue that more needs to be done to address the systemic problems of animal abuse in the industry."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Donald Trump's lawyer has apparently decided that it's his turn to make the gaffes. In an interview with CNN Wednesday, notoriously combative Trump Organization attorney Michael Cohen interrupted host Brianna Keilar when she said that the campaign was \"down,\" Politico reports. \"Says who?\" he asked. \"Polls. Most of them. All of them?\" she replied. \"Says who?\" Cohen asked again. When she repeated it was polls, he responded, \"OK, which polls?” and Keilar said: “All of them.\" USA Today reports that Twitter users were quick to mock Cohen and are now replying \"Says who?\" to all his tweets. Cohen, in what Law Newz calls \"typical lawyer fashion,\" also took issue with Trump's overhaul of campaign staff being called a \"shake-up,\" insisting it was actually a \"change-up.\" Cohen also raised eyebrows when he told Kielar that Trump knows about \"the African-American problem in this country,\" the Independent reports. \"What I meant to say is the problem that exists in the African-American community,\" he later said. (Michael Moore says he \"knows for a fact\" that Trump never really wanted to be president.)"], "length": 1533, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\nIn recent news, Donald Trump's campaign has undergone a significant shake-up, with new senior officials appointed to oversee the campaign. Michael Cohen, Trump's lawyer, was questioned by CNN's Brianna Keilar about the hiring of campaign manager Kellyanne Conway and CEO Stephen Bannon. Cohen's response to Keilar's question about Trump's polling deficit was met with confusion, as he repeatedly asked \"says who?\" when Keilar mentioned polls.\n\nThe exchange between Cohen and Keilar was awkward, with Keilar pointing out that polls show Trump trailing Hillary Clinton in many states. Cohen's response was seen as evasive, and the interview ended with Cohen stating that he thinks the new hires are a great move, but the campaign wasn't ready for them earlier.\n\nThe hiring of Conway and Bannon has been seen as a attempt to remedy Trump's struggling campaign, but the move has been met with skepticism by some. Trump's running mate, Mike Pence, is a strong supporter of the new hires, but others have questioned the timing and effectiveness of the changes.\n\nOn Twitter, the hashtag #SaysWho trended after Cohen's interview, with many users responding with the phrase in response to his repeated questions. The hashtag was also used to mock Cohen's response to Keilar's question about polls.\n\nIn other news, Hillary Clinton remains ahead in most polls, leading by double-digits in Virginia and Colorado, and also edges Trump in Iowa. However, Trump has won at least one poll, a Monmouth University poll of Indiana, where he was ahead of Clinton by 11 points.\n\nOverall, the recent news suggests that Trump's campaign is struggling to gain traction, and the new hires may not be enough to turn the tide. The awkward exchange between Cohen and Keilar has raised questions about the effectiveness of the campaign's leadership and strategy."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The school resource officer suspended after videos of him throwing a black female high-school student around a South Carolina classroom went viral Monday has been fired, CBS News reports. Richland County Sheriff Leon Lott announced the firing of Ben Fields Wednesday after an investigation found his \"use of force was unacceptable\" and he didn't follow procedure during the arrest. According to NBC News, the decision was made after the sheriff's department looked at videos of the incident and interviewed witnesses. \"From the very beginning that's what's caused me to be upset, and continued to upset me, is that he picked the student up and threw the student across the room,\" Lott says. The Department of Justice is also conducting its own investigation into the incident. This isn't the first time Fields, who is white, has faced accusations of excessive force and racial bias. He's been involved in three other such cases dating back to 2005, and he's scheduled to go to trial over a 2013 racial bias incident in January."], "length": 2227, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**South Carolina Sheriff Fires Deputy Accused of Racial Bias and Excessive Force**\n\nIn a move aimed at addressing concerns of racial bias and excessive force, Richland County Sheriff Leon Lott has fired Senior Deputy Ben Fields, a school resource officer who was caught on camera violently removing a student from class. The incident occurred at Spring Valley High School in Columbia, South Carolina, on Monday, and was captured on cellphone videos taken by students.\n\nAccording to the sheriff, Fields used \"pain compliance techniques\" that were not in line with department policies, which include using \"tactical communication to try to talk them into compliance\" and only using physical force when necessary to prevent harm. The student, who has not been identified, suffered injuries, including a cast on her arm, neck, and back injuries.\n\nThe FBI, the Justice Department, and state law enforcement have opened separate investigations into the incident, which has sparked widespread outrage and calls for Fields to be charged with assault. The president of the South Carolina chapter of the NAACP has also called for Fields to be charged with assault.\n\nFields, 34, had been a school resource officer at Spring Valley High School for seven years and had previously been the subject of allegations of racial bias and excessive force. Despite this, he had been praised by some for his actions, with at least one fellow student and some school administrators supporting his behavior.\n\nThe incident has raised concerns about the use of school resource officers and the need for more training and certification. The school district has announced plans to strengthen its training efforts to prevent similar incidents in the future.\n\nIn a statement, Lott said that while Fields' actions were not justified, the student's unruly behavior also contributed to the incident. However, he acknowledged that Fields' actions were \"not acceptable\" and that he was taking responsibility for the incident.\n\nThe firing of Fields marks a significant step in addressing concerns of racial bias and excessive force in the South Carolina justice system. The investigations into the incident are ongoing, and it remains to be seen whether Fields will face any further consequences for his actions."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The man who murdered John Lennon will remain behind bars after his 10th unsuccessful parole hearing, reports the Daily News. Mark David Chapman, now 63, was turned down again this week. No reasons have yet been made public, but the newspaper reports that Yoko Ono sent the parole board a letter asking that Chapman remain in the Wende Correctional Facility in upstate New York—not only for the safety of her and her family, but for his own safety. Chapman, who shot Lennon in New York City in 1980, is up again for parole in about two years. Chapman's wife recently told the UK Mirror that he talked of killing Lennon two months before actually doing so."], "length": 1567, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Breaking News: Gloria Chapman's Husband Mark David Chapman May Be Released After 38 Years**\n\nIn a shocking turn of events, Mark David Chapman, the man who murdered John Lennon in 1980, may be released from prison after 38 years. Chapman's parole hearing is scheduled to take place the week starting August 20, and his wife Gloria, 67, is hopeful that he will be granted freedom.\n\nGloria revealed that her husband had told her he was planning to murder Lennon two months before the infamous event, but she believed him when he said he had dumped the gun and was planning to grow up as an adult. However, she soon discovered that he had lied to her, and she knew he was the assassin.\n\nDespite being urged by friends and even Lennon's widow Yoko Ono to divorce him, Gloria refused, citing her vows to love Mark \"for richer, for poorer, for better, for worse, in sickness, and in health.\" The couple has only been allowed to spend 44 hours together per year during conjugal visits, but Gloria has been praying for Mark's release.\n\nThe news comes as the Internet Archive, a non-profit organization, is working to archive web pages and preserve links to prevent broken links on the web. This effort aims to fix all broken links and make it easier for people to access archived content.\n\nIn other news, the Internet Archive is also working to archive \"No More 404\" sites, which are websites that are being taken down or have broken links. The organization hopes that by archiving these pages, they can preserve the content and make it accessible to the public.\n\nOverall, the news surrounding Mark David Chapman's potential release is a complex and emotional one, and it raises questions about the nature of love, loyalty, and redemption."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["See, this is why all those Twitter profiles are sure to note that retweets do not equal endorsements. CBS News reports the Pentagon's official Twitter account on Thursday retweeted a tweet calling for President Trump's resignation. \"GOP: Stop making sexual assault a partisan issue,\" tweeted \"Proud Resister\" while calling for Roy Moore, Sen. Al Franken, and Trump to all resign or step aside due to allegations of sexual misconduct. The tweet was retweeted—and then soon deleted—by the Department of Defense Twitter account. According to Politico, Pentagon spokesperson Col. Rob Manning confirmed the retweet was made by the \"authorized operator\" of the department's Twitter account. In her own tweet, Pentagon spokesperson Dana White said the call for Trump's resignation was \"erroneously retweeted\" and contained \"content that would not be endorsed by the Department of Defense.\""], "length": 707, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Pentagon Clarifies Mistaken Retweet of Call for Trump's Resignation**\n\nIn a recent incident, the Pentagon's official Twitter account retweeted a call for President Donald Trump's resignation, which was later clarified as an \"erroneous\" mistake. The tweet, posted by the account \"Proud Resister,\" suggested that Trump, along with other figures accused of sexual misconduct, should step aside or resign. The Pentagon's chief spokesperson, Col. Rob Manning, issued a statement explaining that an \"authorized operator\" of the account made the mistake, and the content was quickly deleted. The incident highlights the potential for social media missteps by government officials and the importance of clarifying any endorsements or statements made on official accounts.\n\n**Trump's Sexual Misconduct Allegations Continue to Emerge**\n\nThe recent retweet incident is part of a larger conversation surrounding President Trump's alleged sexual misconduct. In recent months, 16 women have come forward accusing Trump of behaving inappropriately towards them, and he has been caught on camera making comments about grabbing women by their genitalia. The White House has faced pressure to address these allegations, with some calling for Trump's resignation. In response, White House press secretary Sarah Huckabee Sanders has stated that the White House has been clear on its position regarding the allegations, but declined to comment further.\n\n**Twitter's Role in Government Communication**\n\nThe incident highlights the challenges of using social media for government communication. While Twitter can be a powerful tool for reaching a wide audience, it also raises concerns about the potential for missteps or misinterpretation. The Pentagon's official Twitter account, @DeptofDefense, does not include a disclaimer stating that retweets do not constitute endorsements, which may have contributed to the confusion. The incident serves as a reminder for government officials to carefully consider their use of social media and to ensure that any statements or endorsements are clearly clarified."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Peter Jackson's crafts a fantastic vision of the afterlife in The Lovely Bones, say critics, but some feel it came at the expense of the human side of the story of a murdered teen, adapted from the book by Alice Sebold. \"By turns warmly sentimental, serial-killer sinister, and science-fiction fantastical, The Lovely Bones was an unlikely book to achieve worldwide success,\" Kenneth Turan writes in the Los Angeles Times. \"Those mismatched elements come back to haunt\" the film version, \"making the final product more hit-and-miss than unblemished triumph.\" \"Sebold's Lovely Bones is fleshed out with the perilous, irresistible power of sex—a real world of extramarital sex and sex between young lovers in addition to the heinous rape from which moviegoers are shielded.\" But Jackson \"shies from the challenge, shortchanging a story that isn't only about the lightness of souls in heaven but also about the urges of bodies on earth,\" writes Lisa Schwarzbaum at Entertainment Weekly. \"Through Jackson's art\" and the magic of star Saoirse Rogan, Richard Corliss writes at Time, \"the obscenity of child murder has been invested with immense gravity and grace.\" \"Some books are not meant to be adapted to the big screen,\" and The Lovely Bones is one of them, writes Claudia Puig at USA Today. What works in the book, she writes, comes off as \"artificial and emotionless on-screen.\""], "length": 3045, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "The film adaptation of Alice Sebold's novel \"The Lovely Bones\" directed by Peter Jackson has received mixed reviews. The movie follows the story of Susie Salmon, a 14-year-old girl who is murdered by her neighbor George Harvey in 1973. Susie narrates her story from heaven, where she watches her family and friends try to cope with her death.\n\nThe film features a talented cast, including Saoirse Ronan, Stanley Tucci, Mark Wahlberg, and Rachel Weisz. Ronan's performance as Susie is widely praised, bringing a sense of naturalness and honesty to the role. Tucci's portrayal of George Harvey is also notable, bringing a sense of quiet malevolence to the character.\n\nHowever, the film has been criticized for its tone, which veers wildly from lighthearted to heavy-handed. The movie's use of a CGI-generated afterlife, where Susie watches her family and friends, has been particularly criticized for feeling artificial and emotionless.\n\nThe film's plot has also been criticized for its simplification of the novel's complex themes. The book explores the power of sex and the real world, while the film tones down these elements for a more family-friendly audience.\n\nDespite these criticisms, the film has been praised for its visuals and its ability to bring the novel's themes to life. The film's use of miniature models and special effects has been particularly notable, creating a unique and immersive world.\n\nOverall, the film adaptation of \"The Lovely Bones\" is a mixed bag, with both praise and criticism for its tone, plot, and visuals. While it has its flaws, the film is a visually stunning and emotionally powerful exploration of grief, loss, and the afterlife.\n\nIn terms of the news, the film's release has been highly anticipated, with many expecting it to be a major hit. The film's cast and crew, including director Peter Jackson and actress Saoirse Ronan, have been widely praised for their work on the film.\n\nThe film's box office performance has been strong, with many expecting it to be a major commercial success. However, the film's critical reception has been more mixed, with some critics praising its visuals and others criticizing its tone and plot.\n\nOverall, the film adaptation of \"The Lovely Bones\" is a significant event in the world of cinema, with many expecting it to be a major hit. While it has its flaws, the film is a visually stunning and emotionally powerful exploration of grief, loss, and the afterlife."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A video released online appears to show a Mexican soldier executing a suspected oil thief at point-blank range as he lays defenseless on the ground. The heavily edited video, taken from a surveillance camera, shows a car coming under fire during what the military describes as a series of ambushes that left four soldiers and six suspects dead in Palmarito on May 3. As soldiers surround the vehicle, its occupants exit and are forced to lie down in front of the car. Soldiers are then seen dragging a suspect to the same spot. He appears injured but rolls from his back onto his front. Six minutes later, a soldier appears at the side of the frame and appears to shoot the man in the back of the head, per the AP. A dark stain spreads around him, reports Reuters. \"There was already concern about the use of excessive force by the military. Now this video seems to give us the proof,\" says the chairwoman of Mexico's Senate Commission on Human Rights, per the Washington Post, which identifies the man shot as Raul Jimenez Martinez, 46. Mexico's defense ministry, however, says it will cooperate fully with an investigation launched by the attorney general's office on May 4. \"Under no circumstances can conduct contrary to the law and human rights be justified,\" the ministry says. Residents of Palmarito have already taken to the streets to protest the deaths of the suspects, placing the blame on the army, though the defense ministry previously claimed the suspects used residents as human shields."], "length": 1788, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Violence and Impunity in Mexico: A Growing Concern**\n\nA disturbing video has emerged in Mexico, appearing to show a soldier executing a civilian in the street. The footage, released by Mexican media outlets, depicts a soldier shooting a man in the back of the head while he is lying face down on the ground. The incident occurred in Palmarito, Puebla State, during a clash between security forces and suspected oil thieves.\n\nThe video has sparked widespread condemnation, with the Mexican defense ministry urging an investigation into the incident. The ministry stated that prosecutors should \"comprehensively clear up\" whether the video indeed showed the soldier shooting the detained man after a clash last week.\n\nThis incident is not an isolated one, as Mexico has been plagued by violence and impunity in recent years. The country has the second-highest number of murders among countries considered in \"armed conflicts,\" according to a report. In 2014 and 2015, two shootouts between federal security forces and suspected gang members resulted in over 60 lives lost, with accusations of extrajudicial killings.\n\nThe Mexican military has been implicated in several scandals, including the killing of 22 suspects at a warehouse in the Mexico state community of Tlatlaya. An investigation found that soldiers executed at least a dozen suspects after they surrendered, contradicting the military's initial statement that all died in a gunfight.\n\nThe government has pledged to cooperate with the attorney general's office in the investigation, but the incident has raised concerns about the army's use of extrajudicial force. The Mexican National Human Rights Commission has a copy of the video and other recordings of the encounter, and has called for a swift and thorough investigation.\n\nThe video's release has sparked a new scandal for the armed forces, and highlights the need for greater accountability and transparency in Mexico's security forces. The country's ongoing crisis of violence and impunity demands a comprehensive response, including investigations and reforms to address the root causes of the violence."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Connor Cummins' family describes him to WABC-TV as a photography devotee—and that enthusiasm may have cost him his life. The New Jersey man, said to be either 20 or 24 years old, and an 18-year-old pal ascended the scaffolding outside NYC's Four Seasons hotel Wednesday night in search of the perfect panoramic picture—but Cummins may have slipped on railings made slippery by the rain and fell nine stories to his death, cops tell NJ.com. An NYPD spokesman tells the New York Daily News that the initial investigation suggests the men weren't guests of the hotel. Cummins, who originally hails from Rockaway and whose Facebook page indicates he's now a student at the University of Massachusetts Amherst, per ABC, landed on one of the hotel's lower-level rooftops around 11:15pm and was pronounced dead at the scene, reports the New York Post. His friend wasn't injured and was questioned by authorities to fill in the blanks; he was released Thursday morning without being charged. \"He was a loving boy, that's all I can say,\" one of Cummins' relatives tells the Post. \"I don't know what happened.\" (There's more suspicion surrounding a Muslim teen's fatal fall off a Seattle building.)"], "length": 1299, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tragic Death of 20-Year-Old Connor Cummings at Four Seasons Hotel**\n\nA 20-year-old man, Connor Cummings, fell to his death while attempting to take pictures from the scaffolding of the 52-story Four Seasons Hotel in Manhattan. Cummings, a sophomore at the University of Massachusetts Amherst, was accompanied by an 18-year-old friend, Dimitri Olivares, who was also trying to take photos.\n\nAccording to police, Cummings and Olivares climbed the scaffolding in an interior shaft of the hotel, despite it being wet due to rain. As Cummings was about to step through a hatch to the roof, he slipped and fell nine stories down, landing on a lower rooftop. Cummings was pronounced dead at the scene.\n\nThe incident occurred at around 11:15 p.m. on Wednesday, and police believe that Cummings and Olivares were not guests at the hotel. The two were trying to take pictures of the city skyline, and Cummings was described as a photography buff.\n\nOlivares, who was with Cummings when he fell, was interviewed by detectives and released without charges. His family expressed shock and concern, with Cummings' aunt stating that he was not a daredevil and was not trying to take any risks.\n\nThe investigation is ongoing, and hotel officials have stated that they are cooperating with police to help in the investigation. The hotel's safety and security are a top priority, and officials have extended their sympathies to Cummings' family.\n\nCummings was a graduate of Morris Knolls High School and was attending college in Massachusetts. He was also a member of the Alpha Sigma Phi fraternity and had a passion for photography. His friends and family have described him as a loving and down-to-earth person who was always committed to his passions.\n\nThe incident has raised concerns about safety and security at the hotel, and police are reviewing the incident to determine how it occurred."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Protesting truckers are planning a shutdown of their own—on Washington's beltway. Starting on Friday morning, they intend to fill the road \"three lanes deep\" with tractor-trailers, an organizer of \"Truckers Ride for the Constitution\" tells US News and World Report. In a demonstration against what they view as violations of the Constitution, planners like Earl Conlon are calling for \"the arrest of everyone in government who has violated their oath of office.\" Their grievances include truckers' wages, industry regulations, and NSA spying, the Hill reports. They're also accusing President Obama of treason for allegedly putting weapons in the hands of rebels with ties to al-Qaeda. Organizers have received some 3,000 RSVPs, says one. Moving at the 55mph speed limit, they'll leave a lane open for emergency vehicles; vehicles with \"T2SDA,\" or \"Truckers to Shut Down America,\" displayed will also be allowed to pass. \"Everybody that doesn't have a supporter sticker on their window, good luck: Nobody in, nobody out,\" says Conlon. A Facebook page for the event has more than 55,000 \"likes.\""], "length": 1638, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Nationwide Protests Erupt as Truckers Demand Constitutional Reform**\n\nA growing movement of truckers, dubbed \"Truckers for the Constitution,\" is planning a nationwide protest to demand constitutional reform and express their discontent with the federal government. The protest, set to take place on October 11, will involve tractor-trailer drivers intentionally clogging the inner loop of the Washington, D.C. beltway, with the goal of drawing attention to their grievances and forcing lawmakers to take action.\n\nThe truckers, who claim to be operating on extremely low wages and facing exorbitant fuel costs and fees, are unhappy with the Obama administration's regulations and the overall political climate in the U.S. They are also protesting the government's alleged spying on American citizens and the country's mounting debt.\n\nThe protest, which has gained significant attention on social media, is being led by Earl Conlon, a Georgia trucker who is handling logistics for the event. Conlon has stated that the truckers will not be asking for impeachment, but rather seeking the arrest of government officials who have allegedly violated their oath of office. He has identified several politicians, including President Barack Obama, House Minority Leader Nancy Pelosi, and Sen. Dianne Feinstein, as targets for arrest.\n\nThe protest is not without controversy, with many experts warning that attempting to \"arrest\" members of Congress would be a futile effort, as it would likely result in the protesters being arrested themselves for attempted kidnapping. However, Conlon remains undeterred, stating that the truckers will not back down and will continue to push for their demands.\n\nThe protest has sparked a heated debate about the role of the trucking industry in American society and the need for constitutional reform. While some have expressed support for the truckers' cause, others have criticized their tactics as extreme and divisive.\n\nAs the protest approaches, it remains to be seen whether the truckers will be able to achieve their goals and force lawmakers to take action. However, one thing is clear: the movement has sparked a national conversation about the state of the country and the need for reform."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["One of the two Secret Service supervisors to lose their jobs so far over the Colombia sex scandal joked that he was checking out Sarah Palin as he guarded her during the 2008 campaign, the Washington Post finds. Senior agent David Chaney posted at least two pictures of himself with Palin on Facebook, one with the comment, \"I was really checking her out, if you know what I mean.\" Chaney has been forced to resign, and another supervisor has been informed that he will be fired. In an interview with Fox News, Palin said the joke is on Chaney. \"Well check this out, buddy—you're fired!\" she said, calling the scandal \"a symptom of government run amok,\" reports AP. \"It's like, who's minding the store around here?\" she added. \"The president, for one, he better be wary, there, of when Secret Service is accompanying his family on vacation. They may be checking out the first lady instead of guarding her.\""], "length": 3030, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "The Secret Service is embroiled in a prostitution scandal after 11 agents and uniformed officers were accused of participating in a night of carousing in Cartagena, Colombia, ahead of President Barack Obama's visit to the Summit of the Americas. The scandal has led to the dismissal of three employees, including two supervisors, David Chaney and Greg Stokes, who were accused of heavy drinking, visits to a strip club, and payments to women working as prostitutes.\n\nChaney, a 48-year-old supervisor, made light of his official protective work on his Facebook page, joking about a picture of himself standing watch behind Sarah Palin during the 2008 Republican vice presidential campaign. He wrote, \"I was really checking her out, if you know what i mean?\" in the comments section.\n\nPalin responded to the revelations by saying she was disgusted that a Secret Service agent would make jokes about checking out her \"backside\" and called his behavior \"pretty embarrassing.\" She also stressed that the scandal is emblematic of Obama's poor management.\n\nThe scandal has raised questions of accountability and personal conduct in an agency whose top leadership has insisted that the Cartagena incident is an isolated and aberrant case, not a sign of a deeper cultural problem within the institution. Members of Congress who have been briefed on the matter have said 21 men are suspected of bringing as many as 21 prostitutes to their rooms, and 10 military personnel also have been accused of participating.\n\nThe Secret Service has announced that it will conduct a thorough investigation into the allegations and has already taken disciplinary action against the employees involved. The agency's director, Mark Sullivan, has expressed renewed confidence in the agency's ability to protect the president and his family.\n\nThe scandal has also raised questions about the judgment and behavior of Secret Service employees, particularly those in leadership positions. Rep. Peter King (R-N.Y.) said that the 11 agents involved in the scandal underwent drug tests and polygraph exams, and that the agency is expected to take further action.\n\nOverall, the scandal has highlighted the need for greater accountability and oversight within the Secret Service, as well as the importance of maintaining the public's trust in the agency's ability to protect the president and his family."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Power: One day you have it, the next you don't. No one likely knows that better than the world's 50 \"least powerful\" people, described by 24/7 Wall St. as \"well-known individuals around the world who have recently experienced a precipitous loss in stature.\" The top 10: Anthony Scaramucci: Where to begin? Try here, here, and here. Chris Christie: The former head of Donald Trump's transition team has the lowest approval rating of any governor in New Jersey's history. James Comey: The former FBI director lasted not even five months under Trump. Mark Fields: The Ford CEO was canned after shares fell almost 40% during his tenure. Tiger Woods: Blame his DUI arrest and 899th ranking in men's pro golf. Kendall Jenner: Remember that Pepsi commercial? Montgomery Moran: After a wave of issues at Chipotle, the co-CEO stepped down last year. Tomi Lahren: The conservative media personality was suspended from the Blaze after announcing she was pro-choice. Park Geun-hye: The former president of South Korea was impeached and jailed after a corruption scandal involving Samsung. Mitch McConnell: As Senate majority leader, McConnell failed to repeal and replace the Affordable Care Act. Click for the full list, which also includes Sean Spicer, R. Kelly, and Theresa May. (See last year's ranking here.)"], "length": 1240, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "In recent years, numerous high-profile individuals have experienced a significant decline in their stature and influence. A recent list compiled by 24/7 Wall St. identified 50 individuals who have fallen from power, including celebrities, politicians, CEOs, and public figures.\n\nOne notable example is Anthony Scaramucci, the former White House Communications Director, who was fired just 11 days into his tenure. His tumultuous time in the White House was marked by protocol breaches, conflicting statements, and a profanity-laced interview with The New Yorker.\n\nGovernor of New Jersey Chris Christie has also seen his reputation suffer. His approval ratings are among the lowest in the country, and he was ultimately replaced by Mike Pence in the Trump administration. Christie's reputation was further damaged by the Bridgegate scandal, in which his allies created a traffic jam on the George Washington Bridge.\n\nJames Comey, the former Director of the FBI, was fired by President Trump in May 2017, despite serving as the agency's leader since 2013. Comey's termination was met with controversy, with some attributing Trump's electoral victory to the FBI's investigation into Hillary Clinton.\n\nMark Fields, the former CEO of Ford Motor Company, was let go in 2017 after his shares fell by nearly 40% during his tenure. Fields' determination to invest in electric and autonomous vehicle technology was seen as a risk by shareholders, and his efforts to boost profits ultimately failed.\n\nTiger Woods, a professional golfer, has also experienced a significant decline in his stature. His personal life has been marred by controversy, including reports of infidelity and a DUI arrest. Woods' latest scandal has left him ranked 899 in the world, his lowest ranking ever.\n\nThese individuals are just a few examples of those who have fallen from power in recent years. Their stories serve as a reminder that fame, fortune, and influence can be fleeting, and that circumstances can change rapidly."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Washington is braced for the first government shutdown in 17 years, which will happen at midnight tonight unless lawmakers manage to break the deadlock over funding and hammer out a deal. Here's what to expect from a day of drama in DC: The House has passed legislation to delay ObamaCare for a year and repeal a tax on medical devices, so the measure will return to the Senate this afternoon, just hours before the shutdown is due to kick in. Harry Reid is expected to table the anti-ObamaCare amendments with a simple Democratic majority and quickly send it back to the House—but if any opportunity arises for Senate Republicans to use delaying tactics, expect Ted Cruz to lead the way, the Wall Street Journal predicts. There's a chance of a straight up-or-down vote in the House, but if it becomes clear that it will be impossible to break the deadlock by midnight—which appears to be the likeliest scenario—the partial shutdown could still be averted if lawmakers pass a short-term funding bill to buy another week or so of debating time. So who will blink first? Neither Reid nor John Boehner appears likely to give way. Reid is determined to play hardball and not give an inch on ObamaCare, Politico reports. Reid and his allies believe that giving any concessions now will only lead to bigger concessions to avoid a debt default next month—and they think the backlash from a government shutdown will hit the GOP hardest. Boehner, meanwhile, will face the wrath of his party's conservatives if he gives way too soon—but polls show the public will blame his House Republicans if there is a shutdown. If there is a shutdown, it won't be an abrupt halt to government, but more \"like a spending freeze that will gradually spread through the government like ice forming in water,\" Quartz explains. Federal employees, 800,000 of whom will be sent home without pay, will be the first affected, and the shutdown will also affect scientific research, trade negotiations, and the federal programs that guarantee home loans and provide capital to business. Air traffic controllers will still be at work, however, and agencies that deal with national security and law enforcement are exempt from the shutdown. A shutdown of a few days might not do too much damage, but experts warn that the national economy will take a serious hit if it is prolonged. In Washington, DC, however, even a brief shutdown will cost the district an estimated $200 million a day—and affect basic services like trash collection. \"This is serious,\" the director of George Mason University’s Center for Regional Analysis tells the Washington Post. \"The national economy may not notice a shutdown much unless it lasts three or four weeks. But for the Washington area, this is a tsunami.\""], "length": 3547, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Government Shutdown Looms as Congress Fails to Reach Agreement**\n\nThe US government is on the brink of a shutdown as Congress fails to reach an agreement on a spending bill. The House of Representatives passed a plan to fund federal agencies through December 15, but Senate Democrats have vowed to reject any changes to the Affordable Care Act (ACA), also known as Obamacare.\n\nHouse Speaker John Boehner (R-OH) has pushed for a delay in the implementation of the ACA, citing concerns over its impact on the economy. However, Senate Majority Leader Harry Reid (D-NE) has taken a hardline stance, refusing to budge on the ACA. Reid believes that making Republicans cave on the issue will be key to avoiding a shutdown.\n\nThe Washington region, home to the largest concentration of federal workers and contractors, could lose an estimated $200 million a day and see over 700,000 jobs affected if the government shuts down. Local economists warn that the shutdown could have devastating ripple effects on the local economy, including lost income tax revenue and delayed services.\n\nAir traffic controllers are considered essential, but without \"nonessential\" support staff, planes could be delayed. Federal benefits may continue to be processed, but with fewer workers to process them, no new applications would be accepted and \"serious backlogs\" would be expected.\n\nLawmakers have passed legislation to continue paying active military members, but veterans' benefits could run out if a shutdown lasts more than a few weeks. The Congressional Research Service has warned that a shutdown could have significant impacts on national parks and monuments, passport applications, and clinical trials.\n\nAs the deadline for a spending bill approaches, both parties are scrambling to find a solution. Democrats are pushing for a clean stopgap measure to keep the government funded, while Republicans are demanding concessions on the ACA. The outcome is uncertain, but one thing is clear: the nation is on the verge of its first government shutdown in 17 years.\n\n**Key Players:**\n\n* House Speaker John Boehner (R-OH)\n* Senate Majority Leader Harry Reid (D-NE)\n* President Barack Obama\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["NASA is going to the sun. More specifically, it's launching an unmanned probe next year that will travel closer to the star than any spacecraft has done previously. \"It's a spacecraft loaded with technological breakthroughs that will solve many of the largest mysteries about our star,\" says Nicola Fox of the Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory. In less scientific terms, per the Telegraph: \"We will finally touch the sun.\" The nuts and bolts: The mission: The 10-foot probe will launch in July or August of 2018 and eventually get to within 3.7 million miles of the sun, about seven times closer than any previous spacecraft. Eventually, it will be whizzing around the sun at a speed of 450,000 miles per hour, reports CNN. The mission ends in 2025. Corona: The probe will actually fly into the outermost part of the sun's atmosphere, called the corona, per a mission overview at NASA that touts \"humanity's first visit to a star.\" The heat: The probe will rely on a 4.5-inch-thick carbon-composite solar shield to help it withstand temperatures up to 2,550 degrees Fahrenheit, reports Orlando's WKMG. Instruments will remain at room temperature. The name: The probe's name has been changed to the Parker Solar Probe to honor astrophysicist Eugene Parker, who's credited with discovering solar wind. Parker, who turns 90 in June, is a professor emeritus at the University of Chicago, and the Guardian recounts that the theory he put forward in 1958 about a stream of charged particles flowing from the sun was once thought to be \"crazy.\" A first: This is the first time NASA has named a mission after a living scientist. \"I'm certainly greatly honored,\" said the man himself, per Space.com. Two puzzles: Scientists hope to better understand two things in particular: \"How is the solar wind accelerated, and why is the ... corona so much hotter than the solar surface?\" (It's 3 million degrees Fahrenheit vs. 10,000 degrees Fahrenheit.) Why it matters: Generally, solar storms are relatively harmless when they reach Earth, but these particle bursts occasionally wreak havoc on satellites and here on Earth, and they have the potential to be devastating. \"The more we know about how these processes work, the better we can get at predicting when they will happen,\" writes Loren Grush at the Verge."], "length": 10147, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "NASA has announced its upcoming mission to send a spacecraft into the sun's outer atmosphere, the Parker Solar Probe. The mission, scheduled to launch in July 2018, will attempt to get close to the sun's surface, coming within 4 million miles of its surface. The probe will be equipped with a 4.5-inch thick carbon-composite solar shield to protect it from temperatures up to 2,550 degrees Fahrenheit, while keeping the science instruments at room temperature.\n\nThe mission is named after astrophysicist Eugene Parker, who predicted the existence of solar wind in 1958. Parker's theory was initially met with skepticism but was later confirmed by satellite observations. The Parker Solar Probe will study the sun's atmosphere, including the corona, and will help answer questions about how stars work and improve space weather forecasting.\n\nThe mission will also provide insight into the physics of stars and the origins of solar storms that can impact satellites and astronauts. A severe solar storm could cause $2 trillion in damage in the US alone and leave parts of the country without power for a year. The Parker Solar Probe will make 24 close flybys of the sun, with the closest approach being within 3.7 million miles of the sun's surface.\n\nThe mission is a significant step forward in understanding the sun and its impact on our planet. It will also provide valuable data for scientists to better predict space weather events and protect our technological systems from the effects of solar radiation.\n\nThe Parker Solar Probe is the first mission to be named after a living person, and it is a testament to the importance of Parker's work in founding a new field of science. The mission is expected to revolutionize our understanding of the sun and its role in our solar system.\n\nOverall, the Parker Solar Probe mission is a groundbreaking endeavor that will provide valuable insights into the sun's atmosphere and its impact on our planet. It is a significant step forward in space exploration and will help us better understand the complex relationships between our planet and the sun."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["It looks like the hackers that hit Target had lots of other, well, targets. The Department of Homeland Security recently sent retailers and financial service companies a secret memo warning that the Target hit appeared to be part of a larger international campaign, the Wall Street Journal reports, an insight gleaned with the help of Dallas cybersecurity firm iSight Partners. Yesterday, iSight released its own report, saying that a virus it's calling KAPTOXA \"has potentially infected a large number of retail information systems\"—and noting that the \"intrusion operators displayed innovation and a high degree of skill,\" particularly in terms of the \"operational sophistication\" of the hack. The Journal shares this feature by way of example: The virus focused on stealing data during the peak hours of 10am and 5pm; the data was housed in a Target server that the hackers later accessed. The virus attacks point-of-sale systems in a way that is \"new to eCrime,\" subverting traditional efforts to protect consumer data, the report warns, according to NBC. Parts of the code, which is impervious to all known antivirus software, have been online since last spring. It's partly in Russian, which US officials think may indicate a link to organized crime in the former Soviet Union. The finding follows reports of similar breaches at Neiman Marcus. That breach had gone unnoticed since July, the New York Times reports today; Neiman's system was only fully secured Sunday."], "length": 1464, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Major Data Breaches Hit Retailers During Holiday Season**\n\nThe holiday season has seen a series of major data breaches at prominent retailers, leaving millions of customers vulnerable to identity theft and financial loss. Two of the most significant breaches occurred at Neiman Marcus and Target, with the latter's breach being the largest to date.\n\nNeiman Marcus revealed in late December that its computer network had been penetrated by hackers as far back as July, with the breach not being fully contained until Sunday. The company disclosed that customer information, including credit card numbers, had been stolen, but assured customers that Social Security numbers and birth dates were not compromised. Neiman Marcus has faced criticism for not disclosing the breach sooner, with some questioning the timing of its announcement.\n\nIn contrast, Target informed its customers about the breach within a few days of learning about it, citing inquiries from a journalist specializing in computer security. The breach is believed to have occurred in November, with 40 million credit- and debit-card accounts and 70 million other pieces of personal information stolen.\n\nA report from computer firm iSight provides new insight into the Target breach, detailing the type of software used and the regions known to have the software coding prowess to pull off the attack. The report notes that the breach was caused by a type of malware placed in the store's point-of-sale systems, and that the operational sophistication of the compromise activity makes this case stand out.\n\nFurther investigation has revealed that the Target breach may be part of a broader and highly sophisticated international hacking campaign against multiple retailers. A report prepared by federal and private investigators suggests that the attack was part of a coordinated effort, with the source of the attack still unknown.\n\nBoth Neiman Marcus and Target have taken steps to notify affected customers and offer credit monitoring services. However, the breaches have raised concerns about the security of retail systems and the need for greater transparency from companies in the event of a breach.\n\nAs the holiday season comes to a close, customers are advised to remain vigilant and monitor their accounts for any suspicious activity. The breaches serve as a reminder of the importance of data security and the need for companies to prioritize customer protection."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["An escaped lion, a taunting crowd, and an ill-prepared first-response team came together in Kenya to cause what some are saying was the unnecessary, senseless death of the lion, the country's Star reports. Mohawk, a 13-year-old lion who was one of Nairobi National Park's most well-known creatures due to his trademark mane and rep as somewhat of a \"ladies' lion,\" had escaped from an unfenced section of the park and ended up in the town of Isinya, where he was promptly surrounded by a jeering group of men, the Washington Post reports. Mohawk became upset by the noise and the growing crowd—Kenya Wildlife Service officials tell the AP about 400 had surrounded him—and lunged at a 27-year-old man, who was sent to the hospital with bruises and severe lacerations on his back. The wildlife service already had an animal management team on the scene, but those rangers had only rifles, not tranquilizers. After Mohawk pounced on the bystander, the rangers felt they couldn't wait for a second team on its way with tranqs—so they unleashed what the Star says was nine bullets, leaving the animal \"roaring and writhing in pain\" as he died. \"If the rangers had the right equipment, this lion would not have died,\" a local resident tells the paper. \"You can't keep a lion in the midst of people for so many hours without stressing it. We blame them for this heinous act.\" The shooting has prompted plenty of backlash on social media, including a #JusticeForMohawk hashtag, but the wildlife service says it had no choice once the lion started attacking humans. \"This action was taken as a last resort after an escalation of the situation and a concern for public safety,\" the Kenya Wildlife Service says, per CNN. A bunch of lions have busted out of the park lately, driven by what conservationists say is the noise from development projects, including a highway being built through the park. (Same ending, different means for Cecil the lion.)"], "length": 3189, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "In Kenya, a 13-year-old male lion named Mohawk was fatally shot by wildlife rangers after it attacked a man in a crowd. Mohawk had escaped from the Nairobi National Park, where he was one of the most famous lions, due to a territorial dispute with another lion. The incident sparked an outcry among Kenyans, with many calling for the use of tranquilizers instead of lethal force. The Kenya Wildlife Service claimed that the action was taken as a last resort to save lives, but critics argue that the use of lethal force was unnecessary.\n\nThis incident is part of a larger issue of lion escapes from the Nairobi National Park, which has seen several incidents in recent weeks. The park's electric fence, which is meant to keep animals inside, has been compromised due to construction and development projects in the area. Conservationists cite habitat loss as the central reason for the surge in escapes.\n\nIn a separate incident, a lion named Sylvester escaped from Karoo National Park in South Africa, where officials have vowed to put him down once they capture him. The incident has sparked a Twitter hashtag #SaveSylvester and an online petition to rescue the big cat.\n\nThe decline of lion populations is a global concern, with the International Union for Conservation of Nature considering the African lion a \"vulnerable\" species. A study last year found that its population had declined by 50 percent in the past 20 years.\n\nThe situation is further complicated by climate change, which is affecting the availability of prey and habitat for lions. In Kenya, residents have complicated efforts to recapture escaped lions by trying to take selfies with them, which has raised concerns about the safety of both humans and animals.\n\nOverall, the incidents highlight the need for conservation efforts to protect lion populations and their habitats, as well as the importance of finding humane solutions to conflicts between humans and wildlife."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A horrifying story out of Uganda, where a toddler was grabbed and eaten by a leopard Friday at the Mweya Safari Lodge in Queen Elizabeth National Park. The 2-year-old boy's mother is a game ranger at the park and was working, and the boy was with his nanny in the kitchen of the family's home in the park's staff quarters, near a doorway. \"He was seated with the maid when the leopard grabbed and ran with him,\" the boy's father tells the Kampala Post. But a spokesperson for the Uganda Wildlife Authority (UWA) says the boy followed the nanny outdoors: \"The maid was not aware the child followed her. She heard the kid scream for help, she intervened but it was too late the leopard had vanished with it in the bush.\" The staff quarters are reportedly in a protected area that is popular with tourists, USA Today reports, but the family's home is said to be unfenced. A search team ultimately found some of the boy's bones, including his skull, and they were buried over the weekend. The boy's father says he expects compensation from the UWA and that the organization should do more to protect staff and their families. \"UWA gave us the coffin,\" he says. \"I have not talked to them about the incident but I would expect something reasonable to compensate me, although my son's life is gone.\" Leopard attacks on humans are rare in Uganda. The UWA spokesperson says efforts are being made to locate the leopard and possibly relocate it. \"The hunt is on with the intention of capturing the leopard and removing it from the wild because once it has eaten human flesh, the temptations are high to eat another human being, it becomes dangerous,\" he says, per the Telegraph."], "length": 1170, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tragedy Strikes Queen Elizabeth National Park in Uganda: Leopard Attacks and Kills Toddler**\n\nA devastating incident has shaken the nation of Uganda, as a leopard attacked and killed a 2.5-year-old child at Mweya Safari Lodge in Queen Elizabeth National Park. The child, Elisha Nabugyere, was playing at the lodge's staff quarters when the leopard, believed to be the same one responsible for the recent attacks, snatched him and dragged him into the bushes.\n\nThe child's mother, a game ranger at the park, was working at the time of the incident and was not present. The nanny, who was left in charge of the child, heard the child scream for help but was unable to intervene in time. The leopard was later tracked down, and efforts are underway to capture and relocate it from the wild.\n\nThe incident has sparked outrage and calls for increased security measures to protect staff and children at the lodge. The Uganda Wildlife Authority (UWA) has promised to take action, including relocating the leopard, but has not yet provided compensation to the family.\n\nThis tragic event is the fourth unfortunate incident to occur at the UWA in recent weeks, including the death of a pride of 11 lions, a French tourist, and another lion. The UWA has assured the public that they are working to establish the leopard and evaluate options for its relocation.\n\nThe incident has sent shockwaves throughout Uganda, with many expressing their condolences to the family and calling for greater measures to be taken to protect staff and children at the lodge. The UWA has promised to do better in the future, but for now, the family is left to grieve the loss of their beloved child.\n\n**Key Facts:**\n\n* A 2.5-year-old child was killed by a leopard at Mweya Safari Lodge in Queen Elizabeth National Park, Uganda.\n* The child was playing at the lodge's staff quarters when the leopard attacked.\n* The nanny, who was left in charge of the child, heard the child scream for help but was unable to intervene.\n* Efforts are underway to capture and relocate the leopard from the wild.\n* The incident is the fourth unfortunate event to occur at the UWA in recent weeks.\n* The UWA has promised to take action, including relocating the leopard, but has not yet provided compensation to the family."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Archaeologists in Bulgaria think they've found ashes belonging to one of the 12 apostles. The team found a small lead container with ashes while excavating a basilica that dates back to the sixth century AD, reports Archaeology in Bulgaria. The markings on the inch-long vessel match those at the grave of St. John the Apostle in what is now Turkey, leading researchers to surmise that a long-ago Christian from Bulgaria traveled to the grave site and returned home with the sample of ashes. Such journeys were common around that time, reports Novinite. “Probably a pilgrim from the Foros Peninsula went on a pilgrimage to Ephesus, and came back here with this relic, which was then donated to the basilica on Foros,” says the director of the Burgas Regional Museum of History in Bulgaria. (The ancient basilica is located in what is now the Black Sea city of Burgas.) Archeologists also found a Bulgarian royal seal from the 10th century at the dig site, which includes a fortress, notes the Week. (Another archaeological discovery: an ancient underground city in Turkey.)"], "length": 2303, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Archaeologists in Bulgaria have made a significant discovery at the ancient and medieval fortress site of Burgos (also known as Poros) on Cape Foros. A lead vessel containing ashes from the alleged grave of John the Apostle, one of Jesus' apostles, was found in a reliquary that dates back to the 6th century C.E. The reliquary, which was once part of an early Christian basilica, is named for Saint John the Theologian and is decorated with crosses.\n\nThe discovery was made by Milen Nikolov, director of the Burgas Regional Museum of History, who described it as \"one of the most important discoveries\" in the museum's history. According to Nikolov, early Christians believed that relics had healing properties, and John the Apostle's grave in Turkey was a pilgrimage site for early Christians seeking healing.\n\nThe lead tube reliquary is tiny, measuring only 2.2 cm (less than an inch) long and 1.7 cm in diameter. It is dated to the 6th century AD, a time when pilgrimage to the Holy Lands was common among Christians. The reliquary is unique because it has images identical to those found on clay tube reliquaries produced in ancient Ephesus.\n\nThe discovery of the lead tube reliquary is significant because it is one of only 43 known lead tubes from this time period, and it is believed to have originated from the pilgrimage center of Ephesus. The site of Burgos (Poros) has a rich history, with excavations revealing a complex set of fortifications, including walls, ramparts, and towers, that date back to the 4th-6th century AD.\n\nThe discovery of the lead tube reliquary is also notable because it is connected to the cult of St. John the Theologian, who was particularly cherished by early Christians. According to the Bible, John was Jesus Christ's favorite disciple, and he wrote the Book of Revelation, also known as The Apocalypse.\n\nThe discovery of the lead tube reliquary has shed new light on the history of early Christianity and the significance of pilgrimage sites in the ancient world. It is a significant find that will be studied by scholars and historians for years to come."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Expecting parents wondering which company offers the best maternity and paternity leave in the US need look no further. Netflix announced yesterday that it is upping the ante, offering new parents unlimited, fully-paid maternity and paternity leave for the first year after having or adopting a child. The streaming video company based in Los Gatos, Calif., also offers unlimited paid vacation, and it's all toward fostering \"a 'freedom and responsibility' culture that gives our employees context about our business and the freedom to make their own decisions along with the accompanying responsibility,\" writes Tawni Cranz, chief talent officer. The new policy \"deserves high marks for extending leave to fathers,\" reports Fortune, \"as well as understanding that the entire first year after childbirth can be challenging.\" It's a \"landmark perk,\" reports TechCrunch, noting that Yahoo doubled its maternity and paternity leave in 2013 (to 16 paid weeks for moms and eight paid weeks for dads) to compete with packages offered by Facebook and Google because \"the talent is growing up.\" A decade ago big company perks included free lunch and ping pong tables, but now the major moves are about helping employees balance work and home life because \"experience shows people perform better at work when they’re not worrying about home,\" Cranz adds. Of course, employees will have to plan their leave with managers and coworkers in advance to make sure their absence isn't disruptive—behaving, you know, like adults. (See who beat Netflix to the punch.)"], "length": 1112, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Netflix Introduces Unlimited Maternity and Paternity Leave Policy**\n\nIn a significant move to support its employees, Netflix has announced an unlimited maternity and paternity leave policy for all employees. The policy, which was introduced by Chief Talent Officer Tawni Cranz, allows new parents to take off as much time as they want during the first year after a child's birth or adoption. This move is part of the company's efforts to foster a \"freedom and responsibility\" culture that gives employees the flexibility and confidence to balance their work and family responsibilities.\n\nUnder the new policy, employees can return to work on a full-time or part-time basis, and can take subsequent time off later in the year if needed. Netflix will continue to pay employees normally, eliminating the hassle of switching to disability leave. This move is seen as a landmark perk that demonstrates the company's commitment to supporting its employees and attracting top talent.\n\nThe policy is particularly notable for its extension to fathers, who often face significant challenges in balancing work and family responsibilities. By offering unlimited leave, Netflix is recognizing the importance of supporting new parents and allowing them to focus on their families without worrying about work or finances.\n\nWhile the policy has received widespread praise, it is not without its challenges. Unlimited time off policies can require employees to work with their managers and teams ahead of time to ensure that their absence does not impact work projects or deadlines. Additionally, extended absences can strain workplace relations.\n\nOverall, Netflix's introduction of an unlimited maternity and paternity leave policy is a significant step forward in supporting its employees and promoting a better work-life balance. The company's commitment to flexibility and responsibility is likely to be a major draw for top talent in the industry."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A Nazi drinking game played by students from the London School of Economics sparked a brawl and ended with a broken nose, reports the Guardian. The row occurred during a ski trip taken by 150 members of the school's athletic union to Val D'Isere in France. The confrontation occurred after a Jewish student objected to the drinking game, according to the school's Jewish Society. The game involved playing cards arranged on a table in the shape of a swastika. Players were required to \"salute the führer,\" according to the student newspaper. \"Nazi glorification and anti-semitism have no place in our universities,\" said the head of the Jewish Society. A video of the drinking game was briefly posted on Facebook. The school is investigating. \"These are disturbing allegations relating to events which took place on a foreign trip organized by the students' union,\" said a school statement, which added that officials are \"prepared to take disciplinary action. Students must abide by clear standards of behavior. We do not tolerate anti-semitism or any other form of racism.\""], "length": 803, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**News Summary**\n\nIn recent developments, two separate incidents have sparked concerns about racism and antisemitism on university campuses.\n\nAt the London School of Economics (LSE), allegations have surfaced that a Nazi-themed drinking game led to a brawl in which a Jewish student's nose was broken. The incident occurred during a skiing trip organized by the students' union, attended by 150 students from the university's athletics union. According to reports, the game involved playing cards arranged in the shape of a swastika and required players to \"salute the Führer.\" The LSE has launched an investigation into the allegations, with both the students' union and the university taking disciplinary action if proven true.\n\nThe LSE has stated that it does not tolerate antisemitism or any other form of racism, and has emphasized that students must abide by clear standards of behavior. The university's Jewish society has also condemned the incident, with its president, Jay Stoll, calling for a collective attack on the Jewish community.\n\nMeanwhile, a similar incident has been reported at Oxford University, where members of the Conservative Association were accused of singing an antisemitic song with Nazi references. This incident has raised concerns about the prevalence of racism and antisemitism on university campuses, and highlights the need for greater awareness and action to address these issues.\n\nIn a separate development, Alexa Internet has been donating its crawl data to the Internet Archive, with the data being added to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period. This move aims to preserve the internet's history and make it more accessible to researchers and the public.\n\nOverall, these incidents highlight the need for universities and institutions to take a strong stance against racism and antisemitism, and to promote a culture of inclusivity and respect."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A Kansas City couple who went to check out a car they saw on Craigslist were shot and robbed Wednesday night, USA Today reports. They had wanted to purchase the vehicle for their 16-year-old son, who was with them but not injured. Police say the 53-year-old dad and his son had test-driven a 2007 Hyundai Sonata and were discussing payment when the seller said he had to call his wife and momentarily walked away, according to KCTV. That’s when two other men reportedly approached the family and demanded cash, then started shooting. As the dad tried to keep his wife and son out of the barrage of bullets, he was shot four times in the chest, stomach, and legs, while his 55-year-old wife took a bullet to her upper right arm, which was “shattered,” reports the TV station. The men took the wife's purse, which had $3,000 in cash for the car purchase, say police. Authorities believe the incident may be related to an earlier armed robbery that day in which a Kansas City man says he tried to sell his Xbox and gaming accessories to a man who saw his ad on Craigslist, reports the Kansas City Star. As the potential buyer was examining the wares, two other men reportedly arrived sporting a gun; the three men then lifted the Xbox and other items and ran into nearby woods, police say."], "length": 1819, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Double Robbery and Shooting in Kansas City Leaves Family of Four Injured**\n\nA brazen double robbery and shooting occurred in Kansas City, leaving a family of four injured. The incident involved a couple and their 16-year-old son who were attempting to purchase a 2007 Hyundai Sonata from a Craigslist ad. The couple, whose identities have not been released, was shot and robbed of $3,000 after two men pulled guns on them. The 53-year-old father suffered four wounds to his chest, stomach, and legs, while the 55-year-old mother's right upper arm was shattered by a single bullet.\n\nThe shooting may be connected to an earlier armed robbery of a man who answered a Craigslist ad for an Xbox. Police are investigating both incidents, which occurred within two miles of each other. The suspects, described as three black men in their late 20s or early 30s, are still at large.\n\nFour people, including the suspects, are currently being questioned by police. The family's 16-year-old son was not injured in the shooting. The couple's injuries were described as devastating, with the father undergoing surgery overnight.\n\nA reward of up to $7,000 is now being offered for information leading to an arrest in the case. Liberty-based Ferrellgas has contributed $5,000 towards the reward, and police are urging anyone with information to come forward. The incident serves as a reminder to exercise caution when meeting strangers online and to prioritize safety.\n\nThe community has rallied around the injured family, with some residents expressing outrage and concern for the couple's well-being. The incident has also sparked a call to action for online safety, with police advising people to use caution when meeting strangers online and to report any suspicious activity."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Secretive data companies are tracking almost every American's every move online—and compiling and selling disturbingly targeted lists based on that spying, a new Senate Commerce Committee report concludes. That includes lists of rape victims, people suffering from ailments including HIV, AIDS, and dementia, and people with substance abuse problems, a privacy group said in a hearing yesterday, as per CNN. Mailing addresses for police officers and domestic violence shelters—the latter of which are usually protected by law—are also for sale, as are lists based on demographics and economics, including \"Ethnic Second-City Strugglers,\" and \"Rural and Barely Making It.\" The World Privacy Forum urged Congress to act to \"remove unsafe, unfair, and overall just deplorable lists from circulation.\" The Direct Marketing Association released a statement saying that while the lists are occasionally \"used to disparage certain groups,\" they represented a \"tiny minority\" of marketing products. Overall the committee looked at nine companies, some of which refused to explain how they got their data or who they sold it to, Gawker reports. One company admitted it had sold social security numbers and banking information to an alleged identity theft ring."], "length": 1392, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Multibillion-Dollar Data Industry Under Scrutiny**\n\nA recent Senate Commerce Committee report has shed light on the multibillion-dollar data industry, revealing that major data companies are selling deeply personal information to marketers and other third parties. The report, which was based on testimony from nine major data companies and privacy groups, found that these companies collectively own an astounding number of consumer profiles and are selling sensitive information, including lists of rape victims, people with HIV and AIDS, and those suffering from conditions such as cancer and depression.\n\nThe report highlights the lack of transparency and oversight in the data industry, with some companies refusing to explain how they collect and sell data, citing confidentiality clauses in their contracts. Equifax, for example, told the Committee that it had information on specific consumer behaviors, such as whether they purchased a particular soft drink or shampoo product in the last six months.\n\nPrivacy groups have long warned about the dangers of the data industry, and a recent Senate committee hearing brought attention to the issue. The World Privacy Forum, a nonprofit organization, uncovered lists of sensitive information, including the home addresses of police officers and the mailing lists of domestic violence shelters. The organization's executive director, Pam Dixon, called for increased government regulations to protect individual privacy.\n\nThe data industry is estimated to be worth $156 billion, and the lack of transparency and oversight has raised concerns about the potential for harm to consumers. The Senate committee has vowed to continue pushing for information on how data brokers get their data and who they sell it to, and the Federal Trade Commission has also called for increased transparency into data practices.\n\nOverall, the report and hearing highlight the need for greater regulation and oversight of the data industry to protect individual privacy and prevent harm to consumers."}
