he field of astronomy includes the discovery of a new planet, dubbed \"Planet X,\" which is thought to be a gas giant that orbits the sun at a distance of around 10,000 to 20,000 years. The discovery was made using mathematical and computer modeling, and is still theoretical, with no direct observations of the planet yet.\n\nAdditionally, scientists have been searching for signs of alien life in the universe, with some estimates suggesting that there could be up to 1 billion \"Earths\" in the Milky Way galaxy alone. The search for extrater"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Troops loyal to Moammar Gadhafi massacred some 150 detainees last week, according to volunteers who cleared charred bodies from the warehouse that had apparently served as the detainees’ prison. An escaped prisoner told his story to CNN: He and his brother were handcuffed and blindfolded for no apparent reason while out walking earlier this month. They were thrown into the warehouse with 60 others ages 17 to 70. The number inside quickly reached 175 as loyalists continued to imprison people. The detainees were given no food or water for days, said Muneer Own. Finally, the troops said they would set them free—but instead hurled a grenade at them and opened fire. About 25 people were able to escape, Own said; his brother apparently was not one of them. The mass of bodies was discovered when rebels eventually took over the area. Many remain unidentified, part of a growing number of prisoners whose whereabouts remain unknown, notes the Washington Post. Thousands—some of whom have been jailed for decades—could be in underground prisons or mass graves, rebels fear. Click through for one family’s story."], "length": 1196, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Libya: Rebel Forces Continue Search for Missing Fighters and Prisoners**\n\nThe ongoing conflict in Libya has led to a humanitarian crisis, with thousands of people missing, including rebel fighters and prisoners. According to estimates, nearly 60,000 Libyans have disappeared during the six-month-long conflict, with many held in underground prisons or mass graves.\n\nRebel leaders have opened prisons in Tripoli, freeing thousands of prisoners, but only about 10,000 have been accounted for. The search for missing fighters and prisoners continues, with families and friends left to fear the worst.\n\nOne family's wait has ended after Abdul Rauf, a 25-year-old rebel fighter, was released from the notorious Abu Salim prison in Tripoli. Rauf was held for 48 days and subjected to brutal treatment, including being beaten and jabbed with electric prods. He was reunited with his family on Sunday, and his story has brought hope to the broken men who were held in the prison.\n\nMeanwhile, a harrowing story has emerged of Muneer Masoud Own, 33, who was detained by forces loyal to longtime Libyan leader Moammar Gadhafi. Own was held in a warehouse with about 60 others, where they were subjected to abuse, deprivation, and eventually killed. The warehouse was found to contain nearly 150 charred bodies, with many unrecognizable.\n\nThe situation in Libya remains dire, with many families left without answers to the fate of their loved ones. The international community is calling for an end to the violence and a thorough investigation into the disappearances and killings.\n\nAs the conflict continues, the need for humanitarian aid and support for the people of Libya grows. The situation is a stark reminder of the human cost of war and the importance of protecting human rights and dignity."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Losing weight is one thing, keeping it off quite another. And now researchers are finding that among those with coronary heart disease (CHD)—which the Mayo Clinic reports develops from damaged or diseased blood vessels typically caused by blockage and inflammation—yo-yo dieting may dramatically increase the risk of stroke, heart attack, and death. Reporting in the New England Journal of Medicine, researchers say they followed nearly 10,000 men and women with CHD between the ages of 35 and 75 for about five years, monitoring changes in body weight and other health outcomes. The takeaway? \"It's important to lose weight, but this data says you have to keep it off,\" lead researcher Dr. Sripal Bangalore at the New York University School of Medicine tells the New York Times. The highest weight fluctuation was on average 8.6 pounds over five years, while the smallest body weight changes averaged closer to two pounds, reports Medical News Today. Those who were already overweight or obese at the beginning of the study and who experienced the highest level of weight fluctuation experienced 124% more deaths, 136% more strokes, and 117% more heart attacks, meaning these CHD patients were more than twice as likely to suffer these negative outcomes in just a few years than people with CHD whose weight only fluctuated two pounds. This doesn't pinpoint a cause, as pre-existing heart problems could be the culprit, but researchers hope to study this correlation further. (Yo-yo dieting is bad for women's hearts in general.)"], "length": 1368, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Study Suggests Yo-Yo Dieting May Increase Risk of Deadly Heart Attacks and Strokes**\n\nA recent study published in The New England Journal of Medicine has found that rapidly losing and regaining weight, also known as \"yo-yo\" dieting, may increase the risk of deadly heart attacks and strokes in people with coronary artery disease. The study, which followed 9,509 men and women with stable coronary artery disease over a period of five years, found that those who experienced the greatest fluctuations in weight were at a significantly higher risk of heart attack, stroke, and death.\n\nThe study's lead author, Dr. Sripal Bangalore, notes that while it's still important to lose weight, the data suggests that keeping weight off is crucial. \"Many times people are motivated until they lose the weight, and then they feel they can take it easy,\" he said. \"Maybe this information can be used as motivation to keep the weight off.\"\n\nCoronary artery disease, also known as coronary heart disease, is the most common form of heart disease among men and women in the United States, killing over 370,000 people each year. The condition is characterized by the buildup of plaque in the coronary arteries, which can lead to angina, shortness of breath, or other symptoms.\n\nThe study's findings are consistent with previous research that has associated yo-yo dieting with increased risk of poor heart health. Researchers have found that yo-yo dieting may have serious health implications for people with coronary heart disease, including a higher risk of heart attack, stroke, and death.\n\nThe study's authors emphasize that their findings are observational and do not prove cause-and-effect between yo-yo dieting and increased risk of heart attack, stroke, and death. However, they do suggest that weight fluctuation may be a concern for people with coronary heart disease, and that further investigation is needed to understand the relationship between weight changes and heart health.\n\nOverall, the study's findings highlight the importance of maintaining a healthy weight and lifestyle to reduce the risk of heart disease and other serious health conditions."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A Texas woman was caught on surveillance video stealing a wedding ring off the finger of a dead woman Friday, the Odessa American reports. The body of Lois Hicks, 88, was in an open casket at Odessa's Sunset Funeral Home. The woman's family had just left the funeral home after a visitation service when a stranger parked outside, came in, and asked to use the restroom. Instead, the funeral home's manager says, she walked into the room with Hicks' coffin, grabbed the ring off her finger, and left. Hicks' daughter says some of her mother's skin was actually torn off during the theft. \"I can't believe someone would be that low,\" she says. The surveillance video captured the alleged thief's license plate, and the family is pressing charges. The incident is being investigated as a felony, KOSA reports. The suspect is described as a heavy-set white woman who was wearing a brown long-sleeved sweater, black sweat pants, and black sandals at the time; she drove off in a red or maroon four-door Saturn."], "length": 989, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Breaking News: Theft of Wedding Ring from Deceased Woman at Odessa Funeral Home**\n\nA shocking incident occurred at the Sunset Memorial Gardens & Funeral Home in Odessa, Texas, where an 88-year-old woman's wedding ring was stolen from her body during a visitation service. The theft, which took place on Friday, was caught on video surveillance and has left the family and authorities stunned.\n\nAccording to eyewitnesses, a woman, described as a heavy-set white female wearing a brown long-sleeve sweater, black sweatpants, and black sandals, walked into the funeral home and stole the ring from the deceased woman's left hand. The thief also tore off part of the skin from the woman's finger.\n\nThe family of the deceased woman, Lois Hicks, is still grieving and is asking for anyone with information about the theft to come forward. The Odessa Police Department and Odessa Crime Stoppers are investigating the incident, and several shots of the vehicle's license plate from the video recording can be enhanced for close-ups.\n\nThis incident is not an isolated case, as jewelry theft from the dead at funeral homes is not uncommon. However, the brazen nature of this incident has left the funeral home staff and the community shocked.\n\nThe family of the deceased woman is seeking justice and is planning to press charges. Anyone with information about the woman or her whereabouts is encouraged to contact the Odessa Police Department at 432-335-5784 or Odessa Crime Stoppers at 432-333-8477.\n\nThe incident has left the community in shock, and authorities are urging anyone with information to come forward. The Odessa Police Department is working to identify the suspect and bring her to justice."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Drugs are likely to blame for the death of Finley Boyle, the 3-year-old Hawaii girl who died a month after going into cardiac arrest during a root canal at a Kailua dentist's office on Dec. 3, according to the official autopsy report. The report says the combination of sedatives and anesthesia Finley received during the procedure probably caused her death, the AP reports. The report noted that Finley was healthy, and ruled out any signs of an underlying heart condition or an allergic reaction. The medical examiner's office ruled the toddler's death an accident, and no charges have been brought against Dr. Lilly Geyer, KHON reports; however, Finley's family has filed a lawsuit claiming Finley was not monitored for 26 minutes while she was under sedation. Geyer's office has since shut down, but Geyer's attorney calls the Boyle family's allegations \"unproven.\" (Meanwhile, a young mother in Hawaii is in a coma following wisdom teeth surgery.)"], "length": 736, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Three-Year-Old Girl Dies After Dentist Visit, Autopsy Reveals Sedatives and Anesthesia Contributed to Her Death**\n\nA tragic incident involving a three-year-old girl's death after a dentist visit has come to light, with an autopsy report revealing that sedatives and anesthesia administered during the procedure likely contributed to her demise. Finley Puleo Boyle, a healthy child from Kailua, Hawaii, suffered a heart attack during a dental procedure at Dr. Lilly Geyer's office at Island Dentistry for Children on December 3, 2013. She slipped into a coma and died a month later, January 3, 2014.\n\nThe autopsy report, conducted by Honolulu Chief Medical Examiner Dr. Christopher Happy, concluded that the sedatives and local anesthesia administered during the procedure, including Demerol, hydroxyzine, and chlorohydrate, played a role in her death. The report also stated that Finley's teeth were in good shape, with no signs of underlying heart problems or allergic reactions to the sedatives and anesthetics.\n\nAccording to a lawsuit filed by Finley's family, the girl was administered five different drugs, including laughing gas and an injection of lidocaine with epinephrine, without proper monitoring. The lawsuit alleges that the girl was not checked on every five minutes, and it wasn't until 26 minutes after the administration of the sedatives that someone checked on her oxygen levels. Furthermore, the lawsuit claims that no one administered CPR, and it took someone to get a doctor from another office to provide assistance.\n\nThe medical examiner's office ultimately ruled the death an accident, and no criminal charges have been brought against Dr. Lilly Geyer. However, the incident has sparked concerns about the oversight of dental sedation in Hawaii, particularly in light of new state rules that took effect in January 2014, tightening oversight of dental sedation in the state.\n\nThe incident has also raised questions about the safety protocols in place at Dr. Geyer's office and the lack of monitoring during the procedure. The office has since closed, and Dr. Geyer's attorney has called the allegations \"unproven.\""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["OJ Simpson's big moment has arrived. A hearing is underway at which he is trying to convince members of the Nevada Board of Parole that he should go free. \"I haven't made any excuses in the nine years that I've been here and I'm not trying to make an excuse now,\" said the 70-year-old Simpson early in the proceedings, per the AP. Later, he added, \"I am sorry that things turned out the way they did. I had no intent to commit a crime.\" He also appeared to stifle a sob in his appeal to the board. Simpson has been imprisoned since 2008 over an armed robbery and kidnapping case, but he and many legal analysts think that the board will vote to release him. If so, he's expected to be out on Oct. 1. The televised hearing is being livestreamed at various sites, including CNN. Per CNN, one of the first questions posed to Simpson by a board member in regard to the robbery, in which he and other men entered a Vegas hotel room to reclaim sports memorabilia, was, \"What were you thinking?\" Simpson called it a \"big mistake\" and emphasized that he wasn't armed. \"I would never pull a gun on anybody.\" He insisted that he learned only afterward that one of the men with him, who was \"behind me,\" brandished a gun. Another board member noted that he hadn't taken an alcohol-abuse program as he promised he would at his last hearing. Other Simpson quotes: \"I'm at a point in my life where all I want to do is spend as much time with my children.\" \"I've basically spent a conflict-free life.\" Daughter Arnelle Simpson said her father is \"remorseful,\" and she wants him to come home so they can \"move forward,\" adding, \"It's been hard.\""], "length": 2613, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**O.J. Simpson Granted Parole After 9 Years in Prison**\n\nIn a recent development, O.J. Simpson, the former NFL star and convicted felon, has been granted parole after serving nine years in prison for his role in a 2007 armed robbery and kidnapping. Simpson, 70, appeared before a Nevada parole board and apologized for his actions, promising to have no conflicts if released. The board voted unanimously to grant him parole, citing his model behavior in prison and stable release plans.\n\nSimpson's release comes 22 years after he was acquitted of murder charges in the deaths of his ex-wife Nicole Brown Simpson and her friend Ronald Goldman. The case remains a highly publicized and contentious one, with many still grappling with the implications of Simpson's acquittal.\n\nThe parole hearing was widely covered by cable news shows, recalling the \"trial of the century\" and the many months of media attention surrounding Simpson's trial. Simpson's appearance before the board was met with a mix of emotions, with some expressing relief and others criticizing his lack of remorse for his actions.\n\nSimpson's release is also notable for its timing, coming as the country continues to grapple with issues of criminal justice, race, and celebrity culture. The case has been the subject of numerous documentaries and true-crime dramas, including the award-winning \"O.J.: Made in America\" and the FX series \"The People v. O.J. Simpson.\"\n\nIn other news, the Internet Archive has launched an effort to archive web pages and preserve links to ensure that they remain accessible even if the original pages are changed or taken down. The organization aims to fix all broken links on the web, making it easier for users to access and share information.\n\nAdditionally, Twitter users can now add location information to their tweets, allowing them to share their location with others. The feature can be accessed from the web or through third-party applications, and users always have the option to delete their tweet location history.\n\nOverall, these recent developments highlight the ongoing impact of O.J. Simpson's case on American culture and the importance of preserving and archiving online content."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["President Trump ripped into the \"dishonest\" and \"out of control\" media during his press conference Thursday—and it didn't take long for the media to fire back. Fox News anchor Shepard Smith was among those who criticized the president, saying he \"keeps repeating ridiculous throwaway lines that are not true at all and sort of avoiding this issue of Russia as if we're some kind of fools for asking the question,\" the Hill reports. Smith said Trump \"owes it to the American people\" to answer legitimate questions liked the ones about Russia that he dismissed as \"fake news.\" But not all the reviews were so negative. A look at coverage: Yes, he was \"combative,\" observes Mara Liasson at NPR, \"but he was also funny and charming like he was with the press during all those years in New York as a fixture in the tabloids. ... I think that he will get a lot of credit for doing this. I think it will thrill his supporters.\" The Washington Post fact-checks what it describes as 15 \"dubious claims\" from the conference, including Trump's claim that he had the \"biggest electoral college win since Reagan.\" Trump had a total of 304 electoral college votes, which ranks sixth in the eight elections since Reagan's 525-vote 1984 landslide. Only George W. Bush won with fewer than Trump. \"I was given that information,\" Trump said when a reporter challenged him on the statement. The AP takes a close look at Trump's \"I inherited a mess line\" and decides the claim itself is messy. CNN contributor Jeffrey Lord, reacting to scathing reviews: “Lord, I think we saw two different press conferences,\" he said, per Raw Story. \"From my perspective, I thought he was relaxed, he was funny, he was on point. He took the whole issue of the media, and he had a very candid conversation.\" Politico has an exhaustive list of the topics Trump covered during the 77-minute presser, ranging from nuclear holocaust to CNN, which he downgraded from \"fake news\" to \"very fake news.\" BuzzFeed has video of one of the conference's most heavily commented upon moments: when Trump asked a black reporter if she could set up a meeting between him and the Congressional Black Caucus. Presidential historians tell the AP that no president, including Nixon during the Watergate scandal, has publicly turned on the press the way Trump did. \"It was bizarre theater,\" says Rice University history professor Douglas Brinkley. \"He turned a presidential press conference into a reality-TV show in which he can be the star and browbeat anyone who objects to him with the power of his office.\" GOP strategist and CNN contributor Alice Stewart also found a few things to praise in Trump's performance. \"You can't say he's not responsive to the press,\" she tells the Las Vegas Review-Journal. \"He answered all the questions from all over the press corps and put to rest the long-standing dialogue that he shuts down certain news outlets.\" Vox lists nine moments that it suggests back up Jake Tapper's description of the press conference as \"wild\" and \"unhinged,\" including Trump's claim that \"the leaks are real\" but \"the news is fake.\" Late-night hosts approached the press conference as gleefully as a kid unwrapping Christmas presents, and the Washington Post rounds up some of their best lines. \"My guess is he did it because he's mad and he just wanted to blow off some steam,\" Jimmy Kimmel said. \"The tone of the press conference was like if your dad found a pack of cigarettes under your mattress.\" David Graham at the Atlantic looks at the continual theme of complaint in the conference and wonders: Does Trump hate his new job?"], "length": 10933, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "President Donald Trump held a 77-minute press conference on Thursday, during which he addressed various topics, including his administration's accomplishments, the media, and his stance on several issues. The press conference was marked by a mix of confidence, humor, and defensiveness, as Trump pushed back against criticism and accused the media of being dishonest.\n\nTrump began by saying that he had inherited a \"mess\" from his predecessor, Barack Obama, and that his administration was working to tackle the challenges. He also touted his poll numbers, saying that he had a 55% approval rating, and took on the media, accusing them of being \"fake news\" and \"dishonest.\"\n\nThe president also addressed the controversy surrounding his national security adviser, Michael Flynn, saying that he had fired him because Flynn had withheld information from Vice President Mike Pence. Trump denied that he had directed Flynn to discuss sanctions with the Russian ambassador, and said that the leaks about Flynn's conversations with the Russian ambassador were \"fake news.\"\n\nTrump also discussed his stance on Russia, saying that he had nothing to do with the Kremlin and that the leaks about his campaign's contacts with Russian officials were \"fake news.\" He also talked about his plans to replace Obamacare, saying that he would submit a plan to Congress by mid-March.\n\nThe press conference was marked by several tense exchanges with reporters, including a confrontation with CNN's Jim Acosta, who pressed Trump on the issue of his campaign's contacts with Russian officials. Trump responded by saying that Acosta was \"not a good reporter\" and that he was \"trying to be a little bit confrontational.\"\n\nDespite the tension, Trump also showed a more relaxed side, joking with reporters and displaying a sense of humor. He also took questions from several reporters, including a question about his stance on anti-Semitism, which he responded to by saying that he was \"not a bad person\" and that the tone of the media was \"such hatred.\"\n\nOverall, the press conference was a defining moment for Trump's presidency, as he sought to re-energize his base and push back against criticism from the media. While the event was marked by several tense exchanges, it also showed Trump's ability to think on his feet and respond to criticism in a confident and humorous way.\n\nKey Takeaways:\n\n* Trump said that he had inherited a \"mess\" from his predecessor, Barack Obama, and that his administration was working to tackle the challenges.\n* Trump denied that he had directed his national security adviser, Michael Flynn, to discuss sanctions with the Russian ambassador"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["NASA is reporting a glitch with one of the two cooling pumps on the International Space Station, but it says none of the six crew members are in danger, reports CNN. The crew shut down some non-essential electrical systems as a precaution while they figure what went wrong and how to fix it. Best case, it's a software glitch and a relatively easy repair. Otherwise, a spacewalk will be necessary, reports NBC News. It will likely take days or perhaps weeks to sort it all out. \"They're fine for the near future,\" says a NASA spokesman."], "length": 1568, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "The International Space Station (ISS) has experienced a cooling system malfunction, prompting NASA to scale back operations on the orbiting outpost. A pump on one of the station's two external cooling loops shut down after hitting a temperature limit, forcing teams to reroute coolant and prioritize life support systems. The crew of six, consisting of NASA astronauts and Russian cosmonauts, were never in any danger, but the situation required a cutback in normal operations.\n\nExperts suspect a malfunctioning valve may have caused the problem, and mission managers are trying to determine whether a software fix can get the valve working again or whether a spacewalk will be required. The station's life support system is still up and running, and the crew has been working to keep the freezers going.\n\nNASA has not yet decided whether to perform an emergency spacewalk to fix the failed pump. The decision may take a couple of days, and officials are considering the resources available and the urgency of the problem. The ISS has been continuously occupied since 2000, and the current mission is scheduled to go until March 2014.\n\nThe malfunction is one of the \"Big 14\" maintenance issues that NASA and its partners expect to face on an occasional basis during space station operations. The ISS has a history of experiencing cooling system issues, with previous problems requiring spacewalks to fix. However, Russian spacewalks have proceeded without problems, and NASA has made significant progress in resolving the spacesuit issue that Italian astronaut Luca Parmitano faced.\n\nThe space station's external cooling loops are systems that circulate ammonia outside the station to keep equipment cool. The malfunctioning loop was producing too much ammonia, forcing teams to shut down some noncritical systems. The space station's life support system is still up and running, and the crew has been working to mitigate the effects of the malfunction.\n\nOverall, the ISS has experienced a minor cooling system malfunction, but the situation is being closely monitored, and NASA is taking steps to resolve the issue without putting the crew in danger."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["It's one of the most famous weather forecasts of the modern era, and it came out exactly 13 years ago to the day. That would be Aug. 28, 2005, one day before Hurricane Katrina devastated New Orleans. National Weather Service meteorologist Robert Ricks issued a forecast so dire that some news agencies thought it was a hoax, writes meteorologist James Spann in a tweet commemorating the Ricks forecast. As it turns out, Ricks was spot on. \"Most of the area will be uninhabitable for weeks…perhaps longer,\" he wrote. \"At least one half of well constructed homes will have roof and wall failure. All gabled roofs will fail…leaving those homes severely damaged or destroyed.\" And this: \"Water shortages will make human suffering incredible by modern standards.\" (Spann's tweet includes the full text.) The forecast has been credited with saving lives, and it is now in the Smithsonian Museum of American History, as noted by Slate on the 10-year anniversary. Ricks told Brian Williams of NBC News on Sept. 15, 2005, that he went through the warning line by line to verify each doomsday-sounding statement before making it public. He ended up removing nothing. \"I would much rather have been wrong in this one,\" he told Williams. \"I would much rather be talking to you and taking the heat and crying wolf. But our local expertise said otherwise. You know, 'Hey, let's gear up for the big one, this is going to be the big one.'\" (Read about what JJ Watt's crowdsourced fundraiser accomplished in the wake of Hurricane Harvey.)"], "length": 1046, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Historic Weather Forecast and Hurricane Katrina Reflections**\n\nA decade ago, National Weather Service meteorologist Robert Ricks issued a groundbreaking weather forecast that would become one of the most dire and effective ever issued by the National Weather Service. On August 28, 2005, Hurricane Katrina was still a day away, and Ricks knew he had a crucial job to do. Using computers, history, and his fellow forecasters, he crafted a special message that warned of unprecedented destruction, predicting that \"MOST OF THE AREA WILL BE UNINHABITABLE FOR WEEKS. PERHAPS LONGER.\"\n\nRicks' forecast, which included warnings of roof and wall failure, snapped or uprooted trees, and water shortages, was remarkably accurate. The storm made landfall on August 29, causing catastrophic damage and flooding in New Orleans. Ricks' forecast was instrumental in saving lives, but it also came with a heavy emotional toll.\n\nIn a recent interview, Ricks reflected on the experience, saying, \"I would much rather have been wrong in this one. I would much rather be talking to you and taking the heat and crying wolf. But our local expertise said otherwise.\" Ricks' forecast was validated by the devastating images of the storm's aftermath, which showed widespread destruction and human suffering.\n\nThe National Weather Service has since encouraged meteorologists to use bold statements in emergency weather forecasts, as seen in Ricks' example. The agency's efforts to increase the effectiveness of weather communications have been recognized, with meteorologist Gary Szatkowski earning hero status for his \"personal plea\" during Hurricane Sandy's New York City-area landfall in 2012.\n\nToday, Ricks is back at work in New Orleans, alongside co-workers who have no homes and are wearing the clothes they wore that day. The experience has left a lasting impact on him, but he remains committed to his work, saying, \"I would much rather be talking to you and taking the heat and crying wolf. But our local expertise said otherwise.\""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Twitter has promised to take a look at its policies after some sickening abuse over her father's death forced Zelda Williams off the site. After she tweeted a touching tribute to her father, the grieving daughter received messages on Twitter and Instagram blaming her for her father's death, as well as images of her father Photoshopped to show bruises around his neck, Forbes reports. \"We will not tolerate abuse of this nature on Twitter,\" a Twitter exec said in a statement. \"We have suspended a number of accounts related to this issue for violating our rules and we are in the process of evaluating how we can further improve our policies to better handle tragic situations like this one.\" Twitter says the changes will include \"expanding our policies regarding self-harm and private information, and improving support for family members of deceased users.\" An activist who has worked with Facebook to help protect people facing online abuse says the site needs to do more to tackle persistent abusers. \"While I am truly sorry for what the Williams family is experiencing during this time, I am concerned that it takes an event like this to bring heightened attention to a problem that so many face every day,\" she tells the Washington Post. \"As is often the case, it is Williams, the target of abuse, and not her abusers, who's left Twitter,\" she adds."], "length": 1200, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Twitter Takes Steps to Address Harassment After Robin Williams' Daughter is Bullied Off the Platform**\n\nIn a recent incident, Twitter faced backlash after its users bullied Robin Williams' daughter, Zelda Williams, off the platform following her father's death. The bullying, which included messages blaming her for her father's suicide and pictures altered to show bruises around his neck, led to Zelda Williams' decision to stay off Twitter and Instagram for a \"good long time.\" Twitter responded by suspending accounts related to the abuse and promising to update its policies to better handle tragic situations like this one.\n\nHowever, many users, including Imani Gandy, a senior legal analyst at RH Reality Check, pointed out that this incident is just a small part of the larger problem of harassment on the platform. Gandy shared her own experiences of facing constant abuse due to her race, gender, and medical condition, and noted that Twitter's tools, such as blocking accounts and reporting abuse, often do not effectively address the issue.\n\nTwitter's own statement acknowledged that the company has a long way to go in addressing the problem, and that it will be expanding its policies regarding self-harm and private information, and improving support for family members of deceased users. However, critics, including media critic and feminist activist Soraya Chemaly, argue that Twitter's commitment to free expression may be clouding its judgment on this issue.\n\nIn response to the criticism, some users are advocating for more robust protections on the platform. One such solution is the \"Block Together\" program, a free third-party software that allows users to automatically block new accounts that send them direct messages and share their list of blocked accounts with friends.\n\nThe incident has sparked a wider conversation about the need for greater protections on social media platforms to prevent harassment and abuse. As one user noted, \"if Twitter doesn't take steps to protect the character of its community, everyone loses.\""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Australian DJs Michael Christian and Mel Greig have broken their silence on their royal prank call gone horribly wrong, reports the Guardian, tearfully telling an interviewer that they're \"shattered, gutted, heartbroken\" over the death of nurse Jacintha Saldanha. \"No one could've imagined this to happen,\" Christian said. \"Naturally, we're shattered. We're people, too.\" \"There's not a minute that goes by that I don't think about what (Saldanha's) family is going through, and the thought that we may have contributed to it is gut-wrenching,\" says Greig. The DJs meant no harm, adds Christian: \"At every single point it was innocent on our behalf.\" Innocent or not, Greig and Christian \"will not return to the airwaves until further notice,\" says parent company Southern Cross Austereo, which has also axed their show, halted all prank calls, and nixed ads amid an internal review, CNN reports. But the station is doubling down on its decision to air the prank, saying that it had tried to contact King Edward VII's Hospital no fewer than five times to talk about the recording before airing it. \"It is absolutely true to say that we actually did attempt to contact those people on multiple occasions,\" says Southern Cross' CEO. It's not clear if the hospital responded, notes the AFP, but it has mounted a withering assault against the station in the aftermath of Saldanha's death."], "length": 3531, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A prank call made by Australian radio hosts Mel Greig and Michael Christian to a London hospital treating Prince William's wife Kate, Duchess of Cambridge, has led to a global outcry and the death of a nurse who took the call. The call, which was broadcast on air, was made under the guise of Queen Elizabeth II and Prince Charles, and resulted in the nurse, Jacintha Saldanha, being subjected to a series of personal and intimate questions.\n\nSaldanha, 46, was found dead at her home in London on Friday, and her death has been ruled as unexplained. The police are treating the case as a possible suicide, but an inquest is still pending. The Australian radio station 2Day FM, which broadcast the call, has apologized for the incident and suspended all prank calls.\n\nThe DJs, who were impersonating the royal couple, have expressed their deep regret for the death and have apologized to Saldanha's family. They have also undergone counseling and have been taken off the air until further notice. The hospital where Saldanha worked has also condemned the prank call, with the chairman describing it as \"truly appalling\".\n\nThe incident has sparked a global debate about the ethics of prank calls and the responsibility of media outlets to respect the privacy of their subjects. The Australian Communications and Media Authority has announced that it will be investigating the incident, and the police are also looking into the matter.\n\nThe Duchess of Cambridge and her husband, Prince William, have also been affected by the incident, with the palace expressing their sadness and support for Saldanha's family. The incident has also led to a backlash against the radio station, with several advertisers pulling their ads and the Coles supermarket chain removing its advertising from the station.\n\nThe incident serves as a reminder of the importance of respecting the privacy and dignity of individuals, particularly in the context of media and entertainment. It highlights the need for greater accountability and responsibility among media outlets and the importance of considering the potential consequences of their actions."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A 12-year-old was killed and two other boys were injured after a chain link fence electrified by a live underground wire electrocuted them at a city park in Georgia. During football practice at Fleming park in Augusta on Monday, Melquan Robinson hopped a fence to retrieve a ball, WRDW reports, citing the incident report that says when he \"went to come back over the fence, he was electrocuted.\" The seventh-grader was pronounced dead later at Children's Hospital of Georgia, according to the Augusta Chronicle. Two other boys who tried to help their friend were injured and hospitalized, according to reports. The mother of one of them, David Sette, tells WRDW that her son is improving. Per the Chronicle, an adult also was injured in the incident. \"We lost a future leader. We lost a future athlete. We lost a child in our city,\" Melquan's former coach, acting as a spokesman for the bereaved family, tells the Chronicle. The oldest of three children, Melquan loved his family and was very protective of his siblings, he says. Multiple investigations into the incident are reportedly underway, with city officials looking into how the fence became electrified. Georgia Power, offering \"condolences and sympathy,\" said in a statement that the utility \"does not control or maintain the voltage\" that caused the electrocution. Melquan's family will hold a vigil Thursday at the Bernie Ward Community Center. \"This young man was where he needed to be,\" his former coach says, per WRDW. \"He was doing what he loved.\" (A medieval re-enactor was impaled in a freak accident.)"], "length": 2050, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Tragedy struck in Augusta, Georgia, when a 12-year-old boy, Melquan Kwame Robinson, died after being electrocuted while trying to retrieve a football that had gone over a fence at the Fleming Athletic Complex. The incident occurred during football practice on Monday evening, and three other people were also injured, including two of Melquan's friends who tried to help him.\n\nMelquan, a seventh-grader at Morgan Road Middle School, was pronounced dead at 9:56 p.m. at the Children's Hospital of Georgia. His body will be taken to the Georgia Bureau of Investigation lab for an autopsy. The city of Augusta, which owns and operates the community center and athletic complex, has launched investigations into the incident.\n\nAccording to Don Clark, Melquan's former coach and a family friend, the boy was very protective of his siblings and loved his family. Clark praised the courage shown by the two other children who were injured trying to save their friend, saying they were motivated by a desire to help their friend.\n\nThe incident has sent shockwaves through the community, with many parents and teammates expressing their condolences to the Robinson family. A vigil will be held at Bernie Ward Community Center on Thursday at 6 p.m. to honor Melquan's memory.\n\nGeorgia Power has released a statement saying it will work with local officials to conduct the investigation, and the company's preliminary findings indicate that it does not control or maintain the voltage that energized the fences at the complex.\n\nThe incident has also raised concerns about the safety of the athletic complex, with some calling for increased measures to prevent similar incidents in the future. The city of Augusta has canceled all football activities for the rest of the week at Fleming Park.\n\nThe Robinson family is still grieving, and their friends and teammates are coming together to support them. As one of Melquan's friends said, \"This is exactly one of those tests, that as a city and a community, we all need to stand up and move forward toward the challenge.\""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["She wasn't a Target employee, but a woman who sure seemed like one allegedly made off with around $40,000 worth of iPhones from a Virginia store. NBC Washington reports Fairfax County cops are looking for the retail impostor, who they say donned attire resembling a worker's getup, waltzed into the stockroom of the Alexandria location with a box, and loaded the box with dozens of iPhones before taking off. WTOP reports the woman, whose image was caught on tape, seemed to be familiar with how things worked at the store, including employee hours and where the iPhones were stored. Police say the theft occurred March 15, but posted about it on Facebook Monday with a call to \"help us nab an iPhone thief.\" (Target recently had a Boston problem.)"], "length": 438, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Breaking News: Woman Impersonates Target Employee, Steals $40,000 Worth of iPhones**\n\nIn a shocking incident, a woman is suspected of impersonating a Target employee and stealing over $40,000 worth of iPhones from a store in Alexandria, Virginia. The incident occurred on March 15, when the woman, who was familiar with store procedures and employee hours, walked into the 6600 Richmond Highway location and made her way to the stockroom.\n\nAccording to surveillance footage, the woman gained access to the stockroom and took the iPhones, placing them in a box before leaving the store. The suspect was seen getting into a Volvo station wagon and was identified as a woman who is not affiliated with the store.\n\nDetectives from Fairfax County police are currently searching for the woman and have released surveillance footage of her leaving the store. The police are urging anyone with information about the case to come forward and can be reached at 703-691-2131.\n\nThe incident highlights the importance of store security and employee training to prevent such incidents. The police are also investigating how the woman gained access to the store and what measures can be taken to prevent similar incidents in the future.\n\nThe total value of the stolen iPhones is estimated to be over $40,000, and the investigation is ongoing. The public is advised to be vigilant and report any suspicious activity to the authorities."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Apparently there are places even South Park fears to tread. After getting a death threat from the jihadist website RevolutionMuslim.com, the show pulled the prophet Mohammed out of this week's episode, bleeping out all mention of his name, putting “censored” banners over parts of the episode and, where necessary, replacing him with Santa Claus in a bear suit, the Huffington Post reports. Here's the deal: last week's episode (which you can see a clip of here) mocked the taboo against showing the prophet Mohammed, having him show up first hidden inside a U-Haul, and then inside a bear costume. This week's episode was to continue that story. It's unclear whether the extra censorship is intended to appease the irony-challenged folks at RevolutionMuslim or mock them. South Park, incidentally, has tackled the issue before, and even depicted Mohammed on screen once with impunity."], "length": 258, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\nIn recent news, two distinct stories have emerged. \n\nFirstly, it has been reported that Alexa Internet has been donating its daily crawl data to the Internet Archive since 1996. This data is then added to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period, providing a vast archive of the internet's past content. This donation has been ongoing for nearly three decades, showcasing the importance of preserving internet history.\n\nOn a lighter note, a popular cartoon controversy has been making headlines. \"Family Guy\" creator Seth MacFarlane announced plans to air an episode featuring the prophet Muhammad, prompting a heated debate. Cartman and Kyle, two characters from the show \"South Park,\" find themselves at odds over the issue. The two boys engage in a cross-country chase to reach Hollywood first, with the fate of \"Family Guy\" hanging in the balance. This lighthearted yet entertaining storyline highlights the ongoing struggle between free speech and censorship in the world of entertainment."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Following North Korea's most powerful weapons test yet on Sunday, a South Korean lawmaker tells CNN his country's intelligence service believes the North has an intercontinental ballistic missile (ICBM) on the move. Things get pretty vague beyond that: He didn't know the method of transport or when the movement was noticed. But Reuters does pick up some alleged details on those fronts, via an unnamed source quoted by South Korea’s Asia Business Daily. Its source says the ICBM started moving Monday, is only being moved at night, and is headed toward the nation's west coast. Reuters notes there's some likelihood that another test could come on or around Saturday: Sept. 9 is the country's founding day holiday. CNBC notes North Korea conducted a nuke test on Sept. 9, 2016. Two more details that allegedly came from the South's National Intelligence Service briefing: word that additional tunnels have been dug out at the North's nuclear test site that would facilitate future detonations, and suspicions that the next ICBM test firing might be directed toward the North Pacific but aimed at a lower angle, which would in theory allow it to fly further than previous tests, which had more of an upward trajectory. Meanwhile, the White House tells Reuters it has given \"in-principle approval\" to a request by South Korean President Moon Jae-in to abolish a limit regarding warhead weights, a move that would strengthen the power with which it could attack the North, if necessary. Meanwhile, Vladimir Putin on Tuesday gave his take on the situation: North Korea \"will eat grass but will not give up the (nuclear) program if they don't feel safe.\""], "length": 1277, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Global Tensions Escalate as North Korea Conducts Nuclear Test and Prepares for ICBM Launch**\n\nIn recent days, North Korea has made significant strides in its nuclear program, conducting its sixth nuclear test and preparing to launch an intercontinental ballistic missile (ICBM). The test, which was the most powerful ever detonated by the rogue nation, has sparked widespread condemnation from the international community.\n\nAccording to South Korean lawmakers, North Korea is believed to be moving an ICBM, which could be capable of striking the United States. The country has test-fired several missiles this summer, including two long-range ones in July and an intermediate-range one in August that overflew the Japanese island of Hokkaido.\n\nThe South Korean Navy has conducted live-fire drills off the east coast of the Korean Peninsula to check its \"immediate operational readiness\" in response to North Korea's nuclear test. The Navy has also stated that it will \"destroy and bury\" any North Korean ships or submarines that provoke South Korea.\n\nDiplomats at the United Nations have condemned the nuclear test, with Nikki Haley, the American ambassador to the UN, urging her colleagues to adopt the strongest sanctions measures possible to stop Pyongyang's nuclear program. Haley stated that \"enough is enough\" and that North Korea has \"basically slapped everyone in the face in the international community that has asked them to stop.\"\n\nDespite the tensions, South Korean President Moon Jae-in and his American counterpart, President Donald Trump, spoke by phone on Monday and agreed to lift current restrictions on the payload weight of South Korea's ballistic missiles. However, signs of a rift between the two leaders have become apparent, with Trump criticizing South Korea for \"appeasement\" when it comes to dealing with its northern neighbor.\n\nThe situation has also led to a rift between South Korea and Japan, with Trump criticizing South Korea for not doing enough to address North Korea's nuclear threat. The two countries are set to meet at an economic summit in Vladivostok, Russia, alongside Russian President Vladimir Putin and Chinese leader Xi Jinping.\n\nChina, which has historically been North Korea's main ally, has urged for global peace and condemned North Korea's nuclear tests. However, the country has also stated that sanctions are ineffective and will not turn away from the path that will provide for its security.\n\nAs the situation continues to escalate, experts warn that North Korea is likely to launch another ICBM test in the coming days, with the country's founding day on September 9 being a likely target. The international community"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Sheriff's deputies shot and killed a 6-year-old boy Thursday in Texas, WOAI reports. Bexar County Sheriff Javier Salazar says four deputies opened fire on 30-year-old Amanda Jones, a suspected car thief, after they \"cornered\" her outside a mobile home occupied by Kameron Prescott and his family in the town of Schertz. One of the bullets went through the mobile home and hit Kameron in the abdomen. According to CNN, Salazar says deputies performed first aid on the boy, but he was pronounced dead at the hospital. Jones was also killed. \"The deputies are, of course, understandably shaken up,\" Salazar says. He says the shooting is under investigation, but in his opinion the boy's death is \"a tragic accident.\" Salazar says one deputy is \"adamant\" he saw Jones with a gun and that she threatened to shoot him. He says Jones threatened both police and Kameron's family, who didn't know her, just before deputies opened fire. But investigators haven't been able to locate a gun despite the use of a helicopter and dive team. Salazar says they have found a pipe with Jones' blood on it near the scene of the shooting. One deputy at the scene was wearing a body camera, but he blocked it with his rifle during the shooting. Meanwhile, Kameron is being remembered by those who knew him. \"Kameron was a ball of energy, happy, smart, and could strike up a conversation with anyone,” school counselor Maria Morales tells KSAT. “He also had a great sense of humor and caring heart.\""], "length": 2048, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tragic Deputy-Involved Shooting Claims Life of 6-Year-Old Boy in Texas**\n\nA devastating deputy-involved shooting occurred in Schertz, Texas, resulting in the fatal injury of a 6-year-old boy, Kameron Prescott. The incident took place at the Pecan Grove Mobile Home Park, where a woman, identified as 30-year-old Amanda Lene Jones, was being pursued by deputies after a vehicle theft report. Jones, a wanted felon with a history of car theft and other charges, was believed to be armed and threatened to shoot the deputies.\n\nAccording to Bexar County Sheriff Javier Salazar, the deputies opened fire, striking and killing Jones. Unfortunately, a stray bullet also pierced the wall of Kameron's home, striking him in the abdomen. Despite receiving first aid, Kameron was pronounced dead at a hospital.\n\nThe deputies involved in the shooting, identified as John Aguillon, George Herrera, Jesse Arias, and Johnny Longoria, have been placed on administrative leave pending an investigation by the local district attorney. Sheriff Salazar described the incident as a \"tragic accident\" and expressed his condolences to Kameron's family and community.\n\nKameron's teacher, Shanda Ince, described him as a kind and caring individual who loved everyone. School counselor Maria Morales also remembered Kameron as a \"ball of energy\" with a great sense of humor and a caring heart. The Schertz-Cibolo-Universal City Independent School District has established a fund to assist Kameron's family with expenses.\n\nThe incident has left the community in shock, with many wondering what could have been done differently to prevent the tragedy. The investigation into the shooting is ongoing, and officials are still searching for a handgun believed to be in a wooded area."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A judge in Florida has allowed a vegan mom who defied a doctor's orders to regain custody of her infant son, reports WFTV. The strange case began in June when police in Casselberry charged Sarah Markham with neglect and took away 2-week-old Caleb. The charges came after a pediatrician diagnosed Caleb as dehydrated and underweight, and told Markham to take him to the hospital immediately, recounts WKMG. Instead, Markham bought soy formula to supplement her breast milk. When she didn't show up at the hospital, police came to her house, and she told them she wanted a second doctor's opinion and stressed that she did not want her son getting formula from animal products. Today, a Seminole County judge granted her custody again with the provision that she provide a nutritionist's report in 10 days. \"There's no case, there's no abuse, there's no neglect—there's simply a doctor who has been challenged by a mother, and he didn't like it,\" says Caleb's grandfather, Bo Markham. The child has been with his grandparents since he was taken out of his mother's care, and now weighs about 17 pounds. \"He's still on a soy formula,\" says Bo Markham. The criminal neglect charges are still pending, though Sarah Markham's attorney—Mark O'Mara, who defended George Zimmerman—says he expects they will be dismissed now. (PETA wants an Indiana sheriff to feed an alleged cannibal a vegan diet.)"], "length": 1292, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A Seminole County mother, Sarah Anne Markham, was arrested in June for allegedly refusing to take her newborn son to a hospital due to her vegan stance. The baby, Caleb, was diagnosed as dehydrated and losing weight, prompting the mother to seek a second opinion from a \"natural\" or \"vegan\" doctor. However, police found her uncooperative and refused to provide information about the doctor.\n\nMarkham was charged with child neglect and was taken into custody. The baby was placed in state custody and was being treated at a hospital. A judge later threw out the charges, stating that there was no case of abuse or neglect, but rather a disagreement between the doctor and the mother.\n\nIn a surprising turn of events, Markham regained custody of her son Caleb several months later. A judge ruled that she could have custody of the child, with the provision that she provide a nutritionist's report within 10 days. Caleb was found to be thriving on a soy-based formula and was growing healthy.\n\nThe case has sparked debate about the rights of parents to make decisions about their children's care, particularly when it comes to dietary choices. Markham's lawyer, Mark O'Mara, stated that he expects the criminal case against the mother to be thrown out, citing a lack of evidence.\n\nThe case highlights the complexities of balancing parental rights with the need to protect children's health and well-being. While Markham's decision to seek a vegan diet for her son may have been motivated by a desire to provide a healthy alternative, it ultimately put the child's health at risk. The outcome of the case serves as a reminder of the importance of prioritizing a child's physical and emotional well-being above all else."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A new study says one in seven US colon cancer patients is younger than 50—raising questions about why more young people seem to be getting the disease and what can be done about it, reports HealthDay via the Philadelphia Inquirer. \"Colon cancer has traditionally been thought of as a disease of the elderly,\" says lead author Samantha Hendren. \"This study is really a wake-up call to the medical community that a relatively large number of colon cancers are occurring in people under 50.\" Using government data on almost 260,000 colon-cancer patients from 1998 to 2011, the study also says younger patients more often have advanced cancer and undergo surgery (72% compared to 63% of patients over 50). The young are also more likely to use radiation therapy (53% to 48%) and have a slight advantage in surviving for five years (68% to 67%). Younger people are more likely to have advanced colon cancer partly because they get tested after noticing symptoms (like colon blockage, bleeding, and anemia), while people over 50 are advised to begin screenings, says Hendren. \"Unfortunately, these symptoms are often ignored by the patient or doctor or ascribed to something like hemorrhoids,\" a scientist adds, per Reuters. Hendren's team conducted the study after noticing a rise in colon cancer among young people, Medical Daily reports, but that spike remains unexplained. Physical inactivity, obesity, smoking, and \"an effect in our environment\" are all possible causes, a Harvard professor says. More screenings for young people might help, but the yield would be low because under-50s are still less likely to get colon cancer. \"This would be a big and costly change,\" says Hendren, who recommends \"a lot of research\" be done first. (A new killer is expected to top cancer by 2050.)"], "length": 2316, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Colorectal Cancer Rates Rising Among Younger Adults**\n\nA recent study published in the journal Cancer has found that one in seven colon cancer patients in the US is under the age of 50. This trend is concerning, as younger patients are more likely to have advanced stage cancer, but they also live slightly longer without a cancer recurrence due to aggressive treatment.\n\nThe study, led by Dr. Samantha Hendren of the University of Michigan, analyzed data from nearly 260,000 patients diagnosed with colon cancer between 1998 and 2011. The results show that younger patients (under 50) were more likely to be diagnosed with regional or distant malignancies, which are more dangerous than localized tumors. Additionally, younger patients were more likely to undergo surgery (72%) and radiation therapy (53%) compared to older patients.\n\nDespite the advanced stage of cancer, younger patients lived slightly longer without a cancer recurrence, with a five-year survival rate of 95.1% for local tumors, 76% for regional disease, and 21.3% for distant disease. However, the study's findings raise questions about the current guidelines for screening colon cancer, which typically recommend starting at age 50.\n\nExperts warn that the increasing incidence of colon cancer among younger adults may be due to various factors, including environmental changes, lifestyle habits, and genetic predisposition. Dr. Joel Randolph Hecht, director of the gastrointestinal oncology program at the University of California Los Angeles, notes that symptoms such as bleeding, changes in bowel habits, or pain when going to the bathroom can be warning signs of colon cancer.\n\nThe study's lead author, Dr. Samantha Hendren, emphasizes the need for improved risk assessment and screening decisions for younger adults. She suggests that doctors should be on the lookout for these warning signs and consider referring patients with bowel symptoms to a gastroenterologist for a diagnostic workup.\n\nWhile the study's findings are concerning, experts caution that expanding screening guidelines to younger age groups may not be necessary at this point. Dr. Andrew Chan, an associate professor of medicine at Harvard Medical School, notes that the risk of colon cancer is still low among younger adults and that more research is needed to understand the underlying causes of this trend.\n\nOverall, the study highlights the need for increased awareness and vigilance in detecting colon cancer among younger adults, as well as a more nuanced approach to screening guidelines that takes into account individual risk factors and lifestyle habits."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["\"A man getting out of the car to go to the toilet led to the discovery of one of the most important sites in Australian pre-history,\" archaeologist Giles Hamm tells ABC News. Hamm was surveying a section of Australia's Flinders Ranges when his partner, aboriginal elder Clifford Coulthard, had to go to the bathroom. \"Nature called and Cliff walked up this creek bed into this gorge and found this amazing spring surrounded by rock art,\" Hamm says. At the time, Hamm thought the newly discovered human settlement, a site called Warratyi, was only 5,000 years old. It turns out it's closer to 49,000 years old, and that has huge ramifications for our knowledge of ancient Australian civilization, Sky News reports. Hamm and his team published a study on Warratyi this week in Nature. The discovery of Warratyi in the arid interior of the continent means humans either arrived in Australia up to 10,000 years earlier than previously believed—or they expanded across the continent at a much quicker rate, Science Alert reports. Researchers at the site found 4,300 objects, including tools, and 200 bone fragments from multiple animals, including a 5,500-pound marsupial. The bones help explain how early Australians interacted with megafauna, including hunting them. The tools show Australians were using bone and stone axes earlier than believed and are evidence that early Australians developed some technologies on their own rather than adopting them from other peoples as previously believed. (Ninety rocks in Australia could rank up there with Stonehenge.)"], "length": 5408, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Groundbreaking Discovery in Australian Archaeology**\n\nA chance discovery in the Flinders Ranges of southern Australia has revealed a human settlement dating back an astonishing 49,000 years, pushing back the known arrival of Aboriginal Australians in the arid interior by 10,000 years. The site, known as Warratyi, is a rock shelter that contains a treasure trove of artefacts and bones, including the earliest known use of ochre in Australia and evidence of human interaction with megafauna.\n\nThe discovery was made by archaeologist Giles Hamm and his team, who excavated the site over a period of nine years. The artefacts found at Warratyi include burnt eggshells, sharpened stone and bone tools, red ochre and gypsum pigments, and the team believes that these early technologies were developed locally. The site also contains bones from the extinct giant wombat-like species, Diprotodon optatum, and eggs from an ancient giant bird, suggesting that early humans were interacting with Australia's legendary megafauna.\n\nThe dating of the artefacts and fossils at Warratyi has been confirmed using a combination of methods, including quartz grain dating and radiocarbon dating. The results indicate that humans occupied the site from 49,000 to 46,000 years ago, and that they were able to adapt to the harsh conditions of the arid interior.\n\nThe discovery of Warratyi is significant not only because of its age, but also because it challenges certain assumptions about Indigenous Australians. The site provides evidence of a sophisticated and innovative culture that was able to develop and use complex technologies in a challenging environment.\n\nThe findings of the Warratyi discovery are set to rewrite the story of Indigenous Australians and their place in Australian history. The site is a major breakthrough in the field of archaeology and provides a new perspective on the history of human settlement in Australia.\n\n**Key Dates:**\n\n* 49,000 years ago: Humans occupied the Warratyi site\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Britney Spears' father, Jamie, has been in charge of her since she was placed under conservatorship in 2008, and he thinks it's about time for a raise, Radar reports. \"I continue to perform services to protect and care for Britney’s person and to administer her estate in good faith and in her best interests. I continue to have significant oversight duties relating to her visitation with her children,\" he writes in a new petition to the court. In addition to the $16,000 he gets for those duties per month, he also gets $1,200 for office rent, and he wants that amount bumped to $2,000, plus $7,200 in back pay for the increased rent he's paid since last year. Brit is apparently also under strict control when it comes to interviews. She's been promoting her upcoming Vegas residency, and her handlers told radio interviewers that during the five-minute interviews, they could only ask about Vegas or Britney's new album or single, sources tell the New York Daily News. \"So everyone is clear,\" the stations are being informed, \"no personal questions and no funny business.\" But apparently the interviews still aren't going well: One was pulled entirely after DJs deemed it \"a one-sided conversation,\" and during another, the hosts needed to ask a distracted Spears one of their questions twice."], "length": 1845, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Entertainment News**\n\nBritney Spears' handlers have strict guidelines for radio interviews, limiting questions to her Las Vegas residency, new single, and album. However, efforts to control the conversation have backfired, with some interviews turning into one-sided conversations. Spears' representatives argue that the information provided was meant to aid the radio spots, while some deejays claim that the pop star's detached demeanor is a result of her team's interference.\n\nMeanwhile, Martha Stewart discussed her dating life and cleanliness, stating that she's a \"fabulous catch\" and that dirt bothers her. She also posed with guests at the American Made Awards, keeping her distance from others.\n\nBeyoncé posted a photo of herself and Blue Ivy on Instagram, showcasing her \"Mrs. Carter\" tour. The 32-year-old mom is in New Zealand as part of her extended tour, which will conclude on December 22 at the Barclays Center in Brooklyn.\n\nCharlie Hunnam has been skipping scheduled appearances due to the backlash over pulling out of \"50 Shades of Grey.\" He was supposed to attend a benefit gala honoring FX Networks but stayed home.\n\nActors Jim Caviezel, Sylvester Stallone, and Arnold Schwarzenegger appeared together in Times Square after a screening of their film \"Escape Plan.\" Schwarzenegger was spotted eating at two different restaurants, asking to move to a balcony table due to paparazzi.\n\nElvis Duran and Eric Trump are walking across the Brooklyn Bridge to raise awareness of St. Jude Children's Research Hospital's fight against pediatric cancer.\n\nThe Off-Broadway parody \"Bayside! The Musical!\" has been extended for another month after its initial run was set to close on November 23.\n\n**Business News**\n\nBritney Spears' father, Jamie Spears, is seeking more money for his role as her conservator. Court documents reveal that he has been paid $16,000 per month for the past five years, but now wants to extend that compensation. He also claims that his rent for his office has escalated and requests reimbursement for an additional $7,200. Additionally, he asks to spend $2,000 monthly on his office space.\n\nThe court's decision to extend Jamie's control over Britney's life and estate for at least two more years means he will rake in an additional $432,000 from her estate before she has any hope of getting out of the conservatorship."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Benjamin Netanyahu today vowed to destroy Hamas' tunnels at all costs, even as Israel called up an additional 16,000 reservists—bringing the total so far to 86,000 troops. \"We are determined to complete this mission with or without a ceasefire,\" he said of the tunnels' destruction, as per Haaretz. \"Therefore I will not agree to any offer that does not allow the military to complete this important mission for the security of the people of Israel.\" He also issued a get-in-line message to two ministers who have been critical of his actions against Hamas, saying \"beware of what you're doing.\" Netanyahu's statement vis a vis the tunnels comes as ceasefire efforts are sputtering anyway, notes the AP, and on the heels of the worst day of casualties thus far in Gaza."], "length": 4889, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Gaza Conflict Escalates: Israel Presses On with Military Operation**\n\nThe Gaza conflict has entered its fourth week, with Israel continuing its military operation in the Gaza Strip despite international efforts to broker a ceasefire. The Israeli military has called up an additional 16,000 reservists to pursue its campaign, bringing the total number of reservists involved to 86,000.\n\nThe operation has resulted in significant loss of life, with over 1,300 Palestinians, mostly civilians, and more than 50 Israelis killed since July 8. The Israeli military has targeted Hamas' tunnel network, destroying dozens of tunnels and claiming to have neutralized many more.\n\nThe conflict has also seen significant damage to civilian infrastructure, with at least 116 Palestinians and three Israeli soldiers killed in heavy fighting on Wednesday. A U.N. school in the Jebaliya refugee camp was struck by tank shells, killing at least 17 people, and an Israeli airstrike hit a crowded shopping area in Gaza City, killing at least 16 people and wounding over 200.\n\nInternational leaders have condemned the violence, with the U.N. Secretary-General calling the shelling of the U.N. school \"outrageous\" and \"unjustifiable.\" The White House has also criticized the Israeli military's actions, saying that thousands of internally displaced Palestinians are not safe in U.N. designated shelters in Gaza.\n\nEgyptian officials have met with an Israeli envoy about Israel's conditions for a ceasefire, including disarming Hamas. However, Hamas has said it will only halt fire once it receives guarantees that a Gaza border blockade by Israel and Egypt will be lifted.\n\nThe Israeli military has launched over 4,000 strikes against Hamas-linked targets, including rocket launchers and mosques where it says weapons were being stored. Palestinian militants have fired over 2,600 rockets at Israel over the past three weeks.\n\nThe conflict has also seen significant humanitarian concerns, with nearly half of the Palestinians killed so far dying in their homes. The U.N. aid agency for Palestinian refugees has opened 80 of its schools to over 200,000 Palestinians fleeing the violence.\n\n**Key Developments**\n\n* Israel has called up an additional 16,000 reservists to pursue its campaign in Gaza.\n* The Israeli military has destroyed dozens of tunnels and claims to have neutralized many more.\n* At least 116 Palestinians and three Israeli soldiers have been killed in heavy fighting on Wednesday.\n* A U.N. school in the Jebaliya refugee camp was struck by tank shells, killing at least 17"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Snapchat found itself with a marketing sensation on its hands late last year when it began selling funky-looking sunglasses called Spectacles. The trick is they allow users to record quick videos and photos and send them directly to their Snapchat accounts, but the catch was they were available only through vending machines that would turn up in random locales or through a now-shuttered pop-up store in Manhattan, notes New York. Now, however, parent company Snap is selling them online at Spectacles.com for $129. Just how crazy did demand get? CNBC notes that the glasses were selling for up to $5,000 on secondary markets, adding that its own reporters stood in line for 18 hours to get them. (There's a limit of six per household for the new online purchases.) Snap has said the glasses haven't been big money-makers, but TechCrunch thinks the new move is designed to show prospective IPO investors that they could be. The vending machines will go on hiatus for awhile, but they're expected to resurface eventually."], "length": 1089, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Snap Inc. Expands Spectacles Availability, Seeks to Prove Revenue Potential**\n\nIn a significant move, Snap Inc. has made its popular camera-enabled sunglasses, Spectacles, available for purchase online in the United States. The $130 glasses, which allow users to record 10- to 30-second circular videos, were previously only available through vending machines and a store in Manhattan.\n\nThe decision to expand Spectacles availability comes as Snap prepares for its initial public offering (IPO). The company aims to demonstrate that Spectacles can generate revenue for its business, rather than just being a brand stunt. Despite initial positive response, Snap admitted in its IPO filing that the launch of Spectacles has not generated significant revenue.\n\nTo address this, Snap is now selling Spectacles directly through its website, with a purchase limit of six pairs per household. The company is also offering charging cases and cords for $49.99 and $9.99, respectively, which will no longer be sold on Amazon.\n\nThe move is seen as a strategic shift for Snap, which has been exploring various ways to monetize its hardware. By making Spectacles more widely available, the company hopes to increase user engagement and drive revenue. The circular video format pioneered by Snap is also seen as a unique selling point, giving its Snapchat app an edge over competitors like Instagram Stories.\n\nWhile the initial response to Spectacles has been positive, the company faces significant production and operating costs related to the product. Snap's CEO, Evan Spiegel, has acknowledged that the launch of Spectacles has not generated significant revenue, but the company remains optimistic about its potential.\n\nWith Spectacles now available online, users can expect to wait two to four weeks for their glasses to ship. The move marks an important milestone for Snap as it prepares for its IPO and seeks to prove the revenue potential of its innovative hardware."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The seven men accused of the 2014 hazing of a SUNY Albany student that caused his death are already in trouble with the school and the police. Now they've got a new problem on their hands: a lawsuit set to be filed by the fraternity whose name they apparently appropriated. Zeta Beta Tau's national office says the \"rogue\" group of SUNY Albany students charged in the death of 19-year-old Trevor Duffy used the frat's name without authorization and isn't linked to the actual recognized chapter on campus, NBC News reports. \"Zeta Beta Tau is retaining counsel for the purposes of filing suit against these individuals who falsely represented themselves, deceiving the public and damaging the otherwise exceptional reputation of our Fraternity,\" a fraternity statement reads, per News10. Duffy died after allegedly being made to consume nearly half a gallon of vodka in a case that made headlines; his BAC was seven times the legal limit, notes NBC. The national fraternity says its intellectual property—including its name, the ZBT initials, and other materials—are protected under copyright rules, WRGB reports. That means anyone who falsely claims to be affiliated with the frat is fair game for legal action, a move the attorney for one of the accused says he's baffled by. \"It's certainly novel, and I'm not sure why they'd take that step,\" the lawyer for Joseph Angilletta, one of two men arrested just last week in the case, tells the station. \"Clearly one aspect here is they seem to want to distance themselves from this tragic event.\" (Five Baruch College frat members may face up to 20 years behind bars for allegedly taking part in the \"glass ceiling.\")"], "length": 968, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Zeta Beta Tau Fraternity Sues Group Linked to Hazing Death**\n\nA lawsuit has been filed by the Zeta Beta Tau Fraternity against a group of University of Albany students accused of being involved in the 2014 hazing death of student Trevor Duffy. The group, which claimed to be part of the Epsilon Gamma chapter of Zeta Beta Tau, is accused of using the fraternity's name and materials without authorization.\n\nThe fraternity has stated that the group is not recognized by either the fraternity or the university, and that they falsely represented themselves as members of Zeta Beta Tau. The lawsuit claims that the group's actions damaged the fraternity's reputation and are subject to legal action.\n\nThe Zeta Beta Tau Fraternity has a long history, founded in 1898 as the world's first Jewish fraternity, with chapters on over 90 campuses across North America and more than 130,000 undergraduate and alumni brothers worldwide. The fraternity is taking legal action to protect its intellectual property, including its name, symbols, and materials, which are protected by copyright and trademark law.\n\nThe lawsuit is a response to the group's actions, which the fraternity claims were a \"tragic event\" that they want to distance themselves from. The group's lawyer, Paul DerOhannesian, has stated that the lawsuit is \"novel\" and that he is not sure why the fraternity would take such action.\n\nThe case highlights the importance of intellectual property protection and the consequences of unauthorized use of a trademark or copyright. The outcome of the lawsuit is yet to be determined, but it is clear that the Zeta Beta Tau Fraternity is taking steps to protect its reputation and intellectual property."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Kathryn Bigelow's Detroit tells the story of a black singer who takes refuge in a dingy motel to escape the 1967 Motor City riots. Based on real events, the film reunites Bigelow with screenwriter Mark Boal, both of the Oscar-winning film The Hurt Locker. It's got a strong 89% \"fresh\" rating at Rotten Tomatoes. Here's what critics are saying: \"Bigelow drills down into one of American history's most egregious cases of abuse of police power, bringing it to life with visceral detail and slowed-down meticulousness\" in what \"feels like her timeliest movie yet,\" writes Ann Hornaday at the Washington Post. It's \"daring, sophisticated and unforgettably disturbing.\" And \"in scale, scope and the space it offers for a long-awaited moral reckoning, it's nothing less than monumental.\" \"It's hard to overstate just how visceral and harrowing an experience it is,\" writes Lindsey Bahr at the AP, calling Detroit \"a well-made and evocative film\" jam-packed with \"stomach-churning horror.\" She argues there could be more nuance and perspective. But \"maybe anger is all you're supposed to feel when you step outside the theater. Maybe not feeling satisfied with Detroit is the point.\" Chris Klimek says Detroit is \"maddeningly imperfect but still honorable,\" citing \"lightly fictionalized\" aspects of the otherwise true story that set it up \"for maximum outrage.\" This is \"messy work, even when it’s done in good faith,\" he writes at NPR. He applauds John Boyega, previously of The Force Awakens. Radiating a \"Denzel-like calm,\" he shows \"he's a bonafide movie star,\" Klimek writes. Peter Howell's main gripe is that the film \"tries to tell us everything about the circumstances without telling us much about the people.\" But Detroit still makes for \"urgent viewing,\" he writes at the Toronto Star. Why? \"The injustice and anger behind it all feels like current reality, even a half-century on,\" when \"a person's skin can still determine everything from employment opportunities to treatment by police.\""], "length": 3798, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "The recent news articles discuss the film \"Detroit,\" directed by Kathryn Bigelow, which tells the story of the 1967 Algiers Motel incident in Detroit, where three young Black men were killed by police. The film is a powerful and visceral portrayal of the events, but it has also been criticized for its handling of the story and its lack of nuance.\n\nThe film opens with a semi-animated sequence that sets the stage for the events of the story, which begins with a police raid on an unlicensed nightclub that sparks a riot. The story then follows a group of characters, including a rising R&B singer, a private security guard, and a Vietnam vet, as they become embroiled in the violence and chaos of the night.\n\nThe film has been praised for its immersive and realistic portrayal of the events, but it has also been criticized for its lack of character development and its failure to provide a clear resolution to the story. Some critics have also argued that the film's depiction of the police officers is too sympathetic, and that it fails to fully condemn their actions.\n\nDespite these criticisms, the film has been widely praised for its powerful and thought-provoking portrayal of the events. It has been described as a \"harrowing\" and \"visceral\" experience, and it has sparked important conversations about the ongoing struggles of Black Americans and the need for greater accountability and justice.\n\nThe film's director, Kathryn Bigelow, has been praised for her ability to create a sense of immersion and realism, and for her willingness to tackle difficult and complex subjects. However, some critics have argued that she has a tendency to alienate viewers with her quasi-journalistic style, and that she can be too focused on conveying the facts of the story rather than providing a clear narrative or emotional resonance.\n\nOverall, \"Detroit\" is a powerful and thought-provoking film that is sure to spark important conversations and debates. While it may not be perfect, it is a necessary and important work that sheds light on a dark and painful chapter in American history.\n\nIn terms of the broader cultural context, the film is part of a larger conversation about police brutality and systemic racism in the United States. It is a reminder that the struggles of Black Americans are ongoing, and that there is still much work to be done to achieve true equality and justice.\n\nThe film's release has also sparked a wider conversation about the role of art in addressing social justice issues. It highlights the importance of artists and filmmakers in using their platforms to raise awareness and spark change,"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Elon Musk wasn't blowing smoke. The BBC reports on a three-sentence statement issued by six of Tesla's nine board members saying the board had \"met several times over the last week\" to discuss Musk's proposal to take the company private, which he announced via an unexpected tweet Tuesday. The talks \"included discussion as to how being private could better serve Tesla's long-term interests\" and the statement ends by saying the board \"is taking the appropriate next steps to evaluate this.\" The three board members who did not sign the statement are Musk, brother Kimbal Musk, and a board member who is on leave, reports the Wall Street Journal. At MarketWatch, Steve Goldstein flags some unresolved points: The statement didn't elaborate on Musk's claim of \"funding secured\" and raised but didn't answer a question of timing. \"'Last week' predates Tesla's filing on Monday of its 10-Q with the Securities and Exchange Commission, which didn't mention such discussions. News of a takeover would certainly be considered material,\" he writes. Shares of Tesla are currently down about 1%."], "length": 1589, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tesla's Bid to Go Private Raises Questions and Concerns**\n\nTesla's board of directors has confirmed that it will consider Elon Musk's proposal to take the company private, following a series of tweets by Musk that sparked a surge in the company's stock price. The proposal, which would value the company at over $72 billion, has raised questions about the timing, funding, and potential impact on existing investors.\n\nAccording to a statement from several board members, the discussions about going private began last week, with the board meeting several times to discuss the potential benefits and challenges of being a private company. However, the statement did not provide any indication that the board was satisfied with Musk's method or timing for announcing the approach.\n\nMusk's tweet on Tuesday, in which he claimed to have secured funding for the deal, was met with skepticism by regulators and investors. The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) has inquired about the issue, and some have questioned whether Musk's announcement was irregular and potentially misleading.\n\nThe proposal would make Tesla the largest buyout in history, surpassing the purchase of utility TXU Corp in 2007 for $44 billion. However, the deal is still uncertain, and many questions remain unanswered, including how Musk plans to fund the deal and what the \"appropriate next steps\" are for the board.\n\nThe proposal has also raised concerns about the potential impact on existing investors, who may not have the opportunity to retain their shares. Additionally, the company's cash-flow negative status and lack of profitability have raised questions about its ability to raise the necessary finance.\n\nOverall, the situation remains fluid, and it is unclear what the outcome will be. However, one thing is certain: the proposal has sparked a heated debate about the future of Tesla and the role of its CEO, Elon Musk."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Guess who's back? Serena Williams. And she made sure her return to the tennis court Tuesday was a stylish one, the Miami Herald notes, tweeting a picture of herself in a hot pink body suit. \"Look who I spotted on the court. Her first day back,\" Williams wrote. She hadn't played since July thanks to health problems; click here to catch up on what's been going on. Her agent says Williams is \"progressing well\" and has the OK from doctors to do light cardio, but still has not set an exact date for her return to the WTA Tour."], "length": 770, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tennis News: Serena Williams Returns to Training, Kim Clijsters Out for French Open**\n\nSerena Williams, a 13-time Grand Slam champion, has made a successful return to training after a prolonged injury and illness. The tennis star, who hasn't played since July, posted a photo of herself serving in a hot pink full-length body suit on Twitter, announcing her comeback. Williams' agent stated that she is progressing well and will return to the WTA Tour when she feels strong and healthy. However, it is unclear when she will make her comeback, with Wimbledon and the U.S. Open being potential targets.\n\nMeanwhile, world No. 2 Kim Clijsters has sustained a severe foot injury, which will keep her out of the French Open. Clijsters took a bad tumble at her cousin's wedding, suffering a strain of her medial and lateral ligaments in her right ankle and torn ligaments in her ankle joint. She is expected to face a recovery of at least four to six weeks.\n\nIn other tennis news, American Ryan Sweeting won his first ATP title, defeating Kei Nishikori in the U.S. Clay Court Championship in Houston. Sweeting, 23, moved up from No. 93 to No. 71 in the rankings and celebrated his milestone by jumping into a tournament pool.\n\n**Internet News: Alexa Internet Donates Crawl Data to Internet Archive**\n\nAlexa Internet has been donating its crawl data to the Internet Archive since 1996. The data, which is added to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period, provides a valuable resource for researchers and historians. The Internet Archive is a non-profit digital library that aims to preserve the internet's cultural and historical content.\n\n**Other News**\n\nThe U.S. Fed Cup team will rely on 18-year-old Christina McHale and 19-year-old Melanie Oudin to compete in a world group playoff in Stuttgart, Germany. The team will face a tough challenge from the Germans, who are hungry to prove themselves and are not taking the teenagers lightly."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Watch out for stars moving across galaxies or losing power—it could be aliens gathering energy before the lights go out. Dan Hooper, an astronomy and astrophysics professor at the University of Chicago, posted a paper in the preprint journal arXiv.org arguing that aliens in far-off galaxies may be sucking energy from stars to offset risks posed by an expanding universe, LiveScience reports. \"The presence of dark energy in our universe is causing space to expand at an accelerating rate,\" writes Hooper. \"As a result, over the next approximately 100 billion years, all stars residing beyond the Local Group [of galaxies including the Milky Way] will fall beyond the cosmic horizon and become not only unobservable, but entirely inaccessible, thus limiting how much energy could one day be extracted from them.\" Therefore, he argues, an advanced civilization may use something like \"Dyson spheres\"—an imagined solar-powered satellite dating back to 1930s science-fiction, notes LiveScience—to harvest a star's energy or use that energy to move it closer to home. Hooper hinges his case on the work of Russian astronomer Nikolai Kardashev, who argued in 1962 that technologically advanced civilizations would expand via three steps: harvesting resources of their planet, then the nearest star, then all stars in the galaxy and nearby galaxies. So how to spot energy-grabbing aliens? Massive stars emanate certain light wavelengths, which will appear in galaxies' light signatures if stars are drained energy—that is, when humans have powerful enough instruments to detect it."], "length": 1741, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Breaking News: The Quest for Universal Energy and the Search for Extraterrestrial Life**\n\nIn a recent study published on arXiv.org, astrophysicist Dan Hooper proposes a new, apocalyptic twist on Nikolai Kardashev's model of galactic expansion. According to Hooper, advanced civilizations must harvest energy from distant stars to prevent being left alone in the vastness of space. This is due to the accelerating expansion of the universe caused by dark energy, which will eventually make stars inaccessible.\n\nTo achieve this, Hooper suggests creating a swarm of satellites, known as \"Dyson spheres,\" to capture and harness the energy of stars. These mega-structures would be made up of millions of individual solar-collecting satellites, called \"statites,\" which would hover in space and collect energy from the star. The energy would then be beamed back to the home planet.\n\nThis concept is not new, as science fiction author Olaf Stapledon described similar structures in his 1937 novel \"Star Maker.\" Physicist Freeman Dyson also proposed similar structures in a scientific paper in 1960, suggesting that advanced civilizations could be recognized by their waste heat, detectable as infrared radiation.\n\nHooper's proposal is part of a larger discussion about the search for extraterrestrial life. With the possibility of alien civilizations thousands or even millions of years older than ours, the search for universal energy and the detection of unusual light signatures from distant galaxies become crucial.\n\nWhile the idea of Dyson spheres and mega-structures may seem like science fiction, it is a concept that has been explored in various forms of media, including science fiction novels and TV shows. The search for extraterrestrial life and the quest for universal energy are pressing questions that continue to captivate scientists and the public alike.\n\nAs we continue to explore the universe and search for signs of life, the possibility of encountering advanced civilizations becomes increasingly plausible. With the help of technology and scientific discovery, we may one day uncover the secrets of the universe and our place within it."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Police have arrested one of Disneyland's own employees after yesterday's dry-ice explosion in the Toontown section of the park, reports the Los Angeles Times. Christian Barnes, a 22-year-old vendor who sold soda and water from a cart, is being held on $1 million bond. Police say he filled a bottle with dry ice and dropped it in a trash can. The ensuing blast caused a lot of noise and chaos, but no injuries or serious damage, reports AP. No word on motive, but police don't think it's related to previous dry-ice bombs in the Anaheim area."], "length": 650, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Multiple Incidents Rock Disneyland and Surrounding Areas**\n\nA series of incidents has shaken the Disneyland resort in Anaheim, California, leaving visitors and employees on high alert. The most recent incident involved a 22-year-old Disneyland employee, Christian Barnes, who was arrested on suspicion of possessing a destructive device after a dry ice bomb exploded in a trash can in the Mickey's Toontown section.\n\nThe explosion, which occurred on Tuesday evening, forced the evacuation of the area for two hours and caused brief chaos in a portion of the park popular with young children. Fortunately, no one was injured in the incident. Barnes, who worked as an outdoor vendor for the resort, was held on $1 million bail and is cooperating with investigators.\n\nDetectives believe that Barnes placed dry ice inside a water bottle, which was then left in a trash can to create the explosion. The incident does not appear to be connected to similar dry-ice explosions in Anaheim in recent months.\n\nThe arrest has raised concerns about the safety of visitors to the park, with some expressing relief that no one was injured. \"You wouldn't expect situations like that coming to a place like this. That is crazy, that's wild,\" said one visitor.\n\nThe incident is being investigated by the Anaheim Police Department, which is working closely with Disneyland officials to ensure the safety of all visitors. The park has released a statement assuring visitors that it takes matters like this \"very seriously\" and is committed to providing a safe and enjoyable experience for all.\n\nIn other news, a separate incident involving a 22-year-old Disneyland employee has been reported, although the details of this incident are not yet clear.\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n\n* A 22-year-old Disneyland employee was arrested on suspicion of possessing a destructive device after a dry ice bomb exploded in a trash can in Mickey's Toontown.\n* The incident forced the evacuation of the area for two hours and caused brief chaos in a portion of the park popular with young children.\n* No one was injured in the incident, and the suspect is cooperating with investigators.\n* The incident does not appear to be connected to similar dry-ice explosions in Anaheim in recent months.\n* The Anaheim Police Department is investigating the incident and working closely with Disneyland officials to ensure the safety of all visitors."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A former well-known Canadian TV sports anchor is in jail after police say he robbed two banks in as many days in the western province of Alberta last week. Police in the small city of Medicine Hat say Steve Vogelsang robbed a Royal Bank of Canada on Thursday, Oct. 19, and then turned around the next day and robbed a Bank of Montreal, CBC reports. He entered both banks without a disguise and after demanding money, left with an undisclosed sum. Police finally arrested Vogelsang at a nearby hotel at around 3am on Saturday. Per CTV Winnipeg News, Vogelsang, 53, was a longtime news director and sports broadcaster at CKY, now CTV Winnipeg. He also taught journalism at Red River College in Winnipeg from 2002 to 2011. Conor Lloyd, a spokesman for the college, says, “I can confirm he is no longer an employee here.” Vogelsang is scheduled to appear in provincial court on Tuesday."], "length": 1543, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Former Winnipeg broadcast personality and Red River College journalism instructor Steve Vogelsang has been charged with two counts of robbery involving two separate banks in Alberta. The 53-year-old was arrested on Saturday at a hotel in Medicine Hat, Alberta, after police released surveillance footage of a man matching his description robbing two banks in the area.\n\nVogelsang, who worked as a news director and sports anchor at CTV Winnipeg, had a history of financial difficulties. He and his wife separated in 2015 and he moved back to Winnipeg after spending 24 years together. The couple had owned three properties in Nelson, B.C., which they sold for a total loss of $85,000. Vogelsang was also accused of missing mortgage payments on a home in Winnipeg and borrowing money.\n\nIn addition to the bank robberies, Vogelsang has faced other legal issues, including a charge of sexual assault that was stayed in 2017. He was also granted a protection order against a former student in March, who alleged that he had not stopped contacting her despite police asking him to leave her alone.\n\nVogelsang's arrest has shocked his former students and colleagues, who describe him as a tough but fair teacher. \"I'm shocked and upset, in disbelief, as I know many of Steve's former students are,\" said former student Matt Preprost. \"Steve was a tough, but fair teacher—one who built his lessons and rapport with us through a mix of fun and humour, always challenging us to step outside our comfort zones and to be better than our last assignment.\"\n\nVogelsang is currently in custody in Alberta and is expected to appear in court tomorrow to face the robbery charges."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["An Oklahoma girl diagnosed with \"super\" E. coli is facing a life-or-death battle—and worse, she's only 11 months old. \"With this strain of E. coli, it can get in her brain, it can get in her spinal cord, basically cause her to go into cardiac arrest,\" her grandfather, Chris Curtis, tells KFOR. \"And we heard that it was a very, very scary situation.\" Her family says it's not clear how Oakleigh Nance got the infection, which appears resistant to antibiotics, but the CDC is investigating. It all started when Oakleigh was born premature at only 3 pounds and \"started off on 4 liters of oxygen,\" according to a GoFundMe page to help pay her medical bills. \"In the NICU, they used to call her Miss Sassy because of her ability to express her displeasure with her circumstances,\" the page says. Then she went home and was fine for months until a 104-degree temperature and severe urinary tract infection sent her back to the hospital. Since then, she's been diagnosed with moderate ASD and pulmonary stenosis of the heart, stage 3 kidney reflux, and \"a dangerous, antibiotic resistant strain of Pseudomonas,\" says the GoFundMe page. Now doctors are planning a 4 1/2-hour procedure on her kidney, which is functioning at 22%, to possibly prevent other infections. \"I know that God is in control of all else, and when I think about what’s going on in her life, I think about my faith in God,\" says Curtis. \"That’s what sustains me and gets me through it.\" (Read about a brain-eating bacteria that has returned to Louisiana.)"], "length": 1463, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A young Oklahoma infant, Oakleigh Nance, is fighting for her life after contracting a severe case of E. coli, a \"super strain\" of the bacteria. The 11-month-old was born premature and has already undergone several surgeries due to a kidney issue. Recently, she was diagnosed with a critical case of E. coli, which can cause cardiac arrest and other serious complications. The family is concerned about the limited treatment options due to Oakleigh's extensive antibiotic use, making it challenging to combat the resistant bacteria.\n\nOakleigh's condition has sparked a community response, with her family setting up a GoFundMe account to cover medical expenses. The family's faith and support from loved ones are helping them cope with the difficult situation. Oakleigh is scheduled to undergo kidney surgery in late August, which may provide a chance to alleviate her kidney issues and improve her overall health.\n\nIn a separate development, a news passage highlighted the dangers of pseudomonas infections, which can be caused by the bacteria Pseudomonas aeruginosa. These infections can be deadly and are often resistant to antibiotics, making treatment challenging. The passage noted that pseudomonas infections can occur in various parts of the body, including wounds, ears, and lungs, and can spread through medical equipment, cleaning solutions, and other equipment in hospitals.\n\nThe news also shed light on the importance of proper hygiene and precautions to prevent the spread of infections. For individuals with weakened immune systems, such as those in hospitals or with chronic illnesses, it is crucial to take extra precautions to avoid contracting infections.\n\nOverall, Oakleigh's story serves as a reminder of the importance of proper medical care, hygiene, and community support in the face of serious health challenges."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The Portland teen accused of peeing in a city reservoir on Wednesday insists he didn't do it—in a relatively NSFW defense. \"Yeah, it's f---ing retarded, dude,\" 18-year-old Dallas Swonger tells Vocativ in what the site calls an \"expletive-filled interview\" given as Swonger smoked a Newport cigarette. \"I didn't piss in the f---ing water.\" He explains that he did urinate, after a night of skateboarding at Mount Tabor Park, but he did so against the wall of the reservoir, not into the reservoir. \"I was like, 'Dudes I have to piss so bad.' So I just went over to the wall. I leaned up against the wall and pissed on it. Right there on the wall, dude. I don’t know else how to describe it.\" Officials beg to differ (a Water Bureau official got very specific about it, saying that surveillance video showed Swonger making sure \"to get his little wee wee right up to the iron bars\"), and thus decided to dump 38 million gallons of water, but Swonger notes that even if his urine had made it into the water, he doesn't see what the big deal is. \"Dude, I’ve seen dead birds in there. During the summer time I've see hella dead animals in there. Like dead squirrels and s---. I mean, really, dude?\" Vocativ also got amusing quotes from Swonger's mom (\"I’m sorry, he just graduated from high school. He’s trying to get his stuff together\") and one of the friends skateboarding with him that night (\"He just doesn’t make the best decisions. Honestly, he has the potential to do really good\"). Possible criminal charges are still pending, the Oregonian notes. The paper also reports that the water tested clean of urine-related toxins, but all 38 million gallons will still be dumped."], "length": 1963, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A recent incident at the Mt. Tabor Reservoir system in Portland, Oregon, has sparked controversy and raised questions about public health and water management. On Wednesday morning, a 18-year-old man, identified as Dallas Swonger, was caught on surveillance video urinating near one of the reservoirs. However, Swonger claims that he did not urinate in the drinking water supply, but rather on a wall next to it.\n\nDespite Swonger's claims, the Portland Water Bureau has decided to flush 38 million gallons of water from the reservoir, citing concerns about public health. The decision has been met with criticism and ridicule from some, who argue that the risk of contamination is low and that the water is not a significant health hazard.\n\nAnna Harding, co-director of the School of Biological and Population Health Sciences at Oregon State University, notes that the urine would be very diluted and not a significant health risk. However, city officials argue that any risk is too great and that the decision to flush the water is necessary to ensure public health.\n\nThe incident has also sparked debate about the city's water management policies and the role of the City Council in making decisions about public health. Some have questioned the decision to flush the water, citing the potential waste of resources and the environmental impact of the action.\n\nThe situation has also drawn attention to the city's multi-year battle against new federal mandates on open-air reservoirs. Portland leaders opted to stop fighting last year, and the Mt. Tabor Reservoir system must be disconnected from the city water system by the end of 2015.\n\nIn an exclusive interview with Vocativ, Swonger expressed his frustration and disappointment with the situation, stating that he did not urinate in the drinking water supply and that the city's decision to flush the water is \"fucking retarded.\" He also lamented the potential consequences of the incident, which could make him a national and international laughing stock.\n\nThe incident has also raised questions about the city's approach to public health and the role of the City Council in making decisions about public health. The situation highlights the need for clear communication and transparency in public health decisions, as well as the importance of considering the potential consequences of actions taken by city officials."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["North and South Korea will compete together under one unified flag at next month's Winter Olympics in Pyeongchang, in what will be the first such arrangement since 2006. South Korea previously announced North Korea would send officials, athletes, and a cheer squad to the Olympics following talks between the two countries. It has now revealed North Korea's intention to send a 550-member delegation. Among those arriving in South Korea beginning on Jan. 25 will be female ice hockey players joining a team of South Korean athletes (they'll compete under a flag showing the Korean Peninsula in blue on a white background), as well as 230 cheerleaders, 140 orchestra members, and 30 people who will take part in a taekwondo demonstration, report Sky News and CNN. Ahead of the Olympics, skiers from both countries will also train together in North Korea, per CNN. The thawing of relations between the North and South has drawn praise from some. But others, like Japanese Foreign Minister Taro Kono, fear Kim Jong Un only hopes to ease international pressure while continuing to develop a nuclear weapons arsenal. \"It is not the time to ease pressure, or to reward North Korea,\" Kono said Tuesday in Vancouver, Canada, where 20 countries met and agreed to consider tougher sanctions on the North, per Reuters. Warning countries not to be fooled by its \"charm offensive,\" Kono added, \"The fact that North Korea is engaging in dialogue could be interpreted as proof that the sanctions are working.\" China avoided the meeting, saying it showed a \"Cold War mentality.\""], "length": 2659, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Korean Peninsula Sees Diplomatic Breakthrough Ahead of Winter Olympics**\n\nIn a significant diplomatic breakthrough, North and South Korea have agreed to march together under a unified flag at the upcoming Winter Olympics in Pyeongchang, South Korea. The move marks a rare show of unity between the two countries, which have been technically at war since the 1950-53 conflict.\n\nAs part of the agreement, North and South Korea will also field a joint women's ice hockey team at the Winter Olympics, with North Korea sending a 550-member delegation to the games. The delegation will include 230 cheerleaders, 140 artists, and 30 taekwondo players for a demonstration.\n\nThe joint Olympic team is seen as a positive step towards improving inter-Korean relations, with South Korean officials hailing the move as a \"significant step\" towards reconciliation. However, not all South Korean citizens are supportive of the move, with over 100 petitions opposing a joint Olympics team having been sent to the South Korean presidential Blue House website.\n\nThe agreement comes after months of talks between the two countries, which have been marked by a thaw in tensions following a series of high-profile military drills and missile tests by North Korea. The United States has warned that the talks should not be seen as a sign of complacency, with Secretary of State Rex Tillerson stating that North Korea's nuclear and missile programs remain a major concern.\n\nJapan has also expressed caution, with Foreign Minister Taro Kono warning that the world should not be naive about North Korea's \"charm offensive\". China has also criticized the talks, with Foreign Ministry spokesman Lu Kang stating that they demonstrate a \"Cold War mentality\" and will only undermine a settlement of the North Korea problem.\n\nDespite the challenges ahead, the joint Olympic team is seen as a positive step towards improving relations between North and South Korea. The move has been welcomed by international leaders, with the International Olympic Committee (IOC) stating that it will carefully evaluate the consequences of the agreement.\n\nAs the Winter Olympics approach, the world will be watching with interest to see how the joint team performs and whether the agreement marks a lasting shift towards improved relations between North and South Korea."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A homeless man was arrested after hopping the White House fence Tuesday night in an incident captured by CNN's John King USA program, which was filming from the North Lawn. The man was quickly taken into custody by Secret Service agents who approached him with guns drawn. Security officers locked the area down after finding a backpack that had been thrown over the fence nearby. The 41-year-old intruder was charged with unlawful entry and contempt of court for breaking a judicial order requiring him to stay away from the White House. It's not uncommon for the White House fence to be breached, notes the Washington Post: A 6-year-old girl made it onto the White House lawn over the weekend by slipping through the fence instead of going over it. Secret Service agents returned her to her parents."], "length": 536, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Breaking News: Man Arrested for Jumping White House Fence**\n\nA dramatic incident unfolded at the White House on Tuesday evening when a 41-year-old homeless man, James Dirk Crudup, was apprehended by Secret Service officers after jumping the fence. According to authorities, Crudup was charged with unlawful entry and contempt of court, as he had previously been ordered to stay away from the White House due to past incidents.\n\nThe incident occurred when Crudup allegedly climbed the metal picket fence, which is typically secured by Secret Service officers. However, in a surprising twist, a 6-year-old girl had previously successfully entered the White House lawn by going through the fence, not over it, on Sunday night.\n\nWhen Crudup was tackled by Secret Service officers, he was found to be unarmed, and a backpack belonging to him was being examined as a precaution. The incident was broadcast live on CNN's \"John King USA\" program, which was produced from the North Lawn of the White House.\n\nFortunately, no immediate security threats were reported, and the incident ended shortly after 9 p.m. ET when authorities issued an all-clear directive. Crudup is currently in custody and will face charges related to his actions.\n\nThis incident highlights the ongoing security measures in place at the White House and the efforts of the Secret Service to protect the President and the public."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Jon Stewart took over as host of the Daily Show in January 1999, but when did the program truly start becoming the Daily Show that legions of fans would come to love? Try December of that year, when Steve Carell boarded John McCain's campaign bus \"and changed the entire trajectory\" of the show, writes Chris Smith at Vanity Fair. The article is a fascinating oral history of the show's beginning that includes interviews with Stewart, Carell, Stephen Colbert, and pretty much everyone involved. The first year was rough for Stewart as he fought with the holdover writing staff from previous host Craig Kilborn and sought to put his stamp on the show. Things came together in that Carell interview, in which he peppers McCain with softball questions, then springs a policy zinger. After a few seconds of awkward silence, Carell says, \"I was just kidding! I don’t even know what that means!\" And the tense moment subsides. (See the clip.) Carell: \"It was making fun of a gotcha moment. And I think that a lot of what we do on The Daily Show is making fun of journalistic tropes, and I think that was one of them.\" Head writer Ben Karlin: \"I remember seeing it in the editing room. I remember Jon called me down, and seeing it and thinking, Yeah, this is what we should be doing. This is the goal. It was one of Carell’s most incredible moments. He asks McCain a question in a way that no journalists were talking to the candidates. And it was like, Oh s---, we are able, in this weird, unintentional way, to add a level of insight to the process that doesn’t exist. That was really, really exciting.\" Click for the full piece, in which Stewart talks about how that 2000 campaign provided his \"aha moment\" of how to present the show—by \"deconstructing the process.\""], "length": 7229, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "In the early 1990s, Comedy Central's The Daily Show, hosted by Craig Kilborn, was struggling to find its footing. However, with the arrival of Jon Stewart as host in 1999, the show underwent a significant transformation. Stewart brought a new energy and a more satirical approach to the program, which resonated with audiences.\n\nStewart's first few months on the job were marked by clashes with the existing staff and writers, who were resistant to his changes. However, with the help of new writers, including Ben Karlin and David Javerbaum, Stewart was able to establish a new tone and direction for the show.\n\nOne of the key moments in this transformation was the \"fuck-you meeting\" between Stewart and the writers, where Stewart asserted his authority and set the tone for the show's future. This was followed by a series of excursions to New Hampshire to cover the presidential primaries, where correspondents Steve Carell, Mo Rocca, and others began to develop their characters and push the boundaries of what was considered acceptable on the show.\n\nThe breakthrough moment came when Carell asked Senator John McCain a question about his record on pork-barrel politics, which McCain awkwardly sidestepped. This moment marked a turning point for the show, as it began to deconstruct the process of politics and expose the hypocrisy and contradictions of politicians.\n\nThe show's focus on deconstruction and satire resonated with audiences, and The Daily Show quickly became a cultural phenomenon. The show's correspondents, including Carell, Rocca, and others, became household names, and the show's influence extended beyond the world of comedy to the world of politics and journalism.\n\nThroughout its run, The Daily Show was marked by a sense of irreverence and a willingness to challenge the status quo. The show's impact was felt far beyond its own audience, as it helped to shape the national conversation about politics and media."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A North Carolina woman allegedly locked in the trunk of a moving car by her boyfriend was saved by a quick-thinking 911 dispatcher and a lowly flip phone, ABC News reports. The 29-year-old woman called 911 around 4am Jan. 14. The woman had gotten in a fight with her boyfriend, who was \"jealous,\" according to WRAL. Before dispatcher Tim Medlin could get much information from the woman, the call was dropped. He was unable to get her to answer when he called back. Then Medlin remembered a flip phone the 911 communications center keeps around in case the phone lines go down, WTVD reports. He says texting was the only way he could think to contact her without getting \"her in trouble.\" The woman texted Medlin back, letting the dispatcher know she was in a red Mustang headed toward Fayetteville. Verizon provided the location of the woman's phone, and police stopped her and her boyfriend, Nicholas Mattevi, in Fayetteville. Mattevi, 32, was arrested and charged with false imprisonment. \"I can go to bed,\" Medlin recalls feeling upon hearing of the arrest. \"We've done our job.\" He says he's planning to buy a smartphone for the office to replace the old flip phone and make it easier to text if there's a next time. Medlin's boss tells ABC the dispatcher \"really stood by our motto: Failure is not an option.\" (This 911 dispatcher was accused of hanging up on callers.)"], "length": 1325, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A dramatic 911 call in Johnston County, North Carolina, on January 14, 2017, led to the rescue of a woman who was locked in the trunk of a moving car. The woman, who was 29 years old at the time, had been kidnapped by her boyfriend and was being driven towards Fayetteville. \n\nThe 911 call was made by the woman, who was crying and very upset, but the dispatcher, Tim Medlin, was able to understand her and quickly sprang into action. Despite the call dropping and Medlin's multiple attempts to reach the woman failing, he refused to give up and used the office's emergency cellphone to text her. The woman responded, providing Medlin with the make and color of the vehicle and her location. \n\nWith the help of a cellphone provider, Medlin was able to locate the phone and contact the Cumberland County Sheriff's office, who then contacted Verizon Wireless to track the vehicle. The car was spotted by police in Fayetteville, and the woman was rescued unharmed. Her boyfriend, Nicholas Mattevi, was arrested and charged with false imprisonment, a misdemeanor. \n\nThe incident highlights the importance of quick thinking and technology in emergency situations. Medlin's use of a flip phone to text the woman was a crucial factor in her rescue, and his boss has since announced plans to upgrade the team's phones to smartphones. The case serves as a reminder of the critical role that 911 dispatchers play in saving lives and preventing crimes."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Police in Stanislaus County, Calif., say a 20-year veteran of the force was \"executed\" early Sunday after investigating a stolen van and a suspicious person outside Modesto. Sheriff's Deputy Dennis Wallace was shot twice in the head. Police say a suspect was arrested more than 150 miles away later in the day after allegedly carrying out a carjacking, an armed robbery at a convenience store, and an attempted purse-snatching, the Los Angeles Times reports. David Machado, a 37-year-old described by police as a \"known criminal,\" was connected to the Wallace killing after police in Tulane County recognized his tattoos. Wallace, who was married with children, \"was executed,\" Sheriff Adam Christianson told reporters. \"We believe that Dep. Wallace was killed outside of the car and we know for a fact that the gun used in this crime was in direct contact with his head when the trigger was pulled twice.\" He said Wallace, 53, had been involved in programs including youth soccer and early intervention, NBC News reports. \"We need to keep the Wallace family in our thoughts and prayers. We need to keep law enforcement in our thoughts and prayers,\" he said. The Bradenton Herald reports after a knee injury in 2007, Wallace was off for two years without pay and fought a long legal battle to get his job back."], "length": 420, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Breaking News Summary**\n\nIn recent developments, the Internet Archive has launched an initiative to archive web pages as they are created, aiming to preserve links to the original content. This effort, known as \"No More 404,\" seeks to fix broken links on the web by providing a permanent record of archived pages. The project's goal is to ensure that users can access the original content intended by page authors.\n\nTragically, a devastating incident occurred in Modoc County, California, on October 19. Modoc County Sheriff's Deputy Jack Hopkins was fatally shot while responding to a disturbance call. The suspect, 47-year-old Jack Lee Breiner, was confronted by the deputy and shot him instantly. Breiner then engaged another deputy in a gunbattle and was wounded. He has been charged with murder and other crimes in connection with the killing."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Papillion Sophie has lived a pampered life, but that doesn't have to end now that her famous owner—Hollywood legend Lauren Bacall—has died. According to estate papers filed last week, Bacall left $10,000 to her son Sam Robards for Sophie's care and upkeep, Page Six reports. This bequeath isn't a surprise, considering Bacall called herself a \"dog yearner\" in a 2008 interview, Us reports. \"I didn't have a dog growing up in the city with a working mother. As an only child, I yearned for someone to talk to,\" she said. She gave Sophie quite a glitzy life attending book signings and film festivals. Bacall didn't ignore her human family: The New York Daily News reports her $26.6 million estate was mostly split among her three children; her grandkids reportedly got $250,000 each. And two of her employees also received $20,000 and $15,000, respectively. Considering other famous four-legged heirs, Sophie's 10 grand isn't shocking: Leona Helmsley left $12 million to her Maltese and Alexander McQueen left $82,000 to his three dogs. But the oddest may be Dusty Springfield, who gave orders that her cat be serenaded with her own music and \"marry\" another cat."], "length": 1776, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Lauren Bacall, the legendary actress, has left behind a $26.6 million estate, with a special bequest of $10,000 for her beloved dog, Sophie. The actress, who passed away at the age of 89, had a lifelong love for dogs and was particularly close to Sophie, her Papillon. The majority of her estate will be split among her three children, Leslie Bogart, Stephen Humphrey Bogart, and Sam Robards, who will also serve as executors of her estate.\n\nBacall's will also includes provisions for her employees, with Maria Santos and Ilsa Hernandez receiving $20,000 and $15,000, respectively. The actress's grandsons, Calvin and Sebastian Robards, will each receive $250,000, which will be used for college.\n\nIn a unique request, Bacall asked her children to keep her personal papers private, including letters, writings, diaries, and other memorabilia. The estate documents, filed in a rush order, also state that the family plans to auction off her artwork this fall.\n\nBacall's legacy extends beyond her acting career, with a career spanning over eight decades and numerous awards, including two Tony Awards, a Golden Globe, and an honorary Oscar. She was married to Humphrey Bogart and Jason Robards, and had two children with Bogart. Her relationship with Bogart and her subsequent marriage to Robards have been well-documented, and she has spoken publicly about her desire to be remembered for both husbands.\n\nOverall, Lauren Bacall's estate and legacy serve as a testament to her enduring love for her family, her dogs, and her craft."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Fresh off his loss to Mitt Romney at CPAC, Rick Santorum hit the talk shows this morning, and he's downplaying the victory, reports Politico: \"You have to talk to the Romney campaign and how many tickets they bought—we've heard all sorts of things,\" Santorum told CNN. \"Ron Paul has won those (straw polls) because he trucks in a lot of people, pays for their ticket, and they come in and vote. I don't try to rig straw polls.\" Elsewhere on the Sunday dial, as per Politico: Santorum on his chances: \"I think we can do well (in Michigan). I think we can do reasonably well in Arizona, and really make this a two-person race.\" Sarah Palin on Romney: He's \"a great candidate,\" but \"his idea of conservatism is evolving, and I base this on a pretty moderate past that he has had, even in some cases a liberal past. Now that’s a problem. He’s still in the 30 percentile mark ... because we are not convinced.\" Obama chief of staff Jack Lew on the birth control ruckus: The Friday \"accommodation\" is as far as the White House will go. \"There are others who don't have the same objective, they will have to speak for themselves, (but) this is our plan.\" Ron Paul on 'severe conservative' Romney: \"That was the first time I’ve heard that definition, so I guess Mitt will have to tell us exactly what it means. Obviously, he means he’s a serious conservative, and he was trying to defend himself, or portray himself as such, but I don’t know exactly what he was meaning by that.\""], "length": 788, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Recent News Summary**\n\nThe recent news cycle has been dominated by several key stories in the world of politics. \n\nMitt Romney emerged victorious in last weekend's CPAC presidential straw poll, winning 38% of the vote to Rick Santorum's 31%. Santorum responded to the loss by suggesting that Romney may have \"rigged\" the poll by purchasing tickets for his supporters, a claim that Romney's campaign has denied. Santorum also stated that he does not engage in such tactics, but does not want to rule out doing so in the future.\n\nIn another development, Ron Paul expressed confusion over Romney's description of himself as a \"severe conservative.\" Paul stated that he is also interested in being a conservative, but was unclear on what Romney meant by the term.\n\nMeanwhile, the Obama administration has been working to address the controversy over birth control and Catholic institutions. White House chief of staff Jack Lew has stated that the administration's position on the issue has been \"consistent,\" and that the recent \"accommodation\" offered to Catholic institutions is as far as the administration is willing to go. Lew also suggested that health insurers would pay less for birth control-inclusive plans, as they reduce pregnancies.\n\nOverall, these stories suggest that the 2012 presidential election is shaping up to be a contentious and issue-driven campaign."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Fixer Upper stars Chip and Joanna Gaines are having to pay up for EPA violations seen on their hit HGTV show. The couple's business, Magnolia Homes, has agreed to pay a $40,000 fine after the EPA determined 33 of its home renovations were \"conducted without adequate lead paint protections,\" reports Deadline, which notes the potential fine the company faced was as high as $556,000. The EPA notes Fixer Upper footage \"reviewed by EPA did not depict the lead-safe work practices normally required\" though \"Magnolia took immediate steps to ensure compliance\" when it was notified. Chip Gaines, who in March tweeted advice about engaging a professional to check for lead paint when buying or remodeling a home, will also star in a Magnolia-paid video on lead paint safety as part of the settlement, per Vulture. (The couple are expecting their fifth child.)"], "length": 2074, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "The US Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) has reached a settlement with Magnolia Homes, the company behind the popular HGTV show \"Fixer Upper,\" to resolve alleged violations of the Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA) Lead Renovation, Repair and Painting Rule (RRP Rule). The violations were related to home renovations conducted without adequate lead paint protections as depicted on the television program.\n\nThe settlement includes a $40,000 civil penalty, which is significantly lower than the potential fine of $556,000. In addition to the fine, Magnolia Homes will produce an educational video highlighting the dangers of lead-based paint, which will be released within 90 days. The company will also spend $160,000 on a lead-abatement project in Waco, Texas, to reduce the risk of exposure to lead-based paint in at-risk residences.\n\nThe EPA's investigation found evidence of noncompliance at 33 properties, including failure to obtain firm certification, assign a certified renovator, provide home owners or occupants with an approved pamphlet, post signs to clearly define the work area, and comply with work practice standards. However, Magnolia Homes has taken steps to ensure compliance with lead-based paint regulations in future renovation projects.\n\nThe company has also sought to improve public awareness of lead-based paint safety issues associated with renovations in older homes. In a March 2018 episode of \"Fixer Upper,\" Chip Gaines spoke about lead-based paint and depicted some of the steps renovation firms must take to minimize hazards. The company has also promoted lead safety and RRP Rule information on its website and social media accounts.\n\nThe settlement is a significant step towards ensuring compliance with lead-based paint regulations in the renovation industry. The EPA's efforts to educate the public about lead-based paint hazards and promote safe renovation practices are also crucial in reducing the risk of exposure to lead dust and preventing lead poisoning."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["For decades, the human story was one told through signs of modernity—art, tools, burials—found only after Homo sapiens left Africa. Recent discoveries pushing back the date of departure are helping to change that narrative, as are three new studies in Science. Together, they describe the earliest stone tools of their kind in East Africa as well as the earliest evidence for long-distance transport of raw materials in the region, per Science News. Researchers previously believed the Early Stone Age marked by large, primitive tools (hand axes and cleavers) made way for the Middle Stone Age and its smaller tools (spearpoints and blades) around 280,000 years ago. But stone tools found in Kenya's Olorgesailie Basin show hominin groups—it's not clear which species is responsible—had developed these more advanced tools approximately 320,000 years ago. \"We see a smaller technology, a more diverse series of stone tools … designed for specific purposes,\" researcher Rick Potts tells NPR. An expert not involved in the study says the tools are so \"prepared and retouched\" they suggest the Middle Stone Age likely started even earlier. What drove it? Microscopic and chemical analyses of the region's soil show the landscape was rapidly shifting between wet and dry conditions, meaning hominins might have been forced to adapt. But as the technology also coincides with the emergence of Homo sapiens in Africa, it's possible that the human mind deserves credit. The tools themselves suggest the development of trading networks, too. Some were made of rocks carried from 55 miles away, says Potts. Others were made of obsidian from as far as 30 miles away, per the Conversation. (These stone tools are also making waves.)"], "length": 3668, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Recent discoveries in the field of archaeology have shed new light on the evolution of human behavior and technological advancements during the Middle Stone Age. A study published in Science has revealed that Homo sapiens or their close relatives may have developed toolmaking and trading networks as far back as 320,000 years ago, in response to dramatic shifts in the East African climate.\n\nThe research, conducted by a team of international scientists, found that the oldest Middle Stone Age tools discovered in Olorgesailie, Kenya, date back to around 320,000 years ago. These tools, made from obsidian and other materials, were smaller and more specialized than those found in earlier deposits, suggesting a significant technological innovation.\n\nThe study also revealed that the tools were not made locally, but were instead sourced from far away, with some coming from as far as 55 miles away. This suggests that there was a complex network of exchange and trade between different groups of people, which was likely driven by the need to adapt to changing environmental conditions.\n\nThe findings have significant implications for our understanding of human evolution and the development of modern behaviors. They suggest that the \"human revolution\" that is often credited with the emergence of modern humans may not have occurred suddenly, but rather was a gradual process that began much earlier.\n\nThe research also highlights the importance of considering the African archaeological record in a more nuanced and complex way. While many researchers have traditionally viewed the emergence of modern humans as a singular event that occurred outside of Africa, the findings from Olorgesailie challenge this view and suggest that complex behaviors and technological innovations may have emerged in multiple regions and at different times.\n\nOverall, the study provides new insights into the evolution of human behavior and technological advancements during the Middle Stone Age, and highlights the need for a more nuanced and complex understanding of human evolution."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The year's top travel writer is a familiar face, but more so for acting than writing. Andrew McCarthy of Brat Pack fame (yes, Molly Ringwald's true love in Pretty in Pink) has won the 2010 Lowell Thomas award for Travel Journalist of the Year, notes Jim Romenesko's blog at PoynterOnline. McCarthy's freelance articles have appeared in National Geographic Traveler, Bon Appetit, Afar, and the Atlantic, among others. (His piece on Ireland in the latter is here.) \"Word by word, paragraph-by-paragraph, entry-by-entry, McCarthy earns the gold the old-fashioned way—by treating readers to excellent writing and poignant stories,\" declared the judges."], "length": 2024, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**News Summary**\n\nThe past few weeks have seen significant developments in the world of journalism, publishing, and travel. Here's a summary of the key news:\n\n**Journalism and Publishing**\n\n* The Boston Globe has announced the sale of the Vineyard Gazette to Jerome and Nancy Kohlberg, with Richard Reston, son of former New York Times columnist James Reston, retiring as publisher.\n* The California Supreme Court has refused to review lower-court rulings ordering SF Weekly to pay $21 million in damages for trying to drive the rival Bay Guardian out of business by selling ads below cost.\n* The Lowell Thomas Travel Journalism Competition has announced its winners, with Afar Magazine, Washington Post, and San Francisco Chronicle taking top honors as the best travel publications in North America.\n\n**Travel Journalism**\n\n* The Lowell Thomas Travel Journalism Competition has awarded its top honors to Andrew McCarthy, who won the gold for his freelance writing in some of North America's best travel magazines.\n* The competition drew 1,161 entries and awarded over $20,000 in prize money to winners.\n* The judges praised the entries for their quality, creativity, and ability to inspire readers to explore new destinations.\n\n**Ireland's Economic Meltdown**\n\n* Ireland is still reeling from its economic meltdown, which began in the late 1990s and early 2000s.\n* The country's rural areas, such as Doolin in County Clare, are particularly affected, with many families struggling to make ends meet.\n* Despite the economic downturn, traditional Irish music is still thriving in Doolin, with many young musicians learning the old ways and performing in local pubs.\n\nOverall, these news stories highlight the challenges facing the journalism and publishing industries, as well as the resilience of traditional Irish music and culture in the face of economic adversity."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Mass shootings in America aren't a gun problem, they're a mental health problem, according to Donald Trump. He told a rally in Nashville on Saturday that \"it's not the guns, it's the people. It's these sick people,\" the AP reports. \"Many states and many cities are closing their mental health facilities and closing them down, and they're closing them because they don't have the funding. And we have to start looking much stronger into mental health,\" he said. On NBC's Meet the Press on Sunday, Trump explained that gun control wouldn't help because \"they can be sick as hell and they’re geniuses in a certain way. They are going to be able to break the system.” He also told NBC that to avoid copycat killings, the media should have followed the local sheriff's advice and refused to name the gunman after the mass shooting in Oregon. At the Nashville rally, Trump spoke against \"gun-free zones,\" saying people \"would have been a hell of a lot better off\" if there had been \"teachers with guns in that room\" during the Oregon shooting, and said that since he has a handgun carry permit in New York, anyone who attacks him is in for a surprise, CNN reports. He brought up Charles Bronson's Death Wish, got the audience to chant the name of the movie, and said: \"Today you can’t make that movie because it’s not politically correct,\" reports the Guardian. (Jeb Bush took a lot of heat for saying \"Stuff happens\" after the Oregon shooting.)"], "length": 2079, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Donald Trump Defends Second Amendment Rights After Oregon Community College Shooting**\n\nIn the aftermath of a shooting at an Oregon community college that left nine dead, Republican presidential candidate Donald Trump defended Second Amendment rights, channeling 1970s action star Charles Bronson in his remarks. Trump stated that he has a handgun carry permit in New York and that any attacker would be \"shocked\" if he tried to assault him. He also criticized \"gun-free zones\" and suggested that better mental health care would help curb future shootings. Trump's comments on gun control have evolved significantly over the years, and he has faced criticism for his stance on the issue.\n\n**Trump's Comments on Gun Control and Mental Health**\n\nIn a rally in suburban Nashville, Trump said that the gun laws have nothing to do with mass shootings, which he attributed to mental illness. He also mentioned his New York state handgun carry permit and stated that anyone who attacked him would be \"shocked\" because he would emulate Bronson in the vigilante film \"Death Wish.\" Trump's comments on mental health and gun control have been met with criticism from some, who argue that his stance on the issue is not nuanced enough.\n\n**Trump's Tax Plan and Poll Numbers**\n\nIn a sit-down interview with NBC's Meet the Press, Trump defended his newly introduced tax plan, which independent economists estimate will cost $12tn over 10 years. Trump claimed that the major tax cut in his plan would hurt him financially, despite polls showing that he would save a significant amount of money under the current tax code. Trump also pushed back against those who have said he himself would lose a landslide to both Vice President Joe Biden and Vermont Senator Bernie Sanders in a general election matchup.\n\n**Overall**\n\nTrump's comments on gun control and mental health have sparked controversy, with some arguing that his stance is not nuanced enough. However, his tax plan and poll numbers remain a major focus of attention, with some arguing that his economic policies could have a significant impact on the country. As the Republican presidential frontrunner, Trump's comments and policies will continue to be closely watched by the public and the media."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Kanye West is, apparently, $53 million in debt, but he should be able to pay that off in no time thanks to a GoFundMe campaign started by 36-year-old Minnesotan Jeremy Piatt, CNET reports. \"Recently, Kanye let us in on his personal struggle. He is 53 million dollars in debt and it doesn't look like he's going to get Mark Zuckerberg's help that he desperately needs,\" Piatt writes. \"We must open our hearts and wallets for Kanye today. Sure he is personally rich and can buy furs and houses for his family, but without our help, the true genius of Kanye West can't be realized.\" So far, after just a single day of fundraising, $603 has been raised for Kanye (along with a lot of comments like, \"How about you get a job?\"). Piatt tells Mashable that he's spoken to GoFundMe, and only Kanye or his team will be able to access any money raised. As for Zuckerberg—West specifically asked him, as well as Larry Page, to donate money to the Kanye cause when he went on his Twitter rant, and then went on to imply that he knows other tech giants who should help him. \"All you dudes in San Fran play rap music in your homes but never help the real artists…,\" he tweeted, followed by, \"All you guys had meetings with me and no one lifted a finger to help….\" The Guardian did a little investigation into how many \"tech guys\" Kanye actually knows, and found that he really does have something of an ongoing relationship with Facebook and Apple; has had meetings with investors in Silicon Valley and even had some of them at one of his parties; has had interactions with an Instagram co-founder, the Dropbox chief executive, a co-founder of Rap Genius, and Elon Musk; and is good friends with venture capitalist Ben Horowitz. (Here are some of the dumbest GoFundMe campaigns ever.)"], "length": 2906, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Kanye West, a renowned entertainer, fashion designer, and celebrity, has publicly disclosed that he is $53 million in debt. Despite his wealth, West has expressed his need for financial assistance, citing that without help, his true genius cannot be realized. In response, fans and well-wishers have come together to support him through crowdfunding campaigns.\n\nA GoFundMe page, \"Get Kanye Out of Debt,\" was created by Jeremy Piatt, a graphic designer from Minnesota, to raise funds for West's debt. The campaign quickly gained traction, with over $50 pledged in just 48 hours. Piatt, who is a fan of West's work, stated that the arts must be funded, and without funding, great ideas and masterpieces never get made.\n\nHowever, not all efforts to support West were successful. A separate GoFundMe campaign, \"HELP PAY KANYE'S DEBT!\", organized by Robert Kulik in Washington, D.C., raised $0. West's financial woes have also sparked a Twitter debate, with some in Silicon Valley taking note of his plea for financial assistance.\n\nIn other news, a recent article highlighted the worst GoFundMe campaigns of all time, featuring everything from ridiculous requests to help pay for a round-the-world trip for spiritual healing to attempting to erase a bad tattoo decision. The article also discussed the importance of crowdfunding in supporting the arts and visionaries.\n\nAdditionally, Kanye West has been involved in a series of controversies, including a Twitter rant in which he pleaded for Facebook CEO Mark Zuckerberg and Alphabet CEO Larry Page to invest $1 billion in his ideas. West has also released his latest album, \"The Life of Pablo,\" which was pulled down shortly after its release due to alleged copyright infringement.\n\nIn response to West's claims of being \"too cool\" to ask for help, Silicon Valley entrepreneur Aaron Levie tweeted a classic Series A pitch deck line. West has also been involved in a public feud with Taylor Swift, who responded to his claims of being responsible for her fame with a gracious acceptance speech at the Grammy Awards."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Could it be? A Chinese ship searching for Flight 370 has picked up a ping in the southern Indian Ocean, reports CNN and the BBC. Both cite a report in China's Xinhua news agency. The pulse is 37.5 kHz, the frequency for the Malaysian plane's black-box data recorders, says the president of the beacon's manufacturer. All the reports caution that it could turn out to be yet another false hope, however. \"This could be a variety of things,\" says one oceanographer, noting that the frequency is used by lots of instruments. For the record, the Chinese ship detected the signal at 25 degrees south latitude and 101 degrees east longitude, says the state-run Xinhua. \"It is yet to be established whether it is related to the missing jet,\" the report says. The development comes as dozens of ships and planes intensify their sweeps in the search zone, given the finite battery life of the data recorders."], "length": 3925, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Search for Missing Malaysia Airlines Flight 370 Continues**\n\nThe search for Malaysia Airlines Flight 370, which disappeared on March 8, 2014, with 239 people on board, continues to be one of the most extensive and complex in aviation history. A Chinese ship, Haixun 01, has picked up a signal that is believed to be consistent with the flight recorders of the missing plane. The signal, which has a frequency of 37.5kHz, was detected on Saturday by the ship's crew, who reported hearing the sounds but were unable to record them.\n\nThe Australian agency coordinating the search, led by Angus Houston, has confirmed that the signal is \"consistent with the aircraft black box\" but has urged caution, stating that there is no confirmation that the signals and objects are related to the missing aircraft. The search operation has intensified, with dozens of ships and planes joining the effort, and the operation is moving into its most intensive phase before the batteries on the data recorders fade.\n\nThe search area has been expanded to include an area of about 217,000 square kilometers, and two ships, the Australian naval vessel Ocean Shield and the HMS Echo, are using underwater locator capabilities to try to detect an underwater signal emitted by the data recorders. The battery-powered signal fades after 30 days, and the area has been picked on the basis of analysis of the satellite data.\n\nThe search has been ongoing for nearly a month, and while there have been several promising leads, none have been confirmed. The investigation into the disappearance of the plane is ongoing, and a new investigation team has been set up to help co-ordinate the search.\n\n**Key Developments**\n\n* A Chinese ship, Haixun 01, has picked up a signal that is believed to be consistent with the flight recorders of the missing plane.\n* The signal has a frequency of 37.5kHz and was detected on Saturday by the ship's crew.\n* The Australian agency coordinating the search has confirmed that the signal is \"consistent with the aircraft black box\" but has urged caution.\n* The search operation has intensified, with dozens of ships and planes joining the effort.\n* Two ships, the Australian naval vessel Ocean Shield and the HMS Echo, are using underwater locator capabilities to try to detect an underwater signal emitted by the data recorders.\n* The battery-powered signal fades after 30 days, and the area has been picked on the basis of analysis of the satellite data.\n\n**Next Steps**\n\n* The search operation will continue to intens"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["President Trump escalated his Twitter war with CNN on Sunday, and in the process sparked more concern that he is advocating the use of violence against members of the media, the Hill reports. The president tweeted a modified video of a 2007 WWE wrestling match he took part in, obscuring his opponent's head with the CNN logo, which he proceeds to beat with his fists. Trump tweeted the video with the hashtags \"#FraudNewsCNN\" and \"#FNN.\" A CNN spokesman called it \"a sad day when the president of the United States encourages violence against reporters.\" But on ABC News, Homeland Security adviser Tom Bossert suggested CNN and other critics were overreacting. \"No one would perceive that as a threat,\" he said, adding that Trump is \"beaten up in a way on cable platforms that he has a right to respond to.\" The new video comes just a few days after Trump posted two tweets in which he talked about MSNBC's Morning Joe co-host Mika Brzezinski \"bleeding badly from a face-lift.\" Those tweets sparked condemnation from all over the political spectrum, with even Trump's fellow Republicans calling them unpresidential. On Saturday, however, Trump took to Twitter once again to address the backlash, writing, \"My use of social media is not Presidential – it's MODERN DAY presidential,\" Politico reports. At a press conference Thursday, Deputy White House Press Secretary Sarah Huckabee Sanders said Trump \"no way, form or fashion has ever promoted or encouraged violence,\" the Washington Post reports. \"In fact quite the contrary.\""], "length": 1036, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\nIn recent days, several news stories have emerged that highlight President Donald Trump's use of social media and its impact on his presidency. \n\nPresident Trump's homeland security adviser, Tom Bossert, defended the president's tweet showing him body-slamming and punching a person signifying CNN, stating that \"no one would perceive that as a threat.\" However, this defense was met with criticism from CNN contributor Ana Navarro, who argued that the tweet was an incitement to violence and that the president's behavior was unacceptable.\n\nAdditionally, President Trump asserted that his social media use is \"modern day presidential,\" despite facing criticism for his tweets, which have repeatedly sparked controversy since he entered the White House. The president's tweets have targeted the media, with some of his recent posts attacking \"Morning Joe\" co-hosts Joe Scarborough and Mika Brzezinski.\n\nThe controversy surrounding President Trump's tweets has led to concerns among some Republicans, with Nebraska Sen. Ben Sasse calling the tweets \"beneath the dignity\" of the presidential office. Alaska Sen. Lisa Murkowski also expressed her concerns, asking if the president wants to be remembered for his tweets or his accomplishments.\n\nFurthermore, a recent tweet from President Trump about adding location information to his tweets has sparked a discussion about the use of social media and its potential impact on the president's behavior.\n\nOverall, these news stories highlight the challenges faced by President Trump in navigating the complexities of social media and its impact on his presidency."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Apple presented its new smartphone Wednesday, and nestled in with the iPhone 7's much-ballyhooed features was the disclosure that the company is getting rid of the phone's headphone jack—news that wasn't exactly met with universal acclaim, or much acclaim at all. Chris Taylor is one detractor of the headphone ditch, writing for Mashable that, in nearly 20 years of attending Apple product launches, he's \"never heard anything as ridiculous emanate from that stage as I did Wednesday.\" Apple marketing head Phil Schiller used the word \"courage\" to describe Apple's innovation, but that's not how Taylor sees it. \"Courage is marching across the Edmund Pettus bridge in Selma in 1965 [or] facing down a tank in Tiananmen Square,\" he writes. \"Courage, by definition, involves doing something that makes you afraid.\" Apparently Taylor doesn't think Apple was quivering in its workboots when it came up with this plan. He's got plenty of other words instead of \"courage\" in mind, including \"hubris,\" \"arrogance,\" \"greed,\" and his personal favorite, \"stupidity,\" which he attributes to Apple for taking \"one step forward, two steps back\" both technologically (\"wired almost always sounds better than wireless\") and logistically (no more plugging in other peripherals like credit card readers, for example). Meanwhile, although much of the other feedback online is similarly cranky about the headphone jack's demise, Quartz notes that Apple is actually late, not early, in making this move: A handful of Chinese smartphone brands have been fiddling around with the idea for years, and some have already sent these phones, sans jacks, to market."], "length": 2155, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Apple's recent decision to remove the headphone jack from its iPhone 7 and 7 Plus has sparked widespread criticism and outrage. Marketing chief Phil Schiller explained that the move was made to \"move on and do something better for all of us,\" citing \"courage\" as the driving force behind the decision. However, many have mocked Schiller's words, pointing out that the removal of the headphone jack is not a courageous move, but rather a shortsighted and arrogant one.\n\nThe decision to remove the headphone jack has been met with skepticism, with many arguing that there is no technological excuse for the move. Music enthusiasts have long known that wired headphones sound better than wireless ones, and the removal of the headphone jack will only exacerbate this issue. In fact, many have pointed out that the new Lightning connector will require users to purchase expensive dongles to connect their headphones, making the experience even more inconvenient.\n\nThe move has also been criticized for its impact on users who rely on the headphone jack for various purposes, such as charging their phone and using other devices that plug into the jack. The removal of the headphone jack is seen as a step backward, rather than a step forward, and has been likened to San Francisco's efforts to gentrify the middle class.\n\nThe decision has also been met with ridicule on social media, with many users expressing their outrage and disappointment at the move. The hashtag #AppleEvent was trending on Twitter, with many users sharing their reactions to the news.\n\nOverall, Apple's decision to remove the headphone jack from its iPhone 7 and 7 Plus has been widely criticized, with many arguing that it is a shortsighted and arrogant move that will only inconvenience users. The move has sparked a heated debate about the importance of user experience and the role of technology in our lives."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The \"angst\" travelers feel at overcrowded gates may soon be eased at United Airlines terminals, an exec for the airline says. That's because United just began a new initiative designed to relieve that particular \"passenger pain point,\" as CNBC puts it, by changing up its departure protocol: The number of boarding lines has been cut from five to two, and overeager customers are now asked to stay out of the boarding area until their boarding group (one of six in total, including pre-boarders) is announced. \"It's too congested,\" a UA rep says of the current procedure, which often results in arriving fliers having to push their way through throngs of departing ones. Forbes notes customers can also opt in to receive push notifications via the mobile app, meaning they can hang out in nearby restaurants or shops until they receive an alert on their cellphones that it's time to board. Over the past year, United test-drove a variety of boarding options on about 12,000 flights, ending up with this system, which is similar to the way American, Delta, and Southwest queue up. United has been using the procedure at LAX since winter and will now implement it at all locations, per USA Today. It's also spreading passengers around more equitably within its six boarding groups, which includes pre-boarders (e.g., families with young kids, service members), first-class passengers and frequent fliers in groups 1 and 2, and then everyone else in groups 3, 4, and 5. \"The boarding process was one of the top areas customers told us they wanted improved,\" another UA rep says in a statement, noting customer and employee feedback guided the airline on the enhancements. (United had some \"unfortunate dog incidents\" earlier this year.)"], "length": 3434, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**United Airlines Introduces New Boarding Process to Reduce Congestion**\n\nUnited Airlines has rolled out a new boarding process to reduce congestion at gates and improve the overall passenger experience. The new system, which was tested at Los Angeles International Airport since February, features a two-lane, color-coded process that will handle a staggered boarding call.\n\nUnder the new system, passengers will be divided into five groups, with elite and priority passengers boarding first through lane 1. Group 1 includes first- and business-class customers, as well as Platinum- and Gold-level frequent-flyers. Group 2 includes Silver-level frequent-flyers and certain United credit-card holders. Groups 3, 4, and 5 will board through lane 2, with Group 5 including most Basic Economy customers.\n\nThe new system aims to reduce congestion and stress for passengers, who have complained about clusters of travelers around gates and long lines. United says customer feedback was a key factor in the development of the new system, which was designed to improve communication and recognize Premier customers while balancing out the number of passengers in each boarding group.\n\n**United Airlines and Avianca Announce Joint Business Agreement**\n\nUnited Airlines and Avianca have announced a joint business agreement (JBA) that will provide substantial benefits for customers, communities, and the marketplace for air travel between the United States and 19 countries in Central and South America.\n\nThe JBA, which is pending government approval, will integrate the complementary route networks of the two carriers and offer customers many benefits, including integrated, seamless service in more than 12,000 city pairs, new nonstop routes, additional flights on existing routes, reduced travel times, and drive economic benefits for consumers and communities.\n\n**Airlines Struggle to Create Efficient Boarding Process**\n\nThe airline industry has long struggled to create an efficient boarding process that doesn't stress out customers. Both American and Delta have rolled out similar changes in early 2017, and Southwest has its own system since 2007. However, many passengers still feel compelled to stand in line to hold their spot.\n\nUnited's new boarding process is designed to address this issue, but it remains to be seen whether elite-level frequent-flyers will sort themselves out evenly in the updated process. One thing is certain, however: the airline industry continues to grapple with the challenges of creating a boarding process that works for everyone.\n\n**Passenger Numbers Continue to Rise**\n\nUnited Airlines carried close to 155 million passengers in the 12 months ended in August, up 6.1"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Frightened North Carolinians fleeing Irene are jamming highways as the East Coast steels for what could be the biggest hurricane in decades. At least seven states have now declared a state of emergency, and hundreds of flights are being canceled. Gas stations are running out of fuel and ATMs have been emptied of cash as Americans hit the road for a bumper-to-bumper trip out of Irene's angry path. The Category 3 storm is expected to hit the Southeast sometime tomorrow, and hundreds of thousands of residents have been ordered out of their homes in three North Carolina counties. Norfolk, Va., is also ordering residents to evacuate, and Washington, DC, has been forced to postpone Sunday's planned MLK Memorial dedication. \"This is a very, very serious situation,\" a spokeswoman for Dare County told ABC News. \"We have not seen anything like this in the lifetimes of most our residents. Once the storm hits, it will be very difficult to respond to distress calls.\" Irene is some 700 miles wide now and moving at a slow 12mph, which means it can wreak extensive damage in a region over a long period of time; it could cause up to $13.9 billion in damage on the East Coast. The storm is expected to barrel into New York City on Sunday, packing winds of up to 90mph. New Jersey communities are already being evacuated, and hundreds of street fairs have been canceled and elder care facilities and hospitals in low-lying area will be evacuated in New York today. NYC Mayor Michael Bloomberg says residents in low-lying parts of the city should get out today as the transit system is planned to be shut down tomorrow."], "length": 4070, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Hurricane Irene Brings Widespread Destruction and Disruption Across the Eastern Seaboard**\n\nHurricane Irene, a Category 3 storm, is expected to make landfall along North Carolina's Outer Banks on Saturday, bringing widespread destruction and disruption to the Eastern Seaboard. The storm has already caused significant damage and flooding in the Bahamas, and officials have issued hurricane warnings and evacuation orders for several states, including North Carolina, Virginia, Maryland, New Jersey, New York, Delaware, and Connecticut.\n\nThe National Hurricane Center has warned of tidal surges of 5 to 10 feet high in North Carolina, accompanied by \"destructive and life-threatening waves.\" Projections show Irene making landfall between Morehead City, N.C., and Cape Hatteras before pushing north. The storm is expected to inundate the state's coastal areas with 6 to 10 inches of rain, and up to 15 inches in some locations.\n\nAs the storm approaches, officials have set up emergency shelters inland, and the Federal Emergency Management Agency has established a depot for food, water, generators, baby formula, and other emergency supplies. At least 200,000 tourists and residents are affected by evacuation orders in North Carolina alone, with states to the north rushing to prepare their own evacuation plans.\n\nNew York City officials have advised residents to stay out of parks due to the high winds, and Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg has urged all New Yorkers to stay indoors and avoid travel. The Martin Luther King Jr. National Memorial has been postponed indefinitely.\n\nThe storm is expected to bring severe road flooding and widespread electrical outages, especially in the Northeast, where the ground is saturated from recent rains. The National Hurricane Center has warned of the storm's potential to cause widespread damage, with estimated losses of up to $20 billion.\n\nAs the storm approaches, officials are urging residents to take the storm seriously and to follow evacuation orders. The storm is expected to make landfall on Saturday, bringing with it powerful winds, heavy rain, and potentially catastrophic flooding.\n\n**Key Statistics:**\n\n* Category 3 storm with sustained winds of 115 mph\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A Florida police chief got arrested Friday for allegedly soliciting a prostitute and was promptly fired just 10 months into his tenure, NBC Miami reports. According to an arrest report, Miami Gardens Police Chief Stephen Johnson called a number in a \"two-girl special\" prostitution ad and negotiated a $100 deal for two women for 30 minutes. Only problem: The woman he allegedly paid at a motel was really a sheriff's detective. \"The stress overwhelmed me, and I made a very bad decision,\" Johnson tells the Miami Herald in his apology. He was hired last May to repair community relations after former top cop Matthew Boyd resigned amid allegations of harassment and illegal tactics. \"It was one of the most painful things I've ever heard as a manager,\" says the city manager who fired Stephens, the Sun-Sentinel reports. \"It's like a punch in the stomach.\""], "length": 2365, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Miami Gardens Police Chief Stephen Johnson was arrested on February 27, 2015, for soliciting a prostitute as part of an undercover sting operation by the Broward County Sheriff's Office. Johnson, 53, was immediately fired from his position and was being held on a $300 bond. According to the arrest report, Johnson had called an escort ad on backpage.com and arranged to pay $100 for 30 minutes with two prostitutes at a Dania Beach hotel. When he arrived at the hotel room, he was let in and handed over the $100, and two condoms were found in his pocket.\n\nJohnson's arrest was met with swift action, with City Manager Cameron Benson firing him within hours of the news breaking. Assistant Chief Antonio Brooklen was named the interim police chief. Johnson apologized for his actions, citing stress on the job as the reason for his poor judgment. However, his arrest has thrust the police department into a negative spotlight, coming just days after protesters marched into City Hall to express their anger over the fatal police shooting of a mentally ill man.\n\nJohnson's tenure as police chief was marked by controversy, including allegations of racial profiling and harassment. He had previously served as police chief in North Miami and city manager, and had been criticized for his handling of the city's reputation. Despite his efforts to repair the relationship between the police department and the community, his arrest has raised questions about his judgment and leadership.\n\nThe incident has also raised concerns about the police department's ability to maintain a professional image, particularly in light of the recent shooting death of a mentally ill man. The city and police department have vowed to move forward and continue to serve the community, but the incident has left a lasting impact on the department's reputation."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["At least eight people were arrested yesterday in Detroit for protesting water shutoffs in a city where almost 40% of residents live below the poverty line. Dozens picketed in front of a facility affiliated with the city's Water and Sewerage Department to try to stop what activists call a “human rights violation,” reports Al Jazeera America. Police tell MLive that the individuals charged with misdemeanor disorderly conduct range in age from 58 to 70 and include members of the clergy. (Watch video of the protest and arrests here.) The Rev. Bill Wylie-Kellermann, one of the arrested protesters, tells the Detroit Free Press that police tried to \"move us forcibly, and we sat down. … We [were] here to appeal to the workers to stop shutting off the water.\" The coordinator of a local activist group says \"police officers should be arresting rapists and murderers instead of arresting peaceful people.\" A Detroit Police rep counters that protesters still have to follow rules: \"You cannot impede pedestrian or vehicle traffic.\" The Free Press counts 7,210 shutoffs in June, up from 7,556 in April and May combined, as part of what Al Jazeera reports is an effort to recover $175 million in unpaid bills. As of March, nearly half of the city's 323,900 DWSD accounts were delinquent; activists have asked the UN to intervene."], "length": 1911, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Detroit Water Crisis: Protests and Controversy Over Shutoffs**\n\nThe Detroit Water and Sewerage Department (DWSD) has been facing intense criticism and protests over its plan to shut off water services to tens of thousands of nonpaying customers across the city. The department claims it needs to recoup $175 million in outstanding bills, but critics argue that the approach is unfair and targets the most vulnerable residents.\n\nIn recent weeks, activists have resorted to civil disobedience, holding protests and demonstrations outside DWSD offices and blocking trucks carrying shut-off equipment. At least nine activists were arrested on Thursday for disorderly conduct, but the protests are expected to continue. The DWSD has announced a $1 million fund to help low-income customers avoid shutoffs, but many activists believe it is too little, too late.\n\nThe controversy has drawn international attention, with the United Nations calling the water shutoffs a human rights violation. The DWSD has been accused of ignoring commercial deadbeats, with some large entities owing tens of thousands of dollars in back payments. The department has released a list of 40 commercial and industrial accounts with the highest delinquent balances, including a golf course and the State of Michigan.\n\nDetroit police have arrested eight people, including religious leaders, who were protesting the water shutoffs. The arrests were for misdemeanor disorderly conduct, and the protesters were blocking the gates of a company contracted by the DWSD to shut off water to delinquent customers.\n\nThe crisis has also sparked calls for federal action, with Detroit congressman John Conyers requesting \"immediate federal action\" in the form of funding for the problem. The DWSD is reportedly responsible for about $6 billion of Detroit's $18 billion debt, and the water shutoffs have exacerbated the city's poverty rate, which stands at 44%.\n\nAs the situation continues to unfold, activists and critics remain united in their opposition to the DWSD's approach, arguing that it is unfair and targets the most vulnerable residents. The protests and demonstrations are expected to continue, with many calling for a more equitable and sustainable solution to the city's water crisis."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["There were two men at the top of this year's Simon Wiesenthal Center most-wanted Nazi war criminal list—and there is now no chance either one will face a court. No. 2 on the list, Ukrainian-born Vladimir Katriuk, has died in Quebec at the age of 93, the Toronto Star reports. Katriuk, who moved to Canada in 1951 and worked as a beekeeper for more than 50 years, was accused of being a \"particularly active participant\" in a massacre in what is now Belarus in 1943, when he was a member of an SS battalion that allegedly carried out many atrocities, the Guardian reports. Russia charged Katriuk with genocide last month, but Canadian authorities refused a request to send him to Moscow, citing Russia's actions in Ukraine, reports the Globe and Mail. Katriuk had long been under suspicion, but the wheels of justice did not move quickly: In 1999, Canadian authorities concluded he had entered the country under false pretenses, but in 2007 a decision to cancel his citizenship was overturned due to lack of evidence, the Star reports. The man at the top of the most-wanted list is also 93 years old. Gerhard Sommer is still alive in a Hamburg-area nursing home, but German prosecutors say they've dropped their investigation because he suffers from dementia and isn't fit to stand trial, the AP reports. In 2005, he was one of 10 former SS officers an Italian court found guilty of taking part in a massacre of 560 civilians in 1944, but German law didn't permit his extradition."], "length": 2459, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Vladimir Katriuk, a 93-year-old Canadian resident and former member of the Ukrainian battalion of the Waffen SS, has died after a long illness. Katriuk was on the Simon Wiesenthal Center's list of most wanted Nazi war criminals and was accused of participating in a village massacre in 1943 in what is now Belarus. His death has sparked controversy, with the Russian Embassy in Ottawa calling for Canada to support a criminal case against him and the Canadian government refusing to extradite him.\n\nKatriuk had been living in Canada for over 60 years and had obtained Canadian citizenship under false pretenses, according to a 1999 Federal Court ruling. However, the court found no evidence that he participated in war crimes or atrocities against the civilian population during World War II.\n\nThe Russian Embassy in Ottawa had recently called on Canada to deliver Katriuk to Moscow so he could be tried for alleged war crimes, including the 1943 killing of civilians in Khatyn, Belarus. However, Canada has refused to recognize Moscow's annexation of Crimea and its interference in Ukraine, and has instead said it will never extradite Katriuk.\n\nJewish groups and the Ukrainian Canadian Congress have long urged the deportation of Katriuk, citing his alleged involvement in war crimes and atrocities against Jews and others during World War II. However, the Canadian government has refused to strip Katriuk of his Canadian citizenship.\n\nIn related news, a 93-year-old former SS officer suspected of being part of a wartime massacre of civilians in Italy has been shelved from investigation due to dementia. Gerhard Sommer had been found guilty in absentia by an Italian military court and sentenced to life in prison, but was never extradited.\n\nOverall, Katriuk's death has reignited controversy over his alleged involvement in war crimes and atrocities during World War II, and has highlighted the complexities of Canada's relationship with Russia and its handling of Nazi war criminals."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Google's parent company appears to be satisfied with how Sundar Pichai has performed during his four months as Google CEO, judging by the gargantuan $199 million equity award revealed in a securities filing. Alphabet stated that Pichai was awarded 273,328 Class C Google stock units on Feb. 3, bringing his holdings to around $650 million, reports the Wall Street Journal. The award makes Pichai the highest-paid CEO in America, according to the Guardian, which notes that Pichai's fortune is dwarfed by those of Google founders Larry Page and Sergey Brin, who are worth around $34 billion each, and by that of previous CEO Eric Schmidt, who's sitting on around $3 billion. (A former Google employee was awarded exactly $6,006.13.)"], "length": 773, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Google CEO Sundar Pichai Receives Massive Equity Award**\n\nIn a significant move, Alphabet Inc., the parent company of Google, granted CEO Sundar Pichai an equity award valued at nearly $200 million. The award, which was made in February 2014, vests in quarterly increments over the next four years, bringing Pichai's total equity stake in Alphabet to over $600 million.\n\nThe award is one of the largest by a U.S. company and makes Pichai the highest-paid chief executive in the US. The shares, worth $199 million at the time of the award, have a current value of around $182 million due to declines in Alphabet shares.\n\nPichai's new award is still far less than the net worth of Google's founders, Larry Page and Sergey Brin, who are worth $34.6 billion and $33.9 billion, respectively. However, it is a significant increase from his previous holdings, which were valued at around $650 million.\n\nThe award is also notable for its impact on Pichai's compensation package. The grant lifts his total compensation to over $1 billion, making him one of the richest executives in the world.\n\nOther notable executives at Alphabet also received significant awards, including Ruth Porat, the company's chief financial officer, who was handed shares worth around $38 million, and Diane Greene, who was awarded shares worth $42.8 million.\n\nThe awards have sparked controversy, with some critics arguing that the tax deal reached between Google and the UK government is unfair. Google has agreed to pay around $130 million in back taxes since 2005, which is a relatively low effective tax rate of 2-3%.\n\nOverall, Sundar Pichai's massive equity award is a testament to his growing influence and power at Google, and highlights the significant compensation packages being offered to top executives at the company."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["After a social media campaign—and an avalanche of bad publicity—a drug company has done a U-turn and agreed to give a dying 7-year-old boy an experimental drug that could save his life. The FDA allows \"compassionate use\"of unapproved drugs in such cases, but the Chimerix pharmaceutical company had insisted that giving the drug to Josh Hardy, who developed a bone marrow disorder after cancer treatment, would delay efforts to get it to market, CNN reports. Chimerix president Kenneth Moch says Josh will be the first patient in a pilot trial for the drug. \"Being unable to fulfill requests for compassionate use is excruciating, and not a decision any one of us ever wants to have to make,\" says Moch, who tells the Fredericksburg Lance-Star that he and his employees have received death threats amid a flood of pleas to help the Virginia boy. Josh is expected to receive the drug within 48 hours, though his mother fears he is running out of time. \"Even though he is frail, he has a very strong will about him,\" she tells Fox News. \"But things just keep stacking against him, and we just want to do everything we can to give him the opportunity to make a full recovery.\""], "length": 1614, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Breaking News: Chimerix Approves Life-Saving Drug for 7-Year-Old Cancer Patient**\n\nIn a heartwarming turn of events, Chimerix, a pharmaceutical company, has agreed to provide a potentially lifesaving drug to 7-year-old Josh Hardy, a cancer patient from Virginia. The drug, brincidofovir, was initially denied to Josh due to concerns that it would slow down the company's efforts to get the drug on the market.\n\nJosh, who is currently fighting for his life at St. Jude Children's Research Hospital in Memphis, was diagnosed with a rare form of kidney cancer at 9 months old. Over the years, cancer has turned up in his thymus, lung, and bone marrow, and each time he has beaten it. However, a bone marrow transplant left him without much of an immune system, and in February, doctors diagnosed him with an adenovirus that has ravaged his body.\n\nAfter days of pleading with Chimerix executives, Josh's parents received a call from the company's president, Kenneth Moch, informing them that the drug would be made available to Josh. The company has worked with the FDA to expedite additional trials, and Josh will be the first recipient of the drug in a clinical trial.\n\nThe news has sparked widespread attention and support for Josh and his family, with thousands of people taking to social media to express their support and raise awareness about the importance of compassionate use of experimental drugs. Online petitions, charity events, and even a special \"Joshburger\" were organized to show support for the family.\n\nChimerix's decision to provide the drug to Josh has been hailed as a victory for the family and a testament to the power of public pressure and advocacy. The company's president, Kenneth Moch, has praised the support of Josh's family and the public, saying that it has helped bring about a discussion about the need for additional clinical development of the drug.\n\nThe FDA has committed to working expeditiously with Chimerix on the design of a pivotal Phase 3 study that will continue the pilot study with Josh. The drug is expected to be administered to Josh on Wednesday morning, and the family is grateful for the support of the medical community and the public.\n\nThis remarkable story serves as a reminder of the importance of compassion and empathy in the medical community, and the impact that public pressure and advocacy can have on the lives of patients and families in need."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The latest disturbing Hollywood sexual misconduct allegation involves Tom Sizemore, an actor who has already faced drug and domestic violence charges. Multiple cast and crew members tell the Hollywood Reporter that Sizemore, best known for roles in war films including Black Hawk Down, was kicked off a Utah movie set in 2003 for allegedly touching an 11-year-old girl's genitals. They say the day after a Born Killers scene in which the girl sat on Sizemore's lap, she told her parents that he had touched her inappropriately, possibly putting his finger inside her. \"At one point her eyes got just huge, like she could've vomited. I was watching her,\" co-star Robyn Adamson says. \"Later, when I was told about what happened, I knew exactly what it was.\" \"There was never any doubt. He was this guy who was already known for making inappropriate comments, being drunk, being high,\" says production assistant Roi Maufas. \"Then this happens. Guys reached for hammers,\" he says. Producers say Sizemore, who was ordered to leave the set, denied the allegations. Crew members say the girl's parents spoke to police, but ended up not pressing charges, possibly out of fear of ruining her film career. She is now 26 and says she is considering legal action against both Sizemore and her parents. Sizemore, 55, was dropped by his management firm soon after the incident. Former manager Charles Lago tells USA Today that he is \"not surprised\" by the allegations. \"He's the most abhorrent person I've ever met in my life,\" Lago says."], "length": 2624, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Actor Tom Sizemore is facing allegations of child molestation after a 2003 incident on the set of the film \"Born Killers.\" According to The Hollywood Reporter, Sizemore, then 42, was told to leave the Utah set after an 11-year-old actress told her mother that he had touched her genitals during a photo shoot. The incident allegedly occurred during a promotional photo session for the film, where the young actress was seated on Sizemore's lap.\n\nThe incident was never reported publicly at the time, but it has come to light in the wake of the Harvey Weinstein sexual harassment-and-rape scandal. Sizemore has denied the allegations, but a dozen people involved with the production have confirmed that he was sent home over the incident.\n\nThe young actress, now 26, has recently hired a lawyer to explore legal action against Sizemore and her parents. Sizemore's management firm and talent agency dropped him shortly after the allegations surfaced, and he is currently represented by a new boutique firm.\n\nThe incident has sparked outrage among the cast and crew of the film, with many expressing shock and disgust at Sizemore's behavior. Production assistants and crew members have described Sizemore as a \"sleazebag\" who was known for making inappropriate comments and engaging in consistent behavior on set.\n\nThe film's producers have stated that they removed Sizemore from the set as soon as they heard about the allegations and reviewed the photographs from the portrait session, which found to be inconclusive evidence. Despite the allegations, Sizemore was invited back to the set for reshoots in Malibu a couple of months later.\n\nSizemore has a long history of alleged aggressive behavior toward women, including a conviction for battery against a woman in 2003 and multiple arrests for suspected battery. He has also struggled with a drug addiction and has been to rehab.\n\nThe incident has raised questions about the accountability of Hollywood's powerful and the culture of silence that has allowed such behavior to go unchecked for so long. As the industry continues to grapple with the aftermath of the Weinstein scandal, the allegations against Sizemore serve as a reminder of the need for greater transparency and accountability."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Cruz Riojas came from a troubled home: He was reportedly beaten by his stepfather, lived in a decrepit one-room lean-to with six other family members, and wore the same clothes to school every day, according to the Lubbock Avalanche-Journal. He also caused trouble in school and was known as a “holy terror,” says Linda Hooper, a teacher who first encountered Cruz in the early ‘80s, when he was 12 years old. But then an amazing thing happened: He started spending more time with Hooper, who would give him tasks to do around the classroom. He eventually began coming for visits at her home, where she lived with her husband and four daughters, often running the nearly eight miles between their two houses to get away from his stepfather. After an incident with his stepfather in 1983, Cruz’s mother asked if he could stay with the Hoopers for a few days till everything blew over. \"He never left\" after that, Hooper says. \"I had him from then on.\" He became a part of the Hooper family, taking on a paper route to make money and doing better in school, placing out of special education classes by the time he graduated. More than a decade later, he was still part of the Hooper family—but he wanted to make it official by asking the Hoopers to adopt him right before his 30th birthday. “He said he wanted to honor us, and it was a big honor,\" Hooper tells Today. \"I’ve always known he loved me, but this really showed he loved us.” The adoption went through in 1998, but Cruz \"was my son from the day I kept him,” Hooper says. The family is just now telling their story for the first time. (Click for the full article, or read the story of a police officer who found a baby alive at a murder scene—and adopted her 30 years later.)"], "length": 3066, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A remarkable story of love, perseverance, and redemption has unfolded in the lives of Linda Hooper, a dedicated elementary teacher, and Cruz Riojas, the student she adopted and raised as her own. The journey began in 1981 when Cruz, then 12, was sent to Hooper's classroom at Brown Elementary School in Lubbock, Texas. Initially, Cruz's behavior was challenging, with frequent outbursts and violent tendencies, which made it difficult for Hooper to connect with him.\n\nHowever, Hooper's unwavering dedication and compassion eventually broke through to Cruz, who was struggling with physical and emotional abuse at home. As Cruz began to open up to Hooper, he started to transform, and his behavior improved significantly. Hooper's home became a refuge for Cruz, where he received love, support, and a sense of belonging.\n\nIn 1982, Cruz's stepfather physically abused him, prompting Hooper to take him in permanently. Over the next several years, Cruz thrived under Hooper's care, excelling academically and developing a strong bond with his stepfather, Gale, a firefighter. Cruz's mother eventually relinquished custody, and Hooper and Gale formally adopted him in 1998.\n\nToday, Cruz, now 45, is a successful professional, married to Anel Montemayor, and a devoted father to two children. He credits Hooper and Gale with giving him a stable and loving home, which has enabled him to overcome his troubled past. Cruz's story is a testament to the power of human connection and the impact one person can have on another's life.\n\nHooper's selfless act of adopting Cruz has not only changed his life but also hers. She has said that she thinks she has done right in her life by adopting Cruz, and her love and devotion to him have been unwavering. The story of Linda Hooper and Cruz Riojas serves as a heartwarming reminder of the transformative power of love, kindness, and perseverance."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Donald Trump's campaign chief may have closer—and more illegal—ties to pro-Russia interests in Ukraine than he has let on, according to a New York Times report on secret ledgers that anti-corruption investigators have uncovered in Kiev. Investigators say the ledgers list $12.7 million in payments to Paul Manafort from the pro-Russia Party of Regions between 2007 and 2012, when he worked as a consultant for the party. It isn't clear whether Manafort actually received the cash, which investigators say is linked to a network that also bribed election officials. Manafort's lawyer says his client never received the payments and any suspicions are \"probably heavily politically tinged.\" In other coverage: The Hill reports that there doesn't appear to be any love lost between Manafort and former Trump campaign manager Corey Lewandowski: Lewandowski, who was fired after clashing with Manafort, tweeted a link to the NYT Ukraine story minutes after it first appeared. The AP reports that Trump plans to deliver a major foreign policy speech in Ohio Monday that will focus on \"realism,\" with policies including destroying ISIS without engaging in \"nation-building\"—and on new ideological tests for people seeking to enter the US. Politico reports that Trump targeted the media in a series of tweets Sunday, claiming that he would be beating Hillary Clinton by 20% if the \"disgusting\" media covered him honestly. \"I am not only fighting Crooked Hillary, I am fighting the dishonest and corrupt media and her government protection process,\" he tweeted. \"It is not 'freedom of the press' when newspapers and others are allowed to say and write whatever they want even if it is completely false!\" he added. The Wall Street Journal editorial board warned Sunday that Trump's \"window for a turnaround\" is closing and his GOP supporters now face a \"moment of truth\" before he is written off as a lost cause. \"The tragedy is that this is happening in a year when Republicans should win,\" they write."], "length": 2028, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Donald Trump's Campaign Continues to Face Challenges**\n\nThe 2016 US presidential campaign is heating up, with Republican nominee Donald Trump facing criticism from the media and his Democratic rival Hillary Clinton. Trump has been at odds with the media, accusing them of being \"disgusting\" and \"corrupt.\" He has also been under fire for his comments on immigration, national security, and his running mate Mike Pence.\n\n**Trump's Immigration and National Security Plans**\n\nTrump has proposed a new immigration policy, which would stop issuing visas in cases where adequate screenings can't be performed. He has also proposed creating a new, ideological test for admission to the country that would assess a candidate's stances on issues like religious freedom. Additionally, Trump has called for a change in counterterrorism policies, which his running mate Mike Pence has described as a \"change of direction.\"\n\n**Criticism of Trump's Campaign**\n\nThe media has been critical of Trump's campaign, with many outlets accusing him of being uninformed and lacking in experience. Vice President Joe Biden has also weighed in, calling Trump the \"most uninformed presidential nominee in history.\" Trump's former campaign manager Corey Lewandowski has also been critical of his successor, Paul Manafort, in a tweet that has sparked controversy.\n\n**Manafort's Ties to Ukraine**\n\nA New York Times investigation has revealed that Trump's top adviser, Paul Manafort, has ties to a large network that Ukrainian government investigators say was used to loot assets and influence elections. Manafort's involvement in Russian and Ukrainian politics has come under increasing scrutiny as the US election cycle's focus rests on the region.\n\n**Trump's Relationship with the Media**\n\nTrump has a strained relationship with the media, which he accuses of being \"disgusting\" and \"corrupt.\" He has also been critical of the media's coverage of his campaign, accusing them of putting \"false meaning\" into his words. However, many outlets have accused Trump of being dishonest and lacking in self-awareness.\n\n**Clinton's Campaign**\n\nHillary Clinton's campaign has been focusing on issues like jobs, education, and national security. She has also been trying to capitalize on Trump's promises to deport undocumented immigrants, with a new effort to tap into the political power of young, undocumented immigrants.\n\nOverall, the 2016 US presidential campaign is shaping up to be a contentious and unpredictable one, with both Trump and Clinton facing challenges from the media, their opponents, and their own supporters."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["An operation in London has given 9-year-old Kieran Sorkin a pair of ears—made from his ribs, no less. Born deaf with no ears, Kieran was able to hear because of an implanted hearing aid, but he still wanted ears. \"I want people to stop asking me questions,\" he told the BBC. \"I'd like just to look like my friends. I'd also like to be able to wear sunglasses and earphones.\" So surgeons traced an outline of Kieran's mom's ears, removed cartilage from the boy's ribs, and sliced, contoured, and sewed the cartilage. Then they put it in two skin pockets and vacuumed them into the shape of ears. They don't perform any function, but Kieran's plastic surgeon says they can deliver a huge confidence boost: \"If you can change the confidence of a patient at this young age, you can change their whole trajectory in life,\" he said. The ears should last, too, because they're made of cartilage rather than prosthetics, the Guardian reports. Every year about 100 British newborns lack one or both ears (it's called microtia), and the hospital Kieran went to performs the operation about 40 times a year. Scientists hope to one day make ears from the fat tissue of patients, instead of ribs. Kieran might have liked that, since he got so excited on seeing his new ears that he cried \"Wow!\" and giggled—which made his ribs hurt from the operation. (Click to read about a man who needed surgery to remove a tooth growing in his nose.)"], "length": 1651, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A groundbreaking surgery has given a 9-year-old boy, Kieran, a pair of ears created from his own ribs at Great Ormond Street Hospital. Born with bilateral microtia, a rare congenital deformity where the external ear is underdeveloped, Kieran had been struggling with his appearance and social interactions due to his deafness and missing ears.\n\nThe six-hour operation, led by consultant plastic surgeon Neil Bulstrode, involved harvesting cartilage from both sides of Kieran's chest and shaping it into frameworks for his ears. The new ears were then grafted onto Kieran's head under pockets of skin, and the skin was shaped to fit the contours of the new ear using a vacuum.\n\nThe surgery was primarily cosmetic, but it has had a significant impact on Kieran's confidence and quality of life. Before the operation, Kieran had struggled in school due to his appearance and had few friends. However, after the surgery, he has become more confident and is now able to wear sunglasses and earphones like his peers.\n\nThe surgery has also brought psychological benefits, with Bulstrode stating that it can change a patient's whole trajectory in life. The procedure is not invasive, and the new ears are made from cartilage, which should last for a long time.\n\nIn addition to Kieran's surgery, researchers at Great Ormond Street Hospital are working on a new approach to ear reconstruction using stem cells from a child's own fat tissue. This approach, which is still in its early stages, has the potential to be far less invasive than the current method of harvesting rib cartilage.\n\nKieran's family is overjoyed with the results of the surgery, and his parents, Louise and David, have expressed their gratitude to the medical team for giving their son a new lease on life. The surgery is a testament to the advancements in medical technology and the dedication of the medical team at Great Ormond Street Hospital."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["It looks like the honeymoon is over between Trump and Obama. \"Doing my best to disregard the many inflammatory President O statements and roadblocks. Thought it was going to be a smooth transition—NOT!\" Trump tweeted Wednesday. The Los Angeles Times notes Trump's use of \"1990s-style sarcasm.\" Trump had previously commended Obama on his commitment to a smooth transition of power, but a number of things have changed since then, the Washington Post reports. Many saw comments made by Obama during Tuesday's Pearl Harbor anniversary as directed at Trump. The president urged the US not to \"turn inward\" and to \"resist the urge to demonize those who are different.\" Trump is also upset with the Obama administration for allowing the UN to pass a resolution condemning Israeli settlements, as well as with Obama personally for saying in an interview he could have beaten Trump and won a third term. In addition, the White House is defending the Department of Energy for not complying with a request from Trump's transition team for the names of employees involved in climate policy, ABC News reports. Transition team spokesperson Sean Spicer at first said Trump's tweets \"speak for themselves, very clearly,\" but later complimented members of the Obama administration for being \"helpful and generous with their time.\""], "length": 1615, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "In recent days, President-elect Donald Trump has expressed frustration with the transition process, accusing the Obama administration of putting \"roadblocks\" in his way. Trump took to Twitter to express his concerns, stating that he was \"doing my best to disregard the many inflammatory President O statements and roadblocks.\" He also made several tweets criticizing the Obama administration's stance on Israel, including a speech planned by Secretary of State John Kerry on Middle East policy.\n\nTrump's assessment comes amid days of verbal sparring between the outgoing and incoming president. During a ceremony marking the 75th anniversary of the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor, Obama cautioned against \"the urge to turn inward\" and stressed a need to \"resist the urge to demonize those who are different.\" Trump responded by saying that Obama's comments were aimed partly at him.\n\nIn a separate development, former Secretary of State John Kerry is leaving office with a mixed legacy. While he achieved several successes, including the historic Iran nuclear deal and a landmark climate change treaty, he also faced criticism for his inability to halt the carnage in Syria and block Russia's growing influence.\n\nDespite the tensions, President-elect Trump said he had a \"nice conversation\" with President Barack Obama on Wednesday, following a string of tweets in which he expressed frustration with the transition process. Trump also emphasized that he will hold a press conference in early January and that the transition of power is going smoothly.\n\nThe Obama administration has repeatedly committed to a peaceful and smooth transition of power, and Obama has said that he will escort Trump to the Capitol and attend his inauguration on January 20. However, the tensions between the two men remain, and it remains to be seen how they will work together in the coming months."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Pandas may prefer their meals in the form of bamboo, but that doesn't mean their bite is toothless. A man in China recently received a harsh reminder of that after a wild panda bit his leg after authorities pursued the critter onto his lawn, reports AFP via Yahoo. The creature had made its way into a northwestern village, which is near a sanctuary that's home to about 100 wild pandas. \"I saw a panda jump out in front of me, its body completely covered in mud,\" the man told local news. A struggle ensued, in which the bear wouldn't release the man's leg until a rescuer wrapped a coat around the animal's head. Finally, it fled—but not before causing injuries which, China Daily reports, led to eight surgeries over the past year and may lead to amputation. The man sued three government departments and won an $83,000 payout that will cover his medical costs, his lawyer says. (China Daily has a pretty gruesome photo of the injury.) \"As cuddly as they may look, a panda can protect itself as well as most other bears,\" the World Wildlife Fund notes, per AFP. Indeed, panda attacks have led to some nasty consequences in the past."], "length": 794, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Chinese Villager Receives Compensation for Wild Panda Attack**\n\nA Chinese villager, Guan Quanzhi, has been awarded over $80,000 in compensation for an attack by a wild panda in March last year. The incident occurred when the panda, which was being chased by other villagers, ran onto Guan's land and bit him in the leg, requiring seven hours of surgery. Despite the severity of the attack, Guan's lawyer, Wang Chaohui, stated that the compensation of 520,000 yuan ($83,000) will cover his medical bills.\n\nThe attack highlights the potential dangers of wild pandas, which are known to be members of the bear family and have a fearsome bite. Despite their cuddly appearance, pandas are capable of inflicting serious injuries. In fact, the World Wildlife Fund (WWF) notes that a panda bite can be \"very nasty\" and that they use their heavy weight, strong jaw muscles, and large molar teeth to defend themselves.\n\nThe incident also underscores the importance of conservation efforts to protect the giant panda, which is a major generator of tourist revenue in several parts of China. Conservation measures have been credited with a nearly 17% increase in the number of wild giant pandas over the decade to 2013, with the current population reaching 1,864.\n\nThe compensation awarded to Guan is a significant step towards acknowledging the risks associated with wild pandas and providing support to those affected by their attacks. However, more needs to be done to ensure the safety of both humans and pandas in areas where they coexist."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Authorities are combing through more than 1,200 leads in a desperate search for a 9-year-old girl they say was abducted by her uncle May 4, WATE reports. According to the Knoxville News Sentinel, 57-year-old Gary Simpson picked Carlie Trent up from her Tennessee school, telling staff her father had been in an accident. Shortly before, he purchased a child's nightgown, bikini, girls' underwear, lipstick, and nail polish at an area Walmart. The Tennessee Bureau of Investigation has pictures of those items. Carlie's mother Shannon Trent tells People she felt \"sick to my stomach\" when she heard about those purchases. \"I don't know what he's done to her,\" she says. \"I don't think he would hurt her, but if he's capable of kidnapping a child he's capable of anything.\" Authorities believe Carlie is in \"imminent danger.\" Simpson also purchased camping supplies and nonperishable groceries, and authorities think he may be hiding with Carlie in an isolated area. The TBI says there have been rumors online that Simpson is trying to protect Carlie, but it says that couldn't be further from the truth. \"This was not an innocent camping trip, this was a crime,\" a spokesperson tells WATE. Simpson, Carlie's uncle by marriage, and his wife once had custody of Carlie, but her father James Trent had custody at the time of her abduction. Shannon Trent, who hasn't had custody of Carlie in two years, says she \"always had a bad feeling\" about Simpson. \"I should have stuck with my gut,\" she tells People. (This boy's summer with his dad turned into his kidnapping.)"], "length": 2314, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Missing 9-Year-Old Carlie Trent: A Developing Story**\n\nA disturbing case of child abduction has unfolded in Tennessee, with 9-year-old Carlie Trent missing since May 4th. Carlie was taken from her elementary school in Rogersville by her uncle, Gary Simpson, who has been identified as a suspect in the case.\n\n**Investigation and Rewards**\n\nThe Tennessee Bureau of Investigation (TBI) has launched an investigation into the disappearance, with a $10,000 reward offered for information leading to Carlie's safe return. Two local banking executives, Matt Daniels and Tim Williams, have also offered a $10,000 reward, and the U.S. Marshals Service is offering a $5,000 reward.\n\n**Suspect Profile and Motive**\n\nGary Simpson, 57, is Carlie's uncle by marriage and had previously been given custody of her. However, authorities believe that the kidnapping was premeditated, and Simpson has been linked to several suspicious purchases, including girls' underwear, a bikini, and camping gear. Simpson was last seen driving a white 2002 Dodge Conversion Van with Tennessee registration number 173GPS.\n\n**Search Efforts and Amber Alerts**\n\nAn Amber Alert has been issued in Tennessee, and authorities have been searching for Carlie in 11 states, including Missouri, where a similar alert was issued after a possible sighting. However, the Missouri alert has since been canceled. The TBI has also reached out to state park officials and private campgrounds in the area, urging property owners to keep an eye out for any suspicious activity.\n\n**Family's Plea and Concerns**\n\nCarlie's mother, Shannon Trent, has expressed her concerns and frustration with the situation, stating that she \"always had a bad feeling\" about Simpson. She has urged Simpson to come forward and has appealed to the public to help find her daughter. James Trent, Carlie's father, believes that Simpson has an obsession with his daughter and is worried about what he may be putting in her head.\n\n**Current Status**\n\nAs of now, Carlie remains missing, and authorities are continuing to investigate the case. The TBI has received over 1,200 leads, but so far, none have been deemed credible. The public is urged to remain vigilant and to report any information to the TBI or the Rogersville Police Department.\n\nThe search for Carlie Trent continues, and authorities are working tirelessly to bring her home safely."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["CBS is scrapping its Early Show team, replacing co-hosts Harry Smith and Maggie Rodriguez with the anchors of the show's Saturday edition, Chris Wragge and Erica Hill. Other more minor players also are leaving in the switch, which takes place early next year, the LA Times reports. The show has been consistently stuck in third place, well behind Today and Good Morning America. Smith, who survived the last major overhaul of the show in 2002, will stick with the network as Katie Couric's main backup on the CBS Evening News, notes AP. Click here for more."], "length": 1319, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "CBS News has made significant changes to its morning show, \"The Early Show.\" The network has given the show a complete makeover, replacing co-hosts Harry Smith and Maggie Rodriguez with a new team consisting of Chris Wragge and Erica Hill, who currently anchor the Saturday edition of the show. Jeff Glor, who has been anchoring the network's Saturday newscast, will also join the team as a regular news reader. Marysol Castro, formerly of ABC's \"Good Morning America\" weekend edition, will serve as a weather anchor.\n\nHarry Smith, who had the longest tenure on the show, will become the primary substitute anchor for Katie Couric's evening newscast, \"Face the Nation\" and \"Sunday Morning.\" Dave Price, who has been a weather forecaster on the show, will be replaced by Marysol Castro. The new team is set to take over on January 3.\n\nThe changes are an attempt to freshen up the show and improve its ratings, which have consistently ranked behind the morning champ, NBC's \"Today\" show, and ABC's \"Good Morning America.\" CBS News President Sean McManus believes the new team will help the show grow, citing their chemistry and the advantage of introducing new personalities to the morning genre.\n\nHowever, some critics have expressed skepticism about the suddenness of the changes, with veteran television critic David Bianculli calling it a \"bloodbath.\" Bianculli believes that anything done quickly smells of desperation, and that the changes may not be enough to break the show out of third place.\n\nDespite the criticism, the new team is expected to bring a fresh energy to the show, and CBS News is optimistic about its future prospects. The changes mark a significant shift in the network's morning lineup, and it remains to be seen whether the new team will be able to improve the show's ratings and competitiveness."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["With George HW Bush dead at 94, coverage of the life of the 41st president is plentiful. Here are some early highlights: The broad strokes: For a thorough obituary and assessment, start with the Washington Post. Its story notes that while Bush served just one term as president, it was a consequential one. \"The Berlin Wall fell; the Soviet Union ceased to exist; the communist bloc in Eastern Europe broke up; the Cold War ended.\" And Bush's \"firm, restrained diplomatic sense helped assure the harmony and peace with which these world-shaking events played out, one after the other.\" Great detail: In its obituary, the Wall Street Journal notes that Bush flew 58 missions in the Pacific as a Navy pilot in WWII, all with the name of his girlfriend—Barbara—on the side of the plane. He once got shot down, with this video capturing the rescue. Bush is also the only American besides John Adams to be both president and the father of a president. Read his lips: The Guardian recounts some of his Bush's famous quotes, including his \"read my lips\" pledge to not raise taxes. He also dissed broccoli."], "length": 2660, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Former US President George H.W. Bush has passed away at the age of 94. Bush led the US to a swift and decisive victory in the first Persian Gulf War and presided over the peaceful dissolution of the Soviet Union and unification of Germany. He served as the 41st President of the United States from 1989 to 1993 and was the father of President George W. Bush.\n\nBush's presidency was marked by a series of significant events, including the Gulf War, the collapse of the Soviet Union, and the unification of Germany. He also played a key role in the diplomatic efforts that led to the peaceful resolution of the conflict in Nicaragua and the reunification of Germany.\n\nBush's time in office was also marked by a series of challenges, including a recession and a weak economy. He lost his re-election bid to Democrat Bill Clinton in 1992, but his legacy has been reevaluated in recent years.\n\nIn a statement, President Trump remembered Bush as a \"great leader\" and a \"devoted public servant.\" Bush is survived by his wife, Barbara, who passed away earlier this year at the age of 92.\n\nBush's life and legacy were marked by a series of notable events and achievements. He served in World War II, flying 58 missions as a young pilot, and later became a businessman and politician. He served as a congressman, ambassador to China and the United Nations, and director of the CIA before becoming vice president under Ronald Reagan.\n\nAfter leaving office, Bush remained active in public life, working on various charitable and diplomatic efforts. He also developed a close friendship with former President Bill Clinton, with whom he worked on several projects, including tsunami and hurricane relief.\n\nBush's death marks the end of an era, and his legacy will be remembered for generations to come. He will be remembered as a dedicated public servant, a skilled diplomat, and a devoted family man."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["OJ Simpson's parole hearing in Nevada will be broadcast live at 1pm EDT Thursday and there's a strong chance that his path to freedom will be clear by the end of the afternoon. Nobody has registered to testify against the 70-year-old Simpson, who has been in prison for nine years on 12 convictions related to a Las Vegas robbery and will appear before the Nevada Parole Board via videolink, NPR reports. Thursday's hearing will cover four concurrent sentences for use of a deadly weapon and two consecutive sentences for assault with a deadly weapon, the Las Vegas Review-Journal reports. Four board members will vote after the hearing and Simpson could be free by Oct. 1 if they are all in favor of parole. If Simpson is indeed released this fall—which appears almost certainly to be the decision—it will \"renew the rabid curiosity\" in the case and \"produce a spectacle unlike anything we’ve seen since he went behind bars,\" Nancy Armour at USA Today predicts. There has even been talk that Simpson might end up with a reality TV show, but Armour wants Simpson to avoid the spotlight unless he wants to return to the \"toxic mentality that landed him in prison in the first place.\" If the former NFL star does end up with a TV show, the money is likely to go to the families of murder victims Nicole Brown Simpson and Ronald Goldman, CBS News reports. He will still receive an NFL pension estimated at $25,000 per month."], "length": 3982, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "O.J. Simpson, the former NFL star and actor, is set to appear before a Nevada judge on Thursday to make his case for parole. If granted, Simpson could be released from prison as early as October 1, ending his nearly nine-year sentence for armed robbery and other charges. Simpson was convicted in 2008 for his role in a botched robbery of sports memorabilia collectors in Las Vegas.\n\nSimpson's parole hearing comes nearly two decades after he was acquitted of the murders of his ex-wife, Nicole Brown Simpson, and her friend, Ronald Goldman, in a highly publicized trial known as \"the trial of the century.\" The acquittal was met with widespread controversy, with many believing that Simpson had gotten away with murder.\n\nDespite his acquittal, Simpson was found liable for the murders in a civil trial in 1997 and was ordered to pay $33.5 million in damages to the families of Simpson and Goldman. Simpson has been serving his sentence at the Lovelock Correctional Center in Nevada, where he has been described as a model inmate.\n\nSimpson's release from prison would be a significant development in his long and complex history, which includes a successful football career, a string of high-profile acting roles, and a reputation as a charismatic and charming figure. However, his release would also raise questions about his character and whether he has truly changed since his conviction.\n\nThe parole hearing is expected to be closely watched, with media outlets and the public eager to see how Simpson will present himself and whether he will be granted parole. If granted, Simpson would be able to live on his NFL pension, which is estimated to be around $25,000 per month, and would be free to pursue his interests and activities once again.\n\nOverall, O.J. Simpson's parole hearing marks a significant milestone in his long and complex history, and raises important questions about his character, his past actions, and his future prospects."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Alaska yesterday became the third state to legalize marijuana for recreational use, and now a country where you would have thought that was the case already is moving closer to that goal itself. Jamaica's parliament last night approved a law decriminalizing small amounts of pot, the AP reports. People found with 2 ounces or less of marijuana will now simply receive a ticket—not a crime on their record—and cultivation of five plants or fewer is now allowed. A licensing agency was also established to oversee pot cultivation and distribution for medical and scientific efforts. Rastafarians are rejoicing, because the bill also grants them the legal right to use cannabis for sacramental purposes, while tourists who have medical marijuana prescriptions elsewhere can pay for permits to buy a bit of ganja on the island. Jamaica has shied away from decriminalization because it didn't want to risk violating international treaties and provoking US sanctions, the Guardian reports. The process for the new law was described by National Security Minister Peter Bunting as an \"elephantine,\" nearly 40-year effort, ABC Australia reports. \"[The law] eliminates an unnecessary source of friction between police and citizens, and ensures that our young people are not gratuitously shackled with criminal records,\" he said in a statement. Jamaica also hopes to boost its health tourism and medical marijuana industries and make it a major player in the pot product market, the Guardian notes. International exporting, however, remains a no-no. A US counternarcotics official told the AP in an email that \"Jamaican law is of course Jamaica's own business,\" but warned that drug trafficking into the US is still illegal."], "length": 1853, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Jamaica has taken a significant step towards decriminalizing marijuana, with its parliament passing legislation that reduces possession of small quantities to a petty offense. The law, which was passed after decades of debate, makes possession of up to 2 ounces of marijuana a non-arrestable offense that can result in a fine. Cultivation of five or fewer plants on any premises is also permitted.\n\nThe legislation paves the way for the development of a lawful industry for medical ganja and industrial hemp, and allows for the use of marijuana for religious, medical, scientific, and therapeutic purposes. Rastafarians can now legally use marijuana for sacramental purposes, and tourists prescribed medical marijuana abroad will be able to apply for permits to legally buy small amounts of Jamaican weed.\n\nThe move is seen as a significant step towards easing restrictions on marijuana in the Caribbean region. Jamaica's decision is part of an international trend of easing restrictions on marijuana for medical or personal use, with over 20 US states allowing some form of medical marijuana and several countries in the Americas decriminalizing or legalizing marijuana.\n\nHowever, the US has expressed concerns that Jamaica's decision could lead to an increase in marijuana trafficking into the country. The US assistant secretary for counter-narcotics affairs stated that Jamaica and all states party to the UN Drug Conventions must uphold their obligations to combat and dismantle criminal organizations involved in drug trafficking.\n\nDespite these concerns, Jamaica's decision is seen as a positive step towards reducing the stigma associated with marijuana use and promoting the development of a lawful industry for medical ganja and industrial hemp. The country's commerce minister has stated that the cannabis industry holds \"great potential\" for Jamaica, and local scientists have already developed marijuana-derived products, such as \"Canasol,\" which helps relieve pressure in the eyes of glaucoma patients.\n\nOverall, Jamaica's decision to decriminalize marijuana is a significant step towards promoting public health and safety, and reducing the negative impacts of the war on drugs on communities in the Caribbean region."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Search teams in Indonesia may have found the main fuselage of the AirAsia plane where most of the remaining victims and the plane's black boxes are expected to be. Authorities found \"four big parts of the plane we're looking for,\" says the official in charge of the search in the Java Sea, reports Reuters. The biggest of the pieces is about 59 feet long and 18 feet wide, reports AP. The pieces have only been detected, not recovered, and divers hope conditions will permit them to reach the objects tomorrow. So far, only 30 of 162 bodies have been recovered, and most of the victims are believed to be still strapped in their seats. Authorities also said today that AirAsia was in violation of its license by flying to Singapore on a Sunday, the day of the crash, reports the Telegraph. As a result, the airline might have its license revoked in Indonesia. There's still no conclusions yet in what caused the crash, but theories continue to center on stormy conditions. \"Flight 8501 appears to have been trapped in bad weather that would have been difficult to avoid,\" says a report by Indonesia's Meteorology, Climatology and Geophysics Agency."], "length": 2887, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "AirAsia Flight 8501, a passenger jet carrying 162 people, crashed into the Java Sea on December 28, 2014, resulting in the loss of all lives on board. The plane was flying from Surabaya, Indonesia's second-largest city, to Singapore when it disappeared from radar 40 minutes after takeoff. The cause of the crash remains unknown, but bad weather appears to have been a factor, according to a 14-page report released by Indonesia's Meteorology, Climatology and Geophysics Agency.\n\nSearch teams have been scouring the northern Java Sea and coastline of southern Borneo to recover the bodies of victims and locate the wreck of the plane. On January 3, 2015, Indonesian officials announced that they had found four massive objects on the ocean floor, believed to be parts of the plane. The largest object, measuring 18 meters (59 feet) long and 5.4 meters (18 feet) wide, appeared to be part of the jet's body.\n\nDespite the progress, recovery efforts have been hindered by strong currents and towering waves as high as 4 meters (13 feet). Only 30 bodies have been found floating in the choppy waters, and the search continues. The discovery of the wreckage, especially if it is largely intact, would greatly benefit the investigation.\n\nIndonesian authorities have announced the grounding of AirAsia flights from Surabaya to Singapore, citing a lack of permit to fly on Sundays. The airline has suspended all flights from Surabaya to Singapore until further notice. The crash was the airline's first, and it has quickly become a major investigation.\n\nThe search for the plane's black boxes, which contain crucial flight data and cockpit voice recorders, continues. The hope is that the body of the plane will still be largely intact, speeding the investigation. Autopsies will be carried out on some of the bodies, including the pilot and co-pilot, whose remains have not yet been recovered.\n\nThe families of the victims are waiting anxiously for news, and some have expressed frustration with the slow pace of the investigation. However, officials remain hopeful that the wreckage will be found soon, and that the cause of the crash will be determined."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The Telegraph reported yesterday on a crazy court case in the UK: After a pregnant Italian woman, in town for business, had a panic attack, social service workers in Essex got a court order allowing the woman to be forcibly sedated and undergo a C-section so they could take her baby. Fifteen months later, the little girl is still with social service workers, who won't return her to her mother. The case is now \"an international legal row,\" the Telegraph says, and the anonymous woman's lawyers call it \"unprecedented.\" The woman was in Britain in July 2012 for an airline training course, and called police when she suffered the panic attack. They arrived while she was on the phone with her mother, who told police the woman suffered from bipolar disorder and was off her medication, according to a Telegraph columnist. Police took her to a psychiatric facility, and restrained her under the Mental Health Act when she said she wanted to go back to her hotel. She underwent the C-section after having been there five weeks. The case is ongoing; the mother says she has made a full recovery, but a judge nonetheless ruled that her daughter should be put up for adoption. More on the case here and here."], "length": 325, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Internet Archive Efforts to Preserve Web Pages and Fix Broken Links**\n\nThe Internet Archive has launched a series of crawls to archive web pages as they are created, with the ultimate goal of preserving the internet's history and fixing broken links. This initiative aims to capture the original versions of web pages, which will remain intact even if the referenced pages are changed or removed from the web.\n\nBy archiving these pages, the Internet Archive hopes to provide a permanent link to the original content, allowing users to access the intended version of a page even if the original link is no longer valid. This effort is part of a broader goal to preserve the web's integrity and make it easier for users to navigate and access information.\n\nThe Internet Archive is specifically targeting \"No More 404\" sites, which are websites that are working to preserve their own content and prevent broken links. By partnering with these sites, the Internet Archive can help to ensure that their archived content remains accessible and accurate.\n\nOverall, the Internet Archive's efforts to archive web pages and fix broken links are an important step towards preserving the internet's history and making it easier for users to access the information they need."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The writ of habeas corpus lets prisoners appear in court to make their captors justify why they're being held. Until now in the US, those who've used this legal tactic have been human. But a New York judge yesterday ruled that the writ may be used by two lab chimpanzees at Stony Brook University, Science magazine reports. NY Supreme Court Justice Barbara Jaffe ordered a university rep to address a suit filed by the Nonhuman Rights Project by appearing in court May 6 to argue why the college is holding Hercules and Leo. The group, which says the animals are being detained for biomedical experiments, says its ultimate goal is to have the animals released to the Save the Chimps sanctuary in Fort Pierce, Fla., per the NhRP's website. \"This is a big step forward to getting what we are ultimately seeking: the right to bodily liberty for chimpanzees and other cognitively complex animals,\" Natalie Prosin, the project's executive director, tells Science. The case kicked off in December 2013 with lawsuits that also included two chimps held on private property; each case was struck down, sending the NhRP down the appeals path. Not everyone's convinced the upcoming hearing will mean liberation for the chimps. A Pepperdine University law professor tells Science that the judge likely just wants to hear both sides of the story: \"It would be quite surprising if the judge intended to make a momentous substantive finding that chimpanzees are legal persons\" without doing so, he notes. Prosin, meanwhile, says her group isn't stopping with the chimps and will also target the release of other animals. (Click to read about what chimps talk to each other about.)"], "length": 1721, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\nIn recent weeks, two significant developments have shed light on the importance of fundamental rights and the treatment of non-human animals.\n\n**Habeas Corpus and Guantanamo Detainees**\n\nThe US government's revocation of habeas corpus for detainees at Guantanamo Bay has sparked concerns about executive power and the erosion of civil liberties. The Military Commissions Act of 2006 suspended the right to habeas corpus for individuals detained at Guantanamo, including legal residents of the US. However, a recent Supreme Court decision has paved the way for the detainees to challenge their detention, with the court set to hear their cases again in the coming term.\n\n**Chimpanzees and Habeas Corpus**\n\nIn a groundbreaking decision, a New York judge has granted a pair of research chimpanzees, Hercules and Leo, the right to have their day in court. The ruling, which recognizes chimpanzees as legal persons, has significant implications for the treatment of non-human animals in captivity. The Nonhuman Rights Project, which filed the case, has hailed the decision as a major victory and plans to use it to sway judges in other jurisdictions.\n\n**Implications and Future Developments**\n\nThe habeas corpus case involving the chimpanzees has far-reaching implications for the treatment of non-human animals and the limits of executive power. If the US government is willing to recognize chimpanzees as legal persons, it raises questions about the status of other non-human animals, including elephants, great apes, and whales and dolphins. The Nonhuman Rights Project plans to file additional cases in the coming year, with the goal of securing bodily liberty for these animals.\n\nOverall, these developments highlight the importance of protecting fundamental rights and challenging the status quo in areas where non-human animals are treated as property or subject to arbitrary detention."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Police in three states are still hunting for a Range Rover whose occupants opened fire on another car on the Vegas Strip this morning and set off a deadly chain reaction. Cops don't know most of the details, but they say it started with a dispute of some kind in one of the nearby hotels, then spilled into the street, reports the Las Vegas Sun. When the Range Rover caught up to a Maserati at a stop light, shots were fired from the SUV that sent the Maserati crashing into a taxi, which exploded upon impact. Three people—the Maserati driver, the taxi driver, and a taxi passenger—were killed, and three others were injured, reports the LA Times. Police are hoping to piece together more details by interviewing a passenger in the Maserati, who was injured in the melee. Earlier reports that gunfire came from both vehicles as part of a moving shootout were incorrect. The AP collects this quote from a tourist visiting from Manchester, England: \"This doesn't happen where we come from, not on this scale. We get stabbings, and gang violence, but this is like something out of a movie. Like Die Hard or something.\""], "length": 3450, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A deadly shooting occurred on the Las Vegas Strip early Thursday morning, leaving three people dead and at least six injured. The incident began with a dispute at a nearby hotel, which escalated into a violent confrontation involving two vehicles, a silver-gray Maserati and a black Range Rover SUV. Gunshots were fired from the SUV, hitting the Maserati, which then plowed into a taxi cab that exploded in a fireball on impact. The cab driver and passenger were killed, while the Maserati driver was pronounced dead at a hospital. A passenger in the Maserati was among the injured.\n\nThe SUV, which was being sought by authorities, had tinted windows, black tire rims, and paper dealer plates from out of state. Police have contacted authorities in three neighboring states to be on the lookout for the vehicle, which is believed to have been involved in a confrontation at a nearby hotel.\n\nThe incident has raised concerns about safety on the Las Vegas Strip, which is known for its vibrant nightlife and entertainment options. While there have been some incidents in recent weeks, this incident is the latest in a series of violent events to occur on the Strip.\n\nThe victims have been identified as Kenneth Wayne Cherry Jr., 27, who was driving the Maserati, and Michael Boldon, 45, who was driving the taxi cab. Cherry was an aspiring rapper, while Boldon was a taxi driver who had recently moved to Las Vegas to care for his mother.\n\nThe investigation into the incident is ongoing, with police reviewing video footage and interviewing witnesses. Authorities have warned that the suspects in the SUV are considered armed and dangerous, and are urging anyone with information to come forward.\n\nThe incident has also sparked a response from the Las Vegas Convention and Visitors Authority, which has assured visitors that the city is taking steps to ensure their safety. However, the incident has raised questions about the effectiveness of security measures on the Strip, and whether more needs to be done to prevent such incidents from occurring in the future.\n\nOverall, the shooting on the Las Vegas Strip is a tragic reminder of the dangers that can occur in even the most seemingly safe and secure environments."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A 16-year-old girl in Washington state is bruised and dehydrated but very much alive after surviving a plane crash and a long trek through what authorities describe as some of the state's most rugged terrain. Autumn Veatch was on a small plane with her step-grandparents that crashed after departing Kalispell, Mont., on Saturday afternoon, the Bellingham Herald reports. There was no sign of the plane or its occupants until a motorist found Veatch on State Route 20 yesterday afternoon and took her to a general store, where employees called authorities, the AP reports. She was hospitalized with minor injuries. Her father tells the Herald that after the plane crashed into a mountain, it caught fire and she was unable to get her step-grandparents out. She spent about a day near the plane waiting for rescue before following a stream and then a trail out of the woods, her father says. Friends say both father and daughter are completely overwhelmed. \"She did joke that it was a good thing she'd watched all those Survivor shows that she didn't like, but her dad made her watch anyway,\" a family friend tells the Seattle Times. Authorities are still searching for the missing Beech A-35. (In Colombia last month, a mother and baby survived four days in the jungle after a plane crash.)"], "length": 2134, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Teenager Survives Small-Plane Crash in North Cascades Mountains**\n\nA 16-year-old girl, Autumn Veatch, survived a small-plane crash in the North Cascades mountains of Washington state. The Beech A-35 plane, carrying Veatch and her two step-grandparents, Leland and Sharon Bowman, took off from Kalispell, Montana, on Saturday afternoon but failed to complete its flight to Lynden, Washington. The plane's last known position was near Omak, Washington, but it dropped off the radar about an hour later.\n\nVeatch, who was traveling with her step-grandparents, managed to survive the crash and spent two days in the wilderness before being found by a motorist who drove her to safety. She was picked up by a motorist near the east entrance to North Cascades National Park and taken to a general store in Mazama, where employees called 911. The Aero Methow Rescue Service sent a paramedic team to check her out, and she was later taken to a hospital in Brewster for treatment of minor injuries.\n\nVeatch's father, David Veatch, reported that she had been \"walking for a couple of days\" before being found. The girl's condition was described as \"somewhat dehydrated\" due to being out in the elements. The status of the other two people aboard the plane, Leland and Sharon Bowman, remained unclear.\n\nRescuers celebrated Veatch's survival but immediately returned their focus to finding the wreckage, aided by her description of the crash site. Five aircraft equipped with special radios for detecting the missing plane's emergency-locator transmitter searched the mountains on Monday, while ground crews focused on areas between Mazama and Rainy Pass.\n\nThe plane's last phone signal was detected around 3:50 p.m. on Saturday, and the crash site was located near the Easy Pass trail, a popular but difficult hike in the rugged mountain terrain. The Easy Pass trail has a gain in elevation of 2,800 feet and was originally called \"Easy\" Pass because it was the only place to put a trail across \"ragged ridges\" in the area.\n\nVeatch's survival has been hailed as a miracle, and her friends and family are overjoyed at her recovery. The incident has also raised questions about the safety of small-plane travel in the region and the effectiveness of search-and-rescue efforts."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Allegations of illegal wood imports prompted the Justice Department to raid Gibson Guitar facilities in Tennessee last week. NPR aired the complicated story involving the 100-year old Lacey Act, which prohibits imports of endangered species, including plants, into the US. No formal charges have been pressed, but it appears the issue lies with Gibson possibly importing banned Madagascar ebony. Gibson insists the wood confiscated by marshals was legally acquired rosewood from India. Adding some gusto to the story, right-leaning sites assert that Gibson rival CF Martin uses the same wood in some of its guitars, but the company was never investigated. The Landmark Report thinks that's fishy, given that Martin's CEO is a Democratic donor and Gibson's CEO is a GOP backer. Raising the octave even higher, it is possible Michelle Obama ran afoul of the Lacey Act when she presented France's Carla Bruni with a Gibson guitar that may have contained banned wood. However the story plays out, many are frustrated with the retroactive aspect of the law. \"It's a nightmare,\" says a dealer. \"I can't help it if they used Brazilian rosewood on almost every guitar made prior to 1970.\""], "length": 1352, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\nIn recent news, the US Justice Department has raided the Gibson Guitar Corporation in Tennessee, citing allegations of trafficking in illegally obtained wood. The raids, which occurred at two Nashville facilities and one in Memphis, were enforced under the Lacey Act, a century-old endangered species law amended in 2008 to include plants as well as animals.\n\nGibson Guitar CEO Henry Juszkiewicz has denied the allegations, stating that all of its purchases from Madagascar have complied with US and Malagasy law. However, the company has been implicated in wrongdoing, and a court motion filed in June asserts that Gibson's Madagascar ebony was contraband.\n\nThe Lacey Act requires end users of endangered wood to certify the legality of their supply chain all the way to the trees. Environmental Investigation Agency (EIA) director Andrea Johnson has concluded that Gibson knowingly imported tainted wood, despite taking steps to maintain a supply chain that was connected to illegal timber harvests.\n\nThe raids have sparked concerns among the guitar industry, with some companies worried about the retroactive nature of the Lacey Act and the potential for unintended consequences. Others, such as C.F. Martin Guitar Co., support the law and its efforts to reduce illegal logging.\n\nIn unrelated news, Alexa Internet has been donating its crawl data to the Internet Archive since 1996, with the data being added to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period.\n\n**Key Takeaways**\n\n* Gibson Guitar has been raided by the US Justice Department over allegations of trafficking in illegally obtained wood.\n* The Lacey Act is a century-old law that requires end users of endangered wood to certify the legality of their supply chain.\n* Gibson Guitar has denied the allegations, but a court motion asserts that the company's Madagascar ebony was contraband.\n* The guitar industry is concerned about the retroactive nature of the Lacey Act and its potential for unintended consequences.\n* Alexa Internet has been donating its crawl data to the Internet Archive since 1996."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A brief, planned ceasefire failed to materialize in Gaza today, as death continued to rain down on the strip. Israel had offered to hold its offensive to accommodate a visit from Egyptian Prime Minister Hesham Kandil, provided Hamas held its fire as well. But Hamas said it would keep firing, and one Israeli defense minister complained that 50 rockets had flown in from Gaza during Kandil's visit, CNN reports. A Hamas-run TV station reported that Israel had kept firing as well, but Israel says it took a break for at least two hours. In Palestine the death toll has risen to 21, including 13 civilians, Reuters reports. Among those civilians were seven children and a pregnant teenager. Israel's death toll held steady at 3, despite the hundreds of rockets fired into Israel. But Hamas did up the ante by firing rockets near Tel Aviv for the first time. Air-raid sirens sounded (the last time they did so was during the Gulf War) and civilians ran for cover, though the missiles exploded harmlessly, with one going into the sea. \"There will be a price for that escalation,\" Ehud Barak vowed, according to the New York Times. Israel has now hit 250 Gaza targets, while taking further steps toward a ground invasion, calling up 16,000 reservists, the Wall Street Journal reports. It's also steadily sending troops and armored vehicles to the border, notes CNN—between 1,500 and 2,000 are already there, one official said. Israel claims the attacks have weakened Hamas' military capabilities and caused its attacks to lighten, but Hamas denied that, saying it had hit multiple Israeli targets today. Kandil emerged from his meeting with Hamas expressing solidarity, saying the new Egyptian regime would be more active in helping the Palestinians. \"The time has changed,\" he said. \"No longer the Israeli occupation will be able to carry out their attacks against the Palestinians without being held responsible. That time is far bygone.\" For its part, the Obama administration says it's seeking help from Arab countries in calling on Hamas to relent. \"The onus rests squarely on Hamas ... to stop its rocket attacks,\" says a State Department spokesman. UN Secretary-General Ban Ki-Moon is planning diplomatic visits to Jerusalem, Ramallah, and Cairo within days."], "length": 5422, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Escalating Conflict in Gaza Strip**\n\nThe situation in the Gaza Strip has deteriorated further, with both Israel and Palestinian militants exchanging rocket fire and airstrikes. Israel's cabinet has authorized the mobilization of up to 75,000 reservists, preparing for a possible ground invasion of the Gaza Strip. The Israeli military has launched airstrikes and artillery attacks on suspected militant positions, while Palestinian militants have fired over 300 rockets at Israel.\n\n**Humanitarian Crisis**\n\nThe conflict has resulted in significant loss of life, with at least 19 Palestinians and three Israelis killed. Hundreds of Palestinians have been wounded, and many more are feared to be trapped under the rubble of destroyed buildings. The situation has also led to a humanitarian crisis, with schools and homes destroyed, and many residents forced to flee their homes.\n\n**International Response**\n\nThe international community has condemned the violence, with the United States, United Kingdom, and other countries expressing support for Israel's actions. However, some countries, including Egypt, have called for a ceasefire and an end to the violence. Egyptian President Mohammed Morsi has ordered a delegation to Gaza to try to broker a truce, and the United Nations has called for a de-escalation of the conflict.\n\n**Background**\n\nThe conflict has its roots in the Israeli-Palestinian conflict, with Hamas, a Palestinian Islamist group, having fired rockets at Israel in recent months. The situation has been exacerbated by the rise of the Muslim Brotherhood in Egypt, which has close ties to Hamas. The conflict has also been fueled by the Arab Spring, which has led to widespread protests and instability in the region.\n\n**Key Developments**\n\n* Israel's cabinet has authorized the mobilization of up to 75,000 reservists.\n* The Israeli military has launched airstrikes and artillery attacks on suspected militant positions.\n* Palestinian militants have fired over 300 rockets at Israel.\n* At least 19 Palestinians and three Israelis have been killed.\n* Hundreds of Palestinians have been wounded.\n* Egyptian President Mohammed Morsi has ordered a delegation to Gaza to try to broker a truce.\n* The United Nations has called for a de-escalation of the conflict.\n\n**Outlook**\n\nThe situation in the Gaza Strip remains volatile, with both sides showing no signs of backing down. The international community is calling for a ceasefire and an end to the violence, but it remains to be seen whether a resolution can be reached. The conflict has the potential to escalate further, with both sides risking significant loss of life and damage to infrastructure."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Rachel Borch was out for a jog in the woods near her home in Hope, Maine, when a \"ferocious-looking\" attacker with beady eyes and tiny teeth made a beeline for her, reports the Camden Herald. Borch, 21, knew immediately that something was wrong with the raccoon that was charging toward her. \"Imagine the Tasmanian devil,\" she tells the Bangor Daily News of the \"terrifying\" June 3 incident. Yanking out her headphones, she began \"dancing\" around the animal on the narrow path, but she tells the Herald she knew that, one way or another, the animal was going to bite her. She figured her hands would be the best spot, so she offered them up. The raccoon chomped down on her thumb and stayed there, scratching at her legs and arms as she screamed. Now on her knees, she spotted her phone lying submerged in a mud puddle, and had an idea: drown it. \"With my thumb in its mouth, I just pushed its head down into the muck,\" she tells the News. When the raccoon finally stopped moving, she yanked out her finger and raced the three-quarters of a mile home. Two days later, the state confirmed the animal was infected with rabies. Borch got rabies shots, and an animal control officer adds an unpleasant footnote: The infected raccoon may not be alone. \"Not to scare people,\" Heidi Blood tells the News, but \"when there’s one, there’s typically another.\" (Normally nocturnal like raccoons, a crazed beaver attacked a woman paddle-boarder.)"], "length": 1996, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Rabies Outbreak in Maine: Woman Attacked by Rabid Raccoon**\n\nA recent outbreak of rabies in Maine has left residents on high alert. The most recent incident involved a 21-year-old woman, Rachel Borch, who was attacked by a rabid raccoon while jogging in the woods near her home. The raccoon, which had been acting strangely, latched onto her thumb, causing her to drown it in a puddle of water.\n\nBorch, who was bitten on her thumb, received six shots of the rabies vaccine and immunoglobulin, and is expected to receive her last injection on Saturday. She has also been taking antibiotics for the puncture wounds on her hands.\n\nThe incident highlights the risk of rabies in Maine, particularly in wildlife. According to the Maine Center for Disease Control and Prevention, there have been 20 animals, including raccoons, red foxes, and skunks, that have tested positive for rabies in 2017. This is a significant increase from 2016, when 64 animals tested positive for the disease.\n\nThe Maine Warden Service and animal control officers are warning residents to be aware of the risk of rabies in wildlife, particularly in areas where animals may be acting strangely. They are also reminding people to avoid approaching or handling wild animals, even if they appear to be dead, as the rabies virus can live outside the body for 24 hours.\n\nThe incident has also raised concerns about the effectiveness of current rabies prevention measures. While vaccination is required for cats and dogs in Maine, there is no requirement for other animals, such as raccoons. Experts are calling for increased awareness and education about the risks of rabies in wildlife.\n\nIn related news, a Wiscasset man was bitten on both hands by a rabid fox in Topsham earlier this week. The incident highlights the importance of taking precautions when interacting with wildlife, particularly in areas where animals may be acting strangely.\n\nOverall, the recent outbreak of rabies in Maine serves as a reminder of the importance of taking precautions when interacting with wildlife and the need for increased awareness and education about the risks of rabies."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Imagine all the water in the Arctic Ocean. Now, imagine all that and more on Mars. If you visited the Red Planet roughly 4.3 billion years ago, that's what you likely would have found, say NASA scientists. Their new study arrived at that \"solid estimate ... by determining how much water was lost to space,\" says lead author Geronimo Villanueva. The Guardian reports the scientists used three powerful infrared telescopes to analyze two forms of water in Mars' atmosphere: H2O and HDO, in which deuterium (aka \"heavy\" hydrogen) has taken the place of one hydrogen atom. As National Geographic explains, Mars' gravity is weaker than our own; that allowed hydrogen to escape from the atmosphere and into space over time, boosting the amount of deuterium in its water. Scientists compared the ratio of HDO to H2O in Martian water today with that in water from a 4.5 billion-year-old Mars meteorite to arrive at their conclusions, a press release states. They ultimately calculated that Mars was once home to 20 million cubic kilometers of ocean. It could have covered every inch of the planet in water 450 feet deep, but the scientists think a different scenario is more likely: that the ocean covered roughly a fifth of the planet, was located in the northern hemisphere, and was as much as a mile deep. While National Geographic observes the findings back up \"reams of earlier evidence that water once existed on the surface\" of Mars, another study author points out the assessment of the degree of water lost indicates something new. \"The planet was very likely wet for a longer period of time than was previously thought, suggesting it might have been habitable for longer,\" a scientist says. (Mysterious plumes on Mars are stumping scientists.)"], "length": 1999, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Mars' Ancient Ocean Revealed: A Warm and Wet World in its Youth**\n\nRecent studies have shed new light on Mars' ancient past, revealing a warm and wet world that was once home to a massive ocean. Using advanced telescopes and analysis of water vapor in the Martian atmosphere, scientists have determined that an ocean at least a mile deep covered a significant fraction of the planet's surface four billion years ago.\n\nThe findings, published in the journal Science, suggest that Mars was once habitable, with long-standing bodies of water, lakes, deltas, and possibly even seas. The ocean, which covered nearly a fifth of the planet's surface, would have reached a depth of about 137 meters if it had covered the entire planet evenly.\n\nThe discovery was made possible by analyzing the chemical composition of the atmosphere's vapor, which revealed that Mars has a higher proportion of deuterium in its water than Earth does, seven times as high. This imbalance is due to the gradual loss of ordinary hydrogen from the atmosphere, which has been leaking into outer space.\n\nThe ancient ocean is believed to have formed in the northern hemisphere, which has a consistently lower altitude. The water would have been mostly in liquid form, making Mars a much warmer planet than it is today.\n\nThe findings have significant implications for the search for life on Mars. While there is no evidence to suggest that life existed on the planet, the discovery of a warm and wet world in its youth makes it possible that life could have emerged and thrived in the past.\n\nThe research was conducted by a team of scientists led by Geronimo Villanueva, of NASA's Goddard Space Flight Center, and includes data from the Keck II telescope, NASA's Infrared Telescope Facility, and the ESO's Very Large Telescope in Chile.\n\nThe discovery of Mars' ancient ocean is a significant breakthrough in our understanding of the planet's history and its potential for supporting life. As the search for life on Mars continues, this new information provides a fascinating glimpse into the planet's past and its possible habitability."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Four years ago, a chemistry professor got a text from her grad student: If I'm not back in a week, cut me from the doctoral program. Charlotta Turner called him right away: \"He was very sad and crying,” the 48-year-old prof at Lund University in Sweden tells NBC News. \"I could hear that the situation was hopeless and they had to flee.\" The student, Firas Jumaah, was visiting his native Iraq to help family members during a brutal 2014 ISIS attack targeting Yazidis—a religious minority that includes his family. The terror group had just enslaved and massacred Yazidis by the thousand in nearby Sinjar. Now Jumaah and family were planning to flee to the mountains. \"I had no hope at all,\" says Jumaah, per the Local. \"I was desperate.\" But Turner took action. She spoke to Lund University's then-security chief, who contacted a company that sent mercenaries into northern Iraq. Only days later, four armed mercs on two Landcruisers blazed into the place where Jumaah was hiding, and rushed him to Erbil Airport with his wife and two young kids. \"I have never felt so privileged, so VIP,\" he says. \"But at the same time I felt like a coward as I left my mother and sisters behind me.\" Seeing his colleagues back in Sweden, he was speechless: \"I just cried,\" he says. Yet Jumaah finished his PhD and found work at a Malmo pharmaceuticals company, and his family survived. The bill: roughly 60,000 kroner ($6,613), which his family has nearly finished paying. “If they told me to pay 200,000 kronor, I would,” says Jumaah. (The UN is finding fresh ISIS horrors.)"], "length": 1173, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Rescue of Yazidi Family from ISIS in Iraq**\n\nIn 2014, Swedish chemistry professor Charlotta Turner received a distressing text message from her graduate student Firas Jumaah, who was hiding in a disused bleach factory in Iraq with his family. Jumaah, a Yazidi, had fled his home country after ISIS attacked the city of Sinjar, massacring and enslaving thousands of Yazidis. Turner, who had been unaware of the situation, was determined to help her student and his family.\n\nAfter learning of the dire situation, Turner contacted the university's security chief, Per Gustafson, who arranged for a rescue operation. Over a few days, a security company hired by Gustafson sent two Landcruisers carrying heavily-armed mercenaries to the bleach factory, where Jumaah and his family were hiding. The family was rescued and flown to Erbil Airport, where they were reunited with Turner.\n\nThe rescue operation was a unique event, with no other university involved in a similar operation. Turner's actions were motivated by her concern for Jumaah's safety and her desire to protect her student from the violence. The family has since completed their PhD and is now living in Sweden, with the university having almost fully repaid the costs of the rescue operation.\n\nThe story highlights the bravery and selflessness of Turner, who risked her own safety to save her student and his family from the clutches of ISIS. The rescue operation is a testament to the power of human kindness and the importance of standing up against violence and oppression."}
