{"input": "", "answers": ["A woman believed to be in her 20s or 30s held employees of a Jared Vault jewelry store in Mebane, NC, at gunpoint on Monday before making off with jewelry piled into a shopping bag. But this wasn't her first rodeo: The FBI says the same brunette has robbed five other jewelry stores across the South since April, including in Florida, Georgia, South Carolina, and Tennessee, netting more than $450,000 worth of bling, per WNCN. A black man in his 30s or 40s, weighing about 250 pounds, was spotted by cameras during three of the robberies, reports NBC News. The FBI says both suspects \"are considered armed and dangerous.\""], "length": 786, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Armed Jewelry Store Robberies Sweep Southeast**\n\nA brazen jewelry store robbery spree has been terrorizing the Southeast, with a woman believed to be the mastermind behind the crimes. The suspect, described as a white female in her 20s or 30s, 5-foot-8, and 130 pounds, has been linked to six robberies in Florida, Georgia, South Carolina, Tennessee, and North Carolina since April.\n\nThe most recent heist occurred on Monday at a Jared Vault in Mebane, North Carolina, where the suspect forced two workers into a back room at gunpoint, zip-tied their hands, and made off with an undisclosed amount of jewelry. The FBI has released surveillance images of the suspect, warning that she is considered armed and dangerous.\n\nAccording to authorities, the suspect may be working with an accomplice, a black male in his 30s or 40s, 6 feet, and 250 pounds, who was caught on surveillance cameras in three of the robberies. The pair has been linked to jewelry store robberies in the South, netting over $450,000 worth of goods.\n\nThe robberies have been characterized by the suspect's use of a gun and zip-ties to subdue employees, and her ability to evade capture. Authorities are urging anyone with information about the suspects to come forward.\n\nThe investigation is ongoing, with police releasing a call to action for anyone with information to contact the FBI or local authorities. The suspect is described as standing about 5 feet 8, weighing about 120 pounds, and is considered armed and dangerous.\n\nThe jewelry store robberies have left a trail of destruction and financial loss in their wake, with authorities warning that the public should be vigilant and report any suspicious activity."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Modern Family star Ariel Winter has words for those who criticized the gold minidress she wore to an event for the show Wednesday night, People reports. Photos from the screening of the season eight finale showed that Winter's outfit stood out among the clothes worn by the other cast members who attended (Julie Bowen, for example, wore black slacks with a simple top, while Ty Burrell showed up in jeans). USA Today describes Winter's outfit like so: \"Mesh panels showed off her cleavage as well as the tops of her thighs.\" But some of the commentary on Winter's outfit went beyond simply calling the teen overdressed, with some going so far as to say she looked \"slutty.\" On Thursday night, Winter responded on Instagram. \"Why TF does anyone care that I didn't dress casual like everyone else for the panel? Why do I have to be like everyone else? Why can’t people just let other people feel good about themselves and do what they want?\" the 19-year-old wrote. \"WEAR WHATEVER YOU WANT PEOPLE! As long as you feel good about yourself that’s what matters. I know I did. Don’t ever let anyone stifle who you are and how you express yourself. Rant over.\" (Winter says her \"baby voice\" makes her boyfriend uncomfortable.)"], "length": 1415, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Ariel Winter, a 19-year-old actress from the popular TV show \"Modern Family,\" has been making headlines recently for her unapologetic attitude and fashion choices. In a recent interview with Elite Daily, Winter spoke about her social media presence and how she doesn't care what others think. She stated, \"If I can't win, I'm going to work on a relationship with myself, which I also think is the most important thing we can teach young women and men today.\"\n\nHowever, not everyone is a fan of Winter's confidence. During a recent event for the ABC series \"Modern Family,\" Winter wore a gold minidress that some critics deemed too revealing. Some commenters implied that she was trying too hard to look sexy, while others made hurtful comments about her appearance. Winter responded to the criticism on Instagram, telling haters to mind their business and encouraging others to wear what makes them feel good.\n\nWinter's fashion choice was also defended by her co-star Sofia Vergara, who captioned a photo of the group on Instagram, \"Mi Familia Moderna.\" Vergara's response showed support for Winter's confidence and individuality.\n\nIn another development, Winter has been open about her own struggles with self-acceptance and the importance of self-love. She has used her platform to promote positivity and self-empowerment, encouraging her followers to focus on their own self-worth rather than seeking validation from others.\n\nOverall, Ariel Winter's recent news has highlighted the importance of self-acceptance, confidence, and individuality. Her unapologetic attitude and fashion choices have inspired many, and her response to criticism has shown that she is not afraid to stand up for herself and others."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Will Smith stars as Nicky, an established con man who takes on a new apprentice, Australian newcomer Margot Robbie's Jess, in Focus. They both love the con game, but who's playing who? Here's what critics are saying: Richard Corliss at Time is a fan. \"The mix of longtime star and minx on the rise is one tasty element in the success of a movie that approaches the modest goals and effortless allure of a 60-year-old Hitchcock,\" he writes. Smith \"takes a welcome break from glowering sci-fi roles\" and writer-directors Glenn Ficarra and John Requa \"deserve some credit in letting Will be Will in the star's first charm barrage since 2005's Hitch.\" Steven Rea at the Philadelphia Inquirer admits Smith \"shows some of the movie-star wattage that's been missing from his recent pics. He charms.\" There's also \"combustible chemistry\" between Smith and Robbie, but the movie just \"disappoints,\" Rea writes. \"Even if you're willing to forgive its sinkhole plotholes and farthest-fetched conceits, the film ... ultimately makes no sense.\" Betsy Sharkey, on the other hand, says this \"rom-com-con\" is \"an irresistible reminder of all the reasons we first fell for the Fresh Prince so many years ago.\" Writing at the Los Angeles Times, she notes the romantic role of Nicky fits Smith perfectly, while Robbie is \"more than holding her own\" as his match. \"The scams are Rubik's Cube complicated, but what keeps you guessing is whether the romantic connection between Jess and Nicky is real or just another con.\" But Mick LaSalle at the San Francisco Chronicle isn't convinced. In a review titled \"Will Smith just stole $12 from your pocket,\" he argues Focus is \"ridiculous in every detail. It's a movie with no truth that teaches nothing and shows nothing, that has only its audacity to recommend it.\" Nicky and Jess lack \"impact,\" partly because \"Smith is not a strong leading man,\" he says. Viewers are left \"waiting for the trick, which is not the same as being fooled.\""], "length": 2254, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Movie Review: Focus Starring Will Smith and Margot Robbie**\n\nThe highly anticipated movie \"Focus\" has finally hit theaters, and critics are weighing in on its performance. The film, directed by John Requa and Glenn Ficarra, follows the story of a veteran con man, Nicky Spurgeon (played by Will Smith), who takes a young woman, Jess Barrett (played by Margot Robbie), under his wing and teaches her the art of pickpocketing.\n\nThe movie has received mixed reviews, with some critics praising Smith's charm and charisma, while others have found the film to be ridiculous and lacking in substance. The film's plot is described as a \"con\" that is itself an elaborate con, with the movie's con artists using their skills to swindle marks and evade detection.\n\nDespite the mixed reviews, the film has been praised for its lighthearted and playful tone, with many critics noting that it is a fun and entertaining watch. The chemistry between Smith and Robbie is also a highlight of the film, with the two actors bringing a sense of charm and wit to their roles.\n\nIn terms of casting, the film has been criticized for its lack of diversity, with many of the supporting roles being played by white actors. However, the film's lead actors, Smith and Robbie, have been praised for their performances, with Smith bringing his signature charm and charisma to the role of Nicky Spurgeon.\n\nOverall, \"Focus\" is a fun and entertaining film that is sure to delight fans of the con artist genre. While it may not be a masterpiece of cinema, it is a enjoyable watch that is worth checking out.\n\n**Casting Controversy**\n\nIn the past, the film's casting was a subject of controversy, with many actresses being considered for the role of Jess Barrett before Margot Robbie was ultimately cast. The film's writers, Glenn Ficarra and John Requa, have said that they were drawn to Robbie's unique blend of charm and vulnerability, and that they believe she brings a sense of authenticity to the role.\n\n**Will Smith's Career**\n\nThe film marks a significant milestone in Will Smith's career, as it is his first major film role in several years. Smith has been open about his desire to return to more dramatic roles, and \"Focus\" is seen as a step in that direction. The film's success is also a testament to Smith's enduring charm and charisma, which have made him a beloved figure in Hollywood.\n\n**Comparison to Classic Films**\n\nThe film's writers have"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Tonight's Mega Millions prize could end up being the biggest US jackpot ever. It's already hit $636 million; by tonight, it could rise past the $656 million record, Reuters notes. Let's say, for the sake of argument, that you win it (you won't). When you can breathe again, you'll want to take quick action to ensure that you deal with your newfound millions safely and effectively. At CNN, Steve Almasy offers a checklist: First, sign the ticket. You don't want to risk losing it, or having a \"friend\" claim it. While you're at it, you might want to take a selfie with the ticket, then drop it in a safe deposit box. Then, seek out financial experts, but not too many—you want to keep the news quiet as long as possible. \"Start with one experienced attorney and look for a seasoned certified financial planner,\" Almasy writes. Big question: Is there any way you can legally remain anonymous? If you live in South Carolina or a handful of other states, the answer is yes, but generally you'll have a tough time hiding. You might want to hire a PR person. In fact, you might want to slip out of the country until the hubbub over the prize has died down. Didn't win? Don't forget to check if you've qualified for runner-up cash. Last year saw $800 million in unclaimed lottery winnings. Click for Almasy's full piece."], "length": 1747, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Mega Millions Jackpot Reaches Historic Levels**\n\nA record-breaking Mega Millions jackpot of $636 million is up for grabs, with a potential winner just a week before Christmas. The prize has been steadily increasing since Monday, with a surge in ticket sales expected on Tuesday night's drawing.\n\nAccording to Virginia Lottery Director Paula Otto, the prize has grown to its highest level in history, with a potential winner having a 1 in 259 million chance of matching the winning numbers. The jackpot is expected to reach $950 million if no one wins, making it the largest U.S. lottery prize in history.\n\n**Preparation is Key**\n\nFor those who do win, preparation is crucial. Experts recommend signing the ticket, contacting experienced attorneys and financial planners, and planning a trip to avoid unwanted attention. It's also essential to stay anonymous, with only a few states allowing winners to remain private.\n\n**Don't Miss Your Chance**\n\nDon't assume that just because you didn't win the top prize, you won't win anything. Every year, a few people who match five numbers and win the runner-up prize fail to collect their winnings. Check your tickets again to see if you might have won $1 million.\n\n**A Record-Breaking Week**\n\nThe Mega Millions jackpot has been growing rapidly, with a potential winner just a week before Christmas. The prize is expected to surpass the record U.S. jackpot of $656 million, won in March 2012. If no one wins, the prize will keep growing until the next drawing on Friday.\n\nIn conclusion, the Mega Millions jackpot is a historic opportunity for someone to win a life-changing amount of money. With careful planning and preparation, winners can navigate the challenges that come with such a large prize."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["They volunteered to fight fires, but authorities say they were also purposely setting fires over a period of almost two years. After a tip led to a yearlong investigation, 10 volunteer firefighters in North Carolina's Robeson County were arrested Tuesday and charged with arson and related crimes, ABC 11 reports. So far there are 90 charges involved in the case, which involves firefighters from the Fairmont Rural Fire Department and the Orrum Fire Department, and officials say more charges are expected and more arrests could be made. \"I don't understand that. I mean, the job might be boring at times, but you don't go creating fires just to have something to put out,\" a local, who lives near the Fairmont firehouse, tells WRAL. The firefighters are accused of setting fires in woods and at abandoned structures; WRAL and ABC 11 both have the names of those accused—among them are a police officer and a Department of Corrections officer—and specific charges. Authorities say the alleged crimes cost taxpayers thousands of dollars."], "length": 1537, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Multiple Arrests Made in Connection with Arson Conspiracy in Robeson County, North Carolina**\n\nA recent investigation by the Robeson County Sheriff's Office has led to the arrest of 10 volunteer firefighters accused of setting numerous fires in woods and abandoned structures over a nearly two-year period. The firefighters, who work with the Fairmont Rural Fire Department and the Orrum Fire Department, are charged with various felony counts related to arson, conspiracy, and other crimes.\n\nAccording to authorities, the fires were intentionally set by the firefighters, causing significant damage and financial losses to taxpayers. The investigation, which began six months ago, was sparked by a tip and involved multiple agencies, including the State Bureau of Investigation, Alcohol, Tobacco and Firearms, and the NC Forestry Service.\n\nThe 10 arrested firefighters are:\n\n* Melquan Williams, 21, of Fairmont, charged with one count of felony arson and one count of felony conspiracy\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["\"Four trapezoidal bars aligned on a rectangular base\" may not be instantly recognizable. Put that setup in chocolate form, however, and many people would be able to distinguish it as a KitKat. Even if that's the case, the European Union's highest court just ruled that the shape of the sweet treat isn't unique enough to merit trademark protection, meaning other confectioners can potentially produce KitKat knockoffs, the Guardian reports. Nestle, which makes KitKats, has been fighting Cadbury (and then Mondelez, which ultimately bought Cadbury) in the courts for more than a decade on this issue. The BBC notes that Nestle first applied for trademark protection for the candy's shape in 2002, and it was granted that protection four years later. Cadbury's pushback began in 2007, and Wednesday's decision by the European Court of Justice essentially tells the EU trademark office it now has to \"reconsider\" its original decision. What that means, however, is that if the EU Intellectual Property Office finds, based on more recent evidence, that the KitKat bar's shape is recognizable in enough countries, it can retain the trademark protection, per Reuters. The brand name itself of \"KitKat\" is not being contested. (Atari accused Nestle of ripping off one of its popular video games in an ad for KitKat.)"], "length": 1828, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Global Chocolate Industry in Turmoil: EU Court Ruling Sends KitKat Shape Trademark Back to Trademark Office**\n\nIn a significant decision, the European Court of Justice has thrown out an appeal by Nestlé, the maker of KitKat, which argued that it owns the shape of the four-fingered wafer bar. The ruling means that the EU Intellectual Property Office (EUIPO) must review a 2012 decision to uphold Nestlé's trademark on the shape of the KitKat finger wafer over objections raised by Mondelez, the US-based company that owns Cadbury.\n\nThe case has been ongoing for over a decade, with both companies fighting for trademark protection. The EU Court of Justice found that Nestlé had failed to show that consumers in enough EU countries recognized the shape as distinctive. However, the court also dismissed an appeal by Mondelez against some of the grounds for a lower EU court ruling in 2016 that had found the EUIPO was wrong to reject the US firm's complaint.\n\nThe outcome leaves open the possibility that the trademark agency could take account of other evidence, such as new proof that the shape is distinctive to people in more countries, and might preserve protection for KitKat's shape. The brand name \"KitKat\" is not at issue in the case.\n\nThe implications of the ruling are far-reaching, with trademark lawyers seeing it as a significant development for brands operating across the EU single market. The case has also been mirrored by a dispute between the two multinationals in the British courts, where Nestlé objected to a trademark for the purple color used by Cadbury to wrap its Dairy Milk chocolate bars.\n\nThe decision has sparked debate about the future of the KitKat brand, with some suggesting that the EU's failure to protect the trademark could lead to a flood of copycat confectionery. However, others argue that the ruling is a victory for consumers, who will now have access to a wider range of chocolate products.\n\nIn related news, a separate case involving Toblerone, another iconic chocolate brand, was settled out of court. The dispute centered on whether a British retailer's off-brand version of the chocolate bar was an infringement on Toblerone's trademark.\n\nOverall, the EU Court of Justice's decision has sent shockwaves through the global chocolate industry, highlighting the complexities and challenges of trademark law in the EU. As the case continues to unfold, one thing is clear: the future of the KitKat brand and the global chocolate industry will be shaped by the outcome."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Dinosaurs may have been much more like modern birds than we knew—and not just because some had feathers. A new study suggests that mighty dinosaurs of yore didn't roar, contrary to every dinosaur movie you've ever seen. Instead, they made a decidedly less scary sound called a \"closed-mouth vocalization.\" Think of a dove cooing or perhaps an ostrich doing this grunting thing. As the scientists explain in the journal Evolution, per UT News, the sound actually comes out of the neck area after air is pushed through an esophageal pouch. In fact, the animal's mouth is shut the whole time. Lots of birds do this today, and because birds descended from dinosaurs, scientists suspect they may have communicated in a similar way. The fossil record can't prove it, though the Washington Post notes that previous research meshes with the idea, including the belief that dinosaurs had air sacs instead of vocal chords. “A cool thing about this work is the demonstration that closed-mouth behavior evolved many times,” says the lead author. “That suggests it can emerge fairly easily and be incorporated into mating displays\" among different types of animals. A co-author says the study gives the impression of \"a very different Jurassic world. Not only were dinosaurs feathered, but they may have had bulging necks and made booming, closed-mouth sounds.\" But if so, the makers of Jurassic Park didn't get it all wrong: The hiss of the film's velociraptors came from a goose, per Vulture. (Scientists have grown chickens with dinosaur legs.)"], "length": 3836, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Dinosaurs May Have Made Mumbling Sounds, Not Roars**\n\nRecent research suggests that dinosaurs may have made low, throaty whooshes of air, similar to the cooing of a dove or the mumble of an ostrich, rather than the loud roars often depicted in movies and popular culture. The study, published in the journal Evolution, analyzed the vocalizations of birds and found that closed-mouth vocalizations, which involve emitting sound through the skin in the neck area, are more common in larger bird species.\n\nThe researchers, led by Chad Eliason and Tobias Riede, found that closed-mouth vocalizations have evolved independently at least 16 times in birds and are more likely to occur in larger bird species. They also suggest that some non-avian dinosaurs may have had similar vocal abilities, possibly using closed-mouth vocalizations during mating displays.\n\nThe study's findings are based on a statistical analysis of the distribution of closed-mouth vocalizations in birds and other reptilian groups. The researchers identified 52 out of 208 investigated bird species that use closed-mouth vocalization and found that only animals with a relatively large body size (about the size of a dove or larger) use this behavior.\n\nThe implications of this research are significant, as it challenges the traditional depiction of dinosaurs as loud, roaring creatures. Instead, it suggests that some dinosaurs may have made softer, more subtle sounds, which could have been used for communication and mating.\n\n**The Science Behind the Sounds**\n\nThe researchers used a combination of functional and morphological perspectives to understand the evolution of closed-mouth vocalizations in birds. They found that the inflation of an elastic cavity, which is necessary for closed-mouth vocalizations, presents a size-dependent challenge. This means that smaller body sizes may have made it more difficult for dinosaurs to produce closed-mouth vocalizations.\n\nThe study also highlights the importance of considering the behavior and ecology of dinosaurs when trying to understand their vocalizations. The researchers suggest that closed-mouth vocalizations may have been limited to sexually selected vocal displays, which would have co-occurred with open-mouthed vocalizations.\n\n**Future Research Directions**\n\nThe study's findings have significant implications for our understanding of dinosaur biology and behavior. Future research by this collaboration will integrate information from fossils, experimental physiology, gene expression, and sound modeling to understand the sounds that extinct early avian species, and perhaps their dinosaur ancestors, produced.\n\nOverall, the research suggests that dinosaurs may have been more complex and nuanced than previously thought, and that their vocalizations may have played a significant role in"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Cary Heath was arrested Monday at the Texas middle school where he teaches eighth-grade science a day after police say he murdered two of his neighbors, NBC DFW reports. According to the Fort Worth Star-Telegram, shots were fired shortly before 4am Sunday at a home down the street from where Heath lives. Police arrived to find two men dead in the home's driveway. \"From my understanding, the guy had an assault rife,\" a neighbor who woke up to gunshots tells NBC. \"From the looks of it, due to the shell casings, he unloaded the whole clip.\" Police aren't discussing a motive for the killings, though the first 911 call from the scene was regarding a robbery. The 35-year-old Heath is being held on $1 million bond and has been placed on administrative leave. The school notified parents of the situation via automated phone messages, WFAA reports. One mother says \"it's very scary\" that someone suspected of murder was teaching children. \"They should be safe over there to get their education and come home,\" she says. Heath served in the Air Force for 13 years before recently becoming a teacher."], "length": 875, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Multiple News Stories**\n\n**Internet Archive Donation**\n\nAlexa Internet has been donating its crawl data to the Internet Archive since 1996. The data is added to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period, allowing for the preservation of the internet's history.\n\n**Double Homicide in Fort Worth**\n\nA double homicide occurred in Fort Worth, Texas, on October 23, 2016. Two neighbors were found shot to death in their driveway, and a suspect was later arrested. Cary Joseph Heath, a 35-year-old middle school teacher, was arrested on a capital murder charge and is currently being held on a $1 million bond.\n\n**Investigation and Aftermath**\n\nHeath was employed as an eighth-grade science teacher at Permenter Middle School, where he was placed on administrative leave after his arrest. The school district notified parents via an automated phone message, but some parents expressed concern about the suspect's proximity to children. The motive for the shooting is still unknown, and the victims have not been identified.\n\n**Additional Details**\n\nHeath served 13 years in the U.S. Air Force prior to becoming a teacher. He was initially detained for questioning in the case but was not arrested. The police investigation is ongoing, and no further information has been released."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["In an attempt to bury a relic of its past, Vanderbilt University announced Monday that it will pay more than $1 million to remove the word \"Confederate\" from one of its dorms, the Tennessean reports. Chancellor Nicholas S. Zeppos called the inscription on Confederate Memorial Hall \"a reminder of racism, slavery and a very, very bloody Civil War.\" The Nashville university's efforts to change the name in 2002 were halted when the United Daughters of the Confederacy sued. The group’s $50,000 donation in 1933 helped build the dorm. A state appeals court ruled the building could be renamed Memorial Hall only after Vanderbilt gave the United Daughters back their money—$1.2 million in today’s dollars. The university has the cash, thanks to a raft of anonymous contributors who wanted the tie to America’s painful past broken for good. \"It's a symbol that is, for many people, deeply offensive and painful,\" Zeppos told the Tennessean. \"And to walk past it or to have to live in that space is really something that I just don't think is acceptable.” Yet tampering with Confederate imagery remains controversial, and a new state law makes it harder to do so, the AP reports. Efforts by Middle Tennessee State University in Murfreesboro to remove the name of Confederate Gen. Nathan Bedford Forrest from a building could face a tougher climb under the Tennessee Heritage Protection Act, which now requires a vote of two-thirds of the state’s Historical Commission rather than a simple majority."], "length": 1995, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Vanderbilt University has announced that it will remove the name \"Confederate Memorial Hall\" from one of its campus dorms, a decision made after 14 years of effort. The university will pay $1.2 million to the United Daughters of the Confederacy, the organization that originally donated $50,000 to the building's construction in 1933. The decision was made by Chancellor Nicholas S. Zeppos, who stated that the name is a \"symbol of racism, slavery, and a very, very bloody Civil War\" and that it is not acceptable to have it on campus.\n\nThe university has referred to the building as Memorial Hall since 2002, but was blocked in court from changing the name due to the original donation. The agreement to pay $1.2 million was made with anonymous donors, and the United Daughters of the Confederacy will relinquish its naming rights to the building.\n\nThis decision is part of a larger trend of universities across the country re-evaluating the implications of their buildings' names. Middle Tennessee State University is also in the process of removing the name of Confederate Gen. Nathan Bedford Forrest from one of its buildings, although the proposal faces a tougher hurdle due to a new Tennessee law that makes it more difficult to remove Confederate symbols and statues from public places.\n\nThe decision at Vanderbilt University is seen as a positive step towards addressing diversity and inclusivity on campus. Chancellor Zeppos stated that the university is committed to moving forward and addressing its complicated history, and that the decision to remove the name is a step in that direction.\n\nThe move is also part of a larger national conversation about Confederate symbols and statues, following the slaying of nine black churchgoers in Charleston, South Carolina. Republican Gov. Bill Haslam has expressed support for removing the Capitol bust of Forrest, and calls to remove Confederate imagery from public places have multiplied across the South.\n\nOverall, the decision at Vanderbilt University is a significant step towards addressing the legacy of racism and slavery on college campuses, and towards creating a more inclusive and diverse environment for students."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A classy move by a Florida cop is making headlines: Laurie Graber, an officer in Plantation, got the call for a particularly low-level crime—someone had ripped the engagement ring off the finger of an 87-year-old woman bedridden with Alzheimer's in a hospital, reports Fox News. By the bruises on Betty Wagner's finger, it wasn't an easy job, and no wonder—the ring had been there for 67 years. \"I just couldn't imagine what kind of depravity you would have to have to take something off of someone so vulnerable,\" Graber tells WSVN. Upon leaving the hospital, Graber went to JCPenney and shelled out for a replacement ring with her own money. She brought it back to the nurses' station with a note, reports AOL.com: \"It's not much, it's not the same. But 67 years of a promise kept should be recognized.\" Husband Arthur, who placed the original on his soon-to-be wife's hand in 1946, says the gesture has helped him deal with the theft. (Another Florida officer bought groceries for a mom caught shoplifting.)"], "length": 1212, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A heartwarming story of a Florida police officer's selfless act has captured the nation's attention. In June 2014, an elderly woman named Betty Wagner, who suffers from Alzheimer's, had her engagement ring stolen from her hospital bed. The ring, which had been on her finger for 67 years, was a family heirloom that held sentimental value.\n\nOfficer Laurie Graber, who was sent to investigate the case, was deeply moved by the story and decided to take matters into her own hands. She purchased a new ring from J.C. Penney and delivered it to the hospital, leaving a signed note that read, \"A promise kept for 67 years is worth something.\"\n\nThe new ring, a heart-shaped diamond, was a kind gesture that brought comfort to Betty's husband, Arthur, who was devastated by the loss of the original ring. Arthur, who had spent $400 on the original ring in 1946, was overjoyed to see his wife's face light up when she saw the new ring.\n\nThe story highlights the power of kindness and compassion, as Officer Graber's selfless act brought joy and comfort to a family in need. Despite the police investigation, the original ring has not been recovered, and the new ring has become a symbol of the enduring love and commitment between Arthur and Betty.\n\nThe incident has sparked a wave of support and admiration for Officer Graber, who has been hailed as a hero for her kindness and generosity. The story serves as a reminder of the importance of human connection and the impact that one person can have on another's life."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Not a huge surprise: Lawyers for Boston marathon bomber Dzhokhar Tsarnaev today appealed his death-penalty conviction, arguing that \"continuous and unrelenting publicity\" biased jurors and made a fair trial in Boston impossible, NBC News reports. \"Put simply, prejudicial media coverage, events, and environment saturated greater Boston, including the social networks of actual trial jurors, and made it an improper venue for the trial of this case,\" say the papers. The lawyers want another trial at a new location, the AP reports. Earlier this year, the Guardian reminded us that Tsarnaev's appeals will likely delay his punishment for \"at least a decade.\" The April 15, 2013, bomb strike in Boston left three dead and more than 200 maimed or injured."], "length": 1067, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Boston Marathon Bomber Dzhokhar Tsarnaev's Fate Takes a Long Road**\n\nDzhokhar Tsarnaev, the mastermind behind the 2013 Boston Marathon bombing, has been sentenced to death. However, his fate will take years to be finalized due to the lengthy appeals process. Lawyers for Tsarnaev have appealed his conviction and death sentence, arguing that the trial was unfair due to widespread outrage and continuous media coverage.\n\nThe defense team claims that the trial should have been held outside of Boston, where the bombings occurred, as jurors would have been unable to avoid seeing friends and relatives posting about the attack on social media. They also argue that the recent Supreme Court ruling on the vagueness of the definition of a \"crime of violence\" throws many convictions into question.\n\nTsarnaev was convicted of 30 federal charges, resulting in the deaths of three people and injuring over 260 others. He apologized at his sentencing, but his lawyers believe that the publicity surrounding the case made it impossible for him to get a fair trial in the city.\n\nThe appeal claims that the continuous and unrelenting media coverage, as well as the announcement of a new holiday marking the bombings, created an improper environment for the trial. The defense team also argues that the trial was prejudiced by the social media presence of the jurors.\n\nDespite the dramatic news from court, the execution of Tsarnaev is unlikely to happen anytime soon. Only three of 340 prisoners sentenced to federal death row have been executed in the last 50 years, and the process can take years to complete. The Justice Department may attempt to fast-track executions, but experts expect the timeframe to be at least 10 years.\n\nTsarnaev's team has already indicated two strong lines of likely appeal: that the trial should not have been held in Boston and that the defense was given insufficient time to mount a full argument in mitigation. The case highlights the complexities and challenges of the federal death penalty system, which has become increasingly unusual in modern times."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["InterContinentalExchange Inc. has agreed to buy NYSE Euronext, the company that owns the New York Stock Exchange, for $8.2 billion, the two companies announced this morning. ICE, an Atlanta-based commodity exchange, says it intends to leave the NYSE's branding alone, and would explore an IPO for Euronext, spinning it off into a continental European entity, the Wall Street Journal reports. It will pay $33.12 a share for the elder exchange, or about a 37% premium on yesterday's close. You might remember ICE from its last attempt to buy the NYSE, which failed thanks to antitrust concerns from regulators. While the 12-year-old company may lack the name recognition of the iconic NYSE, it's much larger in terms of market capitalization, at $9.3 billion to NYSE Euronext's $5.8 billion—a sign, the New York Times observes, of how completely commodity trading has outpaced stock trading in relevance."], "length": 1042, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Global Market Shift: NYSE to be Acquired by IntercontinentalExchange**\n\nIn a significant move, the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE), a 220-year-old institution, is set to be acquired by IntercontinentalExchange Inc. (ICE) in an $8.2 billion deal. The acquisition, which is expected to be completed soon, marks a major shift in the global market for derivatives, eclipsing the market for stocks.\n\nThe NYSE, once the public face of the stock market, will be acquired by ICE, a commodities and derivatives trading platform. The deal, which is still pending regulatory and shareholder approval, will create a combined company that owns 14 stock and futures exchanges and five clearing operations, making it the largest player in the industry.\n\nThe acquisition is seen as a strategic move by ICE to expand its presence in the market and gain access to the NYSE's equities business. The deal also marks a significant shift towards electronic trading, as the NYSE's floor trading model is set to be replaced by ICE's electronic platforms.\n\nThe acquisition is also expected to have a significant impact on the industry, as it will create a powerful trans-Atlantic company with a major market share in the trading of stocks and derivatives. The deal is also seen as a response to the decline of the stock trading business, as the average number of American stocks traded each day has fallen every year since 2009.\n\nThe acquisition is expected to be completed soon, pending regulatory and shareholder approval. The deal is seen as a major milestone in the industry, marking a significant shift towards electronic trading and the consolidation of the market.\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n\n* The New York Stock Exchange is to be acquired by IntercontinentalExchange Inc. in an $8.2 billion deal.\n* The acquisition marks a significant shift in the global market for derivatives, eclipsing the market for stocks.\n* The deal will create a combined company that owns 14 stock and futures exchanges and five clearing operations.\n* The acquisition is expected to have a significant impact on the industry, as it will create a powerful trans-Atlantic company with a major market share in the trading of stocks and derivatives.\n* The deal is seen as a response to the decline of the stock trading business, as the average number of American stocks traded each day has fallen every year since 2009."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Apparently still smarting from some of Chris Rock's Oscars barbs, the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences invited 683 people—many of them women and minorities—to join in an unprecedented move Wednesday, Reuters reports. The voting group behind the Oscars is largely old, white, and male and was lambasted this year with the hashtag #OscarsSoWhite after two years in a row of all-white acting nominees. In response, the academy is attempting to—as the AV Club puts it—\" add seemingly goddamn everyone it had, for one reason or another, forgotten to invite into its membership.” The actors, directors, and others invited Wednesday include Idris Elba, Eva Mendes, Oscar Isaac, John Boyega, Ice Cube, Greta Gerqig, Michael B. Jordan, Vivica A. Fox, the Wachowskis, James Wan, Luis Guzmán, Kate Beckinsale, Park Chan-wook, James Hong, Michelle Rodriguez, and not one, not two, but three Wayans brothers. Of the new invitees, 46% are women and 41% are people of color. If all 683 accept their invite, women would account for 27% of the more than 7,000 academy members (up from 25%) and minorities would total 11% (up from 8%). “I'm especially happy to be part of such a diverse group. I actually want to hang out and watch movies with most of the people on this list,\" Arab-German director Lexi Alexander tells the Los Angeles Times. “To be honest, I cried a few tears when I started to get congratulation tweets in Arabic.” Other invitees took to Twitter to share similar sentiments. “Excited to use my vote to nominate talent that reflects the real world we live in—DIVERSITY,\" tweets Brie Larson, who won best actress at this year's Oscars."], "length": 682, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Academy of Motion Pictures Arts and Sciences Announces Historic Membership Overhaul**\n\nIn a significant step towards addressing its diversity issues, the Academy of Motion Pictures Arts and Sciences has sent out 683 membership invitations, marking the largest and most diverse class to date. The move comes in response to criticism about the organization's predominantly white, male, and older membership, as well as concerns about the film industry's ongoing diversity problem.\n\nThe new invitees include a significant number of women, with almost half of the total list consisting of female professionals. People of color also make up a substantial portion of the new members, with 41% of the invitees hailing from underrepresented groups.\n\nThe list of invited directors is equally impressive, featuring a diverse range of international heavyweights, including Catherine Breillat, Park Chan-wook, and Apichatpong Weerasethakul. The list also includes a number of prominent female directors, such as Lucrecia Martel and Mia Hansen-Løve.\n\nActing invitees include a diverse range of talent, including Idris Elba, Regina King, and Michelle Rodriguez. The list also features a number of notable actors from underrepresented groups, such as Oscar Isaac and Michael B. Jordan.\n\nThe Academy's commitment to diversity was first highlighted in 2015, when the hashtag #OscarsSoWhite was created in response to the all-white slate of acting Oscar nominees. The movement gained momentum in 2018, with the likes of Spike Lee and Jada Pinkett Smith joining the cause. In response, the Academy pledged to double the number of women and people of color by 2020.\n\nThe release of the latest list of invitees marks a significant step towards achieving this goal, and is seen as a major victory for the #OscarsSoWhite movement. The Academy's efforts to increase diversity and inclusion are likely to have a positive impact on the film industry as a whole, and will be closely watched in the coming years."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The three American hikers detained by Iran weren’t captured in Iraq, says Sarah Shourd: They were beckoned over the border by an armed soldier, she tells the New York Times in an effort to set the WikiLeaks version of the story straight. “We did not actually enter Iran until he gestured to us. We were confused and worried and wanted to go back.\" In Shourd's fullest account of her ordeal to date, she goes into detail about their fateful overnight camping trip, describing \"scores of campfires\" near the waterfall they were hiking to—which abutted Iran. “I think we were extremely unlucky,” says Shourd. “I guess I never believed there would be so many hundreds of people close to a border.” Her interview with the Times comes ahead of a court date for her fellow hikers, which was set for Saturday but has been delayed by Iran until Shourd “can return to Iran or,” if she refuses, her case can “be dealt with differently,” said an Iranian official. But her lawyer says the trial should go forward; neither he nor Shourd, he tells Reuters, were notified of the change."], "length": 1346, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Here is a one-page summary of the news:\n\n**Iranian-American Hikers' Trial Delayed**\n\nThe trial of two American hikers, Shane Bauer and Josh Fattal, who were arrested in Iran in 2009, has been delayed due to the absence of the third suspect, Sarah Shourd. The trial was originally scheduled to begin on Saturday, but it has been postponed indefinitely. Iranian authorities claim that Shourd's absence is due to her health concerns, but her lawyer disputes this, saying that the trial should have proceeded as scheduled.\n\n**Background of the Hikers' Arrest**\n\nThe three hikers, who are from Berkeley, California, were arrested while hiking in northern Iraq in July 2009. They claim that they were unaware they had crossed into Iran and were not aware of any warning signs. The American military report suggests that the hikers' lack of coordination and apparent intent to agitate international policies on Iran led to their arrest. However, the US State Department has denied this version of events, saying that they do not know how the hikers were arrested and that Iran has held them for too long.\n\n**Shourd's Release and Concerns**\n\nSarah Shourd was released from prison in September 2010 on $500,000 bail, after being held for over a year. Her release was seen as a humanitarian gesture due to concerns about her health. However, her lawyer has expressed concerns about the delay in her trial and the lack of transparency from the Iranian authorities.\n\n**Tensions between the US and Iran**\n\nThe delay in Shourd's trial has raised tensions between the US and Iran, which have been bitter foes since the 1979 Islamic Revolution. The US has been trying to prod Iran into restarting talks about its nuclear activities, which they fear are aimed at making an atomic bomb. Iran has said that it is ready to restart talks, but has yet to agree on a date and has suggested that the talks should not address the nuclear issue.\n\n**Other Developments**\n\nIn a separate development, a woman who was jailed in the US for trafficking defense hardware to Iran has spoken out about her treatment in prison, saying that she was tortured. The Iranian government has denied these allegations, but the woman's testimony has raised concerns about human rights abuses in Iran. The US has also expressed concern about the treatment of Iranian prisoners in US jails, with the Foreign Ministry spokesman saying that Iran will pursue all channels to secure their release."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A military coup is under way in Mali, as renegade soldiers stormed the presidential palace in the capital of Bamako today, and soon afterward appeared on state TV to announce that they had booted the president and taken over the country, the BBC reports. The soldiers said they were suspending the constitution, dissolving its institutions and instituting a nationwide curfew. They said they were taking power because of President Amadou Toumani Toure's failure to defeat a rebellion by the Taureg tribes in the north, the AP reports. The military “has decided to assume its responsibilities and end the incompetent and disavowed regime of Amadou Toumani Toure,” the troops said, adding that it “does not in any way aim to confiscate power, and we solemnly swear to return power to a democratically elected president as soon as national unity and territorial integrity are established.” The mutiny comes just a month before Toure was set to leave office legally."], "length": 2184, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Malian Military Coup: Soldiers Seize Control of Presidential Palace**\n\nIn a shocking turn of events, soldiers in Mali have seized control of the presidential palace, ousting President Amadou Toumani Toure just one month before his term was set to end. The mutiny, which began on Wednesday, has left the country in chaos, with reports of gunfire and armoured vehicles deployed to protect the palace.\n\nThe soldiers, who claim to be acting against an \"incompetent regime,\" have imposed a nationwide curfew and suspended the constitution. They have also arrested several ministers, including the foreign minister, and taken over state television and radio stations.\n\nThe mutiny is believed to be sparked by anger among troops over the government's handling of a Tuareg rebellion in the north of the country. The Tuaregs, a traditionally nomadic people, have been waging a rebellion against the central government since mid-January, and the soldiers are reportedly opposed to any potential talks with the rebels.\n\nThe West African regional bloc ECOWAS has condemned the actions of the mutinous soldiers, describing them as \"reprehensible\" and \"misguided.\" The United States and France have also urged the soldiers and government to resolve their dispute through peaceful means.\n\nThe situation remains volatile, with reports of gunfire and unrest continuing to ring out in the capital, Bamako. The whereabouts of President Toure are unknown, but a loyalist military source close to the president has said he is well and in a safe location.\n\nThe coup marks a major setback for Mali, which has had democratic rule for 20 years. The country was seen as a model for emerging democracies, but the crisis has raised concerns about the stability of the region.\n\n**Key Developments:**\n\n* Soldiers seize control of presidential palace in Bamako\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The internet loves obsessing over Game of Thrones, so Hello Giggles wants to know why more people aren't talking about the fact that \"Tommen is also his own cousin.\" Dean-Charles Chapman currently plays King Tommen Baratheon on the show, a role he took over from a younger actor in 2014. But he also played Tommen's cousin, Martyn Lannister, in a few episodes of the show back in the third season, Mashable explains. Hello Giggles thinks the recasting makes complete sense. “Given the Lannister family’s penchant for incest, having several people in that lineage look the same (like exactly the same) would not be a surprise.” Poor Martyn was killed off after being taken hostage by Robb Stark, and Bustle thinks Chapman better hope for a third role as Tommen is \"probably doomed just like his look-alike cousin.\""], "length": 827, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Recent News Summary**\n\nIn a surprising revelation, it has been discovered that Dean-Charles Chapman, the actor who plays King Tommen Baratheon in Game of Thrones, also played another character in the show - Martyn Lannister. Chapman's portrayal of Martyn dates back to Season 3, where he appeared in two episodes, \"Walk of Punishment\" and \"Kissed by Fire.\" Martyn was a squire in the Lannister army who was taken prisoner by Edmure Tully at the Battle of Stone Mill.\n\nThe connection between Tommen and Martyn was first noticed by Hello Giggles, which pointed out that the two characters share a similar appearance. Chapman's dual roles in the show have sparked interest among fans, particularly given the Lannister family's history of incest in the Game of Thrones universe.\n\nThis discovery has also led to speculation about the \"Tommen is a faceless man\" theory, which suggests that Tommen's fate is tied to the faceless men, a group of assassins in the show. While this theory has been largely debunked, the revelation has added another layer of complexity to the show's narrative.\n\nIn a separate development, Alexa Internet has been donating its crawl data to the Internet Archive since 1996, which is added to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period. This data provides a valuable resource for researchers and historians studying the internet's evolution over time.\n\nOverall, these recent news stories highlight the complexities of the Game of Thrones universe and the importance of paying attention to even the smallest details in the show."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["It's all up to President Trump now. The House Intelligence Committee voted along party lines Monday to release a controversial memo that alleges wrongdoing on the part of the Justice Department and the FBI in regard to the Russia investigation, reports Politico. Democrats are fuming over the decision. Trump now has five days to decide whether to allow the memo to be released; his approval is necessary because it's a classified document. The memo alleges that the FBI and the Justice Department improperly used government surveillance during the investigation into Russian election interference and contacts with Trump's campaign, per the AP. The vote to release the memo comes after committee Republicans, led by chairman Rep. Devin Nunes, pushed for its disclosure. The memo addresses a dossier of allegations against Trump compiled by a former British spy, and questions over whether it was improperly used to obtain surveillance warrants. The panel's ranking Democrat, Rep. Adam Schiff, announced the vote results Monday while criticizing the Republicans' decision. Democrats generally say the memo cherry-picks facts and presents a distorted view of things."], "length": 1655, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\nThe US government is embroiled in a controversy surrounding a classified memo written by the House Intelligence Committee, which alleges misconduct by senior officials at the FBI. The memo, which was written by Republicans, accuses the FBI of abusing a classified surveillance program to spy on a Trump campaign aide. The memo has been a source of escalating partisan tension in Congress, with Democrats opposing its release and accusing Republicans of politicizing the intelligence process.\n\nThe memo was shared with FBI Director Christopher Wray and Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein, who expressed concerns about its accuracy and potential impact on national security. Despite these concerns, the House Intelligence Committee voted to release the memo, which will be subject to review by the White House and the Justice Department.\n\nThe controversy surrounding the memo has sparked a wider debate about the role of the FBI and the Justice Department in investigating Russian interference in the 2016 election. The memo has also raised questions about the potential for interference in the special counsel investigation led by Robert Mueller.\n\nIn a related development, two Republican senators, Lindsey Graham and Susan Collins, have urged President Trump to keep a public silence on the investigation into his 2016 campaign's contacts with Russia. The senators also called on Mueller to review whether Trump tried to fire him last June, an accusation the president has labeled \"fake news.\"\n\nThe controversy surrounding the memo has also sparked a wider debate about the role of Congress in investigating the Trump administration. The House Intelligence Committee's decision to release the memo has been criticized by Democrats, who argue that it undermines the integrity of the investigation and could compromise national security.\n\nOverall, the controversy surrounding the memo highlights the deepening partisan divisions in the US government and the ongoing efforts to investigate Russian interference in the 2016 election."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The US in early July hit $34 billion of Chinese goods with an extra 25% tariff; China responded in kind. CNN reports round two could see the US slap tariffs on another $16 billion in goods as soon as this week, but it's a much bigger figure that's attracting attention Wednesday. Bloomberg cites sources who say the White House is thinking about upping the ante and imposing not an additional 10% tariff on $200 billion worth of Chinese goods but an additional 25%. CNN echoes that news via its source. Bloomberg frames the move, which would be revealed in the next couple of weeks, as intended to \"force officials back to the negotiating table through threats of even higher tariffs\"; the last high-level negotiations took place nearly two months ago. A Chinese foreign ministry rep responded to the news thusly, per the AP: \"If the United States takes further measures that escalate the situation, China will definitely fight back. We are determined to safeguard our legitimate and lawful rights and interests.\""], "length": 1393, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**US-China Trade Tensions Escalate: Retaliation Imminent**\n\nThe US-China trade dispute has reached a boiling point, with both sides preparing for a potential escalation of tariffs. The Trump administration is set to impose 25% tariffs on an additional $200 billion of Chinese goods, sparking a warning from China that it will retaliate if the US takes further measures.\n\nThe move, which is not yet finalized, comes as the two countries remain locked in a trade war that has been ongoing for months. The US has already imposed 25% tariffs on Chinese goods worth $34 billion, prompting China to respond with its own tariffs on US goods.\n\nDespite efforts to restart talks, the US and China have yet to reach a deal. Representatives of US Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin and Chinese Vice Premier Liu He are having private conversations, but a specific timetable, issues to be discussed, and format for talks are still unclear.\n\nThe US is seeking concessions from China, including changes to its intellectual property laws and market access. However, China has taken a harder line, with officials vowing to respond with the same amount of tariffs on US products.\n\nThe trade tensions have had a significant impact on global markets, with US stocks closing higher as the prospects for an easing in trade tensions lifted industrial shares. However, the situation remains volatile, with officials in Beijing warning that the US is trying to secure concessions and if China agrees, the US may back off additional tariffs.\n\nThe US-China trade dispute has also had a significant impact on other countries, with allies expressing concern about the potential for a global trade war. The International Monetary Fund has warned that the dispute may undermine the strongest economic upswing in years.\n\nIn a sign of the growing tensions, Chinese officials have signaled that they will focus more on supporting economic growth amid risks from a campaign to reduce debt and the dispute with Trump. The Politburo has also signaled that policy makers will continue to support economic growth, while improving economic policies to make them more forward-looking, flexible, and effective.\n\nOverall, the US-China trade dispute remains a major concern, with both sides preparing for a potential escalation of tariffs. The situation remains volatile, and it is unclear when or if a deal will be reached."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Sony today unveiled its next-generation gaming system, PlayStation 4, saying the console will be part of a new ecosystem focused on hardware, software, and \"the fastest, most powerful gaming network.\" The new console is the Japanese electronics giant's first major game machine since the PlayStation 3 went on sale in 2006. Today's unveiling is Sony's attempt to steal the spotlight, at least until Microsoft unveils its next Xbox in June at the E3 video game expo in Los Angeles. Among the PS4's revisions is an updated controller that adds a touchpad, motion control, and a \"share\" button. The controller also features a light bar, which means a new PlayStation camera can more easily track the device. Sony has struggled lately to keep up with Microsoft and other rivals such as Apple and Samsung. The company is promising nifty mobile devices, sophisticated digital cameras, and other gadgetry as part of its comeback effort. More details at Gizmodo and TechCrunch."], "length": 1594, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "The gaming world has been abuzz with the latest news from Sony's highly anticipated PlayStation 4 (PS4) event. The next-gen platform is designed to shift focus from the living room to the gamer, allowing for seamless playability anywhere, anytime. The PS4 is built on an x86-based personal computer, making it easy for developers to build games, and features a powerful and accessible system.\n\nThe console boasts impressive graphics capabilities, with a live demo of Unreal Engine 4 showcasing fluid and fantastic visuals, comparable to high-end PCs. The graphics are not revolutionary, but rather an improvement over previous console generations. The PS4 also features a redesigned DualShock 4 controller, with a touchpad, share button, and light bar that tracks movement.\n\nOne of the key features of the PS4 is its social aspect, with a new function allowing users to quickly pause and upload gameplay videos, as well as spectator functionality for watching \"celebrities\" gaming. The console will also have a social gaming interface, with integration with Facebook and other social media platforms.\n\nThe PS4 will also have a dedicated video-processing chip, allowing for seamless streaming of video from gaming sessions. The console will also support remote play, with the ability to continue playing on another screen, and will be compatible with the PlayStation Vita.\n\nHowever, the PS4 does not have native PS3 backwards compatibility, but will have PlayStation Cloud, allowing users to stream old titles without the need for discs. The console's pricing and design have not been announced, leaving fans eagerly awaiting more information.\n\nOverall, the PS4 promises to be a powerful and accessible gaming platform, with a focus on ease of development, social features, and seamless playability. With its impressive graphics capabilities and redesigned controller, the PS4 is set to revolutionize the gaming industry."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Scientists at UC Berkeley have made a major advancement in the field of mind reading, reconstructing YouTube videos based on brain scans from people who’d seen them. Researchers would put subjects into an MRI machine and track their brain activity as they viewed videos. Once they’d build a model of how a subject’s mind processed the video, “we could read brain activity for that subject and run it backward … to try to uncover what the viewer saw,” one study coauthor tells ABC News. Using the scans they were then able to reproduce the videos—though they’re blurry. “This is a major leap,” the co-author says. He thinks the technique could eventually be used to reconstruct dreams or memories, if it turns out the brain processes those things similarly to how it processes movies. “It’s our next line of research.” (More details on how the experiment worked, along with a video, at PC Magazine.)"], "length": 1188, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Groundbreaking Brain-Reading Technology Takes a Major Leap Forward**\n\nIn a series of remarkable breakthroughs, researchers from UC Berkeley have made significant strides in brain-reading technology, paving the way for a future where we can see what's going on in someone's mind without visual stimulation. The latest development, published in Current Biology, allows scientists to reconstruct YouTube videos from viewers' brain activity, opening a window into the movies in our minds.\n\nThe study, led by Jack Gallant, involved subjects watching YouTube videos inside a magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) machine for several hours at a time. The team then used the brain imaging data to develop a computer model that matched features of the videos with patterns of brain activity. This model enabled them to read brain activity for the subject and run it backwards through the model to uncover what the viewer saw.\n\nThe reconstructed videos, while blurry, offer a haunting and dream-like interpretation of reality. The technology has the potential to be used to broadcast imagery, including scenes that play out inside our minds independent of vision. This could one day allow people who are paralyzed to control their environment by imagining sequences of movements.\n\nWhile the technology is still in its infancy, it has the potential to revolutionize various fields, including medicine, law enforcement, and even entertainment. However, there are also concerns about the ethics of decoding people's memories and thoughts, which could have significant implications for our understanding of human consciousness.\n\nAs researchers continue to refine this technology, they hope to build models for every part of the visual system, leading to better resolution and more accurate results. The ultimate goal is to understand how the brain works in naturalistic conditions, shedding light on the mysteries of human perception and cognition.\n\nIn a related development, a separate study from UC Berkeley has found a way to see through another person's eyes, reconstructing YouTube videos from viewers' brain activity. This technology has the potential to offer a glimpse into our dreams, memories, and even fantasies, raising questions about the nature of human consciousness and the limits of our understanding.\n\nAs we continue to push the boundaries of brain-reading technology, we must also consider the potential consequences and implications of this technology on our society and humanity as a whole."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["We shouldn't be too worried about the sequester—after all, Congress doesn't exactly seem to be recoiling at the thought. And Roger Simon has a theory on why: Lawmakers' own salaries aren't getting cut, he explains at Politico. Sure, their staffers are facing furloughs that amount to a 20% pay decrease, but members of Congress will keep collecting six-figure salaries. Of course our lawmakers \"might have to figure out how to put those plastic coffee pods in the machines themselves, but these people are not fools,\" he writes. \"They will order out. If they can figure out how to work the telephones.\" In fact, House Republicans are positively excited about the sequester, writes Dana Milbank in the Washington Post, noting their agenda yesterday consisted only of renaming a California NASA research center. They're \"pursuing a considered strategy of deliberate idleness,\" after finally realizing that doing nothing means they get \"a 2.5% cut in all federal spending without coughing up a single dollar in tax increases. They have learned to stop worrying and love the sequester.\" Click for Milbank's full piece; Simon's humorous take is here."], "length": 1365, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Congress Passes NASA Renaming Bill Amidst Sequestration Debate**\n\nIn a move that has been hailed as a tribute to the first man to walk on the moon, the House of Representatives has passed a bill to rename the Dryden Flight Research Center as the Neil A. Armstrong Flight Research Center. The bill, sponsored by Majority Whip Kevin McCarthy, was passed with a voice vote, with only one Democrat, Rep. Donna Edwards, speaking out against the measure.\n\nThe bill's passage comes as Congress is grappling with the sequestration, a series of automatic spending cuts that are set to take effect on March 1. President Obama has called for tax increases and alternative spending cuts to replace the sequester, but Republicans have expressed reluctance to consider such measures.\n\nDespite the sequestration debate, the House has been in a state of inactivity, with many lawmakers spending more time on ceremonial bills and procedural votes than actual legislation. The chamber has been in session for only 20 of the 56 days in 2013, with many lawmakers planning to take a recess at the end of March.\n\nThe sequestration has sparked fears of cuts to various programs, including NASA, border security, and national defense. However, it appears that lawmakers are more concerned with protecting their own salaries and benefits. Congressional staffers will face a 20% pay cut, while members of Congress will continue to receive their six-figure salaries.\n\nThe debate over sequestration has also highlighted the disconnect between lawmakers and the public. Many lawmakers seem more concerned with protecting their own interests than with addressing the needs of their constituents. As one commentator noted, \"Politicians have lost their power to frighten me. I no longer believe the sky is falling.\"\n\nIn a broader sense, the sequestration debate has highlighted the need for more effective and sustainable solutions to the country's fiscal challenges. As the country continues to grapple with the consequences of the sequester, it remains to be seen whether lawmakers will be able to find a way to address the underlying issues driving the debate."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Sen. Elizabeth Warren went after Wells Fargo CEO John Stumpf during a Senate hearing Tuesday with the kind of heat she usually reserves for Donald Trump tweetstorms. \"You should resign ... and you should be criminally investigated,\" NPR quotes Warren as saying. Stumpf was in front of the Senate banking committee after Wells Fargo employees created millions of fake accounts for customers without their permission in order to charge them extra fees. Stumpf said he was \"deeply sorry\" for what happened, New Republic reports. But—according to CNBC—he maintained it wasn't a \"scam\"—Warren's word—but a \"way of deepening relationships\" with customers. Warren accused Stumpf of \"gutless leadership\" for passing responsibility for what happened away from himself and other senior executives while blaming \"thousands of $12-an-hour employees who were just trying to meet cross-sell quotas that made you rich.\" The employees' cross-selling scheme was at least partially responsible for Wells Fargo stock going up, making Stumpf's shares alone gain $200 million in value. While more than 5,000 Wells Fargo employees were fired, none of the senior executives are losing their jobs. Warren said the only way Wall Street will be reformed is if CEOs like Stumpf start seeing jail time. She's one of only a few members of the Senate banking committee that hasn't gotten money from Wells Fargo's PAC."], "length": 1931, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Wells Fargo CEO John Stumpf Under Fire for Cross-Selling Scandal**\n\nSenator Elizabeth Warren called for a criminal investigation into Wells Fargo executives over the cross-selling scandal, which has engulfed the bank. Warren told CEO John Stumpf that he should resign and face criminal investigation for his role in the scandal, which has resulted in millions of fake accounts and billions of dollars in losses for customers. The scandal has led to the firing of 5,300 employees and a $185 million penalty for the bank.\n\nWarren's comments came after a tense hearing where she grilled Stumpf about the scandal and his leadership. She accused Stumpf of \"gutless leadership\" and said that the only way to change the industry is to hold executives accountable for massive frauds. The bank's stock price dipped after Warren's comments.\n\n**US to Recognize Jerusalem as Israel's Capital**\n\nPresident Donald Trump is expected to announce that he plans to recognize Jerusalem as Israel's capital, a decision that has been met with criticism from around the world. The decision is seen as a compromise, as Trump had previously pledged to move the US embassy to Jerusalem but will instead sign a six-month waiver. The move is expected to poison the well for peace talks and lead to violent protests.\n\nThe decision comes amidst a broader shift in Middle East politics, with Saudi Arabia presenting a peace plan that would be more tilted towards the Israelis than any previous plan. The plan would give the Palestinians a state but only noncontiguous parts of the West Bank and limited sovereignty over their own territory.\n\n**Pope Francis and Palestinian Leaders Criticize US Decision**\n\nPope Francis called on the US to respect the status quo in Jerusalem, which both Israelis and Palestinians claim as their capital. The Palestinian representative to the UK, Manuel Hassassian, said that recognition of Jerusalem as Israel's capital would be the \"kiss of death\" to the peace process.\n\nThe decision has been widely criticized, with many seeing it as a betrayal of the US's long-standing commitment to a two-state solution. The move is expected to lead to increased tensions in the region and make it more difficult to achieve a lasting peace agreement."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A hacker going by The Dark Overlord claims to have stolen and released 10 episodes of the new season of Orange Is the New Black more than a month before its official release on Netflix. Variety reports the hacker was demanding an unspecified ransom from Netflix, uploading the first episode of the upcoming fifth season to a file-sharing site Friday as proof. The next nine episodes were uploaded early Saturday morning after The Dark Overlord claims Netflix refused to pay them. The hacker says they're \"quite ashamed to breathe the same air\" as Netflix, as they were being entirely \"reasonable and merciful\" with their ransom offer. Neither Variety nor the AP have been able to—legally—confirm the authenticity of the episodes uploaded by The Dark Overlord. The new Orange Is the New Black episodes were apparently stolen months ago. Netflix says a production vendor \"had its security compromised\"; Variety identifies that vendor as Larson Studios, a post-production facility. The Dark Overlord says they weren't able to steal the final three episodes of the season because they weren't finished yet. The Dark Overlord told the AP in February they wouldn't release any of the stolen episodes; it's unclear why that changed. The hacker also claims to have stolen TV series from ABC, National Geographic, Fox, and IFC via the hack at Larson Studios. They're reportedly seeking a \"modest\" ransom for those as well. The leak of new Orange is the New Black episodes could hurt Neflix's subscriber numbers and stock price. The FBI is investigating."], "length": 1236, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Hacker Leaks \"Orange Is the New Black\" Season 5 Episodes**\n\nA hacker, known as \"thedarkoverlord,\" has released the first 10 episodes of Netflix's \"Orange Is the New Black\" season 5 online, after allegedly failing to receive a response to extortion demands from the streaming service. The hacker claims to have obtained the episodes through a cyberattack on post-production studio Larson Studios in 2016, which also resulted in the theft of unreleased shows from ABC, Fox, National Geographic, and IFC.\n\nThe leaked episodes were shared on the Pirate Bay file-sharing site, and Netflix has confirmed that a production vendor used by several major TV studios had its security compromised. The company is investigating the breach and has not commented on the hacker's demands.\n\nThe hacker, who has also claimed to have stolen shows from other studios, is demanding a ransom in exchange for not releasing the stolen content. The demand is reportedly \"modest,\" but the impact of the leak on Netflix's subscriber growth and stock price remains to be seen.\n\nNetflix had previously announced that season 5 of \"Orange Is the New Black\" would be released on June 9, and the leak may potentially move up the release date. The show's creator, Jenji Kohan, has signed on for all upcoming seasons, and the show has been consistently Netflix's most-watched original series.\n\nThe leak is the latest in a series of high-profile cyberattacks on the entertainment industry, and highlights the growing threat of piracy and hacking in the digital age."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Forget satellite images and aerial searches—the best way to find Malaysia Airlines Flight MH370 may be with mathematical techniques dating back to the 18th century, the BBC reports. That's how Air France flight 447 was found in 2009, using \"Bayesian statistics\" to measure the probability of the plane being in one place or another. Named after Presbyterian minister and mathematician Thomas Bayes, the technique allowed experts to apply several factors to each point on a map: For example, what was the chance it crashed from mechanical failure? How far do planes tend to crash from their last known location? What was the chance that search teams missed debris in various locations? It's like picking a restaurant by balancing how full it is, what your favorite restaurant-review website says, and so on—except that experts hunting for Airbus A330 did that for each point where plane may have crashed in the Atlantic, Five Thirty-Eight notes. It was so hard that the US team of statisticians invited by France gave up, until they de-emphasized one statistic: that a plane's black box emits a signal after a crash 90% of the time. They changed their findings, and presto, the plane was found. Bayesian techniques have helped people find World War II U-boats, men overboard, and sunken treasure, but there's no evidence that Malaysia is employing them now. \"I suspect that they just guess, like professional baseball managers used to do before Moneyball,\" says a biostatistician."], "length": 3727, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Mysterious Disappearance of Malaysia Airlines Flight 370: A Bayesian Perspective**\n\nThe disappearance of Malaysia Airlines Flight 370 on March 8, 2014, remains one of the greatest aviation mysteries of all time. Despite extensive searches, the plane's fate and location remain unknown. Bayesian statistics, a mathematical approach developed by 18th-century Presbyterian minister Thomas Bayes, may hold the key to solving this enigma.\n\nIn 2009, Air France Flight 447 disappeared while flying from Rio de Janeiro to Paris, and statisticians used Bayesian methods to locate the wreckage. The approach involves assessing various scenarios, combining probabilities, and updating models based on new information. This method was instrumental in finding the wreckage, which was discovered two years later.\n\nIn the case of Malaysia Airlines Flight 370, Bayesian statistics can help refine the search area and estimate the probability of finding the plane. The approach considers multiple factors, including the plane's last known location, historical crash data, and the likelihood of deliberate diversion. However, the complexity of the search, with multiple possible locations and conflicting information, makes it challenging to apply Bayesian methods effectively.\n\nStatisticians argue that Bayesian techniques can adapt to changing information and provide a more accurate estimate of the plane's location. However, the lack of data and the vast search area make it difficult to apply these methods. Additionally, the possibility of the plane being deliberately diverted or hidden in a remote area complicates the search.\n\nThe search for Malaysia Airlines Flight 370 has been ongoing for years, with various teams and organizations involved. While some have expressed skepticism about the use of Bayesian statistics in this case, others argue that it is a valuable tool for refining the search area and estimating probabilities.\n\nUltimately, the fate of Malaysia Airlines Flight 370 remains a mystery, and it is possible that the plane may never be found. However, the application of Bayesian statistics in this case highlights the potential of mathematical approaches to solve complex problems and provide insights into seemingly intractable mysteries."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Damascus has been rocked by bombings for the past two days, with at least two large blasts hitting Syria's military headquarters there this morning. The Free Syrian Army claimed responsibility for the attack, said to be the largest since July explosions killed President Bashar al-Assad's brother-in-law and other key aides; the BBC reports that Assad would have been able to hear today's explosions from his palace. Gunfire broke out afterward, and Maya Nasser, a TV correspondent for the Iranian Press TV, was shot dead by sniper fire during a live broadcast, report the New York Times and Press TV. Other government facilities were hit by bombs yesterday, proving the opposition can still get past government security, but the regime is downplaying the scope of the attacks while the opposition says many of Assad's forces have been killed. In other news from Syria: UN Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon called for international action at the General Assembly yesterday, calling the conflict \"a regional calamity with global ramifications,\" the AP reports. Click for more from the General Assembly on Syria. Clashes have also been reported near Syria's border with Israel, leading the Israel Defense Forces to issue a statement declaring that \"fire from Syria leaking into Israel will not be accepted.\""], "length": 1465, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Global Conflict Escalates: Twin Blasts Rock Syria's Army Command Headquarters**\n\nA devastating series of twin blasts targeted Syria's army command headquarters in the capital, Damascus, on Wednesday, sparking hours of sporadic gunbattles and a raging fire. The explosions, which occurred just before 7 a.m., were heard several miles away and shattered the windows of nearby buildings. The blasts were followed by heavy gunfire, with witnesses describing panicked soldiers shooting in the air randomly as they ran.\n\nThe attacks, which were claimed by rebels fighting to topple President Bashar Assad's regime, resulted in the death of a Syrian national, 33-year-old Maya Nasser, a correspondent for Iranian Press TV. Nasser was shot and killed by a sniper while reporting on air. Two other Press TV staff members, including the Damascus Bureau Chief Hosein Mortada, were also injured in the attack.\n\nThe incident is the latest in a series of large blasts targeting security sites and symbols of regime power in Syria. The conflict, which began in March 2011, has killed nearly 30,000 people and has become a regional calamity with global ramifications. The United Nations has called for international action to stop the war, but Russia and China have vetoed Western-backed resolutions aimed at pressuring Assad to end the violence.\n\nThe international community has condemned the attacks, with US President Barack Obama pledging support for Syrians trying to oust Assad. The Syrian government has referred to rebels fighting to topple Assad's regime as terrorists, and has accused foreign powers of providing them with weapons.\n\nThe situation in Syria remains volatile, with clashes continuing in the capital and other parts of the country. The international community is watching the situation closely, with many calling for a peaceful resolution to the conflict."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A strong smell outside a California supermarket turned out to be someone who had decomposed in one of the store's entrance columns, ABC 7 reports. Police were called Saturday to the WinCo Foods in Lancaster after a plumber, told it was likely a septic problem, helped knock brick from the column and saw a shoe and a leg inside. \"It's been over 100 degrees up here every day,\" says LA County Sheriff's Lt. John Corina. \"I can't imagine being inside that column and just baking. It's a strange development, and if he died that way, it's a horrible way to die.\" Police say the victim may be a man who was stopped for a fake license plate Monday night, NBC Los Angeles reports. He crashed the vehicle, ran away from deputies, and got into the supermarket. The man \"ran up the steps where the managers [are],\" an eyewitness tells CBS Los Angeles. \"And somehow he got on the roof. Last I heard, he got away.\" From up there, he could have reached the store fascia and descended into the column. Whether he died from the fall or baked inside is unclear; he was found on his feet with an arm stuck behind his head. \"I saw some gooey liquid and it smelled really foul ... it was oozing out of the pillar onto the pavement,\" says a witness. \"It smelled like death.\""], "length": 1484, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Decomposing Body Found in Supermarket Column in Lancaster**\n\nA shocking discovery was made at a WinCo Foods supermarket in Lancaster, California, when a decomposing body was found inside a column. The body, later identified as 35-year-old Raymundo Rivera, was discovered on Saturday, August 11, 2018, after a store manager reported a strong smell. The Los Angeles County Sheriff's Department is leading the investigation, which may be linked to a suspect who led deputies on a foot chase on Monday, August 6, 2018.\n\nAccording to officials, the suspect, who was being chased for a fake license plate, crashed his car and escaped onto the roof of the supermarket. Witnesses reported that the suspect ran into the store and up the steps to the roof, where he was last seen. However, deputies were unable to locate him, and it is believed that he may have fallen or become trapped in the column.\n\nThe body was found in a state of decomposition, with a shoe and leg visible inside the column. An autopsy was pending to determine the cause of death. The investigation is ongoing, and anyone with information is encouraged to contact the Los Angeles County Sheriff's Department's Homicide Bureau at (323) 890-5500.\n\nThe discovery has left many in shock, with some investigators expressing concern about the conditions in which the body was found. \"It's one of those strange cases,\" said Lt. John Corina. \"He may have gotten inside there and gotten down to try and hide from the deputies and then couldn't get out.\" The incident has also raised questions about the safety of the supermarket and the surrounding area.\n\nThe store was closed as a precaution while the investigation continues, and customers were turned away from the store earlier in the day. The incident is a grim reminder of the dangers of crime and the importance of swift action in responding to emergency situations."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The US has sent two warships to the Black Sea and put planes on standby in Europe as it prepares to evacuate its Olympians from Sochi in the event of an attack. But officials fear their preparations might not be enough, reports NBC News, which describes the effort as \"logistically mind-boggling and diplomatically delicate.\" The big issue: The US can't do much without Russian permission, and it doesn't expect to get that. \"Something that looks like the US cavalry riding to rescue Russia … well, it's just hard to imagine that happening,\" one Russian expert said. The US skiing and snowboarding teams have signed up their own private security company, Global Rescue, which claims it has six aircraft ready for an evacuation if necessary. But having planes isn't the only issue; it's having runways. Sochi's airport is fairly small, so deciding which of the 85 countries competing to use it would be a nightmare. Other teams are also taking special security precautions, the Moscow Times reports. Britain, for example, is sending extra security personnel with its delegation. A German spokesman, meanwhile, said the country trusted Russia, but would \"look closely\" at the situation. \"We will have no aircraft, that is all that I can say.\""], "length": 2600, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "The Sochi Winter Olympics are set to take place in February, amidst heightened concerns about security. A catastrophic terrorist strike at the games would pose a significant challenge for the US, with over 200 American athletes at risk. The US military has plans in place to evacuate Americans using two warships in the Black Sea and planes already on standby in Europe. However, the US government's ability to intervene is limited, as the games are being held in Russia, and any rescue operation would depend heavily on President Vladimir Putin's approval.\n\nExperts warn that the level of danger in Sochi is higher than at any previous Olympics, with several groups threatening to attack the games. The US ski and snowboard team has hired a private security firm, Global Rescue, to protect its athletes in case of an emergency. Global Rescue will have five aircraft on standby and provide athletes with guidelines to minimize the possibility of becoming targets.\n\nRussia has introduced extensive security measures, including 40,000 police officers and 70,000 soldiers patrolling the nearby southern border with Georgia. The country's 58th Army unit will also be deployed to provide security. However, some nations, including Israel and Germany, are confident that Russian security agencies will do their job properly and that the games will be safe.\n\nThe US government has sent FBI agents to Sochi and Moscow for the duration of the games, and has made air and naval assets available to help Russia prevent possible terror attacks. However, the US Olympic Committee has declined to discuss the details of security for the American athletes or what would happen in the event of an evacuation.\n\nThe biathlon team has bought satellite phones for its coaches and is recommending that traveling families of its athletes purchase medical and evacuation insurance. Some American athletes, including those on the high-profile hockey team, have expressed concerns about the safety of the games and have told their families not to travel to Sochi to cheer them on.\n\nThe games take place at a time of frayed US-Russia relations, with the Edward Snowden affair and the Boston Marathon bombings still fresh in the minds of many. A large-scale attack on the games would trigger almost unimaginable complications, with over 85 countries and 2,500 athletes participating. Ultimately, the safety of the athletes will depend on the ability of the Russian authorities to provide sufficient protection from would-be attackers."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A New Orleans judge officially approved Robert Durst's February plea deal on a gun charge and sentenced him to seven years in prison on Wednesday, Reuters reports. For more than a year, the real estate heir has languished in a New Orleans prison after being nabbed with a handgun in his hotel room. He was busted for that felony just one day before the series finale of HBO's The Jinx, in which he famously appeared to confess to three murders by saying, \"What the hell did I do? Killed them all, of course.\" US District Judge Kurt Engelhardt also agreed that Durst can be transferred to a low-security prison with medical facilities in San Pedro, Calif., where the 73-year-old's attorneys say his \"advanced age and poor health\" can be better addressed, per the Advocate. In California, Durst will face a murder charge in the 2000 death of friend Susan Berman, the AP reports. (A Texas judge believes Durst once dumped a cat's head on her doorstep.)"], "length": 2091, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Robert Durst, a real estate heir and celebrity murder suspect, has been sentenced to seven years in prison for a federal gun charge. The sentence was agreed upon as part of a plea deal, which also resolved several pending charges against Durst in New York, Texas, and Louisiana state court. Durst, 73, will serve his time in a low-security federal prison facility on the Pacific coast, where he will await trial in California on a murder charge in connection with the 2000 death of his friend Susan Berman.\n\nDurst has been held in Louisiana since his March 2015 arrest on a charge of being a felon in possession of a firearm. He pleaded guilty to the gun charge in February and has been awaiting sentencing. The sentence was approved by U.S. District Judge Kurt Engelhardt, who also recommended that Durst serve his time in California.\n\nDurst's lawyers have maintained that he is innocent of the murder charge and has requested that he be housed at a federal prison with medical facilities near Los Angeles. Durst has a history of making incriminating statements, including a statement made in an HBO documentary that he had \"killed them all.\" However, his lawyers argue that this statement was made in a moment of weakness and does not reflect his true guilt.\n\nDurst's trial in California is expected to take place in mid-August, and his lawyers are confident that he will be acquitted of the murder charge. The case has garnered renewed interest since the conclusion of the HBO documentary series \"The Jinx,\" which chronicled several police investigations into Durst's life and crimes.\n\nOverall, Durst's sentencing marks a significant development in the case, but his lawyers remain optimistic that he will be exonerated of the murder charge."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The Republican-led House Benghazi committee released its final report Tuesday after two years of investigations. The 800-page report lays out the committee's findings on the 2012 attacks in Libya that left four Americans dead. Here's what you need to know: Benghazi is \"unlikely to be a potent tool for weakening Clinton\" in the general election, as the committee and report have little credibility and aren't seen as serious by many, the Los Angeles Times reports. Easily distilling the massive report, the Washington Post lays out its five \"most serious accusations,\" including that the CIA \"missed warning signs\" and \"misread how dangerous Libya ... was at the time.\" New evidence of “culpability or wrongdoing” on Clinton's part is lacking in the report, despite it being “one of the longest, costliest, and most bitterly partisan congressional investigations in history,\" according to the New York Times. Given a lack of new evidence against Clinton, the Weekly Standard uses the report as a tool for blaming Obama. “The Obama administration knowingly provided the American people a false story about the Benghazi attack, its causes, and its consequences,\" the Standard states. How various government agencies responded to the Benghazi crisis is one of the more interesting aspects of the report. According to Politico, a conference call in the midst of the attack included a conversation about whether Marines should wear their uniforms if deployed. Marines changed in and out of their uniforms multiple times while waiting for an answer and were never deployed. According to the Hill, a Democratic lawmaker on the House Benghazi committee calls it \"one of the saddest exercises I've ever engaged in\" during 20 years in Congress. He accuses Republicans of hiding the final report from Democrats to avoid having it fact-checked. Zeroing in on what it calls the \"most revealing paragraph\" in the report, Vox concludes there's a pattern of spinning \"non-damning facts as damning\" for the Obama administration. The paragraph in question admits there were no US forces close enough to prevent the attack but insists that, in itself, was a failure on the part of the White House. \"I think it's time to move on,\" the AP quotes Clinton as saying after the release of the report, which she says includes nothing not previously discovered by an accountability board."], "length": 3608, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "The recent news has been dominated by the Benghazi attack, which occurred on September 11, 2012, in Libya. The attack resulted in the deaths of four US diplomats, including Ambassador Chris Stevens. The incident has been the subject of intense scrutiny and investigation, with multiple reports and committees releasing their findings.\n\nA House Benghazi report, authored by Republicans, has been released, which criticizes former Secretary of State Hillary Clinton and other officials for their handling of the situation. The report claims that Clinton and her team falsely blamed the violence on an anti-Muslim video, which was later found to be a fabrication. The report also accuses Clinton of not adequately addressing concerns about growing extremism in Libya.\n\nHowever, a Democratic rebuttal to the report has been released, which disputes many of the findings and claims that the Republicans have been overly partisan in their investigation. The Democratic report concludes that there was no stand-down order and no evidence of gun trading, and that Clinton and her team did not adequately address concerns about growing extremism in Libya.\n\nIn addition to the Benghazi report, a State Department report has been released, which details the events leading up to the attack. The report reveals that State Department officials were aware of the growing threat of extremism in Libya, but failed to take adequate action to prevent the attack.\n\nA separate report by the CIA has also been released, which confirms that the attack was a terrorist attack, rather than a spontaneous protest. The report also reveals that the CIA had warned the State Department about the growing threat of extremism in Libya, but that their warnings were ignored.\n\nThe Benghazi attack has also been the subject of a White House press briefing, in which officials claimed that the attack was a terrorist attack, rather than a spontaneous protest. However, the briefing was criticized for its lack of transparency and for failing to address many of the questions raised by reporters.\n\nIn other news, a US soldier from the 632th Maintenance Company of the 87th Supply Battalion of the 3rd Infantry Division has been photographed distributing new uniforms in 2005. The photo was taken by Getty Images and has been used to highlight the contrast between the military's focus on uniforms and the State Department's focus on diplomacy.\n\nFinally, a report by the Internet Archive has been released, which details the efforts to archive web pages and preserve links to broken websites. The report highlights the importance of preserving the internet's history and making it more accessible to the public.\n\nOverall, the recent news has been dominated by the Benghazi attack and the ongoing investigation into its causes. The reports and committees have"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A former police bloodhound handler told a court on Monday that during the search for UVa student Hannah Graham, the animal detected her scent outside the car and apartment of suspect Jesse Matthew—and the scent of \"fear and adrenaline\" where she was allegedly attacked. The handler was testifying during a pretrial hearing where lawyers for Matthew, who is already serving three life terms in prison for a 2005 sexual assault, unsuccessfully challenged the search warrants police obtained for Matthew's home and vehicle, the AP reports. He said the 7-year-old dog, Shaker, traced Graham's scent from downtown Charlottesville to a mulch pile at an industrial site, where the smell of fear was strongest. Matthew, 33, faces a capital murder charge in the 2014 death of Graham, whose body was found on an abandoned property around six weeks after she disappeared. He has also been charged with the 2009 murder of a Virginia Tech student. At Monday's hearing, another police witness testified that, contrary to what the defense claimed, the scent trail was consistent with surveillance videos that showed Graham wandering around, reports WSET. The officer shared text message records indicating that she was lost the night she disappeared.The Washington Post notes that Matthew is seen walking alongside Graham in some of the surveillance videos."], "length": 1203, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Hannah Graham Case: New Developments in Investigation**\n\nA pretrial hearing for Jesse Matthew Jr., the man charged with killing University of Virginia student Hannah Graham, took place in Charlottesville, Virginia. The hearing, which lasted a day, revealed new details about the investigation and the role of a police bloodhound in tracking Graham's scent.\n\nAccording to former Louisa County Sheriff's Office Detective Buck Garner, the bloodhound, Shaker, detected traces of Graham's scent inside Matthew's apartment and on the car door. The dog also found Graham's scent at an industrial site about a mile from downtown Charlottesville, where it alerted Garner to a scent indicating \"fear and adrenaline.\" This suggests that Graham may have been attacked near a mulch pile at the site.\n\nThe search warrant for Matthew's apartment and vehicle was obtained based on the bloodhound's findings, which were supported by witness statements and video surveillance evidence. Witness accounts placed Graham and Matthew together on the night of her disappearance, and one witness reported seeing them go to a bar on the Downtown Mall. Matthew also bought them each drinks using his debit card.\n\nThe defense attorney, Doug Ramseur, challenged the search warrant, arguing that the search dog's findings were inconsistent with witness accounts and video surveillance evidence. However, Albemarle County Circuit Judge Cheryl Higgins ruled that there were no \"instances of deliberate misstatements and reckless disregard\" of the truth in the affidavit supporting the search warrant.\n\nThe prosecution is seeking the death penalty for Matthew, who also faces first-degree murder charges in the 2009 death of Virginia Tech student Morgan Harrington. Matthew is serving a life prison term for a 2005 attempted murder and sexual assault in northern Virginia.\n\nThe case is ongoing, and the court is expected to continue hearing evidence and testimony in the coming days."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A white-collar crook is facing extra prison time for having his son shoot him in the legs with a 20-gauge shotgun, the BBC reports. Shannon Egeland, 43, was already facing 10 years for taking part in a vast mortgage-fraud scheme when he had his 17-year-old son shoot him by a road in Caldwell, Idaho, in an attempt to avoid prison. \"The psychological and emotional destruction defendant caused this minor child is unimaginable,\" US attorney Scott Bradford wrote in a sentencing memo. Back in 2009, Egeland was co-owner of an Oregon development company that defrauded banks out of $20 million in loans that he and a partner used to fund a lavish lifestyle. Facing prison, Egeland took out disability insurance and had his son aim for his legs. Egeland told police he'd been attacked after stopping to assist a pregnant driver, but investigators got suspicious when his pricey BMW, his wallet, and his cellphone weren't taken, the Idaho Statesman reports. Then they learned about his recent insurance, and the whole thing unravelled. Now Egeland is facing an extra 3 years and 10 months for his roadside scheme and had his leg amputated from the shotgun blast. Bradford called him a \"self-absorbed, opportunistic narcissist,\" the Oregonian reports, but Egeland—who is now on three medications for mental health—has cast his own judgment. \"What bothers me the most is my son—the pain is on him,'' he says. \"If I could take it all back, I would, but I can't. That will haunt me the rest of my life.''"], "length": 1541, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A convicted white-collar criminal, Shannon Egeland, has had his jail sentence extended by a judge after he staged a shooting to delay his prison term. Egeland, 43, had his left leg amputated in 2014 after his 17-year-old son shot him in the leg with a shotgun as part of an elaborate insurance scam. The scam involved Egeland applying for a disability insurance policy just seven days before he was set to report for a 10-year federal prison term for his role in a mortgage fraud case.\n\nEgeland's plot backfired, and he was sentenced to 10 years in prison for his role in the mortgage fraud case. However, the judge took into account Egeland's guilty plea to conspiracy to commit wire fraud in the insurance scam and his interstate failure to pay child support, adding three years and 10 months to his original sentence.\n\nDuring the sentencing hearing, Egeland expressed remorse for his actions, stating that the pain was not on him, but on his son, who was manipulated into shooting him. Egeland also acknowledged that he had spent time reflecting on his past actions and was seeking mental health counseling and treatment.\n\nProsecutors described Egeland as a \"menace to society\" who has been on a crime spree since 2004, motivated by greed and a desire to manipulate those around him. The judge ordered Egeland to pay $90,011 in child support and to undergo mental health treatment and counseling while in prison.\n\nEgeland's case highlights the devastating consequences of his actions, not only for himself but also for his son, who was left to deal with the emotional trauma of being manipulated into committing a violent act. The case serves as a reminder of the importance of addressing mental health issues and seeking help when needed."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The Tesla Model X that slammed into a highway barrier in California last week and killed the 38-year-old Apple engineer at the wheel had been on autopilot at the time of the crash, Tesla confirms in a blog post. But the company says driver Walter Huang \"received several visual and one audible hands-on warning earlier in the drive,\" adding that \"the driver’s hands were not detected on the wheel for six seconds prior to the collision.\" Tesla also laid some blame on state highway officials, asserting that \"the reason this crash was so severe is because the crash attenuator, a highway safety barrier which is designed to reduce the impact into a concrete lane divider, had been crushed in a prior accident without being replaced.\" The fact that the vehicle was on autopilot is likely to be controversial because, as ABC News notes, driver Huang had reportedly been concerned about it. His brother told KGO that Huang had brought his Model X back to the dealership complaining that when he used the feature, the car kept swerving toward the very same barrier that it eventually struck in the fatal collision. The NTSB is investigating the crash, including the fire that broke out after the collision. The accident follows another in Arizona in which a self-driving Uber struck and killed a pedestrian."], "length": 2070, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tesla Autopilot Crash: Investigation Ongoing, Safety Concerns Raised**\n\nA fatal crash involving a Tesla Model X occurred on March 23rd, resulting in the death of 38-year-old Walter Huang. The National Transportation Safety Board (NTSB) is investigating the accident, which took place while the vehicle was on Autopilot.\n\nAccording to Tesla, the driver had received several visual and one audible hands-on warning earlier in the drive, but the vehicle's hands were not detected on the wheel for six seconds prior to the collision. The driver had also complained to the dealership about the Autopilot system veering towards a specific barrier on multiple occasions.\n\nThe crash occurred on the 101 Highway near Mountain View, California, where a crash attenuator had been crushed in a prior accident without being replaced. Tesla has never seen this level of damage to a Model X in any other crash.\n\nHuang's brother, Will, stated that Walter had complained \"seven to 10 times\" the car would swivel towards the same exact barrier during Autopilot. The California Department of Transportation (CalTrans) has released a statement saying that replacement work was scheduled, but the fix was delayed due to storms.\n\nThe incident has raised concerns about the safety of Autopilot and autonomous vehicles. Tesla has defended its system, stating that it \"unequivocally makes the world safer for the vehicle occupants, pedestrians and cyclists.\" However, the incident has sparked renewed scrutiny of the technology, with Arizona's governor suspending Uber's license to test autonomous vehicles on public roads.\n\nIn a broader context, the incident highlights the need for improved safety measures on highways, particularly with regards to crash attenuators. The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA) has reported that there is one automotive fatality every 86 million miles across all vehicles from all manufacturers.\n\nOverall, the investigation into the Tesla Autopilot crash is ongoing, and the incident serves as a reminder of the importance of prioritizing safety on our roads."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["After Robin Williams' sudden death, the tributes are rolling in, with many recalling a comic genius grappling with private challenges. A sampling: When it comes to comedy, \"there wasn't a faster brain on the planet,\" writes Tony Hicks in the Contra Costa Times. We'll be hearing \"a litany of tributes about one of the great comedians who ever lived. Much of it will be some of the same adjectives used when any great performer dies—especially before their time. But in Williams' case, most of it will be true.\" Early in his career, \"it was clear that Mr. Williams was one of the most explosively, exhaustingly, prodigiously verbal comedians who ever lived,\" AO Scott writes in the New York Times. And beyond that, \"his essential persona as an entertainer combined neediness and generosity, intelligence and kindness, in ways that were charming and often unexpectedly moving.\" Williams may have gotten his start long before Aladdin, but he gave a \"generous and humble\" gift to the millennial generation, writes Daniel D'Addario at Salon. \"Williams will be remembered for his edgy comedy and for dramatic turns, but for people my age, Williams was our childhood,\" from Mrs. Doubtfire to Jumanji to Flubber. In the Los Angeles Times, Kenneth Turan recalls a moment when, facing an onslaught of photographers, Williams asked him—in the voice of a fly from a 1950s sci-fi flick—to \"help me, Ken. Help me.\" The experience stuck with him and seems particularly resonant today, he writes."], "length": 1921, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Robin Williams, the beloved actor and comedian, passed away on Monday at the age of 63. He was remembered for his incredible talent, generosity, and ability to connect with audiences of all ages. Williams' impact on the entertainment industry was immense, and his legacy will be felt for years to come.\n\nBorn in the 1950s, Williams rose to fame in the 1970s and 1980s with his stand-up comedy and roles in films such as \"Good Morning, Vietnam,\" \"Dead Poets Society,\" and \"The Fisher King.\" However, it was his iconic performance as the Genie in Disney's \"Aladdin\" that cemented his place in the hearts of many. Williams' ability to bring joy and comfort to children and adults alike was unparalleled, and his movies continue to be enjoyed by generations.\n\nWilliams' transition from comedy to drama was also noteworthy. He took on roles that were often dark and complex, showcasing his incredible range as an actor. His Oscar-nominated performances in \"Good Will Hunting\" and \"The Fisher King\" demonstrated his ability to tackle challenging material with ease.\n\nThe news of Williams' passing has sent shockwaves through the entertainment industry and beyond. Many have shared their memories of him, praising his kindness, generosity, and infectious energy. Fellow actors, comedians, and fans have taken to social media to pay tribute to his legacy.\n\nOne journalist who had the privilege of meeting Williams on several occasions described him as a \"wired, complex, brilliantly funny individual\" who was always eager to take on new challenges. Williams' final performance, a stage whisper to a journalist, has become an iconic moment in his career, symbolizing his ability to connect with audiences on a deep level.\n\nAs the world mourns the loss of this talented individual, it is clear that Robin Williams' legacy will continue to inspire and entertain for years to come. His impact on the entertainment industry and the lives of those who knew him will never be forgotten."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Police in the country's smallest state capital are investigating something that hasn't happened there in living memory: a murder. Police in Vermont say they have issued an arrest warrant for 29-year-old Jayveon Caballero, who is accused of fatally shooting Markus Austin in Montpelier early Sunday after an altercation, the Times Argus reports. Witnesses told investigators that Austin, 33, assaulted Caballero’s girlfriend, Desiree Cary, during the altercation outside a bar in the nearby city of Barre early Sunday. Police say Caballero later confronted Austin outside his apartment and shot him dead. The body was found in the parking lot of Austin's apartment complex. A Vermont State Police spokesman says Caballero, who knew Austin before the shooting, fled the town and probably the state after the shooting, WCAX reports. The police spokesman says Caballero is believed to have boarded a Greyhound bus bound for New York City on Sunday morning, which made numerous stops in other states along the way. Later Sunday, Cary, 22, was arrested on apparently unrelated charges of selling heroin and crack cocaine. With around 7,500 residents, Montpelier has the smallest population of any state capital, the AP notes. Police Chief Anthony Facos says this is the city's first murder since a woman shot her husband in the 1920s."], "length": 1264, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Multiple Shootings in Montpelier, Vermont Leave One Dead and Another on the Run**\n\nA weekend shooting in Montpelier, Vermont, has left one man dead and another suspect on the run. Markus Austin, 33, was found shot outside his apartment complex on Sunday morning, and police believe he was killed by Jayveon Caballero, 29, a former Vermont Frost Heaves basketball player.\n\nAccording to police, Caballero and Austin were involved in an altercation outside a bar in Barre on Sunday morning, which escalated into a fight. Witnesses reported that Caballero's girlfriend, Desiree Cary, was also assaulted by Austin during the altercation. Caballero later confronted Austin outside his apartment and shot him.\n\nCary, 22, was taken into custody on Sunday evening on unrelated charges related to a drug investigation. She was released from custody after posting bail.\n\nPolice have issued an arrest warrant for Caballero, who is believed to have boarded a bus in White River Junction on Sunday and may have fled the state. The bus was headed to New York City, but authorities are unsure if Caballero got off at a stop before that.\n\nThe shooting is the first murder in Montpelier in almost a century, and police are working to locate Caballero. Anyone with information about his location or the shooting is asked to contact the Vermont State Police.\n\nThe victim, Markus Austin, was a former member of the Vermont Frost Heaves, a semi-pro basketball team. The incident has sent shockwaves through the small city of Montpelier, which has a population of around 7,500 residents.\n\nThe investigation is ongoing, and police are working to piece together the events leading up to the shooting."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Look up at the sky on Jan. 31 and you may witness a sight unseen for 150 years. For the first time since 1866, a total lunar eclipse will occur with the blue moon, or second full moon of the month, which—like the one visible on New Year's Day—will also be a supermoon. Got all that? When the moon is at or near the closest point to Earth in its orbit (aka, a supermoon), it appears 14% bigger and 30% brighter than full moons that occur at the farthest point in the moon's orbit, though the moon will lose brightness as it enters Earth's shadow, according to NASA. Because of the way Earth's atmosphere bends light, the eclipsed moon will take on a reddish hue, something often referred to as a blood moon, making this rare celestial event \"a super blue blood moon eclipse,\" according to the Miami Herald. The next blue moon eclipse won't occur until 2028. The eclipse will last for 3.5 hours, with the total eclipse stretching for 77 minutes, reports Sky & Telescope. Those along the Pacific Rim from Alaska and northwestern Canada to central and eastern Asia, Indonesia, New Zealand, and Australia will get the best view of totality during the middle of the night. However, partial phases of the eclipse should be visible under a clear sky in central North America, western Asia, India, the Middle East and eastern Europe, per Space.com. North American viewers should look up as the moon sets on the morning of Jan. 31, while those on the opposite side of the world should see a partial eclipse as the moon rises. Not in an ideal viewing area? The Virtual Telescope plans to livestream the total eclipse from Australia, which you can watch here, per Quartz. (Bogus glasses damaged a solar eclipse viewer's eyes.)"], "length": 2076, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Rare Lunar Event: Super Blue Moon Total Eclipse**\n\nA rare and spectacular lunar event is set to occur on January 31, 2018, as the Moon will pass through the Earth's umbra, causing a total lunar eclipse. This event is particularly significant as it is a Blue Moon, which occurs when there are two full moons in a single month. The Moon will also be a Supermoon, appearing 14% larger and 30% brighter than usual due to its proximity to Earth.\n\nThe total lunar eclipse will be visible from eastern Asia, across the Pacific to western North America, and will be visible from Australia. The eclipse will begin at 11:48am UTC and reach its maximum at 1:30pm UTC. Viewers in eastern North America will need to wake up early to catch the partial eclipse.\n\nThe Virtual Telescope Project will provide a livestream of the event, allowing viewers to watch from the comfort of their own homes. The event is a rare opportunity for astronomers and skywatchers to witness a total lunar eclipse, and is expected to attract a large following.\n\n**When and Where to See the Event**\n\n* Date: January 31, 2018\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["America's ambassador to Syria visited one of the cities worst-hit by the crackdown against anti-government protesters yesterday, reports CNN. Robert Ford went to the city of Hama \"to make absolutely clear with his physical presence that we stand with those Syrians who are expressing their right to speak for change,\" an embassy spokeswoman says. An estimated 500,000 people joined an anti-government rally in the city last Friday. The Syrian government accused Ford of going to the city without authorization, saying the visit is \"clear evidence of the US involvement in the ongoing events in Syria and its bids to aggravate the situations which destabilize Syria.'' An embassy spokeswoman says the Syrian government was told a delegation would be visiting Hama, although no Syrian \"handlers\" were taken and officials weren't informed about the ambassador's presence. The city has been a symbol of opposition since 1982, when a crackdown ordered by the present Syrian leader's father killed around 20,000 people, al-Jazeera notes."], "length": 1397, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**US Ambassador Visits Hama, Syria Amidst Protests and Crackdowns**\n\nIn recent days, the city of Hama, Syria, has been at the center of intense protests and violence against the government of President Bashar al-Assad. US Ambassador Robert Ford visited Hama on Thursday as part of an effort to show US support for Syrians fighting for democracy. Ford's visit was seen as a significant gesture of solidarity with the people of Hama, who have been wracked by violence and a general strike following a series of peaceful demonstrations.\n\nThe protests in Hama have been met with a fierce crackdown by security forces, resulting in numerous arrests and deaths. According to reports, at least 13 protesters have been killed across the country, including five in Homs, two in the capital's commercial neighborhood Midan, and six in the Dumair area east of Damascus. Syrian state-run TV blamed the deaths on snipers from \"armed gangs.\"\n\nDespite the violence, the protests in Hama have been largely peaceful, with marchers carrying olive branches and calling for an end to the government's grip on power. The city has become a symbol of resistance to injustice in Syria, with hundreds of thousands of protesters gathering in the streets.\n\nThe US State Department has condemned the violence and called for an end to the crackdowns. US officials have also expressed concern about the posture of the security forces, who have been accused of using excessive force against protesters.\n\nIn response to the protests, the Syrian government has accused the US of interfering in its affairs and attempting to incite an escalation in the situation. The Syrian foreign ministry has summoned the US ambassador for talks, citing the US ambassador's visit to Hama without prior permission.\n\nMeanwhile, an estimated 1,000 Syrians have fled Hama in fear of another military crackdown on protests. The London-based Syrian Observatory for Human Rights has reported that security forces have killed at least 23 civilians in the town of Salamiyah, where many of the fleeing residents have taken refuge.\n\nThe situation in Hama remains volatile, with reports of ongoing violence and arrests. However, the protests have also brought a sense of hope and solidarity among Syrians, who are demanding an end to the government's authoritarian rule and an end to the decades-long grip of the Baath Party on power."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The FBI is reportedly investigating a sophisticated—but unsuccessful—attempt to hack into the Democratic National Committee's voter database. The DNC learned of the attempt on Tuesday, CNN reports. The attack, which was detected by a cloud service provider and a security research company, involved a fake login page intended to gather usernames and passwords for a service called Votebuilder, which hosts the DNC's voter database. It's a method called \"spearphishing,\" according to the New York Times. \"It was very convincing,\" Mike Murray of the cybersecurity firm Lookout tells CNN, adding that it would be tough to tell the real page from the fake one, even if they were side-by-side. The fake login page was ultimately removed by the cloud hosting company DigitalOcean. The company's chief security officer says the threat was identified before the attack was launched. \"We see no evidence that any sensitive data was stolen,\" he says. A DNC official tells the Times that it is not clear who the hackers were in this case. But, the paper recalls, a 2016 hack of the DNC was ultimately traced to Russia. This latest attempt, DNC Chief Security Officer Bob Lord tells the Times, \"is further proof that there are constant threats as we head into midterm elections and we must remain vigilant in order to prevent future attacks.\" Earlier this week, Microsoft reported that it had uncovered new Russian hacking attempts targeting conservative organizations in the US."], "length": 716, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Cyber Threats to Midterm Elections: A Growing Concern**\n\nThe US is facing a growing concern of cyber threats to the midterm elections, with several incidents reported in recent weeks. The Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) is investigating an incident involving the Democratic National Committee (DNC), which was allegedly targeted by hackers.\n\nAccording to officials, the DNC's voter file was the target of a sophisticated attempt to hack into its database. However, the party has confirmed that the incident was an unauthorized \"simulated phishing test\" and not a real attack. The DNC had contacted the FBI on Tuesday morning after detecting the suspicious activity, and it is believed that the potential attack was thwarted.\n\nThis incident is part of a larger trend of cyber threats to the US electoral process. Microsoft has reported that hackers tied to Russian intelligence have targeted the Senate and conservative think tanks in the US, creating fake websites to gather information. Facebook has also identified disinformation efforts targeting people around the world, including a potential campaign to disrupt the midterms.\n\nThe White House cybersecurity coordinator, Rob Joyce, has warned that the cyber age has changed elections forever, with many systems vulnerable to manipulation. The focus has shifted from stealing secrets to imposing national power, and the opportunities for foreign and domestic manipulation are numerous.\n\nThe midterms are just around the corner, and political groups and government organizations are on high alert for new hacks and foreign meddling. The FBI is investigating the incident involving the DNC, and other organizations are taking steps to protect themselves from cyber threats.\n\nIn summary, the US is facing a growing concern of cyber threats to the midterm elections, with several incidents reported in recent weeks. The FBI is investigating an incident involving the DNC, and other organizations are taking steps to protect themselves from cyber threats. The midterms are a critical time for the US electoral process, and it is essential that all necessary measures are taken to ensure the integrity of the elections."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Another fatality in the Boston bombings has been identified: The family of 29-year-old Krystle Campbell says she was killed as she and a friend were cheering on another friend who was racing, reports WCVB. \"This is just a waste,\" says her mother. “Everyone that knew her loved her. She was always smiling. You couldn’t ask for a better daughter. She was the best.” Campbell was a restaurant manager, reports the Boston Herald, which notes an especially wrenching detail: Her father was initially told she was alive, but when he got to the ICU, he realized it was his daughter's friend who had survived. Campbell is the second of the three people killed to be identified. The first was 8-year-old Martin Richard, and his father, Bill, released a statement today thanking supporters and asking for privacy, reports WBZ. \"My dear son Martin has died from injuries sustained in the attack on Boston. My wife and daughter are both recovering from serious injuries. We thank our family and friends, those we know and those we have never met, for their thoughts and prayers. I ask that you continue to pray for my family as we remember Martin. We also ask for your patience and for privacy as we work to simultaneously grieve and recover. Thank you.\" The third victim was a grad student at Boston University, but the person's identity is being withheld until the family says it's OK to release, reports USA Today."], "length": 2030, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Boston Marathon Bombings: A City in Mourning**\n\nThe Boston Marathon bombings on Monday, April 15, resulted in the deaths of three people and injured hundreds more. The victims were identified as Martin Richard, an 8-year-old boy from Dorchester, Krystle Campbell, a 29-year-old restaurant manager from Medford, and a 23-year-old Boston University graduate student from China, whose name has not been released pending family approval.\n\nThe bombings occurred just before 3 p.m. near the finish line of the marathon, and the blasts were followed by a massive police response. The FBI has stated that there are no known additional threats, and officials have promised to \"go to the ends of the Earth\" to find those responsible.\n\nThe city of Boston has come together to support the victims' families and to heal from the tragedy. A vigil was held on Tuesday night for Martin Richard at Garvey Park in Dorchester, and hundreds attended. The Richard family has released a statement thanking the public for their thoughts and prayers.\n\nThe city has also seen a strong response from the community, with many people leaving flowers and balloons outside the homes of the victims. The Salvation Army has mobilized to provide food, drinks, and emotional and spiritual care to those affected.\n\nDespite the tragedy, the city has shown resilience and determination. The mayor has promised that the city will not be intimidated by terror, and the police have increased security measures at bus and train stations and airports.\n\nThe nation has rallied around Boston, with President Obama praising first responders and others who helped the injured. Pope Francis has also sent words of condolence, and the city is holding an interfaith service on Thursday.\n\nAs the city begins to heal, the focus is on supporting the victims' families and finding those responsible for the bombings. The tragedy has brought the community together, and the city will come out stronger in the end."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["There are more obese or overweight people in the world today than there were people of any weight in 1935, the most comprehensive look at worldwide obesity in decades warns. The 188-country study found that there are 2.1 billion overweight or obese people in the world, making up around 30% of the world's population, up from 20% in 1980, USA Today reports. Not a single country has recorded a decline in obesity over the last 30 years. \"We hoped there would be some examples of success that you could latch onto,\" a study co-author says. \"But there's a complete lack of success stories in bringing down obesity.\" The study published in British medical journal the Lancet found that the Middle East and north Africa had seen the biggest weight gains, though the US still has the largest number of obese people, with 87 million of the world's 671 million obese people, reports Reuters. Other countries, however, have even higher obesity rates, including Tonga, where more than 50% of all adults are obese. \"Two-thirds of the obese population actually resides in developing countries,\" says one of the researchers; they warn that obesity rates are rising among rich and poor countries, men and women, and adults and children alike."], "length": 1409, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Global Obesity Epidemic: A Growing Concern**\n\nA recent study published in the Lancet medical journal has revealed that obesity is a pressing public health issue affecting nearly 30% of the world's population, with 2.1 billion individuals worldwide classified as either obese or overweight. The study, which analyzed data from 188 countries over a 33-year period, found that obesity rates have increased by 28% in adults and 47% in children, with no country succeeding in cutting its obesity rate.\n\nThe United States leads the world in obesity, with 13% of its population classified as obese, despite having less than 5% of the global population. Other countries with high obesity rates include Bahrain, Egypt, Saudi Arabia, Oman, and Kuwait. The study also found that obesity is a complex problem fueled by the availability of cheap, fatty, sugary, salty, high-calorie \"junk food\" and the rise of sedentary lifestyles.\n\nThe consequences of obesity are severe, with chronic complications killing approximately 3.4 million adults annually. The World Health Organization has launched a commission to study rising obesity among children worldwide and suggest solutions, with a report due in early 2015.\n\nThe study's findings highlight the need for a comprehensive approach to address the global obesity epidemic. While there is no single cause of obesity, the report suggests that factors such as increased calorie intake, changes in diet composition, decreased physical activity, and changes in the mix of bacteria that live in human guts may contribute to the problem.\n\nThe report's authors emphasize that obesity is not just a cosmetic issue, but a major risk factor for morbidity and mortality. The study's results underscore the importance of addressing the global obesity epidemic through a combination of policy changes, public education, and individual actions to promote healthy lifestyles.\n\n**Key Statistics:**\n\n* 2.1 billion individuals worldwide classified as obese or overweight\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["It's been a year since Katie Holmes filed for divorce from Tom Cruise, and apparently Cruise is feeling sentimental. A source tells UK magazine Look the actor sent Holmes an \"emotional letter\" looking to, at the very least, be friends again, Australia's News Network reports. \"It's like he's finally recognized that Katie will always be the love of his life,\" the source says. Even so, \"It won't have been an over the top letter—it has been a year and I'm sure Tom will have been aware that Katie wouldn't be interested in running straight back into his arms.\" Sources close to Holmes say she was \"touched\" by the letter, which doesn't sound promising for Cruise. \"Tom would no doubt take her back in a second, but I don't think that's what Katie wants,\" says one. (Just to keep things in perspective, we'll point out that Look is the sort of magazine that also runs headlines like, \"Royal Bodyguard Warns: 'Kate Middleton's Baby Is In Danger!'\") In other news, Huffington Post notes that it's Cruise's 51st birthday."], "length": 790, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Weekly News Summary**\n\nThis week, several news stories have made headlines. In the world of entertainment, Katie Holmes has reportedly received an emotional letter from her ex-husband Tom Cruise. The letter, which Cruise allegedly wrote in an attempt to rekindle their friendship, has had a profound effect on Holmes, who has been slowly building a new life for herself in New York City.\n\nCruise and Holmes divorced in July last year, with Holmes filing legal papers to end the marriage. Despite their split, Cruise has taken the surprising step of writing to his ex in an attempt to reconnect. Sources close to the former Dawson's Creek star say that Cruise's letter has been \"cathartic\" for Holmes, who has been \"genuinely touched\" by his words.\n\nIn other news, the world of technology has seen a significant development. Alexa Internet has been donating its crawl data to the Internet Archive, which is then added to the Wayback Machine. This data, which is collected every day, provides a unique insight into the internet's history and will be invaluable for researchers and historians.\n\nIn addition to these news stories, LOOK magazine has released its latest issue, which features a range of trends and fashion must-haves. The magazine's editor has picked out the key trends to start wearing now, from high street fashion buys to celebrity secrets.\n\nOverall, this week's news has been dominated by the personal lives of celebrities, with Cruise and Holmes' reunion making headlines. However, the world of technology has also seen a significant development, with the donation of Alexa Internet's crawl data to the Internet Archive."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The investigation into the mysterious deaths of a billionaire and his wife is now being led by homicide detectives, police say—suggesting the deaths of Barry and Honey Sherman were either murder-suicide or a double murder. Police, who have described the deaths as \"suspicious,\" revealed Sunday that the cause of death for both Shermans was \"ligature neck compression,\" a form of strangulation, the CBC reports. Sources have told the media that the couple were found hanging near an indoor pool in the basement of their Toronto mansion. Police say they haven't found any sign of forced entry, or a note that could explain what happened to 75-year-old pharmaceutical tycoon Barry Sherman and his 70-year-old wife, both renowned philanthropists. The couple's four children have rejected what they say are \"rumors\" that the deaths are being investigated as a murder-suicide, and many of the influential couple's friends agree. \"There is absolutely zero debate in my mind, this was a double homicide,\" says Canadian Sen. Linda Frum, a longtime friend of the couple, per the New York Times. The Globe and Mail reports that just days before the bodies were found, Honey Sherman was enthusiastically making plans for their annual trip to Florida, emailing friends that she would be traveling to the state on Dec. 18 and Barry Sherman would join her a week later. The couple also sent out invitations last week to a dinner party at their winter home in Palm Beach."], "length": 2360, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Canadian billionaire Barry Sherman, 75, and his wife Honey Sherman, 70, were found dead in their Toronto mansion on Friday. The couple's bodies were discovered by a real estate agent who had been helping to sell their home, which was listed for $7 million. According to police, the cause of death for both deceased was ligature neck compression, meaning strangulation.\n\nThe investigation into the deaths is being led by the Toronto homicide team, and police have not confirmed whether foul play was involved. However, some news outlets have quoted police sources suggesting that the case may be a murder-suicide. The Shermans' family has denied this theory, stating that it is \"inconsistent with the rumours regrettably circulated in the media\" and that they are \"shocked\" by the reports.\n\nBarry Sherman was the founder of generic pharmaceuticals giant Apotex Inc., which he founded in 1974. His net worth was estimated to be $4.77 billion, making him the 15th richest person in Canada. He was known for his dedication to his work and was described as a \"workaholic\" by friends and colleagues.\n\nHoney Sherman, on the other hand, was a dedicated philanthropist who served on the boards of several charitable organizations, including the Baycrest Foundation and the York University Foundation. She was also known for her warm and outgoing personality, and was described as \"amazing\" by former Apotex employee Vinay Chopra.\n\nThe couple's deaths have sent shockwaves through the business and philanthropic communities, with many paying tribute to their contributions to the city of Toronto and Canada. Toronto Mayor John Tory described Barry Sherman as a \"reserved man\" who \"created hundreds and hundreds of jobs for people in the city of Toronto,\" while Postmedia CEO Paul Godfrey described Honey Sherman as a \"dedicated philanthropist\" who \"pushed and pushed\" to make a difference in the world.\n\nAs the investigation into the deaths continues, friends and colleagues of the Shermans are struggling to come to terms with the tragedy. The couple's bodies were removed from their home on stretchers, and a makeshift memorial was set up outside their mansion. The police have not confirmed whether there are any suspects or motives, but the case remains a mystery that is being closely watched by the public."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The final round of voting began in Egypt today, and it looks like the Muslim Brotherhood could walk off with a controlling majority of seats in Parliament's lower house. The Muslim Brotherhood's Freedom and Justice Party, the country's mainstream Islamist party, had already won a high percentage of the seats awarded in the first and second rounds of voting. Two of the nine provinces voting today are Brotherhood strongholds, where some of the party's most well-known candidates are running, the New York Times reports. A plurality, not a majority, was originally predicted for the Freedom and Justice Party. A majority would allow the party to govern alone, but the Brotherhood has repeatedly said it plans to form a coalition government, and has aligned itself with more liberal parties rather than the ultraconservative Salafis, who are in second place with up to 25% of the seats so far. Also today, prosecutors began presenting their case against former Egyptian President Hosni Mubarak, Bloomberg notes. His trial resumed last week after a two-month break; state TV today showed Mubarak being carried to and from the courtroom on a gurney. \"He deserves an end of humiliation and indignity,\" said the chief prosecutor, according to the AP."], "length": 2661, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Egypt Holds Final Round of Voting Amidst Ongoing Trial of Hosni Mubarak**\n\nEgypt is holding its final round of parliamentary elections, with Islamist groups expected to consolidate their gains. The Muslim Brotherhood's Freedom and Justice party is leading in the polls, but its leader, Essam el-Erian, has downplayed its electoral strength. The party has played down its chances of winning a clear majority, instead seeking to form a coalition or unity government.\n\nMeanwhile, the trial of former President Hosni Mubarak has resumed, with the chief prosecutor accusing him of imposing \"tyrannical rule\" and devoting the last decade of his power to ensure his son's succession. Mubarak faces charges of complicity in the killing of over 800 protesters, and his two sons, Gamal and Alaa, are also on trial for corruption charges.\n\nThe trial has been bogged down in procedural matters, but the prosecutor has delivered a scathing critique of Mubarak's rule, branding him a corrupt man whose lust for power will forever tarnish his legacy. Mubarak could face the death penalty if convicted of complicity in the killing of protesters.\n\nThe ongoing trial and parliamentary elections come amidst growing opposition to the ruling generals, who say they will not cede power before presidential elections are held by the end of June. The military rulers have scheduled the first session of the new Parliament for January 23, but the Brotherhood has expressed concerns about the lack of power and authority granted to the legislative body.\n\nThe economic situation in Egypt remains dire, with economic growth slowing down due to unrest and a decline in foreign investment. The country's foreign-currency reserves have declined by 44% to $20.2 billion in the first 11 months of 2011, and the benchmark EGX 30 stock index has lost 49% last year.\n\nThe anniversary of the mass rallies that drove Mubarak from power is approaching on January 25, and protesters are preparing to take to the streets to mark the occasion. The trial of Mubarak and the parliamentary elections are expected to have a significant impact on the country's future, with many Egyptians demanding accountability for the actions of the former regime.\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n\n* Islamist groups are expected to consolidate their gains in the parliamentary elections.\n* The trial of Hosni Mubarak has resumed, with the chief prosecutor accusing him of imposing \"tyrannical rule\".\n* Mubarak faces charges of complicity in the killing of over 800 protesters, and"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Police in Vermont say a car ended up almost vertical when the driver swerved quickly in response to her GPS ordering her to \"turn around.\" The car was suspended almost vertically on guide wires attached to a utility pole (see a photo here) in Mendon on Wednesday night, reports the AP. Police say 30-year-old Nabila Altahan of Dorchester, Mass., was headed west on US Route 4 when she passed her intended destination and reacted quickly to the instructions, leaving the road at a significant enough speed to propel the vehicle up the wires. She wasn't injured. (This driver followed her GPS into a lake.)"], "length": 310, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Local Incident Involves Suspended Car on Telephone Wire**\n\nA dramatic incident occurred in Mendon, Vermont, on Wednesday evening when a Toyota Corolla driven by Nabila Altahan was suspended from a telephone wire after the driver reacted too quickly to a GPS system's instructions. The car, which was traveling westbound on Route 4, left the lane of traffic when the GPS device gave sudden directions to turn around, as the driver had passed its intended destination. Altahan's quick reaction resulted in the car becoming airborne and suspending from the guy wire of a telephone pole.\n\nFortunately, Altahan and her passenger were not injured in the incident. Police and emergency responders, including the Rutland City Fire Department, were quickly dispatched to the scene. A witness at a nearby miniature golf course reported seeing the driver jump out of the car as it was suspended from the pole.\n\nThe incident serves as a reminder to exercise caution when following GPS directions, especially in situations where the driver may have passed their intended destination. Thankfully, no one was seriously harmed in this unusual and unexpected incident."}
